 true] [17592186192583 1020 #edb/ref 17592186258453 13194139533864 true] [17592186192583 1020 #edb/ref 17592186261627 13194139533864 true] [17592186192584 1020 #edb/ref 17592186257621 13194139533864 true] [17592186192584 1020 #edb/ref 17592186257631 13194139533864 true] [17592186192584 1020 #edb/ref 17592186258467 13194139533864 true] [17592186192584 1020 #edb/ref 17592186258477 13194139533864 true] [17592186192584 1020 #edb/ref 17592186258487 13194139533864 true] [17592186192584 1020 #edb/ref 17592186260647 13194139533864 true] [17592186192603 1020 #edb/ref 17592186257611 13194139533864 true] [17592186192603 1020 #edb/ref 17592186258457 13194139533864 true] [17592186192604 1020 #edb/ref 17592186258497 13194139533864 true] [17592186192604 1020 #edb/ref 17592186260637 13194139533864 true] [17592186192640 1020 #edb/ref 17592186258344 13194139533864 true] [17592186192640 1020 #edb/ref 17592186258347 13194139533864 true] [17592186192641 1020 #edb/ref 17592186258350 13194139533864 true] [17592186192641 1020 #edb/ref 17592186258353 13194139533864 true] [17592186198246 1020 #edb/ref 17592186260383 13194139533864 true] [17592186198246 1020 #edb/ref 17592186260386 13194139533864 true] [17592186198378 1020 #edb/ref 17592186258732 13194139533864 true] [17592186203956 1020 #edb/ref 17592186259048 13194139533864 true] [17592186212955 1020 #edb/ref 17592186260083 13194139533864 true] [17592186216973 1020 #edb/ref 17592186259412 13194139533864 true] [17592186217100 1020 #edb/ref 17592186258132 13194139533864 true] [17592186256429 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256430 13194139533864 true] [17592186256429 1089 "q-10018" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256429 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256429 1100 "Calculate the sum to infinity of the geometric series $9+0.9+0.09+0.009+⋯$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256429 1113 #uuid "ccfa9d31-45ab-5f64-a4e2-3d1fa5018f4c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256429 1145 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term is ${a}_{1}=9$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{0.9}{9}=0.1.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{1-0.1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{0.9} \\\\  & =\\frac{90}{9} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256430 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256431 13194139533864 true] [17592186256430 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256430 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256430 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256431 13194139533864 true] [17592186256430 1110 #uuid "486b2df1-d1b4-528c-b25e-61fee88dc812" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256431 1034 "10" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256431 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256431 1111 #uuid "110c7289-9e87-588c-8839-082ce62fa8b8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256432 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256433 13194139533864 true] [17592186256432 1089 "q-100235" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256432 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256432 1100 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(2{x}^{4}+4)^{2}}{(x-2)^{3}}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256432 1113 #uuid "d37a6373-85f7-5021-93bf-bde115d91e8c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256432 1145 "Here, we apply the quotient rule and the power rule to get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(2{x}^{4}+4)^{2}}{(x-2)^{3}} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to -1}(2{x}^{4}+4)^{2}}{{lim}_{x\\to -1}(x-2)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{({lim}_{x\\to -1}(2{x}^{4}+4))}^{2}}{{({lim}_{x\\to -1}(x-2))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2(-1)^{4}+4)^{2}}{(-1-2)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6)^{2}}{(-3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{-27} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256433 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256434 13194139533864 true] [17592186256433 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256433 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256433 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256434 13194139533864 true] [17592186256433 1110 #uuid "baff0e2d-9b61-5780-9004-7951a0b3c029" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256434 1034 "-\\frac{4}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256434 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256434 1111 #uuid "3d496f36-d9ee-5b26-b44c-c1968857daf9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256435 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256436 13194139533864 true] [17592186256435 1089 "q-100236" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256435 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256435 1100 "Find ${lim}_{y\\to -2}{({y}^{3}+2)}^{3}(2y+3).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256435 1113 #uuid "91317a16-9fb5-5d43-9550-4a0b2404efdf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256435 1145 "Here, we apply the product rule and the power rule to get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to -2}{({y}^{3}+2)}^{3}(2y+3) & =({lim}_{y\\to -2}({y}^{3}+2)^{3})\\cdot ({lim}_{y\\to -2}(2y+3)) \\\\  & ={({lim}_{y\\to -2}({y}^{3}+2))}^{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{y\\to -2}(2y+3)) \\\\  & =((-2)^{3}+2)^{3}\\cdot (2\\cdot (-2)+3) \\\\  & =(-6)^{3}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =216.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256436 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256437 13194139533864 true] [17592186256436 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256436 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256436 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256437 13194139533864 true] [17592186256436 1110 #uuid "32124313-f85d-5185-9fc8-c729515e34da" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256437 1034 "216" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256437 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256437 1111 #uuid "b4271776-2565-508b-a4c0-05856a0d5c1a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256438 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256439 13194139533864 true] [17592186256438 1089 "q-100264" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256438 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256438 1100 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to 4}(\\sqrt[3]{({x}^{2}-3x-5)^{5}}-\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{3}}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256438 1113 #uuid "855a5e48-e137-5598-94f7-f4832740f0f7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256438 1145 "Applying the sum and root rules, we get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}(\\sqrt[3]{({x}^{2}-3x-5)^{5}}-\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{3}}) & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}\\sqrt[3]{({x}^{2}-3x-5)^{5}}-{lim}_{x\\to 4}\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{{({lim}_{x\\to 4}({x}^{2}-3x-5))}^{5}}-\\sqrt{{({lim}_{x\\to 4}(2x-7))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{({4}^{2}-3(4)-5)^{5}}-\\sqrt{(2(4)-7)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{(16-12-5)^{5}}-\\sqrt{(8-7)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{(-1)^{5}}-\\sqrt{(1)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{-1}-\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256439 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256440 13194139533864 true] [17592186256439 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256439 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256439 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256440 13194139533864 true] [17592186256439 1110 #uuid "d92060c6-8853-5b65-a4a6-b4eb8d49cf80" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256440 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256440 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256440 1111 #uuid "2e7f0189-8bdb-58cf-8aaa-952657ad5d46" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256441 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256442 13194139533864 true] [17592186256441 1089 "q-100273" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256441 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256441 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/fda731fa3c9a3401e9b9e2a7d4c103829d3fe3b14ac9d6f2872b76a319f6ae91.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256441 1113 #uuid "c3c8ab44-204b-5d29-bcb7-af809bc21a89" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256441 1145 "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2}={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x)))}^{3} \\\\  & =(2(1)^{3}-4(1))^{3} \\\\  & =(-2)^{3} \\\\  & =-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3))}^{2} \\\\  & =(2(1)^{4}-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =-8\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256442 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256443 13194139533864 true] [17592186256442 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256442 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256442 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256443 13194139533864 true] [17592186256442 1110 #uuid "540fe2c8-dadb-5db3-8dab-b24c95763f08" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256443 1034 "-8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256443 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256443 1111 #uuid "d5498add-bc06-5903-9efe-ef81027091af" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256444 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256445 13194139533864 true] [17592186256444 1089 "q-100275" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256444 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256444 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/e8aac08a40b3a318cb682afca1783a8d9563bf67f0b51bdff2ead8a9bf28117c.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4)^{2}(4-2f(x))^{5}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256444 1113 #uuid "beb0d5cc-39b2-586c-83f5-8cf543207207" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256444 1145 "From the graph we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)=2.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4)^{2}(4-2f(x))^{5}={lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4)^{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -2}(4-2f(x))^{5}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4)^{2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4))}^{2} \\\\  & ={((-2)^{3}+4)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(-8+4)}^{2} \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}(4-2f(x))^{5} & ={({lim}_{x\\to -2}(4-2f(x)))}^{5} \\\\  & ={(4-2(2))}^{5} \\\\  & ={(4-4)}^{5} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4)^{2}(4-2f(x))^{5} & ={({lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4))}^{2}{({lim}_{x\\to -2}(4-2f(x)))}^{5} \\\\  & =16\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256445 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256446 13194139533864 true] [17592186256445 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256445 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256445 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256446 13194139533864 true] [17592186256445 1110 #uuid "99476705-d581-5b54-a13c-b34652061ee2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256446 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256446 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256446 1111 #uuid "7d6a2bcd-3314-5ee1-8472-b7a09d44d3db" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256447 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256448 13194139533864 true] [17592186256447 1089 "q-100277" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256447 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256447 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ddf4b2d129022e27627afa1b0946f18ef7e98ab2ffe20dea6d609bedce65f84d.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3}(3f(x)-4x)^{2}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256447 1113 #uuid "d37402e0-45e5-5794-8d62-3744bcf93a6d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256447 1145 "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=2.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3}(3f(x)-4x)^{2}={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)-4x)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x))}^{3} \\\\  & =(1-3(1))^{3} \\\\  & =(-2)^{3} \\\\  & =-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)-4x)^{2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)-4x))}^{2} \\\\  & =(3(2)-4(1))^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3}(3f(x)-4x)^{2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)-4x)^{2} \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot (4) \\\\  & =-32\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256448 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256448 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256448 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256448 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256448 1110 #uuid "5df85a12-1e10-5d7f-ad90-818a82b74222" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256449 1034 "-32" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256449 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256449 1111 #uuid "e708382f-3705-58a5-9a18-333922210b38" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256450 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256451 13194139533864 true] [17592186256450 1089 "q-100279" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256450 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256450 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/a4f8b046dd85d258dd897e595b5dba1116e5df763e2e74a196c6b79e2a8a42a5.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x)^{3}(f(x)-4{x}^{2})^{5}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256450 1113 #uuid "e66e2dc4-df2c-55ad-b6a2-790f10160f6d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256450 1145 "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=3.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x)^{3}(f(x)-4{x}^{2})^{5}={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(f(x)-4{x}^{2})^{5}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x)^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x))}^{3} \\\\  & =(3-1)^{3} \\\\  & ={2}^{3} \\\\  & =8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(f(x)-4{x}^{2})^{4} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(f(x)-4{x}^{2}))}^{5} \\\\  & =(3-4(1)^{2})^{5} \\\\  & =(-1)^{5} \\\\  & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x)^{3}(f(x)-4{x}^{2})^{5} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(f(x)-4{x}^{2})^{5} \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-8\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256451 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256452 13194139533864 true] [17592186256451 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256451 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256451 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256452 13194139533864 true] [17592186256451 1110 #uuid "1a8505c7-dc76-5a01-b1fb-0b42c653f484" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256452 1034 "-8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256452 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256452 1111 #uuid "41725411-aa61-591c-bcf8-2a9591aba381" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256453 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256453 1089 "q-100282" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256453 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256453 1100 "Find the exact solution of the equation ${e}^{t}=11.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256453 1113 #uuid "272af2d8-c0a8-526a-8af7-4492ae771147" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256453 1145 "First, we find the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & =11 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}({e}^{t}) & =\\operatorname{ln}(11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nt=\\operatorname{ln}(11)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $t=\\operatorname{ln}(11).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256454 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256455 13194139533864 true] [17592186256454 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256454 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256454 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256455 13194139533864 true] [17592186256454 1110 #uuid "9e3000f4-f290-5e72-a98c-b7f4789a1dc9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256455 1034 "\\operatorname{ln}(11)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256455 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256455 1111 #uuid "c271d2f6-11aa-5258-b730-953ddc737b00" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256456 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256457 13194139533864 true] [17592186256456 1089 "q-100298" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256456 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256456 1100 "Given that ${e}^{6w+3}+7=11,$ then $w=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256456 1113 #uuid "cfa4842f-c68c-5f3f-b781-f769f01ff4ba" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256456 1145 "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6w+3}+7=11 \\\\ {e}^{6w+3} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $w:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6w+3} & =4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}({e}^{6w+3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}(4) \\\\ 6w+3 & =\\operatorname{ln}(4) \\\\ 6w & =\\operatorname{ln}(4)-3 \\\\ w & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{ln}(4)-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $w=\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{ln}(4)-3).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256457 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256458 13194139533864 true] [17592186256457 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256457 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256457 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256458 13194139533864 true] [17592186256457 1110 #uuid "d51a48fe-7a47-538d-bbc8-99c17f4dae50" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256458 1034 "\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{ln}(4)-3)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256458 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256458 1111 #uuid "fb93509c-9330-5833-9c2d-9ba51a1dcb1a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256459 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256460 13194139533864 true] [17592186256459 1089 "q-100309" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256459 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256459 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/b9a95898f98ad5289313a0f0fdea24fa8828d508564c8ce60cec986919f16f50.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{3f(x)+1}{\\sqrt{f(x)+3}}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256459 1113 #uuid "8e227243-5897-55da-a196-c0ae80dd04bf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256459 1145 "From the graph, we get that  ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.$ \n\nApplying the root rule, we get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{3f(x)+1}{\\sqrt{f(x)+3}} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)+1)}{\\sqrt{{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(f(x)+3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1)+1}{\\sqrt{(1)+3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256460 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256461 13194139533864 true] [17592186256460 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256460 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256460 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256461 13194139533864 true] [17592186256460 1110 #uuid "f09d06c1-b038-5f3e-9fc6-ebed92785b51" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256461 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256461 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256461 1111 #uuid "c2ba61a5-7ca0-5c0e-ad58-80bf00371d48" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256462 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256462 1089 "q-100459" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256462 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256462 1100 "If ${216}^{3a+4}={(\\frac{1}{36})}^{6a-2}$ then $a=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256462 1113 #uuid "50d2f9b4-0b9f-53dd-8422-aae20f0d13ad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256462 1145 "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $216={6}^{3}$ and $36={6}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({6}^{3})}^{3a+4}={(\\frac{1}{{6}^{2}})}^{6a-2}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule and the quotient rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{3(3a+4)}=\\frac{1}{{6}^{2(6a-2)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using a negative exponent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{3(3a+4)}={6}^{-2(6a-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(6)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(3a+4) & =-2(6a-2) \\\\ 9a+12 & =-12a+4 \\\\ 21a & =-8 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{8}{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256463 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256463 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256463 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256463 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256463 1110 #uuid "d70aba23-4e6c-5410-8e82-4fd86d1e5e21" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256464 1034 "-\\frac{8}{21}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256464 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256464 1111 #uuid "bd368582-0175-5f86-aa74-163e6a090acf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256465 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256466 13194139533864 true] [17592186256465 1089 "q-100627" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256465 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256465 1100 "If $-7{log}_{3}(x-26)=-42$, then $x=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256465 1113 #uuid "fb813bd0-6d03-5ce7-aeed-ba5a66fe6313" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256465 1145 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7{log}_{3}(x-26) & =-42 \\\\ {log}_{3}(x-26) & =\\frac{-42}{-7} \\\\ {log}_{3}(x-26) & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $3,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}(x-26) & =6 \\\\ {3}^{{log}_{3}(x-26)} & ={3}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${3}^{{log}_{3}(x-26)}=x-26.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-26 & ={3}^{6} \\\\ x-26 & =729 \\\\ x & =755.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256466 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256467 13194139533864 true] [17592186256466 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256466 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256466 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256467 13194139533864 true] [17592186256466 1110 #uuid "681993c1-a32e-56ef-8e99-9cd83ba96239" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256467 1034 "755" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256467 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256467 1111 #uuid "42bf02f2-51de-5cf2-b795-a860c928c6fe" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256468 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256469 13194139533864 true] [17592186256468 1089 "q-100631" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256468 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256468 1100 "If ${log}_{8}x+2{log}_{8}x=2$, then what is the value of  $x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256468 1113 #uuid "64cb2061-188e-5a28-a695-11fcdda45850" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256468 1145 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}x+2{log}_{8}x & =2 \\\\ (1+2){log}_{8}x & =2 \\\\ 3{log}_{8}x & =2 \\\\ {log}_{8}x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $8,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}x & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {8}^{{log}_{8}x} & ={8}^{2/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${8}^{{log}_{8}x}=x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={8}^{2/3} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{64} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256470 13194139533864 true] [17592186256469 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256469 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256469 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256470 13194139533864 true] [17592186256469 1110 #uuid "b23ee9cc-4294-5159-9632-fc1db62d39ad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256470 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256470 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256470 1111 #uuid "07010dc9-1e29-58cf-80ff-96a56d9fba1d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256471 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256472 13194139533864 true] [17592186256471 1089 "q-100633" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256471 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256471 1100 "If $2{log}_{2}x+5{log}_{2}x=1$, then what is the value of  $x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256471 1113 #uuid "4b6581d3-c6a3-525b-95f8-5987b693d04f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256471 1145 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{log}_{2}x+5{log}_{2}x & =1 \\\\ (2+5){log}_{2}x & =1 \\\\ 7{log}_{2}x & =1 \\\\ {log}_{2}x & =\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $2,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}x & =\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}x} & ={2}^{1/7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${2}^{{log}_{2}x}=x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={2}^{1/7} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[7]{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256472 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256473 13194139533864 true] [17592186256472 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256472 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256472 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256473 13194139533864 true] [17592186256472 1110 #uuid "a8c821d2-18f4-53f0-a9fb-a0da48e7e57f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256473 1034 "\\sqrt[7]{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256473 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256473 1111 #uuid "9367309a-4896-58c2-951d-722ff8c0c2c0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256474 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256475 13194139533864 true] [17592186256474 1089 "q-100639" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256474 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256474 1100 "Find $x$ such that $\\operatorname{ln}(8x)=10.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256474 1113 #uuid "b12403ef-850e-548f-8484-3ee9e4f60e11" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256474 1145 "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}(8x) & =10 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}(8x)} & ={e}^{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}(8x)}=8x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x & ={e}^{10} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256475 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256476 13194139533864 true] [17592186256475 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256475 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256475 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256476 13194139533864 true] [17592186256475 1110 #uuid "a8bda06a-5d83-50b7-a84c-d83c1a289d71" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256476 1034 "\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{10}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256476 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256476 1111 #uuid "d302d403-1084-5723-84c3-a82eabb281b6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256477 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256478 13194139533864 true] [17592186256477 1089 "q-100673" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256477 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256477 1100 "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{18}(41)$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256477 1113 #uuid "51214ed3-9217-5f35-8c62-58f93e8509fd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256477 1145 "Let's calculate ${log}_{18}(41)$ by changing the base $18$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=18,$ $X=41,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{18}(41) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}(41)}{{log}_{10}(18)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(41)}{\\operatorname{log}(18)} \\\\  & \\approx \\frac{1.612\\,784}{1.255\\,273} \\\\  & \\approx 1.285\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256478 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256479 13194139533864 true] [17592186256478 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256478 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256478 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256479 13194139533864 true] [17592186256478 1110 #uuid "f19c826c-626c-5bc4-83fd-030e4431b402" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256479 1034 "1.285" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256479 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256479 1111 #uuid "c3dbb7fe-750e-5953-9a22-6daaaa63fb4a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256480 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256481 13194139533864 true] [17592186256480 1089 "q-100674" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256480 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256480 1100 "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{15}(10)$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256480 1113 #uuid "70c8405e-c25e-581b-907c-1bd10f59fc85" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256480 1145 "Let's calculate ${log}_{15}(10)$ by changing the base $15$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=15,$ $X=10,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{15}(10) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}(10)}{{log}_{10}(15)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(10)}{\\operatorname{log}(15)} \\\\  & \\approx \\frac{1}{1.176\\,091} \\\\  & \\approx 0.850\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256481 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256482 13194139533864 true] [17592186256481 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256481 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256481 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256482 13194139533864 true] [17592186256481 1110 #uuid "2da1e781-a4dc-50ed-92d1-c932089c7436" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256482 1034 "0.850" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256482 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256482 1111 #uuid "53710983-408a-5a6c-90ea-b34ff38359ea" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256483 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256484 13194139533864 true] [17592186256483 1089 "q-100675" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256483 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256483 1100 "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{8}(30)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256483 1113 #uuid "c2f720ea-4efd-5c38-87f6-787d4de5f50b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256483 1145 "Let's calculate ${log}_{8}(30)$ by changing the base $8$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=8,$ $X=30,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}(30) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}(30)}{{log}_{10}(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(30)}{\\operatorname{log}(8)} \\\\  & \\approx \\frac{1.477\\,12}{0.903\\,09} \\\\  & \\approx 1.64\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256484 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256485 13194139533864 true] [17592186256484 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256484 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256484 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256485 13194139533864 true] [17592186256484 1110 #uuid "722b0dfa-4a0b-5cc5-9de6-df174301443b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256485 1034 "1.64" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256485 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256485 1111 #uuid "28c51bc3-a49c-557b-86c0-c22af227fbd2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256486 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256487 13194139533864 true] [17592186256486 1089 "q-100676" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256486 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256486 1100 "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{22}(20)$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256486 1113 #uuid "2c2c3dd1-20e9-5368-9ec4-088bb43cb692" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256486 1145 "Let's calculate ${log}_{22}(20)$ by changing the base $22$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=22,$ $X=20,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{22}(20) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}(20)}{{log}_{10}(22)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(20)}{\\operatorname{log}(22)} \\\\  & \\approx \\frac{1.301\\,030}{1.342\\,423} \\\\  & \\approx 0.969\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256487 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256488 13194139533864 true] [17592186256487 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256487 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256487 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256488 13194139533864 true] [17592186256487 1110 #uuid "601db05c-084b-5144-a803-3fc02ba6ccb5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256488 1034 "0.969" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256488 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256488 1111 #uuid "28a98f76-933b-551d-9eab-cfe391420e07" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256489 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256490 13194139533864 true] [17592186256489 1089 "q-100706" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256489 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256489 1100 "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{log}(x-2)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}(256).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256489 1113 #uuid "b2471a67-c72a-575a-8f10-13bf4266225f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256489 1145 "First, we use the power rule to simplify the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}(256) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}({256}^{1/4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}(4) \\\\ x-2 & =4 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=6$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}(6-2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}({256}^{1/4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}(256).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=6$ is a valid solution." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256490 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256491 13194139533864 true] [17592186256490 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256490 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256490 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256491 13194139533864 true] [17592186256490 1110 #uuid "632752f1-0d8d-5dfe-b7aa-4366797c3958" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256491 1034 "6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256491 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256491 1111 #uuid "a1d337be-4dbd-5c73-afde-d1f2e603c6c7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256492 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256493 13194139533864 true] [17592186256492 1089 "q-100724" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256492 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256492 1100 "If $4\\operatorname{ln}x-2=10,$ what is the value of $x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256492 1113 #uuid "28439940-5e97-5723-8894-149495e6acc9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256492 1145 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{ln}x-2 & =10 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{ln}x & =12 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}x & =3 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}x} & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}x}=x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256493 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256494 13194139533864 true] [17592186256493 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256493 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256493 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256494 13194139533864 true] [17592186256493 1110 #uuid "de28d2f2-f820-5484-b1c6-4b3d3d73663c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256494 1034 "{e}^{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256494 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256494 1111 #uuid "2d9f78c7-b00b-5d6d-854b-cf843da8c6e4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256495 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256496 13194139533864 true] [17592186256495 1089 "q-100737" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256495 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256495 1100 "If $11\\operatorname{ln}x-7=15,$ what is the value of $x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256495 1113 #uuid "4b63ef9f-9d27-55a5-91e1-cd4230085c7e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256495 1145 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11\\operatorname{ln}x-7 & =15 \\\\ 11\\operatorname{ln}x & =22 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}x & =2 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}x} & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}x}=x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256496 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256497 13194139533864 true] [17592186256496 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256496 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256496 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256497 13194139533864 true] [17592186256496 1110 #uuid "67a37061-8884-5cce-a5ce-ff3fa2c26def" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256497 1034 "{e}^{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256497 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256497 1111 #uuid "b7b27e24-4c89-53c9-9a57-6d238728d0f0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256498 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256499 13194139533864 true] [17592186256498 1089 "q-100771" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256498 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256498 1100 "Solve the equation ${log}_{5}({v}^{2}+36)={log}_{5}(-16v-28).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256498 1113 #uuid "501f700f-e881-502f-a49c-ed376789d61f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256498 1145 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}({v}^{2}+36) & ={log}_{5}(-16v-28) \\\\ {v}^{2}+36 & =-16v-28 \\\\ {v}^{2}+16v+64 & =0 \\\\ (v+8)^{2} & =0 \\\\ v & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $v=-8$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}({v}^{2}+36) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{5}(-16v-28) \\\\ {log}_{5}((-8)^{2}+36) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{5}(-16(-8)-28) \\\\ {log}_{5}(100) & ={log}_{5}(100).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v=-8$ is a valid solution." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256499 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256500 13194139533864 true] [17592186256499 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256499 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256499 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256500 13194139533864 true] [17592186256499 1110 #uuid "2552e629-ed36-5d2e-a592-f43ee66159f7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256500 1034 "-8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256500 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256500 1111 #uuid "23c87e17-e068-58e6-b62b-35ecceb2fd6a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256501 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256502 13194139533864 true] [17592186256501 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256504 13194139533864 true] [17592186256501 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256506 13194139533864 true] [17592186256501 1089 "q-100989" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256501 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256501 1100 "If $a=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=j-3k$, then $a-3b=$\n\nRow 1, column 1: {{r1c1}}\n\nRow 2, column 1: {{r2c1}}\n\nRow 3, column 1: {{r3c1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256501 1113 #uuid "ca9737e4-6571-57db-8572-7d99e7234d46" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256501 1145 "First, notice that\n\n$$\nb=j-3k=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute $a-3b,$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-3b & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =⟨-6,-8,11⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256502 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256503 13194139533864 true] [17592186256502 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256502 1041 "r1c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256502 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256503 13194139533864 true] [17592186256502 1110 #uuid "a47fbca2-eeb1-52f3-9480-f2d0f75bab35" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256503 1034 "-6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256503 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256503 1111 #uuid "4572ec97-bfa4-5060-b8f4-cbf12f7c3e97" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256504 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256505 13194139533864 true] [17592186256504 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256504 1041 "r2c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256504 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256505 13194139533864 true] [17592186256504 1110 #uuid "ccd71f6f-8015-5fb7-b775-c33622baa306" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256505 1034 "-8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256505 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256505 1111 #uuid "ff6aa3ec-8276-54a8-b2c9-eaa021198cd3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256506 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256507 13194139533864 true] [17592186256506 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256506 1041 "r3c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256506 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256507 13194139533864 true] [17592186256506 1110 #uuid "6ceb37b1-8699-5b9c-9eba-dc3db48f29bf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256507 1034 "11" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256507 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256507 1111 #uuid "4e2c1459-2e82-5849-8376-a3f7512617f0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256508 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256509 13194139533864 true] [17592186256508 1089 "q-101004" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256508 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256508 1100 "Let $C(3,8,3)$ and $D(-5,0,-6).$ Find $|c+d|,$ where $c$ and $d$ are position vectors of $C$ and $D$, respectively.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256508 1113 #uuid "d0e28f21-d62b-50a2-a5e7-709c0e5d467d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256508 1145 "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OC}=c=3i+8j+3k, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OD}=d=-5i-6k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates of $c+d$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c+d & =(3i+8j+3k)+(-5i-6k) \\\\  & =-2i+8j-3k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|c+d| & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{8}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+64+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{77}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256509 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256510 13194139533864 true] [17592186256509 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256509 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256509 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256510 13194139533864 true] [17592186256509 1110 #uuid "eea4ff69-fefd-5dbe-a891-b6cbaf653531" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256510 1034 "\\sqrt{77}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256510 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256510 1111 #uuid "7dc3c841-d079-5e34-8f06-54f8a744419b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256511 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256512 13194139533864 true] [17592186256511 1089 "q-101008" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256511 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256511 1100 "Let $R(-1,1,4)$ and $S(8,-2,0).$ Find $|3r+s|,$ where $r$ and $s$ are position vectors of $R$ and $S$, respectively.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256511 1113 #uuid "3212fc88-66cd-514c-8c22-3497355c10c8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256511 1145 "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OR}=r=-i+j+4k, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OS}=s=8i-2j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates of $3r+s$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3r+s & =3(-i+j+4k)+(8i-2j) \\\\  & =5i+j+12k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3r+s| & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+1+144} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{170}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256512 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256513 13194139533864 true] [17592186256512 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256512 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256512 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256513 13194139533864 true] [17592186256512 1110 #uuid "5b628e84-1b00-593b-802a-bde3fac36696" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256513 1034 "\\sqrt{170}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256513 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256513 1111 #uuid "f2447bf3-cf3d-5961-a778-5fabc47860c8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256514 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256515 13194139533864 true] [17592186256514 1089 "q-101019" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256514 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256514 1100 "Let $A(6,7,6)$ and $B(8,9,5).$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overset{\\to }{AB}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256514 1113 #uuid "e20ebdd4-c7c2-56fa-9bfe-46ac87997401" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256514 1145 "First, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=6i+7j+6k, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=8i+9j+5k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}-\\overset{\\to }{OA} \\\\  & =b-a \\\\  & =8i+9j+5k-(6i+7j+6k) \\\\  & =2i+2j-k \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{|\\,\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\,| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{|\\,\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\overset{\\to }{AB} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ⟨2,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256515 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256516 13194139533864 true] [17592186256515 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256515 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256515 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256516 13194139533864 true] [17592186256515 1110 #uuid "bd11dc2f-7a5a-5c9a-82c3-ffed82fec8be" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256516 1034 "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3}⟩" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256516 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256516 1111 #uuid "ff8ce8d9-c2af-5d66-a92b-8af27dd7e627" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256517 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256518 13194139533864 true] [17592186256517 1089 "q-101283" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256517 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256517 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{4x-3}-3}{x-3}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256517 1113 #uuid "dcdef879-b89d-50b8-80c4-887502ec74b8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256517 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{4x-3}-3}{x-3} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4(3)-3}-3}{3-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{9}-3}{3-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{4x-3}-3}{x-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(\\sqrt{4x-3}-3)(\\sqrt{4x-3}+3)}{(x-3)(\\sqrt{4x-3}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(\\sqrt{4x-3})^{2}-{3}^{2}}{(x-3)(\\sqrt{4x-3}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{4x-3-9}{(x-3)(\\sqrt{4x-3}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{4x-12}{(x-3)(\\sqrt{4x-3}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{4(x-3)}{(x-3)(\\sqrt{4x-3}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{4x-3}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{4(3)-3}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{9}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256519 13194139533864 true] [17592186256518 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256518 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256518 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256519 13194139533864 true] [17592186256518 1110 #uuid "4b02b9b9-c5e3-5283-8cd8-754b72a740d1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256519 1034 "\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256519 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256519 1111 #uuid "d13820a1-ba4a-5136-8fb8-a1221aba9da4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256520 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256521 13194139533864 true] [17592186256520 1089 "q-101287" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256520 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256520 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{5x+6}-4}{x-2}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256520 1113 #uuid "36d75191-edf3-543e-8a14-a081f1ebcbf0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256520 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{5x+6}-4}{x-2} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5(2)+6}-4}{2-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{16}-4}{2-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{5x+6}-4}{x-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(\\sqrt{5x+6}-4)(\\sqrt{5x+6}+4)}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{5x+6}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(\\sqrt{5x+6})^{2}-{4}^{2}}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{5x+6}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{5x+6-16}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{5x+6}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{5x-10}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{5x+6}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{5(x-2)}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{5x+6}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5x+6}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5(2)+6}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{16}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256521 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256522 13194139533864 true] [17592186256521 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256521 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256521 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256522 13194139533864 true] [17592186256521 1110 #uuid "ca6a3251-a073-53a9-83e3-b4e5a74fd2c7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256522 1034 "\\frac{5}{8}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256522 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256522 1111 #uuid "3e12db33-cf16-5171-9f31-62a450f113d4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256523 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256524 13194139533864 true] [17592186256523 1089 "q-101289" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256523 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256523 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3x+4}-2}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256523 1113 #uuid "e2dca2c7-e791-5c46-b7d1-0efafbd3a36b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256523 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=0,$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3x+4}-2} & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{3(0)+4}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{4}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3x+4}-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{3x+4}+2)}{(\\sqrt{3x+4}-2)(\\sqrt{3x+4}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{3x+4}+2)}{(\\sqrt{3x+4})^{2}-{2}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{3x+4}+2)}{3x+4-4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{3x+4}+2)}{3x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{3x+4}+2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3(0)+4}+2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4}+2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256524 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256525 13194139533864 true] [17592186256524 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256524 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256524 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256525 13194139533864 true] [17592186256524 1110 #uuid "a9343017-3844-5b91-8ba3-a792c44abc57" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256525 1034 "\\frac{4}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256525 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256525 1111 #uuid "3605469f-e409-5a67-b3c3-c8fb261b444b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256526 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256527 13194139533864 true] [17592186256526 1089 "q-101625" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256526 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256526 1100 "If $p+2=3(p-3)+6,$ then $p=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256526 1113 #uuid "486b29b8-ce2e-59f6-a2b3-65c777f54464" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256526 1145 "First, we expand the parentheses on the right-hand-side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p+2 & =3(p-3)+6 \\\\ p+2 & =3\\cdot p-3\\cdot 3+6 \\\\ p+2 & =3p-9+6 \\\\ p+2 & =3p-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must now bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side. \n\nNotice that we have $p$ on the left-hand side and $3p$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the right-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side. \n\nThe first step is to remove the $p$ from the left-hand side of the equation. We do this by subtracting $p$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p+2 & =3p-3 \\\\ p+2-p & =3p-3-p \\\\ 2 & =2p-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we remove the $-3$ from the right-hand side. We do this by adding $3$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2p-3 \\\\ 2+3 & =2p-3+3 \\\\ 5 & =2p\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} & =\\frac{2p}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =\\frac{\\cancel{2}p}{\\cancel{2}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =p \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256527 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256528 13194139533864 true] [17592186256527 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256527 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256527 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256528 13194139533864 true] [17592186256527 1110 #uuid "12ffd4e0-bfb9-5b97-9e69-8e43ad3307ba" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256528 1034 "\\frac{5}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256528 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256528 1111 #uuid "4de44592-363f-53b0-9452-63f02463612d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256529 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256530 13194139533864 true] [17592186256529 1089 "q-101719" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256529 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256529 1100 "Given that $y=7-4{log}_{3}x$, find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}3}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256529 1113 #uuid "5712e498-177c-5ff5-9270-29807a04505c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256529 1145 "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}a}.\n$$\n\nTo find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7-4{log}_{3}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7)-4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{3}x) \\\\  & =0-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{x\\operatorname{ln}3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1/\\operatorname{ln}3$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1/\\operatorname{ln}3} & ={(-\\frac{4}{x\\operatorname{ln}3})\\,|}_{x=1/\\operatorname{ln}3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256530 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256531 13194139533864 true] [17592186256530 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256530 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256530 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256531 13194139533864 true] [17592186256530 1110 #uuid "daadac8d-e9fa-50ed-9624-0e832b2c29a3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256531 1034 "-4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256531 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256531 1111 #uuid "e6ece883-5457-512b-817a-6a540f5cafb4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256532 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256533 13194139533864 true] [17592186256532 1089 "q-101723" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256532 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256532 1100 "Given that $y=4{e}^{x}-3{log}_{7}x$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=3.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256532 1113 #uuid "0d14b209-f1c5-59b3-ba28-3ccabf73112a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256532 1145 "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4{e}^{x}-\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}7}\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope  of the tangent at $x=3$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=3} & ={(4{e}^{x}-\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}7})|}_{x=3} \\\\  & =4{e}^{3}-\\frac{3}{3\\operatorname{ln}7} \\\\  & =4{e}^{3}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}7}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256533 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256534 13194139533864 true] [17592186256533 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256533 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256533 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256534 13194139533864 true] [17592186256533 1110 #uuid "affa72be-28fd-5889-9af5-8b7396495f2b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256534 1034 "4{e}^{3}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}7}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256534 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256534 1111 #uuid "43a0d3cd-43bd-5fac-bb10-990cb8b26cd8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256535 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256536 13194139533864 true] [17592186256535 1089 "q-101730" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256535 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256535 1100 "Consider the following table of values for $f(x).$ Knowing that the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-5,5]$ is $-1,$ find the value of $p.$ \n\n| $x$ | $\\,-5\\,$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,3\\,$ | $\\,5\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,4p+1\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,5\\,$ | $\\,10\\,$ | $\\,p+6\\,$ | $\\,8\\,$ |\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256535 1113 #uuid "a9ca4c60-8351-5109-8b21-60704b041116" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256535 1145 "Using the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(5)-f(-5)}{5-(-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(p+6)-(4p+1)}{5+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-3p}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=-1$ on the interval $[-5,5].$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\frac{5-3p}{10}=-1.\n$$\n\nSolving for $p,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5-3p}{10} & =-1 \\\\ 5-3p & =-10 \\\\ -3p & =-10-5 \\\\ -3p & =-15 \\\\ p & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256536 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256537 13194139533864 true] [17592186256536 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256536 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256536 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256537 13194139533864 true] [17592186256536 1110 #uuid "accec121-d526-5711-937f-a3434f20da60" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256537 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256537 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256537 1111 #uuid "92f610ab-f996-522b-87af-a4586c3c9d90" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256538 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256539 13194139533864 true] [17592186256538 1089 "q-101992" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256538 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256538 1100 "Which set represents all odd integers greater than $8?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256538 1113 #uuid "46e08706-7515-5d4c-aacf-8fa37890b6f0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256538 1145 "To express the desired set, we start with the first odd integer greater than $8,$ which is $9.$ The next odd integer is $11,$ the one after that is $13,$ and so on, which we denote with an ellipsis:\n\n$$\n{9,11,13,…}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256539 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256540 13194139533864 true] [17592186256539 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256539 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256539 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256540 13194139533864 true] [17592186256539 1110 #uuid "65dc58dd-a0d2-5b42-99f2-71db2dabf621" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256540 1034 "{9,11,13,…}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256540 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256540 1111 #uuid "a31d182b-1683-5fb8-be8c-2fa373c3d8b6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256541 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256542 13194139533864 true] [17592186256541 1089 "q-102007" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256541 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256541 1100 "Given the inequalities $-4<x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le x<4$, express their common set of solutions as an interval.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256541 1113 #uuid "910e0483-5a9e-5329-b440-7a4f84a1a399" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256541 1145 "We start by sketching the solutions to the two inequalities separately:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/e862d5e61f97b3b1180f07dd80244524534ace031b1b67bb7b681ac1f245de26.png)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/3ade64e9cfb1b5ab14ac31631f870e12b7b597e382c88715f97ca1df081e68d7.png)\n\nThe common set of values consists of the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/4dcbbe3e83c10d02a84d61ab5800edcb38c93f48b23f72026440e8f88b1edca5.png)\n\nTherefore, the common set of values is\n$[-1,2].$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256542 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256543 13194139533864 true] [17592186256542 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256542 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256542 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256543 13194139533864 true] [17592186256542 1110 #uuid "3dbff47b-b9f2-5ad0-a06a-f3b835005058" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256543 1034 "[-1,2]" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256543 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256543 1111 #uuid "e6d1dce9-004e-5769-8953-fb9fe6ec2684" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256544 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256545 13194139533864 true] [17592186256544 1089 "q-102008" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256544 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256544 1100 "Given the inequalities $-2<x\\le 6$ and $-8\\le x\\le 4$, express their common set of solutions as an interval.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256544 1113 #uuid "8e633958-5056-5065-a6af-d8ca7e7764aa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256544 1145 "We start by sketching the solutions to the two inequalities separately:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d27fc4b96afb9056bd7850c1e1c89aa2723574c2dc28c828ec3b01fb0e784f5c.png)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/1dd975bf8c3016e337391f1f5884c5e2b6e1da831c569b89b5dfa850043c01bf.png)\n\nThe common set of values consists of the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/4e4205a4cfdb20ca75723691267ffc55f57d7584c657217a90294eef9e64a07b.png)\n\nTherefore, the common set of values is\n$(-2,4].$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256546 13194139533864 true] [17592186256545 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256545 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256545 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256546 13194139533864 true] [17592186256545 1110 #uuid "efb54118-162c-5ecb-8b80-6722864fce5b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256546 1034 "(-2,4]" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256546 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256546 1111 #uuid "4d2a80c7-7b90-50dc-b319-e47dabd527e8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256547 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256548 13194139533864 true] [17592186256547 1089 "q-102015" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256547 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256547 1100 "Given the inequalities $0<x<5,$ $\\,x\\le 1,$ and $\\,x>4$, What sets, expressed in interval notation, represents their common set of values?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256547 1113 #uuid "f6a98b18-dfc3-507c-9faa-9a24dd2c20e2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256547 1145 "To find the common set of values, we graph the inequalities on a number line and find where they overlap:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/a187bf318e77f11eb2468bc23f9b84a3b8fd715ea43f07fe9c7e083ae5861491.png)\n\nFrom the graph above, we determine that there are no common points." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256548 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256549 13194139533864 true] [17592186256548 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256548 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256548 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256549 13194139533864 true] [17592186256548 1110 #uuid "84ed4f1a-9c1b-5d24-bcd2-4e1d4d74ae0a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256549 1034 "\\emptyset" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256549 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256549 1111 #uuid "1b5bf6fa-fcf6-5644-ae0f-ae3b74445740" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256550 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256551 13194139533864 true] [17592186256550 1089 "q-102051" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256550 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256550 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/1f3d0b514bc4ec701b6ac755b82cec7246a7a819400108e1733759eb0a2fec86.png)\n\nGiven the sets $A={o,p,q,r}$ and $B={q,r,v,w},$ find $A\\cup B.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256550 1113 #uuid "63afb8fc-ab5c-5b73-8ab7-253c50b7871e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256550 1145 "The union $A\\cup B$ consists of all the elements that are in either $A$ or $B,$ or both. To represent $A\\cup B,$ we will shade in all the area that is contained in either $A$ or $B,$ or both.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/7984b0cb81df816388aa0d5cbf860fc4bdad9f7e871e8671d4616ded02bcaf5b.png)\n\nThe elements $o,p,q,r,v,w$ are in either $A$ or $B$, or both. Therefore, the union of $A$ and $B$ is\n\n$$\nA\\cup B={o,p,q,r,v,w}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256551 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256552 13194139533864 true] [17592186256551 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256551 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256551 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256552 13194139533864 true] [17592186256551 1110 #uuid "137655b4-22eb-5c03-ba11-69c8e5009d16" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256552 1034 "{o,p,q,r,v,w}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256552 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256552 1111 #uuid "cebe20ba-7f2c-5e59-97a5-23ff4f7bb940" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256553 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256554 13194139533864 true] [17592186256553 1089 "q-102071" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256553 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256553 1100 "If $X={23,28,33,48}$ and $Y={28,36,52}$, then $X\\cup Y=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256553 1113 #uuid "883f571c-63e8-52a3-8d1c-45c027a3154a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256553 1145 "The set $X\\cup Y$ is the set of all elements belonging to either $X$ or $Y,$ or both. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X\\cup Y & ={23,28,33,48}\\cup {28,36,52} \\\\  & ={23,28,33,36,48,52}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256554 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256555 13194139533864 true] [17592186256554 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256554 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256554 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256555 13194139533864 true] [17592186256554 1110 #uuid "cfe2ecd4-5c36-5519-b5fa-5f897066c174" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256555 1034 "{23,28,33,36,48,52}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256555 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256555 1111 #uuid "bf14f836-8934-58d7-a6fb-cf2ca368ee79" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256556 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256557 13194139533864 true] [17592186256556 1089 "q-102077" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256556 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256556 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/e495695d9def7518780fb2b9e44f11e890da4c70c2dff7c6525936b2684219e1.png)\n\nGiven the sets $S={8,10,11}$ and $T={10,12,15},$ find $S\\cap T.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256556 1113 #uuid "fde102b2-05fb-5f0b-a8aa-3b7a81681d73" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256556 1145 "The intersection $S\\cap T$ consists of all the elements that are in both $S$ and $T.$ To represent $S\\cap T,$ we will shade in all the area that is contained in both $S$ and $T.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/5f807aaf7a026cc52f53f4a5f50afab269da5f2dfdcb835f6166891ada864f72.png)\n\nOnly the element $10$ is in both $S$ and $T.$ Therefore, the intersection of $S$ and $T$ is\n\n$$\nS\\cap T={10}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256557 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256558 13194139533864 true] [17592186256557 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256557 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256557 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256558 13194139533864 true] [17592186256557 1110 #uuid "4623e151-7175-5517-b3ff-7afdb59a23b4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256558 1034 "{10}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256558 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256558 1111 #uuid "074fa524-3867-542a-b63f-1113e37be26d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256559 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256560 13194139533864 true] [17592186256559 1089 "q-102106" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256559 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256559 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d9cf9a5c61cbf1796cf6682b129b4c05171c364c7b34e7cce176ba27e9c9cb24.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nI. - $x=-1$\nII. - $x=2$\nIII. - $x=4$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256559 1113 #uuid "6b31ab2b-c4c5-5adb-93e3-725f33b127ef" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256559 1145 "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=4.$ Since there is an asymptote at $x=4,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=4$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"I and II only\".\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/95967eb82512aae261936a6c09e2f474c72c9a130d3ecb527f62408822d88c96.png)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256560 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256561 13194139533864 true] [17592186256560 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256562 13194139533864 true] [17592186256560 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256563 13194139533864 true] [17592186256560 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256564 13194139533864 true] [17592186256560 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256565 13194139533864 true] [17592186256560 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256566 13194139533864 true] [17592186256560 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256567 13194139533864 true] [17592186256560 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256568 13194139533864 true] [17592186256560 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186256560 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256560 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256566 13194139533864 true] [17592186256560 1110 #uuid "cbf8d454-a5e4-5c07-b361-eea7a481c457" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256561 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256561 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256561 1111 #uuid "ce416271-d959-5f29-a613-74527db0fb36" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256562 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256562 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256562 1111 #uuid "8803f1cd-97ad-5de2-a183-cf168dc86ebb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256563 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256563 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256563 1111 #uuid "a1943369-4326-5ce8-828f-6f497c0387c8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256564 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256564 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256564 1111 #uuid "1a6b88e9-537f-5014-904c-2f3c5e74a690" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256565 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256565 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256565 1111 #uuid "0fe4a6d4-d9a3-509d-a10f-a48daed15eb4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256566 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256566 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256566 1111 #uuid "3d948c4c-a856-5a84-a62a-0e142b8c5423" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256567 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256567 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256567 1111 #uuid "8b0aa4ce-2595-57a5-9575-ac87a8f8dd0a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256568 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256568 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256568 1111 #uuid "e969ed7d-a030-5bbe-b5ff-e06b6ecf3ffc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256569 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256570 13194139533864 true] [17592186256569 1089 "q-102119" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256569 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256569 1100 "If $A={13,15,17,21}$ and $B={15,16,17,18},$ then $A\\cap B=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256569 1113 #uuid "b4b16d38-0e29-5036-a451-a2e4f5753235" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256569 1145 "The set $A\\cap B$ is the set of all elements belonging to both $A$ and $B.$ Since $A$ and $B$ have only the elements $15$ and $17$ in common, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cap B & ={13,15,17,21}\\cap {15,16,17,18} \\\\  & ={15,17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256570 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256571 13194139533864 true] [17592186256570 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256570 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256570 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256571 13194139533864 true] [17592186256570 1110 #uuid "0bee0454-525a-52ad-ac65-0916786427ab" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256571 1034 "{15,17}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256571 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256571 1111 #uuid "0e685e3e-5264-599c-a932-2280f481f375" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256572 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256573 13194139533864 true] [17592186256572 1089 "q-102146" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256572 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256572 1100 "Let $A$ and $B$ be the set of all multiples of $4$ greater than $0$ and the set of all multiples of $5$ greater than $0,$ respectively. What is $A\\cup B?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256572 1113 #uuid "a4c01791-56db-55fe-a66c-3347a83865ab" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256572 1145 "The multiples of $4$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nA={4,8,12,16,20,24,28,32,36,40,44,48,…}.\n$$\n\nLikewise, multiples of $5$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nB={5,10,15,20,25,30,…}.\n$$\n\nNow, $A\\cup B$ is the *union* of $A$ and $B,$ which means that we want to find the elements that are in either $A$ or $B,$ or both. In other words, $A\\cup B$ denotes the set of natural numbers that are either multiple of $4$ or multiple of $5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cup B & ={4,8,12,16,20,24,28,32,36,40,44,48,…}\\cup {5,10,15,20,25,30,…} \\\\  & ={4,5,8,10,12,15,16,20,24,25,28,30,32,35,36,40,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256573 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256574 13194139533864 true] [17592186256573 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256573 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256573 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256574 13194139533864 true] [17592186256573 1110 #uuid "48fcf4db-1be4-5325-a077-35f00f80f7b2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256574 1034 "{4,5,8,10,12,15,16,20,24,25,28,30,32,35,36,40,…}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256574 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256574 1111 #uuid "699702c0-627a-584c-a5ab-4742f8868355" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256575 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256576 13194139533864 true] [17592186256575 1089 "q-102185" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256575 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256575 1100 "If $y=\\frac{1}{4x},$ find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256575 1113 #uuid "34bf4bc2-4f77-5ec2-b028-851ecd7a1784" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256575 1145 "We first rewrite the function as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{4x}=\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the constant multiple and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-1){x}^{-1-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256576 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256577 13194139533864 true] [17592186256576 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256576 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256576 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256577 13194139533864 true] [17592186256576 1110 #uuid "cad7fde3-82c0-59d2-800f-c2f0934ff17b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256577 1034 "-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256577 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256577 1111 #uuid "7ed996f2-f9db-5f3a-9c53-ce638160902a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256578 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256579 13194139533864 true] [17592186256578 1089 "q-10229" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256578 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256578 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/70fc259b5863513eabaf6b67eeb5e6b8debcb40d4a91d096f6515cb9f2efe642.png)\n\nDetermine the length of the rectangular solid above given that its volume is $720\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256578 1113 #uuid "9ca0f34d-dd75-5cc4-8c72-6f6261fa4014" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256578 1145 "We can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nV=720,\\,w=5,\\,h=8\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain the following\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}720 & =l\\cdot 5\\cdot 8 \\\\ 720 & =40l \\\\ l & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length is $18\\,\\text{in}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256579 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256580 13194139533864 true] [17592186256579 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256579 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256579 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256580 13194139533864 true] [17592186256579 1110 #uuid "e8abd8ea-8047-58e3-bcca-e8e135152a4b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256580 1034 "18\\,\\text{in}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256580 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256580 1111 #uuid "82b33d88-74f5-5fc2-b734-76d15bbe80cf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256581 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256582 13194139533864 true] [17592186256581 1089 "q-102332" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256581 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256581 1100 "If $A={a,c,d,f,g,i}$ and $B={a,b,e,f,h,i,j},$ then $A\\cap B=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256581 1113 #uuid "a33d78df-c474-59f3-94e1-c9f5996f520b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256581 1145 "The set $A\\cap B$ is the set of all elements belonging to both $A$ and $B.$ Since $A$ and $B$ have only the elements $a,$ $f,$ and $i$ in common, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cap B & ={a,c,d,f,g,i}\\cap {a,b,e,f,h,i,j} \\\\  & ={a,f,i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256582 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256583 13194139533864 true] [17592186256582 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256582 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256582 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256583 13194139533864 true] [17592186256582 1110 #uuid "d63f76be-e0c8-5092-a03c-1779aeec6536" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256583 1034 "{a,f,i}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256583 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256583 1111 #uuid "4b3535b1-2fac-5ad8-8d92-98e7842585d3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256584 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256585 13194139533864 true] [17592186256584 1089 "q-102613" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256584 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256584 1100 "Find the instantaneous rate of change of $f(x)=10{x}^{2}$ at the point $x=3$ by computing the limit of a difference quotient.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256584 1113 #uuid "0cb34dbc-b9db-5b93-9599-5e4c5f547a2f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256584 1145 "*Method 1:*\n\nBy the definition, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(3)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(3+h)-f(3)}{h}.\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=10{x}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(3) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{10(3+h)^{2}-10(3)^{2}}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{90+60h+10{h}^{2}-90}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{60h+10{h}^{2}}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(60+10h) \\\\  & =60+(0) \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n*Method 2:*\n\nBy the definition, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(3)={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=10{x}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(3) & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(\\frac{10{x}^{2}-10(3)^{2}}{x-3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(\\frac{10({x}^{2}-{3}^{2})}{x-3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(\\frac{10(x-3)(x+3)}{x-3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}10(x+3) \\\\  & =10(3+3) \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256585 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256586 13194139533864 true] [17592186256585 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256585 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256585 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256586 13194139533864 true] [17592186256585 1110 #uuid "1ec0a6c9-bc80-514c-9741-89967e14c3d8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256586 1034 "60" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256586 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256586 1111 #uuid "82be2e6e-182f-52e7-b27f-4339541b6bf8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256587 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256588 13194139533864 true] [17592186256587 1089 "q-102626" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256587 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256587 1100 "The velocity $v$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $v(t)={e}^{2-3t}.$ Calculate the acceleration of the particle at time $t.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256587 1113 #uuid "3ec12c4d-f13a-5615-b8ec-dbfa0807307e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256587 1145 "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2-3t}) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {e}^{2-3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256588 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256589 13194139533864 true] [17592186256588 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256588 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256588 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256589 13194139533864 true] [17592186256588 1110 #uuid "43eacefa-8e8f-50c8-8475-f621f03d0c37" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256589 1034 "-3\\cdot {e}^{2-3t}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256589 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256589 1111 #uuid "98a89e0a-4efc-593f-a84a-3b33c40032d5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256590 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256591 13194139533864 true] [17592186256590 1089 "q-10289" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256590 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256590 1100 "Find the derivative of  $f(x)=4{x}^{6}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256590 1113 #uuid "2da3e499-592f-56ab-a8b2-fa769f0b592f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256590 1145 "We apply the constant multiple and power rules, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 6{x}^{6-1} \\\\  & =24{x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256591 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256592 13194139533864 true] [17592186256591 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256591 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256591 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256592 13194139533864 true] [17592186256591 1110 #uuid "e2c01cef-97c9-5591-9025-3a5af6e30b08" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256592 1034 "24{x}^{5}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256592 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256592 1111 #uuid "1e1aaf8a-3d5f-5835-811e-3a659778203e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256593 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256594 13194139533864 true] [17592186256593 1089 "q-102961" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256593 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256593 1100 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}?$\n\nI. - The domain of $f(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\nII. - $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty $\nIII. - The point $(-1,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256593 1113 #uuid "02ff78bf-c744-52ed-b02f-687ebfd84d9a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256593 1145 "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/abafb7d55b4baa0b06f20c3de2b44d5925f7286f078aaa76d71aded7c9c9a001.png)\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the domain of $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is false since $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is false since $f(-1)=\\sqrt[3]{-1}=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256594 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256595 13194139533864 true] [17592186256594 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256596 13194139533864 true] [17592186256594 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256597 13194139533864 true] [17592186256594 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256598 13194139533864 true] [17592186256594 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256599 13194139533864 true] [17592186256594 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256600 13194139533864 true] [17592186256594 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256601 13194139533864 true] [17592186256594 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256602 13194139533864 true] [17592186256594 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186256594 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256594 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256598 13194139533864 true] [17592186256594 1110 #uuid "ec2ed87d-8da5-5cf0-924f-c476dada74aa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256595 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256595 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256595 1111 #uuid "c3adeb09-5140-5fca-9700-c994dedc9c7e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256596 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256596 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256596 1111 #uuid "48683ef0-cb9a-5a42-a5dc-8c5f4b4180fa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256597 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256597 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256597 1111 #uuid "8e191c58-93fc-5a21-8269-2e823b43a26e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256598 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256598 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256598 1111 #uuid "1fab48ab-c6dd-5c37-b4c3-b974a79cd885" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256599 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256599 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256599 1111 #uuid "1a378210-8ecf-5ba8-af29-1ab0d287301c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256600 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256600 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256600 1111 #uuid "e2129c7c-0469-5c30-af1b-889b4fad5941" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256601 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256601 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256601 1111 #uuid "05eb3693-4523-5ff9-ae6d-14f814a75511" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256602 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256602 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256602 1111 #uuid "6564a1f4-1e4f-5275-86da-a58ccba15532" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256603 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256604 13194139533864 true] [17592186256603 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256606 13194139533864 true] [17592186256603 1089 "q-10318" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256603 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256603 1100 "Find the values of $k$ for which the quadratic equation $-15{x}^{2}+kx-60=0$  has one distinct real solution.\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256603 1113 #uuid "a8ca7739-ac12-5a8f-a94e-0d532b4daccf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256603 1145 "The equation has one distinct real solution if its discriminant is zero. So, we need to find the values of $k$ so that the discriminant is zero.\n\nFirst, we calculate the discriminant. We have $a=-15,$ $b=k,$ and $c=-60,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(k)^{2}-4(-15)(-60) \\\\  & ={k}^{2}-3\\,600\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the values of $k$ such that the discriminant is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=0 \\\\ {k}^{2}-3600 & =0 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =3600 \\\\ k & =\\pm \\sqrt{3600} \\\\ k & =\\pm 60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation will have one distinct real solution if $k=\\pm 60.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256604 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256605 13194139533864 true] [17592186256604 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256604 1041 "root-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256604 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256605 13194139533864 true] [17592186256604 1110 #uuid "cb5ec21e-e3fb-5152-965f-9a7b693fb214" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256605 1034 "-60" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256605 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256605 1111 #uuid "aa8219dd-5426-535a-8746-a20de862801e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256607 13194139533864 true] [17592186256606 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256606 1041 "root-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256606 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256607 13194139533864 true] [17592186256606 1110 #uuid "17516546-529b-517f-a2ff-3650e714700d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256607 1034 "60" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256607 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256607 1111 #uuid "c77f485b-642c-5f6c-a6a5-59814eca6ecb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256608 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256609 13194139533864 true] [17592186256608 1089 "q-103216" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256608 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256608 1100 "If ${a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}$ with ${a}_{1}=-2$ and ${a}_{2}=0,$ then ${a}_{3}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256608 1113 #uuid "9cc04cfe-5c1c-58c0-b534-2ea8f4897bac" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256608 1145 "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1+2} & ={a}_{1+1}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{2}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =0+(-2) \\\\ {a}_{3} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256609 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256610 13194139533864 true] [17592186256609 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256609 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256609 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256610 13194139533864 true] [17592186256609 1110 #uuid "4a58f9f1-7f33-5634-9a0d-d31653864f38" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256610 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256610 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256610 1111 #uuid "36d47bf5-d562-56c1-981f-81ce1bfb0996" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256611 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256612 13194139533864 true] [17592186256611 1089 "q-103217" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256611 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256611 1100 "If ${a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}$ with ${a}_{1}=4$ and ${a}_{2}=5,$ then ${a}_{3}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256611 1113 #uuid "d61383ab-6ca0-5c21-90fc-da34d0ca7d99" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256611 1145 "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1+2} & ={a}_{1+1}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{2}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =4+5 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256612 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256613 13194139533864 true] [17592186256612 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256612 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256612 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256613 13194139533864 true] [17592186256612 1110 #uuid "7c87d266-0ee7-554e-9da5-6db1aebe77e7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256613 1034 "9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256613 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256613 1111 #uuid "8ffa1a3f-0c84-5504-a5b3-3da85ca9f1c5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256614 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256615 13194139533864 true] [17592186256614 1089 "q-103222" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256614 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256614 1100 "What is the fourth term of the following sequence?\n\n$$\nf(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n)\\,,\\,f(1)=-2\\,,\\,f(2)=0,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256614 1113 #uuid "0798bf8d-4438-5b94-a6a0-6a186fe4b314" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256614 1145 "The fourth term is represented by $f(4).$\n\nTo compute $f(4),$ we first need to compute $f(3).$ So, we substitute $n=1$ into the recursive formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n+2) & =f(n+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(1+2) & =f(1+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =f(2)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =0+(-2) \\\\ f(3) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to compute $f(4),$ we substitute $n=2$ into the recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2+2) & =f(2+1)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & =f(3)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & =-2+0 \\\\ f(4) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fourth term of the sequence is $f(4)=-2.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256615 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256616 13194139533864 true] [17592186256615 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256615 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256615 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256616 13194139533864 true] [17592186256615 1110 #uuid "309ffe6e-1a0d-5db1-8e6e-7d5c01c645e2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256616 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256616 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256616 1111 #uuid "bbb57b6c-f1e6-5978-b142-a098b7bb0037" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256617 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256618 13194139533864 true] [17592186256617 1089 "q-103223" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256617 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256617 1100 "If $f(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n)$ with $f(1)=-1$ and $f(2)=1,$ then $f(3)=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256617 1113 #uuid "22ea45b9-f009-53ac-920f-698b600ba579" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256617 1145 "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\nf(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1+2) & =f(1+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =f(2)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =1+(-1) \\\\ f(3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(3)=0.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256618 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256619 13194139533864 true] [17592186256618 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256618 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256618 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256619 13194139533864 true] [17592186256618 1110 #uuid "0531825e-15d9-55ac-8251-aacc8bb9e54a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256619 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256619 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256619 1111 #uuid "7149c441-adba-55e5-97a8-21d1a37322d9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256620 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256621 13194139533864 true] [17592186256620 1089 "q-103226" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256620 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256620 1100 "If $f(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n)$ with $f(1)=0$ and $f(2)=2,$ then $f(4)=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256620 1113 #uuid "81f8219b-91a8-52ec-8e9f-7635a6f67073" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256620 1145 "To compute $f(4),$ we first need to compute $f(3).$ So, we substitute $n=1$ into the recursive formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n+2) & =f(n+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(1+2) & =f(1+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =f(2)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =2+0 \\\\ f(3) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to compute $f(4),$ we substitute $n=2$ into the recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2+2) & =f(2+1)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & =f(3)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & =2+2 \\\\ f(4) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(4)=4.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256621 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256622 13194139533864 true] [17592186256621 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256621 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256621 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256622 13194139533864 true] [17592186256621 1110 #uuid "a8c0e081-89dc-5965-9267-824eb2083173" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256622 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256622 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256622 1111 #uuid "c9442754-d4d9-588b-9302-dd7a2e482a5b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256623 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256624 13194139533864 true] [17592186256623 1089 "q-103257" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256623 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256623 1100 "Consider the sequence starting from ${a}_{1}=\\frac{6}{5}$  with the property $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=\\frac{3}{4}$ for all $n\\ge 1.$ Write the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence using sigma notation.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256623 1113 #uuid "55a081fd-27a0-5f3f-9ea6-07fb5a7d4d01" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256623 1145 "Notice that the sequence is a geometric sequence since the ratio of any two successive terms is constant.\n\nThe first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=\\frac{6}{5},$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general term ${a}_{n}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}{(r)}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{-1}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the infinite geometric series can be written in sigma notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{8}{5}){(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256624 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256625 13194139533864 true] [17592186256624 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256624 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256624 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256625 13194139533864 true] [17592186256624 1110 #uuid "c60e023d-40ef-56d7-b144-373de5481d2b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256625 1034 "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{8}{5}){(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256625 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256625 1111 #uuid "ba118855-a6fa-579c-909c-306784f43523" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256626 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256627 13194139533864 true] [17592186256626 1089 "q-103615" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256626 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256626 1100 "Give an expression equivalent to $\\frac{18x-6}{3}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256626 1113 #uuid "86d93e27-a5e1-5b45-a92f-58373db870a8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256626 1145 "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18x-6}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{18x}{3}-\\frac{6}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{18}{3}\\cdot x-\\frac{6}{3} & = \\\\ 6\\cdot x-2 & = \\\\ 6x-2. & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256627 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256628 13194139533864 true] [17592186256627 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256627 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256627 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256628 13194139533864 true] [17592186256627 1110 #uuid "0d5d45e9-970b-5ec6-8525-0a0e9a5292bb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256628 1034 "6x-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256628 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256628 1111 #uuid "58aa1852-7367-551e-a37a-aae4cfbe78ae" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256629 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256630 13194139533864 true] [17592186256629 1089 "q-104021" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256629 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256629 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/25dbe28eeed5a93d9813f6eab6fd73ebade61bfb2b7a76c933b61cdde46e78e6.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256629 1113 #uuid "0ead3adb-42a6-500f-89f0-27da9f251d69" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256629 1145 "The graph breaks at $x=-2,1$ and $3,$ meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through these points.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-2,1,3.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256630 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256631 13194139533864 true] [17592186256630 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256630 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256630 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256631 13194139533864 true] [17592186256630 1110 #uuid "ca9eb716-5caa-52e1-80e8-963f5aee39ca" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256631 1034 "-2,1,3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256631 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256631 1111 #uuid "c74ff2f8-7620-5e16-8c0c-5130f766deff" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256632 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256633 13194139533864 true] [17592186256632 1089 "q-104149" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256632 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256632 1100 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=\\sqrt{2}?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}-2}, & x<\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\frac{4}{{x}^{2}-2}, & x>\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256632 1113 #uuid "6d7bd088-f78b-52b5-add1-d0b106096967" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256632 1145 "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- We have that $f(\\sqrt{2})$ is not defined since $x=\\sqrt{2}$ does not belong to the domain of $f(x).$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=\\sqrt{2}$ because $f(\\sqrt{2})$ does not exist." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256633 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256634 13194139533864 true] [17592186256633 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256633 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256633 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256634 13194139533864 true] [17592186256633 1110 #uuid "da682f48-66c1-5963-aadc-81ca63c722cc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256634 1034 "f(\\sqrt{2})" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256634 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256634 1111 #uuid "72967815-1c78-5e64-b056-c9ec20a72911" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256635 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256636 13194139533864 true] [17592186256635 1089 "q-104159" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256635 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256635 1100 "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\sqrt{3}$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {\\sqrt{3}}^{\\,-}}f(x)=9,$ then what is $f(\\sqrt{3})?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256635 1113 #uuid "146303a4-6968-5cfe-bc52-997b521163c5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256635 1145 "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\sqrt{3}.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to \\sqrt{3}}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf(\\sqrt{3})={lim}_{x\\to \\sqrt{3}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {\\sqrt{3}}^{\\,-}}f(x)=9.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256636 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256637 13194139533864 true] [17592186256636 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256636 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256636 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256637 13194139533864 true] [17592186256636 1110 #uuid "04bf3706-2aec-5d95-8407-21ebd5fa2088" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256637 1034 "9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256637 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256637 1111 #uuid "d8503c3b-e02d-5ad3-9012-3f65dcd1c70a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256638 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256639 13194139533864 true] [17592186256638 1089 "q-104247" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256638 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256638 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/8dbc5d9ca713eadced5cdf2c3616bab04e6a7f5a88f305b6d9abb3e6b89f7463.png)\n\nAt which points is the function $y=f(x)$ above left-continuous but is not continuous?\n\n$x$: {{x}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256638 1113 #uuid "c868e391-1415-56f9-8288-1cafe3a76a66" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256638 1145 "To determine whether $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the left-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nLet's check this for $x=0$, at $x=2$ and at  $x=3.$\n\n- At $x=0$ the function is not left-continuous because \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=3\\ne 2=f(0).\n$$\n\n- At $x=2$ the function is not left-continuous because \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=0\\ne -2=f(2).\n$$\n\n- At $x=3$ the function is left-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=1=f(3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=3$ only." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256639 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256640 13194139533864 true] [17592186256639 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256639 1041 "x" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256639 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256640 13194139533864 true] [17592186256639 1110 #uuid "4233fae9-564f-5755-a113-2112874404ad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256640 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256640 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256640 1111 #uuid "b1609cda-4b6a-556a-a7cc-d54d04c54353" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256641 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256642 13194139533864 true] [17592186256641 1089 "q-104282" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256641 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256641 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/6a250c587a1c12845759eefdda74faac240c7cec2c63e1d6a6180b1b09292637.png)\n\nAt which points is the function $y=f(x)$ above not continuous but is right-continuous?\n\n$x$: {{x}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256641 1113 #uuid "84f94ec2-29d7-53da-9169-3c19a469e54b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256641 1145 "To determine whether $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the right-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nThe function has three discontinuities: $x=-2$, $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\n- At $x=-2$  the function is neither left- nor right-continuous because it's undefined at this point.\n\n- At $x=0$ the function is not right-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=0\\ne -1=f(0).\n$$\n\n- At $x=2$ the function is right-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=-2=f(2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=2$ only." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256642 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256643 13194139533864 true] [17592186256642 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256642 1041 "x" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256642 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256643 13194139533864 true] [17592186256642 1110 #uuid "cdfed29c-0003-53c7-9de5-749accda86d0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256643 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256643 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256643 1111 #uuid "b715ad1a-ee55-5582-a671-bbfdbb363d8b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256644 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256645 13194139533864 true] [17592186256644 1089 "q-104305" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256644 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256644 1100 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{10}{3-{x}^{2}}$, find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256644 1113 #uuid "1d411e15-efea-5149-8fe7-10e8ce23e151" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256644 1145 "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=10$ and $v(x)=3-{x}^{2},$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{'}(x) & =0, \\\\ {v}^{'}(x) & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0)\\cdot (3-{x}^{2})-(10)\\cdot (-2x)}{{(3-{x}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{20x}{{(3-{x}^{2})}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256645 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256646 13194139533864 true] [17592186256645 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256645 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256645 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256646 13194139533864 true] [17592186256645 1110 #uuid "ba86529c-b98b-5ba7-ad46-35571f3da4e7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256646 1034 "\\frac{20x}{{(3-{x}^{2})}^{2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256646 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256646 1111 #uuid "b933c957-ebae-554d-b937-23a566ec9979" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256647 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256648 13194139533864 true] [17592186256647 1089 "q-104309" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256647 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256647 1100 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{3-2x}{2{x}^{2}-2}$, find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256647 1113 #uuid "466a6abc-d194-5c18-a31e-c8516267f1eb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256647 1145 "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=3-2x$ and $v(x)=2{x}^{2}-2,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{'}(x) & =-2, \\\\ {v}^{'}(x) & =4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)(2{x}^{2}-2)-(3-2x)(4x)}{{(2{x}^{2}-2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4{x}^{2}+4-12x+8{x}^{2}}{{(2{x}^{2}-2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{2}-12x+4}{4{({x}^{2}-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x+1}{{({x}^{2}-1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256648 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256649 13194139533864 true] [17592186256648 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256648 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256648 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256649 13194139533864 true] [17592186256648 1110 #uuid "19342e98-c7a9-5b5c-803e-795c11b1f081" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256649 1034 "\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x+1}{{({x}^{2}-1)}^{2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256649 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256649 1111 #uuid "8097de06-6461-5621-941f-80228f745605" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256650 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256651 13194139533864 true] [17592186256650 1089 "q-104316" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256650 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256650 1100 "Find ${f}^{'}(x)$ if $f(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256650 1113 #uuid "667f744b-3445-5e28-b209-637d4bc0f280" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256650 1145 "We calculate the derivative using the product rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{csc}x)+{e}^{x}(-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}x(1-\\operatorname{cot}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256651 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256652 13194139533864 true] [17592186256651 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256651 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256651 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256652 13194139533864 true] [17592186256651 1110 #uuid "692f1fb9-ba55-500b-b7b4-7c538b1494c8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256652 1034 "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}x(1-\\operatorname{cot}x)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256652 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256652 1111 #uuid "31cfb0d3-2500-519b-978d-acfa938d00af" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256653 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256654 13194139533864 true] [17592186256653 1089 "q-104437" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256653 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256653 1100 "Find the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ if $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cot}x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256653 1113 #uuid "1bd6438f-7499-53a3-942a-e79c9cbb8468" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256653 1145 "First, we take the derivative using the product rule:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{cot}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}x+x(-{csc}^{2}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}x-x{csc}^{2}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in the derivative and get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{\\pi /4} & =\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\frac{\\pi }{4}{csc}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256654 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256655 13194139533864 true] [17592186256654 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256654 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256654 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256655 13194139533864 true] [17592186256654 1110 #uuid "f8c669a5-2de0-52a1-99da-42dd787e5ab5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256655 1034 "1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256655 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256655 1111 #uuid "7b8dff62-ea2b-5502-b1ee-11af84236123" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256656 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256657 13194139533864 true] [17592186256656 1089 "q-104652" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256656 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256656 1100 "Given that $\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{{t}^{7}}}={t}^{n},$ what is $n?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256656 1113 #uuid "297ab20b-08c4-5fef-9db0-2144be93be03" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256656 1145 "First, we use that $\\frac{1}{{t}^{7}}={t}^{-7}$, then we convert the radical to a fractional exponent, and finally, we apply the power rule: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{{t}^{7}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{t}^{-7}} & = \\\\ {({t}^{-7})}^{1/3} & = \\\\ {t}^{(-7)\\cdot (1/3)} & = \\\\ {t}^{-7/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $n=-\\frac{7}{3}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256657 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256658 13194139533864 true] [17592186256657 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256657 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256657 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256658 13194139533864 true] [17592186256657 1110 #uuid "b04a25bf-fa89-50dc-84d6-5b32bd7d2347" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256658 1034 "-\\frac{7}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256658 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256658 1111 #uuid "38fc0134-3e32-5936-adb6-119a15f96bb5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256659 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256660 13194139533864 true] [17592186256659 1089 "q-106046" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256659 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256659 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/cd847efd8b91b1c622e93b1ee97035bcee803a79a603aa7b4beee8fae77abf06.png)\n\nThe equation of the curve above is $y=-{x}^{2}+2x+k.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256659 1113 #uuid "79f2ac8f-706d-5c75-bc5d-c89a9e2fab4f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256659 1145 "Notice that if we substitute $x=0$ into the given equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{x}^{2}-x+k \\\\ y & =-(0)^{2}+2(0)+k \\\\ y & =k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nmeaning that the curve intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,k).$ Based on the graph, we see that we must have $k=5$ since our parabola intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,5).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256660 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256661 13194139533864 true] [17592186256660 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256660 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256660 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256661 13194139533864 true] [17592186256660 1110 #uuid "f2a39660-f172-58c9-b080-c4ef45aa5957" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256661 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256661 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256661 1111 #uuid "29957c6e-c46a-5da1-952f-b90fda674c62" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256662 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256663 13194139533864 true] [17592186256662 1089 "q-106090" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256662 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256662 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/f367a4318a1b9d3c2e582f515a2c57058067ed4546f588dc30282f7dd34aac89.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. - ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=-1$\nII. - ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3$\nIII. - ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256662 1113 #uuid "2b16aa53-4832-5f75-8c64-41571c8255c0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256662 1145 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ approaches $-2,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $0,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=3.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $-1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=2,$ and consequently \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\n \nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $2,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1,$ and consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n As a result,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x).\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256663 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256664 13194139533864 true] [17592186256663 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256665 13194139533864 true] [17592186256663 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256666 13194139533864 true] [17592186256663 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256667 13194139533864 true] [17592186256663 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256668 13194139533864 true] [17592186256663 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256669 13194139533864 true] [17592186256663 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256670 13194139533864 true] [17592186256663 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256671 13194139533864 true] [17592186256663 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186256663 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256663 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256671 13194139533864 true] [17592186256663 1110 #uuid "07029c89-c1ae-54b6-a2f2-8eca277ca242" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256664 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256664 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256664 1111 #uuid "6e9ab2bb-e1b9-5170-9c76-b31d705dee86" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256665 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256665 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256665 1111 #uuid "611e0a96-0804-5d12-9945-f9de947f2ae4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256666 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256666 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256666 1111 #uuid "67487606-7811-5761-a83c-868f3a1183f3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256667 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256667 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256667 1111 #uuid "2836c62e-4424-585e-b336-1f4274344dea" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256668 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256668 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256668 1111 #uuid "e3842f0e-4a4c-51b1-8fd8-b5c2cbfcf200" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256669 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256669 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256669 1111 #uuid "b5842364-149e-5b82-ab88-a434e6e3a47a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256670 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256670 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256670 1111 #uuid "42d6f634-f8fc-582e-9020-d11ee4f8e8c7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256671 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256671 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256671 1111 #uuid "dcb2485d-327c-5235-8e3e-ceed6d3a2ef4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256672 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256673 13194139533864 true] [17592186256672 1089 "q-106719" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256672 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256672 1100 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}(4x)}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256672 1113 #uuid "313d80f2-5348-5223-83dd-06c7adf91fd9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256672 1145 "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $(x+1)^{2}≫\\operatorname{ln}(4x)$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}(4x)}=\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256673 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256674 13194139533864 true] [17592186256673 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256673 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256673 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256674 13194139533864 true] [17592186256673 1110 #uuid "8dfd924f-7edc-5d23-9219-17a0551e652d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256674 1034 "\\infty" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256674 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256674 1111 #uuid "bf871154-dc63-5122-8395-bdd89168a5e0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256675 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256676 13194139533864 true] [17592186256675 1089 "q-106723" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256675 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256675 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}(x+2)}-20)=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256675 1113 #uuid "9b1b8ae9-0781-5016-a848-e131e2cd0081" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256675 1145 "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${x}^{3}≫\\operatorname{ln}(x+2)$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}(x+2)}=\\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}(x+2)}-20)=\\infty \n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256676 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256677 13194139533864 true] [17592186256676 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256676 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256676 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256677 13194139533864 true] [17592186256676 1110 #uuid "128d9980-d249-5545-9983-717c12b6222b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256677 1034 "\\infty" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256677 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256677 1111 #uuid "50ff054f-4330-5216-be2a-d8a2342715bb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256678 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256679 13194139533864 true] [17592186256678 1089 "q-107059" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256678 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256678 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{\\sqrt{x-1}-2}{x-5}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256678 1113 #uuid "cab1e7b8-4832-542d-b640-2493abb21591" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256678 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{\\sqrt{x-1}-2}{x-5} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5-1}-2}{5-5}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{\\sqrt{x-1}-2}{x-5} & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x-1}-2)(\\sqrt{x-1}+2)}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{x-1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x-1})}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{x-1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{x-1-4}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{x-1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{x-5}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{x-1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5-1}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256679 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256680 13194139533864 true] [17592186256679 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256679 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256679 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256680 13194139533864 true] [17592186256679 1110 #uuid "294a7179-3399-56aa-8698-72d45ca63d34" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256680 1034 "\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256680 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256680 1111 #uuid "5392be73-c2fc-5483-9a69-e3f002bb5aa2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256681 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256682 13194139533864 true] [17592186256681 1089 "q-107246" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256681 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256681 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/2d6d392d4e0f773f962289075ffa07cd26353ebc00ec50e7473a5c992824417f.png)\n\nWhat is the name of the solid shown above?" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256681 1113 #uuid "2a4b869e-ef91-5ea2-9f20-27bb98e30ff5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256681 1145 "The solid is a **right cylinder**. \n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The solid has two parallel circular bases. So, it's a cylinder.\n\n- The axis that passes through the centers of the circles is perpendicular to the bases. So, it's right." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256682 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256683 13194139533864 true] [17592186256682 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256684 13194139533864 true] [17592186256682 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256685 13194139533864 true] [17592186256682 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256686 13194139533864 true] [17592186256682 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256687 13194139533864 true] [17592186256682 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256688 13194139533864 true] [17592186256682 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186256682 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256682 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256684 13194139533864 true] [17592186256682 1110 #uuid "180de416-69ab-5c18-9a3a-a094db6648c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256683 1034 "Oblique cylinder" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256683 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256683 1111 #uuid "4c61f9f3-2a54-5019-a166-6ad0885eabb6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256684 1034 "Right cylinder" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256684 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256684 1111 #uuid "a77fc4ff-c00e-5410-babf-3a6afcbd4f10" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256685 1034 "Sphere" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256685 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256685 1111 #uuid "5bc115f3-10a1-5c32-9f8a-ca39b3d811c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256686 1034 "Pyramid" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256686 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256686 1111 #uuid "4ebaf498-6c55-523c-b224-d3a9c1b5fc5d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256687 1034 "Cone" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256687 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256687 1111 #uuid "4c6e5ea9-ddc3-5a4e-99b3-62670139ebd0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256688 1034 "Prism" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256688 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256688 1111 #uuid "4a3728e3-ed0a-565b-81d5-e8fac6415c12" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256689 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256690 13194139533864 true] [17592186256689 1089 "q-10726" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256689 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256689 1100 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=5{x}^{15}-4{x}^{8}+7{x}^{5}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256689 1113 #uuid "9f25ae3e-b1ef-5b98-82be-6340c3c3c87e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256689 1145 "Here, we apply the addition, constant multiple, and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{15}-4{x}^{8}+7{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{15})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{8})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{15})-4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{8})+7\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 15{x}^{(15-1)}-4\\cdot 8{x}^{(8-1)}+7\\cdot 5{x}^{(5-1)} \\\\  & =75{x}^{14}-32{x}^{7}+35{x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256690 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256691 13194139533864 true] [17592186256690 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256690 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256690 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256691 13194139533864 true] [17592186256690 1110 #uuid "a91ce7e6-caa0-5d60-a558-541eae1f4ecf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256691 1034 "75{x}^{14}-32{x}^{7}+35{x}^{4}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256691 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256691 1111 #uuid "05b6d182-004d-566c-bf31-7147d1ecb86c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256692 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256693 13194139533864 true] [17592186256692 1089 "q-10730" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256692 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256692 1100 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=7{x}^{5}-3{x}^{2}-5x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256692 1113 #uuid "36a4921a-bbad-5ced-a8e4-cc7bcc79bde0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256692 1145 "Here, we apply the addition, constant multiple, and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{x}^{5}-3{x}^{2}-5x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{x}^{5})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x) \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5})-3\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-5\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =7\\cdot 5{x}^{(5-1)}-3\\cdot 2{x}^{(2-1)}-5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =35{x}^{4}-6x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256693 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256694 13194139533864 true] [17592186256693 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256693 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256693 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256694 13194139533864 true] [17592186256693 1110 #uuid "bdd19de4-1a36-51ea-8dd9-1bd4b8fc094c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256694 1034 "35{x}^{4}-6x-5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256694 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256694 1111 #uuid "016b1f26-e0db-5257-867e-b7eac24ccb23" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256695 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256696 13194139533864 true] [17592186256695 1089 "q-107412" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256695 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256695 1100 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3x-2}{4-{x}^{2}},$ which of the following statements is correct?\n\nI. - ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty $\nII. - ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=\\infty $\nIII. - ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty $" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256695 1113 #uuid "6d755804-fa3d-550b-b69e-19c5ae177d05" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256695 1145 "First, let's factor the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3x-2}{4-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+2)(2x-1)}{(x-2)(x+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is  $x\\ne \\pm 2,$ and we notice that the function can be simplified:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=-\\frac{2x-1}{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we see that the function has the only vertical asymptote $x=2.$ So statement II is false.\n\nTo find  ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x),$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=2$ from the left and right.\n\n| $x$ | $1.99$ | $1.999$ | $2$ | $2.001$ | $2.01$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $298$ | $2\\,998$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $-3\\,002$ | $-302$ |\n\nAll values are positive and get larger and larger as $x\\to 2$ from the left and all values are negative and get smaller and smaller as $x\\to 2$ from the right . Therefore, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty \\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=\\infty \n$$\n\nConsequently, statement I is true and III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is correct. A plot of the function is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/9e96d6a81813c1c89a498d0206234f2685f0a53c1bab626ce7430d84f9c0f506.png)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256696 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256697 13194139533864 true] [17592186256696 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256698 13194139533864 true] [17592186256696 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256699 13194139533864 true] [17592186256696 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256700 13194139533864 true] [17592186256696 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256701 13194139533864 true] [17592186256696 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256702 13194139533864 true] [17592186256696 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256703 13194139533864 true] [17592186256696 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256704 13194139533864 true] [17592186256696 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186256696 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256696 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256700 13194139533864 true] [17592186256696 1110 #uuid "c015e4bb-470c-5e78-af9e-eb498bed15fd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256697 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256697 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256697 1111 #uuid "d0065993-5db1-59b7-b027-72e0e57586de" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256698 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256698 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256698 1111 #uuid "5e1463bc-1f3b-5336-a780-7bc5670543e0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256699 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256699 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256699 1111 #uuid "ae643bc0-1d01-5856-baa8-16a8ad37ccc7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256700 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256700 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256700 1111 #uuid "2f05f808-bfc8-53eb-aa7f-a85bd2ed8eae" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256701 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256701 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256701 1111 #uuid "1854f7ef-55c8-5515-b0b5-534e35b760f1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256702 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256702 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256702 1111 #uuid "5067d5e3-0ba8-5ccd-9a52-a879bfff4ca5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256703 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256703 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256703 1111 #uuid "ecdff9dc-3802-5ee7-8876-4d6bd1a9a576" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256704 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256704 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256704 1111 #uuid "83f1c6d3-6da2-5556-9017-c5aadc76a5a5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256705 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256706 13194139533864 true] [17592186256705 1089 "q-107420" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256705 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256705 1100 "Calculate ${f}^{'}(x)$ if $f(x)={x}^{3}-{e}^{1-3x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256705 1113 #uuid "c88af4d4-fee6-50f4-a079-e773fbb850de" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256705 1145 "First, we express $y={e}^{1-3x}$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=1-3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{1-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the sum rule of differentiation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-{e}^{1-3x}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-(-3{e}^{1-3x}) \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+{e}^{1-3x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256706 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256707 13194139533864 true] [17592186256706 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256706 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256706 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256707 13194139533864 true] [17592186256706 1110 #uuid "65aabc0e-ccf5-5ae1-8ca8-6753b995425a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256707 1034 "3({x}^{2}+{e}^{1-3x})" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256707 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256707 1111 #uuid "fd87005a-4579-54cf-b44d-797224c53bdd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256708 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256709 13194139533864 true] [17592186256708 1089 "q-107458" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256708 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256708 1100 "If $f(x)={2}^{3-{x}^{3}},$ find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256708 1113 #uuid "35c8058a-5738-5793-b8ff-a2abf524886b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256708 1145 "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={2}^{u},\\,u=3-{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}2,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}2\\cdot (-3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-3{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}2 \\\\  & =-3{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{3-{x}^{3}}\\operatorname{ln}2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256709 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256710 13194139533864 true] [17592186256709 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256709 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256709 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256710 13194139533864 true] [17592186256709 1110 #uuid "3751607b-b3a6-5d0c-b23c-f55d83a06d76" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256710 1034 "-3{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{3-{x}^{3}}\\operatorname{ln}2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256710 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256710 1111 #uuid "5c9213b1-b1fe-58b1-9b88-d91f70eb6ba9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256711 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256712 13194139533864 true] [17592186256711 1089 "q-107469" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256711 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256711 1100 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}(4-2x)$ at the point $(1,\\operatorname{ln}2).$\n\n$y =$: {{y}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256711 1113 #uuid "5abbc5ce-58de-5d2f-bd7e-ce5e196f9c90" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256711 1145 "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{ln}u,\\,u=4-2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-2x}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{4-2x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\cancel{2}}{\\cancel{2}\\cdot (2-x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={-\\frac{1}{2-x}|}_{x=1}=-\\frac{1}{2-1}=-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(1,\\operatorname{ln}2)$ is $m=-1.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\operatorname{ln}2 & =-1(x-1) \\\\ y & =-x+1+\\operatorname{ln}2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256712 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256713 13194139533864 true] [17592186256712 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256712 1041 "y" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256712 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256713 13194139533864 true] [17592186256712 1110 #uuid "3a9136ad-7eb7-53ca-8f13-15c67675376c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256713 1034 "-x+1+\\ln2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256713 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256713 1111 #uuid "42f97ca5-3776-55ef-9527-31668d5a00d5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256714 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256715 13194139533864 true] [17592186256714 1089 "q-107499" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256714 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256714 1100 "Express $z=\\frac{6}{-1+\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256714 1113 #uuid "0c205fa0-d12d-510f-bf38-5376ffd48764" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256714 1145 "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $-1-\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{6}{-1+\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{(-1+\\text{i})(-1-\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{(-1)^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{1+1} \\\\  & =3(-1-\\text{i}) \\\\  & =-3-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=-3-3\\text{i},$ the magnitude is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}(z).$ Since $x=-3$ and $y=-3$ are both negative, $z$ lies in the third quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z) & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}|\\frac{-3}{-3}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}1 \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}))\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256715 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256716 13194139533864 true] [17592186256715 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256715 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256715 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256716 13194139533864 true] [17592186256715 1110 #uuid "07aa1240-3085-53f7-95b8-d6b037a48ffb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256716 1034 "3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}))" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256716 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256716 1111 #uuid "7a99a198-0f4f-5492-b567-df73208d6cdd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256717 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256718 13194139533864 true] [17592186256717 1089 "q-107542" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256717 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256717 1100 "The cubic polynomial $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=-2,{x}_{2,3}=1\\pm 2\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $3.$ Find $f(x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256717 1113 #uuid "091a471e-7959-5ecb-b617-deff448e5df9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256717 1145 "Since $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=-2$ and ${x}_{2,3}=1\\pm 2\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=3,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =3(x-(-2))(x-(1+2\\text{i}))(x-(1-2\\text{i})) \\\\  & =3(x+2)(x-(1+2\\text{i}))(x-(1-2\\text{i}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(x+2)(x-(1+2\\text{i}))(x-(1-2\\text{i})) & = \\\\ 3(x+2)({x}^{2}-x(1+2\\text{i})-x(1-2\\text{i})+(1+2\\text{i})(1-2\\text{i})) & = \\\\ 3(x+2)({x}^{2}-2x+5) & = \\\\ 3({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2}-4x+5x+10) & = \\\\ 3({x}^{3}+x+10) & = \\\\ 3{x}^{3}+3x+30 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+3x+30.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256718 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256719 13194139533864 true] [17592186256718 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256718 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256718 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256719 13194139533864 true] [17592186256718 1110 #uuid "ce97edf4-f926-5ff4-bb52-8fb2c6314566" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256719 1034 "3{x}^{3}+3x+30" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256719 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256719 1111 #uuid "db1df5ae-3bbb-5988-9dc1-bb854273428b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256720 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256721 13194139533864 true] [17592186256720 1089 "q-107615" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256720 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256720 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/02a014f78ffde4ae39fb76e4fff32fd4e8789d436f296ee865f2be220dce549e.png)\n\nFind the volume of the rectangular prism shown above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256720 1113 #uuid "632cef4f-7740-505b-810d-f1a534b0a2cb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256720 1145 "We can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=5\\,\\text{m},\\,w=\\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{m},\\,h=\\sqrt{15}\\,\\text{m}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV=5\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\sqrt{15}=5\\sqrt{150}=25\\sqrt{6}\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256721 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256722 13194139533864 true] [17592186256721 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256721 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256721 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256722 13194139533864 true] [17592186256721 1110 #uuid "0482ccbd-22f0-5a50-a675-df9fbcc9fe12" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256722 1034 "25\\sqrt{6}\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256722 1103 