olid is a square, find the width of the solid.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256726 1113 #uuid "334c2f91-1d22-56da-8cb1-a0b5225f3f04" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256726 1145 "We can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSince the side face of the rectangular solid is a square, we have that \n\n$$\nw=h.\n$$\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nV=392,\\,l=8,\\,h=w\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =lwh \\\\ V & =l{w}^{2} \\\\ 392 & =8\\cdot {w}^{2} \\\\ {w}^{2} & =49 \\\\ w & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the width is $7\\,\\text{yd}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256727 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256728 13194139533864 true] [17592186256727 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256727 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256727 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256728 13194139533864 true] [17592186256727 1110 #uuid "8c55627c-05ab-58a6-ab6a-90e72b992534" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256728 1034 "7\\,\\text{yd}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256728 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256728 1111 #uuid "8441e73c-3c42-565f-86a4-6af1030518c4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256729 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256730 13194139533864 true] [17592186256729 1089 "q-10764" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256729 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256729 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/5f6bf9cffbcfb9cf223f42cc1430570ee311541b969176bc303b41e4668dcca8.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows a correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y.$ Which of the following is the most appropriate regression model for this data?" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256729 1113 #uuid "636eda17-e825-56ec-8d5a-51448a16fe2c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256729 1145 "As values of $x$ increase, the corresponding values of $y$ decrease rapidly, too. It seems like the points on the plot are scattered along an exponential curve, as shown below.\n\nTherefore, among the given options, the most appropriate regression model is *exponential.*" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256730 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256731 13194139533864 true] [17592186256730 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256732 13194139533864 true] [17592186256730 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256733 13194139533864 true] [17592186256730 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256734 13194139533864 true] [17592186256730 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256735 13194139533864 true] [17592186256730 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186256730 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256730 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256731 13194139533864 true] [17592186256730 1110 #uuid "532b01d3-9c91-5197-9c7e-20cd08c120d4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256731 1034 "Exponential" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256731 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256731 1111 #uuid "e18d1e12-9df4-5acf-9419-c22b0b477135" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256732 1034 "Cubic" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256732 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256732 1111 #uuid "a0c309b0-c958-544f-b676-1ca328b37e2a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256733 1034 "Quadratic" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256733 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256733 1111 #uuid "97fe2927-499a-51d6-8932-ae6d072c6129" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256734 1034 "Linear" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256734 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256734 1111 #uuid "192857fc-12d6-59b9-9ad1-1c2a01a283d5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256735 1034 "Logarithmic" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256735 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256735 1111 #uuid "0a22ee4c-874e-5337-b4c8-99eb56686ce2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256736 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256737 13194139533864 true] [17592186256736 1089 "q-107659" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256736 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256736 1100 "Express the following arithmetic series using sigma notation.\n\n$$\n2+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{3}{2}+…-\\frac{41}{4}-12\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256736 1113 #uuid "4f95e576-a69f-50d3-a4ad-5ca5441cb0c4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256736 1145 "The first term ${a}_{1}=2$ and the common difference is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =2+(n-1)(-\\frac{7}{4}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{7}{4}n+\\frac{7}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe don't know the index of the last term, but we can find it using the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-12 \\\\ \\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}n & =-12 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4}n & =\\frac{15}{4}+12 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4}n & =\\frac{63}{4} \\\\ n & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are $9$ terms in total, from $n=1$ to $n=9.$ So, the sigma notation for the given sum is:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{9}(\\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}n)\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256737 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256738 13194139533864 true] [17592186256737 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256737 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256737 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256738 13194139533864 true] [17592186256737 1110 #uuid "49686207-7b26-5609-809c-0d7218e5e071" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256738 1034 "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{9}(\\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}n)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256738 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256738 1111 #uuid "c3eb03e6-7355-530b-a62a-5ab90976e93d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256739 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256740 13194139533864 true] [17592186256739 1089 "q-107668" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256739 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256739 1100 "Express the following arithmetic series using sigma notation.\n\n$$\n-4-11-18+…-109-116\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256739 1113 #uuid "114b36be-4de0-5cb7-978d-00c318e1a7d2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256739 1145 "The first term ${a}_{1}=-4$ and the common difference is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =-11-(-4) \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-4+(n-1)(-7) \\\\  & =-4-7n+7 \\\\  & =-7n+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe don't know the index of the last term, but we can find it using the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-116 \\\\ -7n+3 & =-116 \\\\ -7n & =-119 \\\\ n & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are $17$ terms in total, from $n=1$ to $n=17.$ So, the sigma notation for the given sum is:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{17}(-7n+3)\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256740 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256741 13194139533864 true] [17592186256740 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256740 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256740 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256741 13194139533864 true] [17592186256740 1110 #uuid "88aca771-c3f9-5d92-9c15-d20b0f5a4ec7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256741 1034 "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{17}(-7n+3)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256741 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256741 1111 #uuid "cdb1809c-a2b2-5756-af00-14adab920c87" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256742 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256743 13194139533864 true] [17592186256742 1089 "q-107712" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256742 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256742 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/03a606251639b69e722613f9c2ccbe2bd576633a1051b4e1abeeecf33f90ad18.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a milk carton. Three-eighths of the carton is filled with milk, forming a cube. Find the volume of the empty space in the carton.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256742 1113 #uuid "a3bf6537-6bba-55c4-8bda-93d506025b1f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256742 1145 "The milk in the carton creates a cube. Since the height of the milk is $3\\,\\text{in},$ the side length of the cube is also $3\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nAlso, the height of $3\\,\\text{in}$ shown in the diagram represents three-eighths of the carton's height. Therefore, the height of the carton is\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{3}\\cdot 3=8\\,\\text{in}.\n$$\n\nThe empty space in the carton is a rectangular prism that is $3\\,\\text{in}$ long, $3\\,\\text{in}$ wide, and $8-3=5\\,\\text{in}$ high.\n\nWe can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=3\\,\\text{in},\\,w=3\\,\\text{in},\\,h=5\\,\\text{in}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV=3\\cdot 3\\cdot 5=45\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that there is $45\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}$ of empty space in the carton." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256743 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256744 13194139533864 true] [17592186256743 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256743 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256743 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256744 13194139533864 true] [17592186256743 1110 #uuid "b151f3e1-f754-508e-8adb-6917f945ee1b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256744 1034 "45\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256744 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256744 1111 #uuid "e71cecb4-796b-5499-8f93-b0d32b82c4f4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256745 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256746 13194139533864 true] [17592186256745 1089 "q-107749" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256745 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256745 1100 "Find the sum of the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n5+11+17+23+⋯+83\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256745 1113 #uuid "c01979e1-2f0d-57eb-bf22-2ecf15ad2571" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256745 1145 "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, ${a}_{N}$ is the last term, and $N$ is the total number of terms.\n\nFor our series, we have ${a}_{1}=5$ and the common difference is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =11-5 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =5+(n-1)(6) \\\\  & =5+6n-6 \\\\  & =6n-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we know, the last term is ${a}_{N}=83.$ Putting this value in the formula for the $n$th term obtained above, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & =6N-1 \\\\ 83 & =6N-1 \\\\ 84 & =6N \\\\ N & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting ${a}_{1}=5$, ${a}_{14}=83$, and $N=14$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{14} & =\\frac{14}{2}(5+83) \\\\  & =7\\cdot 88 \\\\  & =616\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256746 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256747 13194139533864 true] [17592186256746 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256746 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256746 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256747 13194139533864 true] [17592186256746 1110 #uuid "8532ae84-3559-5cd7-b556-eff814ccedbd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256747 1034 "616" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256747 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256747 1111 #uuid "37cfaf94-1a46-5072-bebb-e1a08259948a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256748 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256749 13194139533864 true] [17592186256748 1089 "q-107751" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256748 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256748 1100 "Find the sum of the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n(-14)+(-20)+(-26)+(-32)+⋯+(-314)\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256748 1113 #uuid "06cac020-1582-549c-9faf-4dee975a31ad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256748 1145 "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, ${a}_{N}$ is the last term, and $N$ is the total number of terms.\n\nFor our series, we have ${a}_{1}=-14$ and the common difference is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =-20-(-14) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-14+(n-1)(-6) \\\\  & =-14-6n+6 \\\\  & =-6n-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we know, the last term is ${a}_{N}=-314.$ Putting this value in the formula for the $n$th term obtained above, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & =-6N-8 \\\\ -314 & =-6N-8 \\\\ -306 & =-6N \\\\ N & =51\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting ${a}_{1}=-14$, ${a}_{51}=-314$, and $N=51$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{51} & =\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}) \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{2}\\cdot (-14-314) \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{2}\\cdot (-328) \\\\  & =-8\\,364\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256749 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256750 13194139533864 true] [17592186256749 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256749 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256749 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256750 13194139533864 true] [17592186256749 1110 #uuid "cdd765af-f333-566b-8af6-2696251ec62c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256750 1034 "-8\\,364" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256750 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256750 1111 #uuid "0f702305-766f-5dd0-8437-9e8a07a18a6f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256751 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256752 13194139533864 true] [17592186256751 1089 "q-107760" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256751 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256751 1100 "Calculate the sum of the first $20$ terms of the following arithmetic series. \n\n$$\n13+17+21+⋯\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256751 1113 #uuid "34c81d61-3e3f-5632-9eae-10ea56529564" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256751 1145 "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nWe are given the total number of terms $N=20$ and the first term is ${a}_{1}=13.$ Now, the common difference $d$ can be found as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =17-13 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${a}_{1}=13$, $d=4$, and $N=20$ into the above formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{20} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{2}(2\\cdot 13+(20-1)\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{2}\\cdot (26+76) \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{2}\\cdot 102 \\\\  & =10\\cdot 102 \\\\  & =1\\,020\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256752 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256753 13194139533864 true] [17592186256752 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256752 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256752 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256753 13194139533864 true] [17592186256752 1110 #uuid "894a8a89-af82-5b7b-828c-769b05f9756a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256753 1034 "1\\,020" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256753 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256753 1111 #uuid "af802da0-192c-5cc1-a289-cc9a48a4a188" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256754 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256755 13194139533864 true] [17592186256754 1089 "q-107794" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256754 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256754 1100 "Calculate the sum of the first $15$ terms of the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n-1+(-\\frac{2}{5})+\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}+⋯\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256754 1113 #uuid "e928ff1c-3648-56d1-a33b-01de56cefa49" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256754 1145 "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nWe are given the total number of terms $N=15$ and the first term is ${a}_{1}=-1.$ Now, the common difference $d$ can be found as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}-(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${a}_{1}=-1$, $d=\\frac{3}{5}$, and $N=15$ into the above formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{15} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}(2(-1)+(15-1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot (-2+8.4) \\\\  & =7.5\\cdot 6.4 \\\\  & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256755 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256756 13194139533864 true] [17592186256755 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256755 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256755 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256756 13194139533864 true] [17592186256755 1110 #uuid "81f67404-4664-57c5-a7ff-495c9b6fa304" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256756 1034 "48" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256756 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256756 1111 #uuid "cdb382b0-20e2-5cac-aa00-9a26b5d81f32" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256757 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256758 13194139533864 true] [17592186256757 1089 "q-108192" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256757 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256757 1100 "Stephanie is a geologist who began studying land subsidence due to a geological fault. The table below shows the land subsidence  $C,$ in centimeters, $n$ weeks after the study began. How many centimeters of subsidence were there at the beginning of the study?\n\n| $n$ (weeks) | $10$ | $15$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $C$ (centimeters) | $11$ | $13.5$ | $16$ |\n\nYou may assume that the relationship between $n$ and $C$ is linear.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256757 1113 #uuid "b7eae905-c57c-5087-add9-0cd565ddeaa1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256757 1145 "Notice that the number of centimeters increases by $2.5$ every $5$ weeks. In other words, the number of centimeters increases by\n\n$$\n\\frac{2.5}{5}=0.5\n$$\n\ncentimeters per week. Thus, if $A$ is the amount of subsidence at the beginning of the study, then we can model the total subsidence $C$ after $n$ weeks using the equation\n\n$$\nC=A+0.5n.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we substitute one of the pairs of values from the table into this equation and solve for $A.$ After $n=10$ weeks the subsidence was $C=11$ centimeters. We substitute these values into the equation and solve for $A,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =A+0.5n \\\\ 11 & =A+0.5(10) \\\\ 11 & =A+5 \\\\ A & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, there were $6$ centimeters of subsidence when the study began." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256758 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256759 13194139533864 true] [17592186256758 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256758 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256758 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256759 13194139533864 true] [17592186256758 1110 #uuid "c2df93ae-3252-5ea5-9051-ef23a01b6c04" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256759 1034 "6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256759 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256759 1111 #uuid "2f18187b-0b51-5ce0-872d-df9e45afe42e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256760 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256761 13194139533864 true] [17592186256760 1089 "q-108374" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256760 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256760 1100 "Calculate ${log}_{125}(625)$ without using a calculator.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256760 1113 #uuid "4051d6e3-232c-59f8-9d4f-58de8b478ba5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256760 1145 "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=125,$ and $X=625.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $5,$ we set $b=5.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{125}(625) & =\\frac{{log}_{5}(625)}{{log}_{5}(125)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{5}({5}^{4})}{{log}_{5}({5}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256761 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256762 13194139533864 true] [17592186256761 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256761 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256761 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256762 13194139533864 true] [17592186256761 1110 #uuid "3f15f019-2ba2-5997-922b-a7d174fc823e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256762 1034 "\\frac{4}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256762 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256762 1111 #uuid "f6bae5cc-a920-50b7-932f-de5a6e7134cf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256763 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256764 13194139533864 true] [17592186256763 1089 "q-108505" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256763 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256763 1100 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for ${x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(y)=8{x}^{3}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256763 1113 #uuid "5b263ad9-b2a7-5fb2-9db2-def03733fabf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256763 1145 "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(y)$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(y) & =8{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(y)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8{x}^{3}) \\\\ [({x}^{2})(\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{dy}}{\\text{d}x})+(2x)(\\operatorname{ln}(y))] & =24{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+2x\\operatorname{ln}(y) & =24{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =24{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{ln}(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y(24{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{ln}(y))}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =24y-\\frac{2y\\operatorname{ln}(y)}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256764 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256765 13194139533864 true] [17592186256764 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256764 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256764 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256765 13194139533864 true] [17592186256764 1110 #uuid "707359cd-9aa2-5eaf-a532-de8fecafb21d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256765 1034 "24y-\\frac{2y\\operatorname{ln}(y)}{x}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256765 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256765 1111 #uuid "8bf06412-fbcf-5324-85b0-d8b4d21b566e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256766 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256767 13194139533864 true] [17592186256766 1089 "q-108660" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256766 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256766 1100 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for ${x}^{2}y-4{y}^{3}=8x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256766 1113 #uuid "d3ec1c97-2b02-5dff-90e9-709a0139d59d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256766 1145 "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y-4{y}^{3} & =8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}y-4{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8x) \\\\ [{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy]-12{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy-12{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-12{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8-2xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-12{y}^{2}) & =8-2xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{8-2xy}{{x}^{2}-12{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256767 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256768 13194139533864 true] [17592186256767 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256767 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256767 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256768 13194139533864 true] [17592186256767 1110 #uuid "481e1c47-35b5-5102-af61-c60cf880797a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256768 1034 "\\frac{8-2xy}{{x}^{2}-12{y}^{2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256768 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256768 1111 #uuid "3e608401-e3fb-53b0-b54b-05e7ec4e2463" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256769 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256770 13194139533864 true] [17592186256769 1089 "q-108681" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256769 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256769 1100 "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\,2)$ for the curve ${x}^{2}{y}^{3}=2.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256769 1113 #uuid "4e293890-de10-5cd4-971c-ec26d5c69bb9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256769 1145 "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}{y}^{3}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{3})+{y}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{y}^{3}\\cdot 2x & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2x{y}^{3}}{3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\,2)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(1/2,\\,2)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{2y}{3x})}_{(1/2,\\,2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{3\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256770 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256771 13194139533864 true] [17592186256770 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256770 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256770 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256771 13194139533864 true] [17592186256770 1110 #uuid "f3f99b3d-1b4b-527c-a02a-4fbabeb0b9fb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256771 1034 "-\\frac{8}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256771 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256771 1111 #uuid "8cf7a164-cbcf-5dbf-b601-0f0124c38bf3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256772 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256773 13194139533864 true] [17592186256772 1089 "q-108753" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256772 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256772 1100 "What is the index of the term $104$ in the following arithmetic sequence?\n\n$$\n4,\\,9,\\,14,\\,…\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256772 1113 #uuid "97acde06-7ca9-5d94-b594-1bd2bd789eb6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256772 1145 "Since ${a}_{1}=4$ and \n\n$$\nd=9-4=5,\n$$\n\nwe can construct the formula for the $n$th term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =4+(n-1)(5) \\\\  & =4+5n-5 \\\\  & =5n-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives ${a}_{n}=104,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =5n-1 \\\\ 104 & =5n-1 \\\\ 105 & =5n \\\\ 21 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $105$ is the $21$st term of the sequence." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256773 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256774 13194139533864 true] [17592186256773 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256773 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256773 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256774 13194139533864 true] [17592186256773 1110 #uuid "58811d5d-80b6-5d2c-a9b9-9f2b21d3a4c5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256774 1034 "21" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256774 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256774 1111 #uuid "e7ca1c0e-9830-57d1-87c2-d2f47a0da86d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256775 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256776 13194139533864 true] [17592186256775 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256778 13194139533864 true] [17592186256775 1089 "q-10878" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256775 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256775 1100 "Find where the normal line to the curve $y={x}^{2}$ at the point $(3,9)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256775 1113 #uuid "99463695-96b3-52fc-80ac-01a64fec0d6b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256775 1145 "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(3)=2(3)=6,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-9 & =-\\frac{1}{6}(x-3) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{1}{2}+9 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{19}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{19}{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{19}{2} & =\\frac{1}{6}x \\\\ x & =57.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(57,0).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256776 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256777 13194139533864 true] [17592186256776 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256776 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256776 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256777 13194139533864 true] [17592186256776 1110 #uuid "39467e20-e20d-5502-9bf8-881bbc694917" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256777 1034 "57" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256777 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256777 1111 #uuid "852cbd16-a914-5ad4-9056-4d9ec5c12889" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256778 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256779 13194139533864 true] [17592186256778 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256778 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256778 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256779 13194139533864 true] [17592186256778 1110 #uuid "a3b98be8-fbca-50cf-a182-625dd393f4fc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256779 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256779 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256779 1111 #uuid "46c65590-ea51-547b-a16f-ef7e7566fc67" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256780 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256781 13194139533864 true] [17592186256780 1089 "q-108995" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256780 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256780 1100 "The position $x$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $x(t)=t{e}^{t}.$ Calculate the velocity at time $t.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256780 1113 #uuid "6cf72795-fd62-5248-9d55-fccd7b6e1ff9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256780 1145 "The velocity is the derivative of the position function $x(t)$ with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}+t{e}^{t} \\\\  & =(t+1){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256781 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256782 13194139533864 true] [17592186256781 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256781 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256781 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256782 13194139533864 true] [17592186256781 1110 #uuid "33204d84-015d-51e5-9dc3-4050ca3a6982" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256782 1034 "(t+1){e}^{t}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256782 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256782 1111 #uuid "65df2beb-a8e1-50e9-b7fa-3f3002d40d66" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256783 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256784 13194139533864 true] [17592186256783 1089 "q-109020" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256783 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256783 1100 "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in millimeters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=9t-{t}^{3},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256783 1113 #uuid "80fa2c46-8e7b-5923-bd32-30e6745dadc9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256783 1145 "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(9t-{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =9-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=2,$ the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =9-3(2)^{2} \\\\  & =9-12 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed is $|-3|=3\\,\\text{mm/s}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256784 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256785 13194139533864 true] [17592186256784 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256784 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256784 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256785 13194139533864 true] [17592186256784 1110 #uuid "6acd0a6f-2c84-5a21-a496-7782553c6286" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256785 1034 "3\\,\\text{mm/s}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256785 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256785 1111 #uuid "924df5ca-d6d8-5158-9795-be1c18058829" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256786 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256787 13194139533864 true] [17592186256786 1089 "q-10907" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256786 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256786 1100 "Find ${f}^{''}(-1)$ if $f(x)=4{x}^{5}-3{x}^{4}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256786 1113 #uuid "307089e8-baf9-5482-9f9b-5553f2129d31" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256786 1145 "Computing the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & ={(4{x}^{5}-3{x}^{4})}^{'} &  \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4{x}^{4}-4\\cdot 3{x}^{3} \\\\  & =20{x}^{4}-12{x}^{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{''}(x) & ={(20{x}^{4}-12{x}^{3})}^{'} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 20{x}^{3}-3\\cdot 12{x}^{2} \\\\  & =80{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the second derivative at $x=-1$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{''}(-1) & =80(-1)^{3}-36(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =-80-36 \\\\  & =-116.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256787 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256788 13194139533864 true] [17592186256787 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256787 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256787 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256788 13194139533864 true] [17592186256787 1110 #uuid "ca84a72e-54ab-56a5-b51a-2a49c1154ad5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256788 1034 "-116" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256788 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256788 1111 #uuid "9d5f8d8b-bf63-5cc8-a242-6b35439d3217" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256789 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256790 13194139533864 true] [17592186256789 1089 "q-110040" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256789 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256789 1100 "A website receives $v(h)$ visits every $h$ hours. What are the units of $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}h}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nWrite the units in the form \"[output units] per [input unit]\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256789 1113 #uuid "c773c57f-ca50-5bf6-b884-84eac176a3dd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256789 1145 "We have that the time $h$ is measured in hours and the number of visits is measured in visitors. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h: & \\text{hours} \\\\ v(h): & \\text{visitors}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}h}$ measures a change in $v$ over a change in $h.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}h}: & \\frac{\\text{visitors}}{\\text{hours}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}h}$ is measured in \"visitors per hour \"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256790 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256791 13194139533864 true] [17592186256790 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256790 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256790 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256791 13194139533864 true] [17592186256790 1110 #uuid "316d870c-3af4-574b-8b2e-fc81731c9bcd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256791 1034 "Visitors per hour" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256791 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256791 1111 #uuid "54f6f675-87c1-50bf-ae96-b0ca58aac52e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256792 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256793 13194139533864 true] [17592186256792 1089 "q-110047" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256792 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256792 1100 "If $h(x)={3}^{2x+4},$ find ${({h}^{-1})}^{'}(x)$ as a function of ${h}^{-1}(x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256792 1113 #uuid "7308db96-948c-5f8f-99e0-2a51c699fda9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256792 1145 "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y={3}^{2x+4},$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx={3}^{2y+4}\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={h}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({3}^{2y+4}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & ={3}^{2y+4}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}3\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & ={3}^{2(y+2)}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}({3}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & ={9}^{y+2}\\operatorname{ln}9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{9}^{y+2}\\operatorname{ln}9} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{9}^{-y}}{81\\operatorname{ln}9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${h}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={h}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{h}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{{9}^{-{h}^{-1}(x)}}{81\\operatorname{ln}9} \\\\ {({h}^{-1})}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{{9}^{-{h}^{-1}(x)}}{81\\operatorname{ln}9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256793 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256794 13194139533864 true] [17592186256793 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256793 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256793 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256794 13194139533864 true] [17592186256793 1110 #uuid "328a0aad-9468-5453-9137-15073cf0ac80" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256794 1034 "\\frac{{9}^{-{h}^{-1}(x)}}{81\\operatorname{ln}9}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256794 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256794 1111 #uuid "9d7e6958-14c6-585b-a067-97090886ba60" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256795 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256796 13194139533864 true] [17592186256795 1089 "q-110080" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256795 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256795 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/9cd032667b7138c5456cf9fa1a1cc954f1c8003779ac54fa58f6bbad296db4a1.png)\n\nWhat systems of inequalities defines the shaded region shown above?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256795 1113 #uuid "399c8dfe-cb86-5d6a-b44f-74bba4001d09" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256795 1145 "Consider the line $2x+y=1.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Writing the equation of this line in slope-intercept form, we have\n\n$$\ny=-2x+1.\n$$\n\nNow, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>-2x+1,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n2x+y>1.\n$$\n\nNow consider the line $y=x.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies  *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\"  inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny<x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+y>1 \\\\ y<x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256796 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256797 13194139533864 true] [17592186256796 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256796 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256796 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256797 13194139533864 true] [17592186256796 1110 #uuid "772e26ca-ee00-5682-8c86-cb49edb6fb71" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256797 1034 "y<x" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256797 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256797 1111 #uuid "210cc233-6585-5a6d-b06c-9bc1bdb493a7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256798 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256799 13194139533864 true] [17592186256798 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256801 13194139533864 true] [17592186256798 1089 "q-110144" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256798 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256798 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/9d4a5a15c0fc8487aa827a275375c07f048f38ed76fd94b9c40223ff7c38fa9c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the graphs of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$, where $g(x)=f(x+a)+b.$ What are the values of $a$ and $b?$\n\n$a$: {{a}}\n\n$b$: {{b}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256798 1113 #uuid "ce12f197-2895-5a7b-ab67-64d3d897a2dc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256798 1145 "Notice that every point on the graph $y=g(x)$ is two units *to the right* and six units *up* of the corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x).$ \n\nSo, we conclude that $g(x)=f(x-2)+6,$ and therefore $a=-2$ and $b=6.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256799 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256800 13194139533864 true] [17592186256799 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256799 1041 "a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256799 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256800 13194139533864 true] [17592186256799 1110 #uuid "8ab543ab-afd4-5116-a449-3a144754a7ed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256800 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256800 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256800 1111 #uuid "bc6fd4af-ae32-5b31-9841-c3e3f540ec96" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256801 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256802 13194139533864 true] [17592186256801 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256801 1041 "b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256801 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256802 13194139533864 true] [17592186256801 1110 #uuid "cf29d8e0-883f-555e-b637-a654725199f1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256802 1034 "6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256802 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256802 1111 #uuid "e104378d-2249-50b8-a6f1-04beef5efc04" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256803 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256804 13194139533864 true] [17592186256803 1089 "q-110195" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256803 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256803 1100 "The geometric series $1+5+25+125+625$ can be written using sigma notation as\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256803 1113 #uuid "dbfdc4aa-e0a1-5cb8-a5f8-802705527624" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256803 1145 "We must express the given series in the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, ${a}_{1}=1$ and $N=5.$\n\nTo find $r,$ we calculate the ratio of the first two terms in the series:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{5}{1}=5\n$$\n\nTherefore, expressing the geometric series given in sigma notation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+5+25+125+625 & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}1\\cdot {5}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}{5}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256804 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256805 13194139533864 true] [17592186256804 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256804 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256804 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256805 13194139533864 true] [17592186256804 1110 #uuid "ac18b7f5-d430-595d-868a-e6af9ce80ab6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256805 1034 "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}{5}^{n-1}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256805 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256805 1111 #uuid "05b767e5-a9aa-524d-91a0-5c0860dc8bcb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256806 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256807 13194139533864 true] [17592186256806 1089 "q-110200" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256806 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256806 1100 "The geometric series $2+8+32+128$ can be written using sigma notation as\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256806 1113 #uuid "8349b03f-f80a-5400-be8a-3eaa57417e1a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256806 1145 "We must express the given series in the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, ${a}_{1}=2$ and $N=4.$\n\nTo find $r,$ we calculate the ratio of the first two terms in the series:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{8}{2}=4\n$$\n\nTherefore, expressing the geometric series given in sigma notation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+8+32+128 & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}2\\cdot {4}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256807 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256808 13194139533864 true