6814 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256814 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256814 1111 #uuid "211dd85d-2832-52ce-8e1e-34f5dc81217e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256815 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256816 13194139533864 true] [17592186256815 1089 "q-110277" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256815 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256815 1100 "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}\\frac{1}{9}(5)^{n-1}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256815 1113 #uuid "3e0062d6-cbd1-58f3-a005-e59a201ee54b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256815 1145 "Since the given series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{9},$ $r=5$ and $N=7.$ If we substitute these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}\\frac{1}{9}(5)^{n-1} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{5}^{7})}{1-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{5}^{7})}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{36}\\cdot (-78\\,124) \\\\  & =\\frac{19\\,531}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256816 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256817 13194139533864 true] [17592186256816 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256816 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256816 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256817 13194139533864 true] [17592186256816 1110 #uuid "0d921919-d38f-5c37-be04-22304885e161" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256817 1034 "\\frac{19\\,531}{9}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256817 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256817 1111 #uuid "7fc8b167-ef15-5146-9667-67a32a66e6bb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256818 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256819 13194139533864 true] [17592186256818 1089 "q-110328" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256818 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256818 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=3x+\\sqrt{x}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{'}(2).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256818 1113 #uuid "1dda7663-69d0-5c1d-b3aa-82594e0c8df7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256818 1145 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{'}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{'}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ 3x+\\sqrt{x} & =2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =2-3x \\\\ x & =4-12x+9{x}^{2} \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-13x+4 & =0 \\\\ (9x-4)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{9},\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChecking the solutions, we notice that only $x=\\frac{4}{9}$ satisfies the equation $3x+\\sqrt{x}=2.$ Therefore, we discard $x=1$ and conclude that ${f}^{-1}(2)=\\frac{4}{9}.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{'}(\\frac{4}{9}),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3x+\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {f}^{'}(x) & =3+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{'}(\\frac{4}{9}) & =3+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{(\\frac{4}{9})}} \\\\ {f}^{'}(\\frac{4}{9}) & =3+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {f}^{'}(\\frac{4}{9}) & =\\frac{15}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{'}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}(\\frac{4}{9})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256819 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256820 13194139533864 true] [17592186256819 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256819 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256819 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256820 13194139533864 true] [17592186256819 1110 #uuid "c07f2208-d0a3-5e70-9c26-0d4c4b241668" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256820 1034 "\\frac{4}{15}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256820 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256820 1111 #uuid "c9e3c5ab-0da9-504a-88