86256824 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256825 13194139533864 true] [17592186256824 1089 "q-110539" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256824 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256824 1100 "If ${81}^{2a}=\\frac{1}{{3}^{a-1}}$ then $a=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256824 1113 #uuid "f61369c5-9b9f-545c-8059-dd0509d17c85" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256824 1145 "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $81$ as ${3}^{4},$ and then we rewrite the right-hand side using the power rule of exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{81}^{2a} & =\\frac{1}{{3}^{a-1}} \\\\ {({3}^{4})}^{2a} & =\\frac{1}{{3}^{a-1}} \\\\ {3}^{8a} & =\\frac{1}{{3}^{a-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using a negative exponent: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{8a} & ={3}^{-(a-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(3),$ we equate the exponents and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8a & =-(a-1) \\\\ 8a & =-a+1 \\\\ 9a & =1 \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256825 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256826 13194139533864 true] [17592186256825 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256825 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256825 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256826 13194139533864 true] [17592186256825 1110 #uuid "40273622-2870-5090-97f5-a4f10a95baac" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256826 1034 "\\frac{1}{9}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256826 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256826 1111 #uuid "9aab07c9-d4d4-520d-8b13-0b521041dab9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256827 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256828 13194139533864 true] [17592186256827 1089 "q-110540" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256827 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256827 1100 "Find ${f}^{'}(x)$  for $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}2x}{9}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256827 1113 #uuid "113190a3-0cee-5e3d-a690-7a607d9c17f5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256827 1145 "The formula for the derivative of arcsine is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsin}x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}2x}{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsin}2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256828 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256829 13194139533864 true] [17592186256828 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256828 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256828 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256829 13194139533864 true] [17592186256828 1110 #uuid "7448f2c5-cb58-504e-b50b-ff0ab239c6d1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256829 1034 "\\frac{2}{9\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256829 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256829 1111 #uuid "964e576b-9a7c-572e-b767-246ad5880293" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256830 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256831 13194139533864 true] [17592186256830 1089 "q-110621" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256830 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256830 1100 "Calculate ${f}^{'}(x)$ for $f(x)=\\operatorname{arccos}(5+3{x}^{2}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256830 1113 #uuid "cc4b610a-b353-55a8-9b6f-c012e84f0426" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256830 1145 "The formula for the derivative of arccosine is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arccos}x)=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\operatorname{arccos}(5+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{(5+3{x}^{2})}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{(5+3{x}^{2})}^{2}}}\\cdot (6x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6x}{\\sqrt{1-{(5+3{x}^{2})}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256831 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256832 13194139533864 true] [17592186256831 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256831 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256831 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256832 13194139533864 true] [17592186256831 1110 #uuid "39849619-04af-5c04-bca7-d7637b271613" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256832 1034 "-\\frac{6x}{\\sqrt{1-{(5+3{x}^{2})}^{2}}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256832 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256832 1111 #uuid "342d55ec-0115-5f99-9daa-a43c2924eddf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256833 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256834 13194139533864 true] [17592186256833 1089 "q-110768" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256833 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256833 1100 "If $\\operatorname{log}(2x)+5=5,$ then $x=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256833 1113 #uuid "7cfeb4b6-43fa-5ecb-97ca-0bb7ce89abb9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256833 1145 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}(2x)+5 & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}(2x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $10,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}(2x) & =0 \\\\ {10}^{\\operatorname{log}(2x)} & ={10}^{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${10}^{\\operatorname{log}(2x)}=2x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={10}^{0} \\\\ 2x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256834 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256835 13194139533864 true] [17592186256834 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256834 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256834 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256835 13194139533864 true] [17592186256834 1110 #uuid "5bf1c538-27c7-5ccf-8b87-dd65b49ef275" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256835 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256835 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256835 1111 #uuid "07f16a31-68cf-50b0-a4f8-6115e3e39831" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256836 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256837 13194139533864 true] [17592186256836 1089 "q-110798" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256836 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256836 1100 "Solve ${log}_{2}(x-2)={log}_{2}(2x-3)$ for $x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf none exist, enter a phrase such as \"No real solutions\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256836 1113 #uuid "cbf7b752-cbd4-57de-8347-862618b357a1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256836 1145 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}(x-2) & ={log}_{2}(2x-3) \\\\ x-2 & =2x-3 \\\\ -x & =-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}(x-2) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{2}(2x-3) \\\\ {log}_{2}(1-2) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{2}(2\\cdot 1-3) \\\\ {log}_{2}(-1) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{2}(-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, ${log}_{2}(-1)$ is not a real number.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation has no real solutions." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256837 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256838 13194139533864 true] [17592186256837 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256837 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256837 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256838 13194139533864 true] [17592186256837 1110 #uuid "ba70de7f-710d-52b0-9dbf-4fe40595a65c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256838 1034 "No real solutions" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256838 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256838 1111 #uuid "c626e4c3-0071-5e2d-8f3c-bb8499222473" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256839 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256840 13194139533864 true] [17592186256839 1089 "q-110799" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256839 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256839 1100 "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{log}({x}^{2}+4)=\\operatorname{log}(2x+4).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256839 1113 #uuid "6b3de9cf-84d9-5ffb-a26d-3666f3702b94" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256839 1145 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}({x}^{2}+4) & =\\operatorname{log}(2x+4) \\\\ {x}^{2}+4 & =2x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=0$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}(2x+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}({0}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}(2(0)+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}(4) & =\\operatorname{log}(4).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}(2x+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}({2}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}(2(2)+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}(8) & =\\operatorname{log}(8).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=0,2.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256840 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256841 13194139533864 true] [17592186256840 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256840 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256840 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256841 13194139533864 true] [17592186256840 1110 #uuid "b1274f57-0d34-5aee-9ab9-5b91fb8f9816" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256841 1034 "0,2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256841 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256841 1111 #uuid "41fa9c06-6750-5d76-a1fb-74763af261b5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256842 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256843 13194139533864 true] [17592186256842 1089 "q-111362" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256842 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256842 1100 "Given that  $(3{s}^{2}+2s+4)({s}^{2}+3s+2)=3{s}^{4}+a{s}^{3}+b{s}^{2}+cs+8,$ calculate $a+b+c.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256842 1113 #uuid "55058b06-dd5d-5caa-a687-35d85c6cc189" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256842 1145 "We distribute all three terms of the trinomial in the first set of parentheses to the trinomial in the second set of parentheses. Then, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{s}^{2}+2s+4)({s}^{2}+3s+2) & = \\\\ 3{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+3s+2)+(2s)({s}^{2}+3s+2)+(4)({s}^{2}+3s+2) & = \\\\ 3{s}^{4}+9{s}^{3}+6{s}^{2}+2{s}^{3}+6{s}^{2}+4s+4{s}^{2}+12s+8 & = \\\\ 3{s}^{4}+11{s}^{3}+16{s}^{2}+16s+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=11,$ $b=16,$ and $c=16.$ So, \n\n$$\na+b+c=11+16+16=43.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256843 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256844 13194139533864 true] [17592186256843 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256843 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256843 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256844 13194139533864 true] [17592186256843 1110 #uuid "668faa38-775f-5725-a250-436066030f64" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256844 1034 "43" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256844 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256844 1111 #uuid "9c396da7-833f-5cec-855b-b86b88810cce" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256845 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256846 13194139533864 true] [17592186256845 1089 "q-111717" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256845 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256845 1100 "$-\\sqrt{-8}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256845 1113 #uuid "dd5a7ee1-e31e-503f-8ce9-191a315a879f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256845 1145 "The imaginary unit $\\text{i}$ is defined as:\n\n$$\n\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given number can be rewritten as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{-8} & =-\\sqrt{8\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{8}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{8}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256846 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256847 13194139533864 true] [17592186256846 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256846 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256846 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256847 13194139533864 true] [17592186256846 1110 #uuid "88078637-009a-59cf-9066-8c70f1bd6a5c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256847 1034 "-\\sqrt{8}\\,\\text{i}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256847 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256847 1111 #uuid "a6aa369d-885f-586d-bdfd-9f032c4d4a1a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256848 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256849 13194139533864 true] [17592186256848 1089 "q-111721" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256848 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256848 1100 "$-\\sqrt{-15}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256848 1113 #uuid "f9a7660b-b110-5991-98aa-28a0f141dba4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256848 1145 "The imaginary unit $\\text{i}$ is defined as:\n\n$$\n\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given number can be rewritten as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{-15} & =-\\sqrt{15\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{15}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{15}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256849 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256850 13194139533864 true] [17592186256849 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256849 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256849 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256850 13194139533864 true] [17592186256849 1110 #uuid "01cbce41-64f7-5c3b-9548-cb2aa37324c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256850 1034 "-\\sqrt{15}\\,\\text{i}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256850 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256850 1111 #uuid "563fb3fe-f33f-5523-a72f-de46890b952a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256851 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256852 13194139533864 true] [17592186256851 1089 "q-111741" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256851 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256851 1100 "$\\sqrt{-180}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256851 1113 #uuid "b18cb4ef-a56d-5053-8e77-e1968dc8458c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256851 1145 "The imaginary unit $\\text{i}$ is defined as:\n\n$$\n\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\n$$\n\nSince $180=36\\cdot 5,$ the given number can be rewritten as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{-180} & =\\sqrt{180\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36\\cdot 5\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256852 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256853 13194139533864 true] [17592186256852 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256852 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256852 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256853 13194139533864 true] [17592186256852 1110 #uuid "daf0aa97-1b4c-50f1-ae63-1d2eb35230a6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256853 1034 "6\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256853 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256853 1111 #uuid "447005c9-6710-5f95-becc-6f02a7e746d6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256854 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256855 13194139533864 true] [17592186256854 1089 "q-112042" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256854 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256854 1100 "Factor the expression ${r}^{4}-(5s)^{4}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256854 1113 #uuid "79c6e0be-06e2-559d-80d7-97545e89753f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256854 1145 "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{r}^{4}-(5s)^{4}={({r}^{2})}^{2}-{((5s)^{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({r}^{2})}^{2}-{((5s)^{2})}^{2} & =({r}^{2}+(5s)^{2})({r}^{2}-(5s)^{2}) \\\\  & =({r}^{2}+25{s}^{2})({r}^{2}-(5s)^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares. Therefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({r}^{2}+25{s}^{2})({r}^{2}-(5s)^{2}) & =({r}^{2}+25{s}^{2})(r+5s)(r-5s)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256855 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256856 13194139533864 true] [17592186256855 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256855 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256855 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256856 13194139533864 true] [17592186256855 1110 #uuid "f169cd56-1140-5eae-bf99-095fbc578cf5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256856 1034 "({r}^{2}+25{s}^{2})(r+5s)(r-5s)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256856 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256856 1111 #uuid "9ed374db-f5a0-53d7-a45d-279793672c5b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256857 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256858 13194139533864 true] [17592186256857 1089 "q-112044" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256857 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256857 1100 "Factor the expression $81{m}^{4}-{n}^{4}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256857 1113 #uuid "653b723d-c78b-5196-a990-9f8d17649f57" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256857 1145 "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n81{m}^{4}-{n}^{4}={(9{m}^{2})}^{2}-{({n}^{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{(9{m}^{2})}^{2}-{({n}^{2})}^{2}=(9{m}^{2}+{n}^{2})(9{m}^{2}-{n}^{2})\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares: \n\n$$\n9{m}^{2}-{n}^{2}={(3m)}^{2}-{n}^{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9{m}^{2}+{n}^{2})(9{m}^{2}-{n}^{2}) & =(9{m}^{2}+{n}^{2})(3m+n)(3m-n)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256858 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256859 13194139533864 true] [17592186256858 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256858 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256858 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256859 13194139533864 true] [17592186256858 1110 #uuid "481a6b4b-d2e2-55f5-b008-269a01e68a2a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256859 1034 "(9{m}^{2}+{n}^{2})(3m+n)(3m-n)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256859 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256859 1111 #uuid "ebee3dc9-4221-5947-a670-12da139e9b23" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256860 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256861 13194139533864 true] [17592186256860 1089 "q-112045" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256860 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256860 1100 "Factor the expression ${j}^{4}-81{k}^{4}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256860 1113 #uuid "b9eff020-73a6-55b5-a5ce-93f7544df575" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256860 1145 "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{j}^{4}-81{k}^{4}={({j}^{2})}^{2}-{(9{k}^{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{({j}^{2})}^{2}-{(9{k}^{2})}^{2}=({j}^{2}+9{k}^{2})({j}^{2}-9{k}^{2})\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares:\n \n\n$$\n{j}^{2}-9{k}^{2}={j}^{2}-{(3k)}^{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({j}^{2}+9{k}^{2})({j}^{2}-9{k}^{2}) & =({j}^{2}+9{k}^{2})(j+3k)(j-3k)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256861 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256862 13194139533864 true] [17592186256861 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256861 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256861 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256862 13194139533864 true] [17592186256861 1110 #uuid "c7ac8d6c-480e-527d-8148-9e8ad4d8a254" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256862 1034 "({j}^{2}+9{k}^{2})(j+3k)(j-3k)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256862 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256862 1111 #uuid "1361249a-36d7-57c4-bdd3-eb4790195c0d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256863 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256864 13194139533864 true] [17592186256863 1089 "q-112382" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256863 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256863 1100 "After taking a pill containing $300$ milligrams of a particular drug, the quantity of the drug in a patient's body decreases at a rate of $c(t)=300\\cdot {2}^{-0.7t}$ milligrams per hour, where $t$ is the time, in hours, since the patient consumed the pill. What will be the rate of decrease after $5$ hours? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256863 1113 #uuid "7a6d442f-4688-5203-a723-98f8e649492f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256863 1145 "Since we want to determine the rate of decrease in the quantity of the drug in the patient's body after $5$ hours, we have $t=5.$\n\nThus, the rate of decrease in the quantity of the drug after $5$ hours will be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c(5) & =300\\cdot {2}^{-0.7(5)} \\\\  & =300\\cdot {2}^{-3.5} \\\\  & \\approx 27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of decrease will be $27$ milligrams per hour." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256864 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256865 13194139533864 true] [17592186256864 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256864 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256864 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256865 13194139533864 true] [17592186256864 1110 #uuid "57fa2f2d-5fc0-5725-9f07-9fc0207ec675" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256865 1034 "27" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256865 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256865 1111 #uuid "7d43a382-c034-5630-8dba-d0491c7e917d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256866 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256867 13194139533864 true] [17592186256866 1089 "q-112509" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256866 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256866 1100 "Joseph is measuring the height reached by his lime tree by recording the following two numbers for different values of $t,$ where $t$ is the time, in years, since he planted the tree:\n\n- $H(t)$ - The tree's height, in centimeters\n\n- $r(t)$ - the rate at which the tree's height is increasing.\n\nWhat is the relationship between $H(t)$ and $r(t)?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256866 1113 #uuid "3f4c5624-08a5-5d28-9dbb-7ddefb779501" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256866 1145 "The function $H(t)$ gives the tree's height after $t$ years. Then,\n\n$$\n{H}^{'}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}H(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }H}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${H}^{'}(t)$ gives the rate at which the height of the tree increases at time $t$, which is the same as $r(t).$ Therefore, the relationship between $H(t)$ and $r(t)$ is ${H}^{'}(t)=r(t).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256867 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256868 13194139533864 true] [17592186256867 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256867 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256867 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256868 13194139533864 true] [17592186256867 1110 #uuid "cbf5d2b0-c275-5a08-a54b-aa11285096a1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256868 1034 "H'(t)=r(t)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256868 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256868 1111 #uuid "69d547a5-adac-5547-ba62-a093c4262da3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256869 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256870 13194139533864 true] [17592186256869 1089 "q-112579" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256869 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256869 1100 "The budget of a school varies over the year. The budget $B,$ in thousands of dollars, after $t$ months since the beginning of the school can be modeled by the function $B(t)=(t-1)^{2}{e}^{-t}+10$ thousands of dollars. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the budget as a function of $t?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256869 1113 #uuid "1d895573-240c-53b0-84aa-628f79fd54ee" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256869 1145 "The instantaneous rate of change of the budget is equal to ${B}^{'}(t).$ \n\nWe take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{'}(t) & =2(t-1){e}^{-t}-(t-1)^{2}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(2t-2-{t}^{2}+2t-1) \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(4t-3-{t}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the school budget is ${e}^{-t}(4t-3-{t}^{2})$ thousands of dollars per month." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256870 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256871 13194139533864 true] [17592186256870 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256870 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256870 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256871 13194139533864 true] [17592186256870 1110 #uuid "f64d4b93-4068-5741-b0af-7a069f7f3785" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256871 1034 "{e}^{-t}(4t-3-{t}^{2})" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256871 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256871 1111 #uuid "709b6546-0492-5232-93ce-5810ccae4867" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256872 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256873 13194139533864 true] [17592186256872 1089 "q-112591" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256872 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256872 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/7b0ca55d089bea13471e9de6dd26093a1a9d477937106c92be26d5a469778c8a.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $x=-2$ is a simple root.\nII. - $x=0$ is a root of odd multiplicity.\nIII. - $x=2$ is a root of even multiplicity." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256872 1113 #uuid "f8190dc0-5e3e-595b-8144-fb26d1f0ac64" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256872 1145 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The root $x=-2$ is a simple root because the graph crosses the \n$x$-axis at the point and the curve is not tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=0$ has an odd multiplicity because the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Statement III is true. The root $x=2$ has an even multiplicity because at $x=2$ the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256873 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256874 13194139533864 true] [17592186256873 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256875 13194139533864 true] [17592186256873 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256876 13194139533864 true] [17592186256873 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256877 13194139533864 true] [17592186256873 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256878 13194139533864 true] [17592186256873 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256879 13194139533864 true] [17592186256873 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256880 13194139533864 true] [17592186256873 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256881 13194139533864 true] [17592186256873 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186256873 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256873 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256874 13194139533864 true] [17592186256873 1110 #uuid "2b6e0b01-1512-5734-99de-0dd680458bf2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256874 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256874 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256874 1111 #uuid "7d9b7624-8b63-5c92-9e87-99970e683bdd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256875 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256875 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256875 1111 #uuid "30a05358-5f44-575b-a9de-ce1822364fc6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256876 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256876 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256876 1111 #uuid "930f846a-0de4-5297-bf95-95ab3ae4dead" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256877 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256877 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256877 1111 #uuid "a56ef72d-5597-55cb-9d06-627f7b68b3dc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256878 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256878 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256878 1111 #uuid "ffaf444c-54a3-5b3a-aced-cf563009d2c3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256879 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256879 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256879 1111 #uuid "a78c54fe-b2e5-5eb5-83ac-6e9175fcf819" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256880 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256880 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256880 1111 #uuid "19b0761b-be8e-589d-b2c9-ce99c5c71159" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256881 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256881 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256881 1111 #uuid "dec88327-3398-521e-bf43-d73f8b93ee45" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256882 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256883 13194139533864 true] [17592186256882 1089 "q-112786" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256882 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256882 1100 "What is the multiplicity of the root $x=4$ in the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+32?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256882 1113 #uuid "e3bc7886-a3e3-5609-abf7-ad1cbadc0e93" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256882 1145 "Since $x=4$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x-4)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x-4)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-4)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $-6$ | $0$ | $32$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $-8$ | $-32$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-2$ | $-8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x-4}={x}^{2}-2x-8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\np(x)=(x-4)({x}^{2}-2x-8).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x-4)(x+2)(x-4) \\\\  & =(x+2)(x-4)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x-4)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=4$ has multiplicity $2.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256883 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256884 13194139533864 true] [17592186256883 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256883 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256883 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256884 13194139533864 true] [17592186256883 1110 #uuid "9160b3a0-d236-519e-9988-64bbfb4e234a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256884 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256884 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256884 1111 #uuid "89c9475a-c940-57e5-b093-63cdc1c0e4be" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256885 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256886 13194139533864 true] [17592186256885 1089 "q-112805" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256885 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256885 1100 "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x)=5{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}$ have multiplicity $2?$\n\n$x =$: {{x}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256885 1113 #uuid "65d26569-d024-5df5-80e7-ebdb725c458d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256885 1145 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. \n\nFor this polynomial, we can factor by noting that the greatest common factor of $5{x}^{3}$ and $-15{x}^{2}$ is $5{x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =5{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2} \\\\  & =5{x}^{2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $x$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- The factor $(x-3)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=3$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256886 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256887 13194139533864 true] [17592186256886 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256886 1041 "x" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256886 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256887 13194139533864 true] [17592186256886 1110 #uuid "070c4ccc-7e7e-523e-a68f-8973b72aec99" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256887 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256887 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256887 1111 #uuid "537ab16a-e40c-5f97-a951-d58de51d5252" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256888 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256889 13194139533864 true] [17592186256888 1089 "q-112837" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256888 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256888 1100 "Calculate the discriminant of the quadratic equation $4{x}^{2}-3x=0.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256888 1113 #uuid "6d21dfae-6d8c-52e5-8ced-deddffba77c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256888 1145 "We have $a=4,$ $b=-3,$ and $c=0.$ So, we calculate the discriminant as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-3)^{2}-4(4)(0) \\\\  & =9-0 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256889 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256890 13194139533864 true] [17592186256889 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256889 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256889 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256890 13194139533864 true] [17592186256889 1110 #uuid "3f66180a-6c98-506d-b01a-627d14bd739f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256890 1034 "9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256890 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256890 1111 #uuid "c8ed3567-e2e2-5776-b081-4666187091d5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256891 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256892 13194139533864 true] [17592186256891 1089 "q-112848" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256891 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256891 1100 "Consider the quadratic equation ${x}^{2}-6x+9=0.$ How many distinct real solutions does it have?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256891 1113 #uuid "651268f9-ded3-54c1-9fe8-21b92e019084" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256891 1145 "To quickly determine the number of solutions, we can compute the discriminant. We have $a=1,$ $b=-6,$ and $c=9,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-6)^{2}-4(1)(9) \\\\  & =36-36 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the discriminant is zero $(D=0),$ the equation has one distinct real solution." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256892 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256893 13194139533864 true] [17592186256892 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256892 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256892 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256893 13194139533864 true] [17592186256892 1110 #uuid "ecbbe986-b8f4-5dfd-8cb7-e10017d88391" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256893 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256893 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256893 1111 #uuid "5afaf879-395b-53d8-8fb1-82cbf20f0b1c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256894 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256895 13194139533864 true] [17592186256894 1089 "q-112849" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256894 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256894 1100 "Consider the quadratic equation $4{x}^{2}-12x+9=0.$ How many distinct real solutions does it have?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256894 1113 #uuid "a95f3ae6-2de7-53c9-a211-7238246c0d4d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256894 1145 "To quickly determine the number of solutions, we can compute the discriminant. We have $a=4,$ $b=-12,$ and $c=9,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-12)^{2}-4(4)(9) \\\\  & =144-144 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the discriminant is zero $(D=0),$ the equation has one distinct real solution." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256895 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256896 13194139533864 true] [17592186256895 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256895 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256895 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256896 13194139533864 true] [17592186256895 1110 #uuid "31bc67ec-dff9-543a-85d5-e152981038ba" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256896 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256896 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256896 1111 #uuid "15c4698d-ad65-523e-bee5-6a8a35c0bf9a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256897 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256898 13194139533864 true] [17592186256897 1089 "q-112867" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256897 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256897 1100 "According to the rational roots theorem, What could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+10?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256897 1113 #uuid "9e4c35d0-80ea-555f-a42d-fa766d0cba07" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256897 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $10,$ and the leading coefficient is $8.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }10}{\\text{factor of }8}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{-5}{4}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(-5)$ is a factor of $10$ and the denominator $(4)$ is a factor of $8.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256898 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256899 13194139533864 true] [17592186256898 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256898 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256898 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256899 13194139533864 true] [17592186256898 1110 #uuid "99d9f829-2ab1-507e-a54e-e1c936f87e15" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256899 1034 "-\\frac{5}{4}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256899 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256899 1111 #uuid "f1100322-f9f9-5124-a50e-27f9ee343c35" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256900 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256901 13194139533864 true] [17592186256900 1089 "q-112871" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256900 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256900 1100 "According to the rational roots theorem, What could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{7}-12{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}-3x+10?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256900 1113 #uuid "284a91b3-0aac-5ff1-be86-b1f45987e7b5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256900 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $10,$ and the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }10}{\\text{factor of }4}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{5}{4}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(5)$ is a factor of $10$ and the denominator $(4)$ is a factor of $4.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256901 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256902 13194139533864 true] [17592186256901 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256901 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256901 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256902 13194139533864 true] [17592186256901 1110 #uuid "2935c1f0-080f-504b-bd7e-60952c1de321" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256902 1034 "\\frac{5}{4}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256902 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256902 1111 #uuid "51b3db7c-057e-5c4b-934f-a7beba01180a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256903 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256904 13194139533864 true] [17592186256903 1089 "q-112878" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256903 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256903 1100 "According to the rational roots theorem, What **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{5}-5{x}^{3}+6?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256903 1113 #uuid "25b2159c-942d-5822-b9a6-b71a06f24251" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256903 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $6$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }6}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $4$ does not take the above form. The value $4$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{4}{1},$ and the numerator $(4)$ is not a factor of $6.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256904 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256905 13194139533864 true] [17592186256904 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256904 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256904 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256905 13194139533864 true] [17592186256904 1110 #uuid "26f63a45-44a6-5c2e-990a-9e8fb70acb02" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256905 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256905 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256905 1111 #uuid "d247b06a-64f3-560a-a0df-357046193290" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256906 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256907 13194139533864 true] [17592186256906 1089 "q-113033" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256906 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256906 1100 "Simplify ${({\\text{i}}^{4})}^{26}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256906 1113 #uuid "dbdb58f0-667e-5aa7-8bff-41bad7b32ac0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256906 1145 "Let's recall the first few powers of $\\text{i},$ where $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{i}}^{0} & =1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{1} & =\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{3} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot \\text{i}=(-1)\\cdot \\text{i}=-\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{4} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot {\\text{i}}^{2}=(-1)\\cdot (-1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{({\\text{i}}^{4})}^{26}=(1)^{26}=1.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256907 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256908 13194139533864 true] [17592186256907 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256907 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256907 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256908 13194139533864 true] [17592186256907 1110 #uuid "17777363-aed8-56fb-b2c6-70ad72085ac5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256908 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256908 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256908 1111 #uuid "663a2306-738c-57ca-bff9-92357aaf260b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256909 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256910 13194139533864 true] [17592186256909 1089 "q-113087" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256909 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256909 1100 "$9{\\text{i}}^{-4}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256909 1113 #uuid "92f2f0b7-de4f-58b0-acaa-26e31d056305" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256909 1145 "First, using the laws of exponents, we can write\n\n$$\n9{\\text{i}}^{-4}=\\frac{9}{{\\text{i}}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nWe now recall that ${\\text{i}}^{4}=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{{\\text{i}}^{4}}=\\frac{9}{1}=9.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256910 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256911 13194139533864 true] [17592186256910 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256910 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256910 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256911 13194139533864 true] [17592186256910 1110 #uuid "456339f4-a0d9-5d0b-a8d0-490f373da9dc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256911 1034 "9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256911 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256911 1111 #uuid "4fc8407b-d6ef-533c-b4f4-5ab84427489d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256912 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256913 13194139533864 true] [17592186256912 1089 "q-113092" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256912 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256912 1100 "Find the roots of $f(w)=\\frac{{w}^{2}-3w-10}{2{w}^{2}-10w}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256912 1113 #uuid "ee249868-10fd-54ab-8257-c7e4ccf74f80" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256912 1145 "The roots of $f(w)$ are the solutions to the equation $f(w)=0.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{{w}^{2}-3w-10}{2{w}^{2}-10w}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(w) & =\\frac{{w}^{2}-3w-10}{2{w}^{2}-10w} \\\\  & =\\frac{(w+2)(w-5)}{2w(w-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(w+2)\\cancel{(w-5)}}{2w\\cancel{(w-5)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(w+2)}{2w}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $w:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w+2 & =0\\,⟹\\,w=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to $f(w)=0$ is $w=-2.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256913 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256914 13194139533864 true] [17592186256913 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256913 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256913 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256914 13194139533864 true] [17592186256913 1110 #uuid "74321424-c74a-5f83-a2a8-7acffeed9912" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256914 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256914 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256914 1111 #uuid "3ba8a4f9-6db3-5c67-98b1-6ca1d1ca2f8b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256915 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256916 13194139533864 true] [17592186256915 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256918 13194139533864 true] [17592186256915 1089 "q-113164" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256915 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256915 1100 "What are the solutions of the equation $6{y}^{2}+14=0?$\n\nNegative imaginary: {{negative-imaginary}}\n\nPositive imaginary: {{positive-imaginary}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256915 1113 #uuid "f045a7ea-6b22-523f-a4fe-043221867170" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256915 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{y}^{2}+14 & =0 \\\\ 6{y}^{2} & =-14 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-\\frac{14}{6} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking square root, we obtain:\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{7}{3}}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $y$ using the imaginary unit $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{7}{3}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\text{i} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256916 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256917 13194139533864 true] [17592186256916 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256916 1041 "negative-imaginary" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256916 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256917 13194139533864 true] [17592186256916 1110 #uuid "46583d46-e46a-5fed-b26f-67c5a99473a0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256917 1034 "-\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{3}\\text{i}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256917 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256917 1111 #uuid "79d02542-9ea5-5dce-b67a-ce1a7b5795ce" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256918 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256919 13194139533864 true] [17592186256918 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256918 1041 "positive-imaginary" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256918 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256919 13194139533864 true] [17592186256918 1110 #uuid "611c9f31-aaae-52ad-88f4-5571e339168b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256919 1034 "\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{3}\\text{i}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256919 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256919 1111 #uuid "22966360-3580-5e45-a572-1b114c1c62b2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256920 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256921 13194139533864 true] [17592186256920 1089 "q-113208" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256920 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256920 1100 "Simplify $\\frac{2w-5}{5w}+\\frac{w+7}{5w}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256920 1113 #uuid "152bb5fb-6b78-5bf6-87a1-aa5093238c97" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256920 1145 "We have a common denominator of $5w,$ so we add the numerators and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2w-5}{5w}+\\frac{w+7}{5w} & = \\\\ \\frac{2w-5+w+7}{5w} & = \\\\ \\frac{(2w+w)+(-5+7)}{5w} & = \\\\ \\frac{3w+2}{5w} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256921 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256922 13194139533864 true] [17592186256921 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256921 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256921 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256922 13194139533864 true] [17592186256921 1110 #uuid "eaf3fab3-040b-59c3-a6f8-393f329831ac" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256922 1034 "\\frac{3w+2}{5w}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256922 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256922 1111 #uuid "77d190b9-3215-5394-b1d1-61f53b289954" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256923 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256924 13194139533864 true] [17592186256923 1089 "q-113228" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256923 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256923 1100 "Given that $\\frac{3}{2z}=p$ and $\\frac{2}{z}=q,$ what is $p-q?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256923 1113 #uuid "783bfb90-6184-5e8a-b664-e0e6d1dc5933" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256923 1145 "We wish to subtract the following fractions:\n\n$$\np-q=\\frac{3}{2z}-\\frac{2}{z}\n$$\n\nIn order to subtract the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $2z$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2z}-\\frac{2}{z} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{2z}-\\frac{2}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{z} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{2z}-\\frac{4}{2z} & = \\\\ \\frac{3-4}{2z} & = \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2z} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256924 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256925 13194139533864 true] [17592186256924 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256924 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256924 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256925 13194139533864 true] [17592186256924 1110 #uuid "3bee3d3b-1394-5c6f-9067-2e4105836080" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256925 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2z}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256925 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256925 1111 #uuid "501932a3-ba5d-56e0-a8d4-32cd9e20e496" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256926 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256927 13194139533864 true] [17592186256926 1089 "q-113229" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256926 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256926 1100 "Given that $\\frac{7}{5d}=g$ and $\\frac{2f}{d}=h,$ what is $g-h?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256926 1113 #uuid "14660835-267f-58f5-b918-aac4bda4695d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256926 1145 "We wish to subtract the following fractions:\n\n$$\ng-h=\\frac{7}{5d}-\\frac{2f}{d}\n$$\n\nIn order to subtract the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $5d$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{5d}-\\frac{2f}{d} & = \\\\ \\frac{7}{5d}-\\frac{5}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2f}{d} & = \\\\ \\frac{7}{5d}-\\frac{10f}{5d} & = \\\\ \\frac{7-10f}{5d} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256927 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256928 13194139533864 true] [17592186256927 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256927 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256927 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256928 13194139533864 true] [17592186256927 1110 #uuid "d48fb929-3776-5058-bd67-18d5b2d30c0f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256928 1034 "\\frac{7-10f}{5d}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256928 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256928 1111 #uuid "71507084-d077-551e-b813-1960adbe78dd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256929 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256930 13194139533864 true] [17592186256929 1089 "q-113302" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256929 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256929 1100 "Simplify $\\frac{3w}{v(2+w)}-\\frac{5}{4v(2+w)}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256929 1113 #uuid "0e80c14c-c7e8-540f-b03b-760b7afaf9a7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256929 1145 "In order to subtract the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $4v(1+w)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3w}{v(2+w)}-\\frac{5}{4v(2+w)} & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{4}\\cdot \\frac{3w}{v(2+w)}-\\frac{5}{4v(2+w)} & = \\\\ \\frac{12w}{4v(2+w)}-\\frac{5}{4v(2+w)} & = \\\\ \\frac{12w-5}{4v(2+w)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256930 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256931 13194139533864 true] [17592186256930 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256930 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256930 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256931 13194139533864 true] [17592186256930 1110 #uuid "63fb932b-9122-530a-b4d5-034337355a73" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256931 1034 "\\frac{12w-5}{4v(2+w)}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256931 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256931 1111 #uuid "76fa3985-f3c7-55bf-a7e0-5ef5d389ce4f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256932 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256933 13194139533864 true] [17592186256932 1089 "q-113308" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256932 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256932 1100 "Simplify $\\frac{t}{(t-3)(u+4)}+\\frac{5}{u+4}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256932 1113 #uuid "7c739dfc-d398-5a70-9f99-96abdc52d307" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256932 1145 "In order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $(t-3)(u+4)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $t-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{t}{(t-3)(u+4)}+\\frac{5}{u+4} & = \\\\ \\frac{t}{(t-3)(u+4)}+\\frac{t-3}{t-3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{u+4} & = \\\\ \\frac{t}{(t-3)(u+4)}+\\frac{5(t-3)}{(t-3)(u+4)} & = \\\\ \\frac{t+5(t-3)}{(t-3)(u+4)} & = \\\\ \\frac{t+5t-15}{(t-3)(u+4)} & = \\\\ \\frac{6t-15}{(t-3)(u+4)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256933 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256934 13194139533864 true] [17592186256933 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256933 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256933 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256934 13194139533864 true] [17592186256933 1110 #uuid "056d9d45-d2b5-5d01-adec-bbca6fd8c112" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256934 1034 "\\frac{6t-15}{(t-3)(u+4)}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256934 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256934 1111 #uuid "ec3e4295-1d50-5ed7-adfd-ecb9993c4b47" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256935 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256936 13194139533864 true] [17592186256935 1089 "q-113311" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256935 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256935 1100 "Simplify $\\frac{5m}{n(m-4)}+\\frac{n}{m-4}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256935 1113 #uuid "ae19e4b0-11a7-5a6d-8c85-2a0f9d7eb465" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256935 1145 "In order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $n(m-4)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5m}{n(m-4)}+\\frac{n}{m-4} & = \\\\ \\frac{5m}{n(m-4)}+\\frac{n}{n}\\cdot \\frac{n}{m-4} & = \\\\ \\frac{5m}{n(m-4)}+\\frac{n\\cdot n}{n(m-4)} & = \\\\ \\frac{5m}{n(m-4)}+\\frac{{n}^{2}}{n(m-4)} & = \\\\ \\frac{5m+{n}^{2}}{n(m-4)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256936 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256937 13194139533864 true] [17592186256936 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256936 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256936 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256937 13194139533864 true] [17592186256936 1110 #uuid "1daf8d4e-f8da-5961-af79-01959a2b049b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256937 1034 "\\frac{5m+{n}^{2}}{n(m-4)}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256937 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256937 1111 #uuid "a9c560f5-a103-57a7-a6db-bee28965fb4a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256938 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256939 13194139533864 true] [17592186256938 1089 "q-113479" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256938 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256938 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/65348161e386fac14a98e8440d32d3883b5865a6d1eecfcd58e12931730ad0b8.png)\n\nThe graph represents the function $y=\\frac{a}{x-b}+k.$ What is the value of $a+b+k?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256938 1113 #uuid "fa5f1c4b-9c02-56f6-92b6-f8a6f5b1eec5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256938 1145 "First, notice that the horizontal asymptote of the graph is $y=2,$ while the vertical asymptote is $x=1.$ This means that $k=2$ and $b=1,$ and we can re-write our function as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{a}{x-1}+2.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are given that the point $(2,5)$ lies on the graph. Substituting its coordinates into the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{a}{x-1}+2 \\\\ 5 & =\\frac{a}{2-1}+2 \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{a}{1} \\\\ a & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+b+k=3+1+2=6.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256939 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256940 13194139533864 true] [17592186256939 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256939 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256939 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256940 13194139533864 true] [17592186256939 1110 #uuid "bfc220ac-902f-57ac-b892-b71e8daa3106" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256940 1034 "6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256940 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256940 1111 #uuid "3a8760a4-fc31-5c9a-9404-6afacf46f9b6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256941 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256942 13194139533864 true] [17592186256941 1089 "q-113523" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256941 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256941 1100 "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+3x-3}.$\n\n$x =$: {{x}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256941 1113 #uuid "c37cf0e8-bbca-5ef1-ad17-b5e71311507a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256941 1145 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+3x-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+2)}{{x}^{2}(x-1)+3(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+2)}{({x}^{2}+3)(x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+3)(x-1)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x-1=0$ gives the solution $x=1.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+3=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256942 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256943 13194139533864 true] [17592186256942 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256942 1041 "x" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256942 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256943 13194139533864 true] [17592186256942 1110 #uuid "5d03e6ea-0b8d-5cd4-ad47-a3accdda2667" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256943 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256943 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256943 1111 #uuid "070582c7-9118-59ae-8fb8-cefbfc2e8342" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256944 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256945 13194139533864 true] [17592186256944 1089 "q-113547" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256944 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256944 1100 "Find all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+x+1}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf none exist, enter a phrase such as \"No vertical asymptotes\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256944 1113 #uuid "89758aa9-2520-5f08-bcf3-4dd38de13609" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256944 1145 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}(x+1)+(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+1}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x+1}}{\\cancel{(x+1)}({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ does not have any vertical asymptotes." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256945 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256946 13194139533864 true] [17592186256945 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256945 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256945 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256946 13194139533864 true] [17592186256945 1110 #uuid "6ce9b096-dc68-52ed-9491-356c80fb6f40" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256946 1034 "No vertical asymptotes" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256946 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256946 1111 #uuid "5b59731b-ccb9-53c5-82cf-caaaeebe66fd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256947 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256948 13194139533864 true] [17592186256947 1089 "q-113550" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256947 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256947 1100 "Determine the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x}{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+x-3}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf none exist, enter a phrase such as \"No vertical asymptotes\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256947 1113 #uuid "c1d02b50-c109-51fe-8f01-39a10ce1834c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256947 1145 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x}{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+x-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(x-3)}{{x}^{2}(x-3)+(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(x-3)}{(x-3)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cancel{(x-3)}}{\\cancel{(x-3)}({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256948 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256949 13194139533864 true] [17592186256948 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256948 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256948 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256949 13194139533864 true] [17592186256948 1110 #uuid "1ca49ee6-7b3e-50f6-b6b8-2580dda21a0b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256949 1034 "No vertical asymptotes" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256949 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256949 1111 #uuid "91ab451d-c25c-5f68-b886-6bf28b6f2808" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256950 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256951 13194139533864 true] [17592186256950 1089 "q-113665" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256950 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256950 1100 "Calculate $\\int {x}^{20}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256950 1113 #uuid "e90d408c-709e-5393-a17d-371e43b6c060" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256950 1145 "We apply the power rule with $n=20$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{20}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{20+1}}{20+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{21}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{21})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{21}\\cdot 21{x}^{20} & = \\\\ {x}^{20}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256951 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256952 13194139533864 true] [17592186256951 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256951 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256951 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256952 13194139533864 true] [17592186256951 1110 #uuid "09e4d1c2-b39b-5ff7-9a7b-349f1361e3a1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256952 1034 "\\frac{x^{21}}{21}+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256952 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256952 1111 #uuid "3eaec2f0-8f10-5311-b25a-8b563b04ea27" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256953 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256954 13194139533864 true] [17592186256953 1089 "q-113670" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256953 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256953 1100 "Calculate $F(x)$ given that $f(x)={x}^{17}$ and ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256953 1113 #uuid "c1be2173-4358-509d-aadb-1a56b1fb187c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256953 1145 "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply the power rule with $n=17$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{17}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{17+1}}{17+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{18}}{18}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{18}}{18}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{18}}{18})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{18}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{18})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{18}\\cdot 18\\,{x}^{17} & = \\\\ {x}^{17}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256954 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256955 13194139533864 true] [17592186256954 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256954 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256954 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256955 13194139533864 true] [17592186256954 1110 #uuid "ac9dfa85-845a-5ba5-b3e7-27c62c522776" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256955 1034 "\\frac{x^{18}}{18}+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256955 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256955 1111 #uuid "fcdfa4b8-3f42-52a0-925a-b6e9a0f0e9b8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256956 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256957 13194139533864 true] [17592186256956 1089 "q-113674" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256956 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256956 1100 "Find the antiderivative of ${x}^{1/4}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256956 1113 #uuid "5356a9fa-e335-59c8-a9df-2ca4e329df90" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256956 1145 "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/4+1}}{(\\frac{1}{4}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{(\\frac{5}{4})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/4})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}{x}^{5/4-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{1/4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256957 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256958 13194139533864 true] [17592186256957 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256957 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256957 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256958 13194139533864 true] [17592186256957 1110 #uuid "c5f96349-8a29-5afd-877d-204cd6f3c12e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256958 1034 "\\frac{4x^{5/4}}5+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256958 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256958 1111 #uuid "c2aaa22b-0b94-5d33-bb53-a4566194273b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256959 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256960 13194139533864 true] [17592186256959 1089 "q-113680" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256959 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256959 1100 "Calculate the integral of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256959 1113 #uuid "5140beb3-e3a4-5acd-b183-1ab9a73f7f42" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256959 1145 "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}={x}^{3/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{3}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{3/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/5+1}}{(\\frac{3}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{8/5}}{(\\frac{8}{5})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{8/5}}{8}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{8/5}}{8}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{8/5}}{8})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{8}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{8/5})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{8}\\cdot \\frac{8}{5}{x}^{8/5-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{3/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256960 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256961 13194139533864 true] [17592186256960 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256960 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256960 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256961 13194139533864 true] [17592186256960 1110 #uuid "801bd2c2-973d-5705-ab11-335245e532ae" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256961 1034 "\\frac{5x^{8/5}}8+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256961 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256961 1111 #uuid "4fb18591-c696-5c60-869e-611f0289638e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256962 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256963 13194139533864 true] [17592186256962 1089 "q-113695" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256962 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256962 1100 "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256962 1113 #uuid "7638fecf-7803-5557-84d8-ceacc22a4c8d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256962 1145 "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}={x}^{2/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{2}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2/5+1}}{(\\frac{2}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/5}}{(\\frac{7}{5})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{7/5}}{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{7/5}}{7}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{7/5}}{7})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7/5})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{7}\\cdot \\frac{7}{5}{x}^{7/5-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{2/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256963 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256964 13194139533864 true] [17592186256963 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256963 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256963 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256964 13194139533864 true] [17592186256963 1110 #uuid "69df822c-4648-58a5-8477-aefaa6ba44a3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256964 1034 "\\frac{5x^{7/5}}7+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256964 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256964 1111 #uuid "7f338e2e-b40a-5439-b0c1-97997693af49" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256965 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256966 13194139533864 true] [17592186256965 1089 "q-113705" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256965 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256965 1100 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4/3}}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256965 1113 #uuid "955521d3-c252-5d10-a38e-265439ed4f5c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256965 1145 "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4/3}}={x}^{-4/3}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{4}{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-4/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-4/3+1}}{(-\\frac{4}{3}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/3}}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}+C \\\\  & =-3{x}^{-1/3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{{x}^{1/3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256966 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256967 13194139533864 true] [17592186256966 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256966 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256966 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256967 13194139533864 true] [17592186256966 1110 #uuid "d3c9f094-adb0-53b0-9aa6-7016a1ce117b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256967 1034 "-\\frac{3}{{x}^{1/3}}+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256967 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256967 1111 #uuid "6464c877-fb70-5f4d-a76d-c49ff97dec49" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256968 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256969 13194139533864 true] [17592186256968 1089 "q-113733" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256968 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256968 1100 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{3{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256968 1113 #uuid "5000ff4a-e3b3-58d1-a0bd-1d7994a6fbf1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256968 1145 "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{3{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {x}^{-6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (\\frac{{x}^{-6+1}}{-6+1})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (\\frac{{x}^{-5}}{-5})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{15}\\cdot {x}^{-5}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{15{x}^{5}}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256969 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256970 13194139533864 true] [17592186256969 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256969 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256969 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256970 13194139533864 true] [17592186256969 1110 #uuid "690b8984-894a-57f2-9ea0-88435e2b71ef" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256970 1034 "-\\frac{1}{15{x}^{5}}+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256970 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256970 1111 #uuid "0a2c3266-3d0f-507e-b850-a468ca8259d1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256971 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256972 13194139533864 true] [17592186256971 1089 "q-113734" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256971 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256971 1100 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{3}{2{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256971 1113 #uuid "3a81fa0c-31fb-5051-b2d0-003cfd048725" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256971 1145 "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{2{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int {y}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{{y}^{(-2+1)}}{-2+1})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{{y}^{-1}}{-1})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {y}^{-1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2y}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256972 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256973 13194139533864 true] [17592186256972 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256972 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256972 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256973 13194139533864 true] [17592186256972 1110 #uuid "cc93091e-76d6-5cc3-bd0c-1fa89719ada4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256973 1034 "-\\frac{3}{2y}+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256973 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256973 1111 #uuid "22921c45-70c8-597c-9f21-de18a688de47" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256974 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256975 13194139533864 true] [17592186256974 1089 "q-113737" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256974 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256974 1100 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{5}{{x}^{11}}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256974 1113 #uuid "e26de700-a75c-58a7-9db4-45270d8fe36b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256974 1145 "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{{x}^{11}}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int {x}^{-11}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\frac{{x}^{-11+1}}{-11+1})+C \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\frac{{x}^{-10}}{-10})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{10}\\cdot {x}^{-10}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{10}}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256975 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256976 13194139533864 true] [17592186256975 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256975 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256975 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256976 13194139533864 true] [17592186256975 1110 #uuid "190a0b49-3535-5497-9cf4-9b9e7e6b42c5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256976 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{10}}+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256976 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256976 1111 #uuid "bc4ecf5b-73dd-5f51-a27b-6fe96c61b5ad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256977 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256978 13194139533864 true] [17592186256977 1089 "q-113739" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256977 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256977 1100 "Calculate $\\int \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256977 1113 #uuid "ccbc8acc-7efb-5aec-8fb6-53294e0c01ed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256977 1145 "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\sqrt{5}\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\int {x}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot x+C \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $\\int k\\,\\text{d}x=kx+C.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256978 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256979 13194139533864 true] [17592186256978 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256978 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256978 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256979 13194139533864 true] [17592186256978 1110 #uuid "62e2f70d-6975-5b33-8ace-a4e0d4c31768" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256979 1034 "\\sqrt{5}x+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256979 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256979 1111 #uuid "b2a892e5-2d4c-5509-a17f-11fb7a7ff6c4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256980 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256981 13194139533864 true] [17592186256980 1089 "q-113741" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256980 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256980 1100 "Calculate $\\int {e}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256980 1113 #uuid "701bad97-e15a-56c7-8c5c-1a7984152952" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256980 1145 "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={e}^{2}\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\int {x}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot x+C \\\\  & ={e}^{2}x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $\\int k\\,\\text{d}x=kx+C.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256981 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256982 13194139533864 true] [17592186256981 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256981 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256981 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256982 13194139533864 true] [17592186256981 1110 #uuid "f1e90087-cd48-558c-afc6-ecc2f2f9692a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256982 1034 "{e}^{2}x+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256982 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256982 1111 #uuid "10239815-a8cf-5315-b008-717cf7348261" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256983 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256984 13194139533864 true] [17592186256983 1089 "q-113742" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256983 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256983 1100 "Calculate $\\int (-5)\\,\\text{d}y.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256983 1113 #uuid "ae7da13b-8fe6-5af8-ba08-e04d213a5d46" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256983 1145 "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-5)\\,\\text{d}y & =-5\\int 1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-5\\int {y}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-5\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =-5\\cdot y+C \\\\  & =-5y+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $\\int k\\,\\text{d}x=kx+C.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256984 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256985 13194139533864 true] [17592186256984 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256984 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256984 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256985 13194139533864 true] [17592186256984 1110 #uuid "db136630-c656-5fea-a964-52c000eee69b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256985 1034 "-5y+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256985 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256985 1111 #uuid "d5e0a3c5-10ee-5686-be92-765de4fb495d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256986 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256987 13194139533864 true] [17592186256986 1089 "q-113781" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256986 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256986 1100 "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[4]{x}-{x}^{5}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256986 1113 #uuid "73897e00-bd3f-5a2c-b0a5-3ce83bc59e81" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256986 1145 "Applying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[4]{x}-{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{1/4}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int {x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{1/4+1}}{(\\frac{1}{4}+1)}-\\frac{{x}^{5+1}}{(5+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{(\\frac{5}{4})}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{(6)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt[4]{{x}^{5}}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256987 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256988 13194139533864 true] [17592186256987 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256987 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256987 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256988 13194139533864 true] [17592186256987 1110 #uuid "19d5778e-c306-5