db/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257113 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/19d372d897d3bff77348bf0e08fdae344243901f533da6dab6017b7833534045.png)\n\nWhat is the value of $x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257113 1113 #uuid "764f27f2-ac09-5d2f-9141-43d1eab813c4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257113 1145 "Note that $∠AOB$ and $∠COD$ are right angles. Hence,\n\n$$\nm∠AOB={90}^{∘},\\,m∠COD={90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSince the measure of a full angle is ${360}^{∘},$ we can form an equation and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOB+m∠BOC+m∠COD+m∠DOA & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {90}^{∘}+2x+{90}^{∘}+(8x+{30}^{∘}) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {210}^{∘}+10x & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 10x & ={150}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={15}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257114 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257115 13194139533864 true] [17592186257114 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257114 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257114 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257115 13194139533864 true] [17592186257114 1110 #uuid "ff6a97bc-3c12-5f1e-920d-ec4b24a3bd84" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257115 1034 "{15}^{∘}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257115 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257115 1111 #uuid "c5333780-be76-575d-8a64-79b318f11854" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257116 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257117 13194139533864 true] [17592186257116 1089 "q-114961" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257116 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257116 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d624edaffd8c90de40041d419cf65bcf8f3af5d31c8d88d1123bd8f3d7555166.png)\n\nWhat is the length of the major axis of the ellipse given above?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257116 1113 #uuid "1bd10278-9b76-5fcf-980b-136026be1670" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257116 1145 "The major axis is the longest diameter of the ellipse, which is the line segment bounded by the points $(-3,1)$ and $(5,1).$ So, the length of the major axis is $5-(-3)=8.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257117 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257118 13194139533864 true] [17592186257117 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257117 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257117 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257118 13194139533864 true] [17592186257117 1110 #uuid "d452168e-bfbc-5305-927c-94998c46c6d4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257118 1034 "8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257118 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257118 1111 #uuid "1f74fd72-dd28-524a-9c7f-1084f8649375" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257119 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257120 13194139533864 true] [17592186257119 1089 "q-115211" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257119 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257119 1100 "Solve  ${x}^{2}\\ge 27-2{x}^{2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257119 1113 #uuid "3ae8abfe-59ac-511c-ad3e-1628dc957268" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257119 1145 "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & \\ge 27-2{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{x}^{2} & \\ge 27 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\ge 27 \\\\ {x}^{2} & \\ge 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n*Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Equation*\n\nTaking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & \\ge 9 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & \\ge \\sqrt{9} \\\\ |x| & \\ge 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have \n\n$$\nx\\le -3\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [3,\\infty ).$\n\n*Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve*\n\nStarting from ${x}^{2}\\ge 9$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-9\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $y={x}^{2}-9$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the roots are $x=\\pm 3.$ \n\nSince the leading coefficient of ${x}^{2}-9$ is positive, the parabola opens upward. Therefore, the solution to the given inequality ($y\\ge 0$) lies outside the roots (and include them).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/f277a8b22194fae2d932f6f39ac28e869955486820c195f5e027a9bc49cd493c.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [3,\\infty ).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257120 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257121 13194139533864 true] [17592186257120 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257120 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257120 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257121 13194139533864 true] [17592186257120 1110 #uuid "a9f79357-735d-5cc3-af29-fca7d8c42c99" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257121 1034 "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [3,\\infty )" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257121 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257121 1111 #uuid "097c6127-d90f-5d83-9444-202402f82e1a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257122 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257123 13194139533864 true] [17592186257122 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257125 13194139533864 true] [17592186257122 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257127 13194139533864 true] [17592186257122 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257129 13194139533864 true] [17592186257122 1089 "q-11532" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257122 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257122 1100 "Calculate $2H-3L$, where\n$H=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$L=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRow 1, column 1: {{r1c1}}\n\nRow 1, column 2: {{r1c2}}\n\nRow 2, column 1: {{r2c1}}\n\nRow 2, column 2: {{r2c2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257122 1113 #uuid "68388deb-c99d-58dc-919b-11e840c4302b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257122 1145 "We multiply each entry of $H$ by $2$ and each entry of $L$ by $3,$ and then subtract the resulting matrices, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2H-3L & =2[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 3 & 2\\cdot (-2) \\\\ 2\\cdot 2 & 2\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 0 & 3\\cdot 1 \\\\ 3\\cdot 2 & 3\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6-0 & -4-3 \\\\ 4-6 & -2-3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -7 \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257123 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257124 13194139533864 true] [17592186257123 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257123 1041 "r1c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257123 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257124 13194139533864 true] [17592186257123 1110 #uuid "50d71f43-0198-534a-a76e-78a5c7ff66fc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257124 1034 "6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257124 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257124 1111 #uuid "f00ea6a0-13f9-519c-9283-1433fc6754ad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257125 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257126 13194139533864 true] [17592186257125 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257125 1041 "r1c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257125 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257126 13194139533864 true] [17592186257125 1110 #uuid "de2d9f41-421d-5340-a0d1-369099542203" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257126 1034 "-7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257126 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257126 1111 #uuid "12c3c137-8602-5f1d-96d8-8266ec5a6048" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257127 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257128 13194139533864 true] [17592186257127 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257127 1041 "r2c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257127 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257128 13194139533864 true] [17592186257127 1110 #uuid "8e14cc66-77c0-5c25-ba6e-f662695dd674" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257128 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257128 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257128 1111 #uuid "3e54d8f1-4f94-5d30-b05f-3a8b2466941f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257129 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257130 13194139533864 true] [17592186257129 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257129 1041 "r2c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257129 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257130 13194139533864 true] [17592186257129 1110 #uuid "440e4888-7883-5d03-99f9-c1a1ee936bb1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257130 1034 "-5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257130 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257130 1111 #uuid "a707a8e1-6dba-5c6c-96e5-30b0f82458c0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257131 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257132 13194139533864 true] [17592186257131 1089 "q-115324" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257131 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257131 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/8c266bd4087fbfad0f81de882dc72cf03e81d420897f1f1b1a3b430827a77f2a.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $(x+2)(x-2)\\ge 0.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257131 1113 #uuid "7a8e5c30-d271-53bb-8418-8317e11f8e2d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257131 1145 "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n(x+2)(x-2)\\ge 0\n$$\n\nis a \"*greater than or equal to*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=(x+2)(x-2)\n$$\n\nis *above* the $x$-axis or *touches* this axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/1b6d9689cf4ac9d36507b4fc3ba0ea77ac34ee8a4cf5cd28a5592c834f48ecec.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x\\le -2$ or $x\\ge 2.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257132 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257133 13194139533864 true] [17592186257132 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257132 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257132 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257133 13194139533864 true] [17592186257132 1110 #uuid "b53af6e4-df94-59e3-bb64-b0a27745178d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257133 1034 "x\\ge 2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257133 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257133 1111 #uuid "6fa84f31-40f0-5b3c-8b32-5a84fa150210" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257134 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257135 13194139533864 true] [17592186257134 1089 "q-115511" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257134 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257134 1100 "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(2,4)$ and which passes through the point $(4,1).$\n\n$y =$: {{y}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257134 1113 #uuid "6c976869-dd96-5219-84a7-962a7edc295e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257134 1145 "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(2,4).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(4,1),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=1$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ (4-2)^{2} & =4p(1-4) \\\\ 4 & =-12p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{3})(y-4) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-\\frac{4}{3}(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257135 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257136 13194139533864 true] [17592186257135 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257135 1041 "y" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257135 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257136 13194139533864 true] [17592186257135 1110 #uuid "77a1bb95-efd6-5816-8d00-b1ae1239d5ad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257136 1034 "-\\frac34(x-2)^2+4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257136 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257136 1111 #uuid "d26e455a-be78-54a6-8ee0-868b455d0058" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257137 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257138 13194139533864 true] [17592186257137 1089 "q-115521" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257137 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257137 1100 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{ln}^{2}x}{x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257137 1113 #uuid "724e9328-9953-5c76-8644-8a4bbc630b36" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257137 1145 "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{ln}^{2}x=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{ln}^{2}x}{x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[{ln}^{2}x]^{'}}{{[x]}^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}x}{x})}{1} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}x}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}x)^{2}}{x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}x}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[2\\operatorname{ln}x]^{'}}{[x]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{x})}{1} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257138 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257139 13194139533864 true] [17592186257138 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257138 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257138 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257139 13194139533864 true] [17592186257138 1110 #uuid "23895971-4cf6-5c53-acb6-56e9293293d5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257139 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257139 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257139 1111 #uuid "3f6ea9c2-57f9-5209-a617-348346886abc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257140 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257141 13194139533864 true] [17592186257140 1089 "q-115525" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257140 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257140 1100 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{cos}x)}{\\operatorname{tan}x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257140 1113 #uuid "bb5aa37c-ec5b-54e4-abf6-b459e31e9391" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257140 1145 "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{cos}x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{tan}x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{cos}x)}{\\operatorname{tan}x} & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{cos}x)]^{'}}{{[\\operatorname{tan}x]}^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x})}{{sec}^{2}x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x})}{(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}x})} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x{cos}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257141 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257142 13194139533864 true] [17592186257141 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257141 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257141 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257142 13194139533864 true] [17592186257141 1110 #uuid "f6b5eeff-0c16-5b3c-866a-0de7f8c2e22a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257142 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257142 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257142 1111 #uuid "9f581248-f993-5ff1-a21c-eb63648ee127" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257143 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257144 13194139533864 true] [17592186257143 1089 "q-115529" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257143 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257143 1100 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}2x}{\\operatorname{cot}x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257143 1113 #uuid "96a5dc35-6a1c-5615-a025-affa6405f5ff" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257143 1145 "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}2x=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}2x}{\\operatorname{cot}x} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{cot}2x]^{'}}{[\\operatorname{cot}x]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(-2{csc}^{2}2x)}{-{csc}^{2}x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}2x})}{(\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{2{sin}^{2}x}{{sin}^{2}2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{2{sin}^{2}x}{{(2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x)}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{2{sin}^{2}x}{4{sin}^{2}x{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{2\\cancel{{sin}^{2}x}}{4\\cancel{{sin}^{2}x}{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{2{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{cos}^{2}0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257144 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257145 13194139533864 true] [17592186257144 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257144 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257144 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257145 13194139533864 true] [17592186257144 1110 #uuid "888c9f31-93fc-54f1-8edb-6253391ea8fd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257145 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257145 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257145 1111 #uuid "bcbb8b1b-e9f7-5e53-aba6-dd8c34f37f84" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257146 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257147 13194139533864 true] [17592186257146 1089 "q-115818" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257146 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257146 1100 "Find the domain of $m(x)=-\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt[8]{2x+6}+2.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257146 1113 #uuid "04cac8d8-af4c-5d63-ad0a-aa9d84b0fb91" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257146 1145 "The domain of $y=\\sqrt[8]{x}$ is \n\n$$\nx\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $m(x)=-\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt[8]{2x+6}+2,$ we replace $x$ with $2x+6$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n2x+6\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+6 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2x & \\ge -6 \\\\ x & \\ge -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=m(x)$ is $x\\in [-3,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=m(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ed39939f068dfa0d93b3177deee4c4a822aaaae821911b83153e29eb7a0ccac6.png)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257147 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257148 13194139533864 true] [17592186257147 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257147 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257147 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257148 13194139533864 true] [17592186257147 1110 #uuid "b3b0bafd-d05d-5701-a299-8160e6bdf2c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257148 1034 "[-3,\\infty)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257148 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257148 1111 #uuid "37e9f0d5-d775-5b27-b8dd-388be0c6d41e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257149 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257150 13194139533864 true] [17592186257149 1089 "q-11590" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257149 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257149 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d773ba5052937e20997d5039fe4de6958862c4553cbe0edd8fdfe12eeb5de8fa.png)\n\nGiven the above sample space, find $P({X}^{'}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257149 1113 #uuid "18160ae9-a2e7-570f-b9a7-71d46ee99dc2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257149 1145 "The Venn diagram tells us that $P(X)=0.925.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({X}^{'}) & =1-P(X) \\\\  & =1-0.925 \\\\  & =0.075.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257150 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257151 13194139533864 true] [17592186257150 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257150 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257150 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257151 13194139533864 true] [17592186257150 1110 #uuid "c1cb15b4-dbff-5269-a982-d08e38e581a4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257151 1034 "0.075" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257151 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257151 1111 #uuid "94828c53-dd84-5288-9dd4-b29c5a5e99d5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257152 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257153 13194139533864 true] [17592186257152 1089 "q-11602" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257152 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257152 1100 "If $y=2{x}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7},$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $x=2.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257152 1113 #uuid "66349321-873f-5a15-988a-584637cc50c3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257152 1145 "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=2{x}^{3}$ and $v(x)=\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7}.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=6{x}^{2},\\,{v}^{'}(x)=\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7}}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =(6{x}^{2})(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7})+(2{x}^{3})(\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7}}) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7}+\\frac{4{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})|}_{x=2} & =6(2)^{2}\\sqrt{2(2)^{2}+7}+\\frac{4(2)^{4}}{\\sqrt{2(2)^{2}+7}} \\\\  & =24\\sqrt{15}+\\frac{64}{\\sqrt{15}} \\\\  & =\\frac{360+64}{\\sqrt{15}} \\\\  & =\\frac{424}{\\sqrt{15}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257153 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257154 13194139533864 true] [17592186257153 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257153 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257153 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257154 13194139533864 true] [17592186257153 1110 #uuid "3954b683-3c10-54a5-a17c-d7be4469cc36" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257154 1034 "\\frac{424}{\\sqrt{15}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257154 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257154 1111 #uuid "50fbfbd6-0fca-5aa3-bfda-d1b0e47fdc04" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257155 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257156 13194139533864 true] [17592186257155 1089 "q-11603" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257155 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257155 1100 "If $y=3{x}^{2}{({x}^{3}-1)}^{4}$, find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $x=1.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257155 1113 #uuid "cee486c2-ed27-5e12-b275-9a37215d5458" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257155 1145 "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=3{x}^{2}$ and $v(x)={({x}^{3}-1)}^{4}.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=6x,\\,{v}^{'}(x)=12{x}^{2}{({x}^{3}-1)}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =(6x)(({x}^{3}-1)^{4})+(3{x}^{2})(12{x}^{2}({x}^{3}-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =6x({x}^{3}-1)^{4}+36{x}^{4}{({x}^{3}-1)}^{3} \\\\  & =(6x({x}^{3}-1)+36{x}^{4})\\,{({x}^{3}-1)}^{3} \\\\  & =(6{x}^{4}-6x+36{x}^{4})\\,{({x}^{3}-1)}^{3} \\\\  & =(42{x}^{4}-6x)\\,{({x}^{3}-1)}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $x=1$, we substitute $x=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})|}_{x=1} & =(42\\cdot {1}^{4}-6\\cdot 1){({1}^{3}-1)}^{3} \\\\  & =36\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257156 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257157 13194139533864 true] [17592186257156 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257156 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257156 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257157 13194139533864 true] [17592186257156 1110 #uuid "856d08f2-0c65-5782-b962-59c19dfd35d8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257157 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257157 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257157 1111 #uuid "538fbf14-cdc0-5657-af83-f8c31618f4c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257158 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257159 13194139533864 true] [17592186257158 1089 "q-116199" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257158 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257158 1100 "Find the solution of $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x\\le x+\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257158 1113 #uuid "e171ae29-e8a4-5154-8cf5-f799b433e6ca" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257158 1145 "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x & \\le x+\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{5}{2} & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}-4x-5) & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(x+1)(x-5) & \\le 0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-5) & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+1)(x-5)=0.$ The roots are $x=-1$ and $x=5,$ and they split the number line into $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-1),$ $(-1,5)$ and $(5,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+1)(x-5)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+1)(x-5)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+1)(x-5)$ is zero or negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-1,5).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-1$ and $x=5.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x\\le x+\\frac{5}{2}$ for all $x\\in [-1,5].$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257159 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257160 13194139533864 true] [17592186257159 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257159 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257159 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257160 13194139533864 true] [17592186257159 1110 #uuid "b5524af5-d05e-5356-866e-1d3ddab40d3c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257160 1034 "x\\in [-1,5]" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257160 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257160 1111 #uuid "f0312e17-e90a-5cec-a973-4fe0cd198064" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257161 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257162 13194139533864 true] [17592186257161 1089 "q-116201" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257161 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257161 1100 "Find the solution of $\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{10}x\\le 2.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257161 1113 #uuid "31b9e724-be24-5c0d-8724-b3dd3a9b5157" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257161 1145 "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{10}x & \\le 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{10}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{10}x-2 & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{10}({x}^{2}+x-20) & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{10}(x+5)(x-4) & \\le 0 \\\\ (x+5)(x-4) & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+5)(x-4)=0.$ The roots are $x=-5$ and $x=4,$ and they split the number line into $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-5),$ $(-5,4)$ and $(4,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+5)(x-4)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+5)(x-4)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+5)(x-4)$ is zero or negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-5,4).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-5$ and $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{10}x\\le 2$ for all $x\\in [-5,4].$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257162 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257163 13194139533864 true] [17592186257162 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257162 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257162 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257163 13194139533864 true] [17592186257162 1110 #uuid "7eed7fe2-f8ea-5f28-83ae-f1607966dce1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257163 1034 "x\\in [-5,4]" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257163 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257163 1111 #uuid "001edcb1-c7f0-5737-ba45-b733679d2763" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257164 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257165 13194139533864 true] [17592186257164 1089 "q-116627" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257164 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257164 1100 "The area under the curve $y=(x+1)^{3}$ between $x=0$ and $x=4$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257164 1113 #uuid "99dc60bd-f005-5d9a-815d-b21949c9faa6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257164 1145 "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nWe're told that there are $n$ subintervals. The endpoints indicate that $a=0$ and $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-0}{n}=\\frac{4}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[f(0+j\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[f(\\frac{4j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\frac{4}{n}{(\\frac{4j}{n}+1)}^{3}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257165 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257166 13194139533864 true] [17592186257165 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257165 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257165 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257166 13194139533864 true] [17592186257165 1110 #uuid "5ae21e4d-4efb-5b65-9186-e6c8a1d3c95c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257166 1034 "{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\frac{4}{n}{(\\frac{4j}{n}+1)}^{3}]" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257166 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257166 1111 #uuid "d038d527-f95c-5714-972a-e967c6f9f5f2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257167 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257168 13194139533864 true] [17592186257167 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257171 13194139533864 true] [17592186257167 1089 "q-11670" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257167 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257167 1100 "A nutritionist has $25$ patients. Of his patients, $10$ of them are smokers, and $15$ of them have diabetes. We randomly select a patient among the $25$ patients. \n\nDefine $A$ to be the event \"the patient is a smoker.\" What is ${A}^{'}$ described in natural language, and what is $P({A}^{'})?$\n\nComplement event: {{complement-event}}\n\nComplement probability: {{complement-probability}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257167 1113 #uuid "d2dcf9c8-21c2-5756-902c-6ae34835148c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257167 1145 "The **complement** of an event $A,$ denoted ${A}^{'},$ is the *opposite* of $A.$ That is to say, ${A}^{'}$  is the set of outcomes in which $A$ *does not* happen.\n\nHere, ${A}^{'}$ is the opposite of the event \"the patient is a smoker.\" So ${A}^{'}$ is the event \"the patient doesn't smoke.\" \n\nNow, let's calculate the probability $P({A}^{'}).$  There are $25$ patients, and $10$ of them are smokers, so there are $25-10=15$ non-smokers. Therefore, \n\n$$\nP({A}^{'})=\\frac{15}{25}=0.6.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257168 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257169 13194139533864 true] [17592186257168 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257170 13194139533864 true] [17592186257168 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139533864 true] [17592186257168 1041 "complement-event" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257168 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257170 13194139533864 true] [17592186257168 1110 #uuid "402bb104-7405-5d66-aabd-ca4094fb9864" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257169 1034 "The patient is a smoker" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257169 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257169 1111 #uuid "71109537-5d34-5063-b2cc-92d751d6c0ea" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257170 1034 "The patient is not a smoker" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257170 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257170 1111 #uuid "1694fcf5-640e-59e5-9571-f2a08d9ddcad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257171 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257172 13194139533864 true] [17592186257171 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257171 1041 "complement-probability" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257171 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257172 13194139533864 true] [17592186257171 1110 #uuid "b1f30e9b-cffc-5a2e-98ab-4d61c92e0d17" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257172 1034 "0.6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257172 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257172 1111 #uuid "5a35fb8f-d89c-565c-9fb6-7a7ddaee8c0e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257173 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257174 13194139533864 true] [17592186257173 1089 "q-116735" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257173 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257173 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/63fe9cf3a61a5cef09f9c19e16a18318338faa6837eeb77b83f49b636157bb95.png)\n\nA sphere is decreasing in volume at a rate of $54\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ Calculate the rate at which the radius of the sphere is decreasing at the moment when the radius is $3\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257173 1113 #uuid "b814e78f-cd5b-5605-af13-b03739b1afc4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257173 1145 "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon and $V$ its volume. Both $r$ and $V$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-54\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=3\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the volume formula for the sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-54\\pi $ and $r=3,$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-54\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi (3)^{2} \\\\ -54\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 36\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the sphere is decreasing at a rate of $1.5\\text{ cm/s}$ when the radius is $3\\,\\text{cm}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257174 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257175 13194139533864 true] [17592186257174 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257174 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257174 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257175 13194139533864 true] [17592186257174 1110 #uuid "c4cb20c6-81af-595e-9c19-2f33f54f599a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257175 1034 "1.5\\,\\mathrm{cm/s}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257175 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257175 1111 #uuid "081e7f40-9868-5dc2-a380-4bc4830e2152" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257176 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257177 13194139533864 true] [17592186257176 1089 "q-116814" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257176 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257176 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/2e19b7a47baa207e10614aea3887b943bce87e759e10a204f516ac78b1539569.png)\n\nFind the measure of angle $B.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257176 1113 #uuid "5d7893f2-23b8-5b1b-b710-b842231b1ba3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257176 1145 "Since the sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is ${180}^{∘}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{40}^{∘}+3x+(7x-{10}^{∘}) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {30}^{∘}+10x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 10x & ={150}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={15}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nm∠B=3x=3({15}^{∘})={45}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257177 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257178 13194139533864 true] [17592186257177 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257177 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257177 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257178 13194139533864 true] [17592186257177 1110 #uuid "24e9cc90-4d03-5d55-82e0-dda75d65eb22" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257178 1034 "{45}^{∘}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257178 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257178 1111 #uuid "45ae285e-dde4-5553-9057-3c9ba16bf14f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257179 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257180 13194139533864 true] [17592186257179 1089 "q-116964" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257179 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257179 1100 "What values is in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257179 1113 #uuid "f94e32f6-7fb6-5970-bcb4-34f10163497e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257179 1145 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is in the domain of the function." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257180 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257181 13194139533864 true] [17592186257180 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257180 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257180 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257181 13194139533864 true] [17592186257180 1110 #uuid "11d4b53d-4b09-5f75-ac3f-a0cae0b7e836" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257181 1034 "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257181 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257181 1111 #uuid "e5d1cf60-e8de-5a9d-a628-c991718d7d82" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257182 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257183 13194139533864 true] [17592186257182 1089 "q-116968" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257182 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257182 1100 "What values is **not** in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257182 1113 #uuid "8fd2f5d3-ed41-5722-b4e0-f7436fc89d15" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257182 1145 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the value $y=\\frac{5}{2}$ is not in the range of the function." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257183 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257184 13194139533864 true] [17592186257183 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257183 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257183 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257184 13194139533864 true] [17592186257183 1110 #uuid "7519cd05-eb6f-5c7c-be4e-fa766b0b5e78" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257184 1034 "\\frac{5}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257184 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257184 1111 #uuid "bf497dd6-679d-5131-ac91-2d47cf6c54f2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257185 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257186 13194139533864 true] [17592186257185 1089 "q-117039" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257185 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257185 1100 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x?$\n\nI. - $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\nII. - $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$\nIII. - $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257185 1113 #uuid "4c2df21e-7d98-55cc-bb9d-c4c6dd3c0b6c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257185 1145 "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$  lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{5\\pi }{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257186 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257187 13194139533864 true] [17592186257186 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257188 13194139533864 true] [17592186257186 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257189 13194139533864 true] [17592186257186 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257190 13194139533864 true] [17592186257186 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257191 13194139533864 true] [17592186257186 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257192 13194139533864 true] [17592186257186 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257193 13194139533864 true] [17592186257186 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257194 13194139533864 true] [17592186257186 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257186 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257186 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257190 13194139533864 true] [17592186257186 1110 #uuid "6892c31f-549c-572f-9ddb-e3cfa0dc1374" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257187 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257187 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257187 1111 #uuid "0ceaa316-e3e1-55b2-ae3c-d9260d2939da" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257188 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257188 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257188 1111 #uuid "ad2f7299-d4e7-5c89-8447-6e85fda04ee2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257189 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257189 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257189 1111 #uuid "78468aff-5ac8-572d-a1b3-a73174ef4604" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257190 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257190 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257190 1111 #uuid "16f4a9c6-555a-5cc0-a62a-a0ef7a517e5b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257191 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257191 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257191 1111 #uuid "9e7e9d7e-7fd9-5a94-b6f2-b010d98