1 #uuid "695fe791-b8c2-529f-bd48-86e408e634fd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257227 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257227 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257227 1111 #uuid "d2a96266-1e97-5508-9b0e-56ea0b1de739" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257228 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257228 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257228 1111 #uuid "252ec23b-ce4f-5c47-921c-ef7e64c323a9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257229 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257229 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257229 1111 #uuid "fd553f76-6c99-5537-b8d7-b6e36bb5e883" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257230 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257230 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257230 1111 #uuid "12d7c230-fbe4-5468-aa94-253457d2cee2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257231 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257232 13194139533864 true] [17592186257231 1089 "q-117339" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257231 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257231 1100 "What values is in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257231 1113 #uuid "af21f9ad-d3fc-504f-8809-fa4c9ea3ace4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257231 1145 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $y=\\frac{4}{9}$ is in the range of the function." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257232 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257233 13194139533864 true] [17592186257232 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257232 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257232 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257233 13194139533864 true] [17592186257232 1110 #uuid "1bdbc2fc-3af5-5eaa-a32a-7a2244e463f1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257233 1034 "\\frac{4}{9}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257233 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257233 1111 #uuid "9a40c3e6-33ed-5435-94e0-b07c6d7b3520" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257234 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257235 13194139533864 true] [17592186257234 1089 "q-117373" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257234 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257234 1100 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x?$\n\nI. - $(1,\\frac{5\\pi }{4})$\nII. - $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\nIII. - $(0,\\pi )$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257234 1113 #uuid "e42e861e-a315-5ce3-9bfd-ad15ea97bc42" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257234 1145 "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(1,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{5\\pi }{4},1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,\\frac{5\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{4},-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(0,\\pi ).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\pi ,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\pi $ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,\\pi )$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257235 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257236 13194139533864 true] [17592186257235 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257237 13194139533864 true] [17592186257235 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257238 13194139533864 true] [17592186257235 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257239 13194139533864 true] [17592186257235 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257240 13194139533864 true] [17592186257235 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257241 13194139533864 true] [17592186257235 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257242 13194139533864 true] [17592186257235 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257243 13194139533864 true] [17592186257235 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257235 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257235 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257243 13194139533864 true] [17592186257235 1110 #uuid "a87b1ada-f070-5a16-8efc-b76311bcca9a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257236 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257236 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257236 1111 #uuid "d44d8b18-33a5-5a24-b9bd-673cc51f97b8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257237 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257237 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257237 1111 #uuid "58c68d78-b88a-515f-83c6-1c3d4bbca69c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257238 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257238 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257238 1111 #uuid "f5724fe7-92ec-56ed-a3bc-f56af12e586e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257239 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257239 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257239 1111 #uuid "99b79ba0-fff4-5683-abf9-c0c4c9a1e11b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257240 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257240 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257240 1111 #uuid "4f137b7e-1902-5bdc-a1d0-dc1767719059" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257241 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257241 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257241 1111 #uuid "2071b92f-10b1-5ac3-a15c-76aba9bb7fdf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257242 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257242 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257242 1111 #uuid "995ae627-9d8b-576a-bcee-28576e7ed6af" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257243 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257243 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257243 1111 #uuid "63618dd2-0e11-5abc-82d7-1a61bb98c70c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257244 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257245 13194139533864 true] [17592186257244 1089 "q-117401" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257244 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257244 1100 "$\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{1}{2})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257244 1113 #uuid "5bc8c542-2e1c-5460-9d76-b1be87bfdf81" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257244 1145 "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{1}{2}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive.\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the values in the first quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257245 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257246 13194139533864 true] [17592186257245 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257245 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257245 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257246 13194139533864 true] [17592186257245 1110 #uuid "8169f86e-5c63-5c12-9f7b-a6351a6e7c01" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257246 1034 "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257246 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257246 1111 #uuid "00545941-9f49-558d-a5dd-3a3be620efba" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257247 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257248 13194139533864 true] [17592186257247 1089 "q-117568" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257247 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257247 1100 "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}(2x-1)?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257247 1113 #uuid "78732451-2db0-5c4e-aa11-0192b0cac8c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257247 1145 "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$\n\nSince $A=\\frac{3}{2},$ we have that the amplitude is $|A|=|\\frac{3}{2}|=\\frac{3}{2}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257248 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257249 13194139533864 true] [17592186257248 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257248 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257248 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257249 13194139533864 true] [17592186257248 1110 #uuid "f8ab1859-ecad-56e1-a2c1-455c5286b3f3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257249 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257249 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257249 1111 #uuid "0e2e8619-b6be-52f0-85e5-f414a28c3328" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257250 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257251 13194139533864 true] [17592186257250 1089 "q-117575" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257250 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257250 1100 "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=-5\\operatorname{sin}(3x-\\pi )-3?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257250 1113 #uuid "8910d732-402d-5d51-b841-b7875aafc6e4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257250 1145 "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{sin}(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$\n\nSince $A=-5,$ we have that the amplitude is $|A|=|-5|=5.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257251 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257252 13194139533864 true] [17592186257251 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257251 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257251 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257252 13194139533864 true] [17592186257251 1110 #uuid "0f3c7636-e490-520a-b803-7e863dbc2962" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257252 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257252 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257252 1111 #uuid "a8ce3e53-e1f5-5591-a4eb-114c90c884d1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257253 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257254 13194139533864 true] [17592186257253 1089 "q-117580" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257253 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257253 1100 "Calculate the period of the function $y=\\operatorname{cos}(12x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257253 1113 #uuid "da8a775b-a69b-5a24-98f0-d3d6e285343e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257253 1145 "Given a function in the form $y=A\\operatorname{cos}(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=12.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{12}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257254 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257255 13194139533864 true] [17592186257254 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257254 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257254 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257255 13194139533864 true] [17592186257254 1110 #uuid "7fc8e984-fb84-5322-88b0-26593ad8969b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257255 1034 "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257255 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257255 1111 #uuid "456126d4-4b0c-584c-a371-14f640a4cc7f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257256 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257257 13194139533864 true] [17592186257256 1089 "q-117583" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257256 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257256 1100 "What is the period of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{8x}{3}+{20}^{∘})?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257256 1113 #uuid "7ecd9674-b0aa-5c61-a990-f67926f0e4db" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257256 1145 "Given a function in the form $y=A\\operatorname{sin}(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{8}{3}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{(\\frac{8}{3})}={135}^{∘}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257257 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257258 13194139533864 true] [17592186257257 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257257 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257257 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257258 13194139533864 true] [17592186257257 1110 #uuid "1295d0dd-7a4e-52dc-a91e-818961ebef76" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257258 1034 "{135}^{∘}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257258 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257258 1111 #uuid "1a79d73f-bdcc-5c64-a1f0-f83e3a81cc67" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257259 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257260 13194139533864 true] [17592186257259 1089 "q-117874" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257259 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257259 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/95ba91361c17f357321f7a4b5e44fb6a26306408f1d2431de9cccfcd003af343.png)\n\nCalculate the height $h$ of the cylinder above given that its volume is $100\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}$ and its radius is $5\\,\\text{ft}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257259 1113 #uuid "3b6b2adc-2acf-5470-a549-3189a0646fe7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257259 1145 "The volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $V=100\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}$ and $r=5\\,\\text{ft}$ into the formula and solving for $h,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\ 100\\pi  & =\\pi (5)^{2}h \\\\ 25\\pi h & =100\\pi  \\\\ h & =4\\,\\text{ft}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257260 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257261 13194139533864 true] [17592186257260 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257260 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257260 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257261 13194139533864 true] [17592186257260 1110 #uuid "938926a7-1270-5c19-bbfa-91591dea5715" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257261 1034 "4\\,\\text{ft}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257261 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257261 1111 #uuid "89766376-ea74-5b96-a7b2-2b0bf2259661" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257262 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257263 13194139533864 true] [17592186257262 1089 "q-118638" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257262 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257262 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/0beb4cb3deb6bf528f399d93f4fad9e9f4ddb141f7187608bdf9a9bd047eb9a7.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $\\overset{⟷}{YZ}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{WX}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257262 1113 #uuid "28fafe7a-73c7-5a73-a89e-2b98b4be1492" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257262 1145 "By the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point on the perpendicular bisector of a segment is equidistant from the endpoints of the segment. Hence, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}WZ & =XZ \\\\ 2x+2 & =17-x \\\\ 3x & =15 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257263 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257264 13194139533864 true] [17592186257263 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257263 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257263 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257264 13194139533864 true] [17592186257263 1110 #uuid "70bc7d00-d148-5978-b0f0-bbea8032d6aa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257264 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257264 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257264 1111 #uuid "b7c4db9d-25e2-5919-b3ae-fcdeff4ae1ad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257265 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257266 13194139533864 true] [17592186257265 1089 "q-118705" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257265 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257265 1100 "An electric oven in the shape of a cube has a side length of $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{yd}.$ What is the volume of the oven?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257265 1113 #uuid "7a21337e-3e5a-51b3-bf08-4393c6f42c40" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257265 1145 "We need to find the volume of the oven.\n\nThe volume $V$ of a cube can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nV={s}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the side length of the oven.\n\nSubstituting $s=\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{yd}$ into the formula, we get \n\n$$\nV={(\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{yd})}^{3}=\\frac{8}{27}\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the oven is $\\frac{8}{27}\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257266 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257267 13194139533864 true] [17592186257266 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257266 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257266 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257267 13194139533864 true] [17592186257266 1110 #uuid "347549b9-f010-5796-ba6b-0b4c4671ca4f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257267 1034 "\\frac{8}{27}\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257267 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257267 1111 #uuid "28958a92-cdfd-563c-bf7b-698f8dd3dfc5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257268 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257269 13194139533864 true] [17592186257268 1089 "q-118780" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257268 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257268 1100 "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation ${x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}}-9{x}^{7}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257268 1113 #uuid "6548405c-a613-5075-b99b-e7774982d1db" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257268 1145 "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}},$ which has order $8.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $8.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257269 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257270 13194139533864 true] [17592186257269 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257269 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257269 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257270 13194139533864 true] [17592186257269 1110 #uuid "42064ebd-20a9-5478-a842-ced33dee8f82" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257270 1034 "8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257270 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257270 1111 #uuid "5ca617c8-ae0c-50ae-9246-ed19fda55c63" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257271 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257272 13194139533864 true] [17592186257271 1089 "q-118782" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257271 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257271 1100 "Which of the following are second-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - $9x{y}^{''}+8{y}^{'}=4$\nII. - $\\sqrt{{y}^{''}+x}=3y$\nIII. - ${y}^{‴}+{x}^{2}{y}^{''}=3$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257271 1113 #uuid "c6fe84cb-75e2-55de-b6df-ded3330a421e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257271 1145 "A differential equation is of second-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{''}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{''}.$\n\n- Equation II is also a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{''}.$\n\n- Equation III is not a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257272 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257273 13194139533864 true] [17592186257272 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257274 13194139533864 true] [17592186257272 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257275 13194139533864 true] [17592186257272 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257276 13194139533864 true] [17592186257272 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257277 13194139533864 true] [17592186257272 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257278 13194139533864 true] [17592186257272 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257279 13194139533864 true] [17592186257272 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257280 13194139533864 true] [17592186257272 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257272 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257272 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257278 13194139533864 true] [17592186257272 1110 #uuid "257a23f9-a24a-5b27-a803-2fd9ff28766b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257273 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257273 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257273 1111 #uuid "5bd11e8a-9d28-5792-a51b-1ee774af6cb2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257274 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257274 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257274 1111 #uuid "3f1de0e4-6581-50d8-b4dd-417ce299ef49" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257275 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257275 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257275 1111 #uuid "fd82d032-b6b4-5f9f-bb42-9bc13998c9b2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257276 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257276 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257276 1111 #uuid "6c9a1d31-55dc-5cac-9de6-5d54020933fe" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257277 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257277 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257277 1111 #uuid "a3956880-699f-50c3-98e2-75b8a47f798d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257278 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257278 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257278 1111 #uuid "89b70b1a-c0f9-503a-8b4f-42025621f37c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257279 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257279 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257279 1111 #uuid "a75eaa53-ae4e-5fee-a937-f991724017e4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257280 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257280 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257280 1111 #uuid "81ccf827-073c-5b1e-8402-506130440329" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257281 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257282 13194139533864 true] [17592186257281 1089 "q-118786" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257281 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257281 1100 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nI. - $(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{'}=8y,\\,y(-1)=3$\nII. - $8{y}^{'}-xy=3$\nIII. - $3{y}^{''}=4y,\\,y(-1)=0,\\,{y}^{'}(-1)=1$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257281 1113 #uuid "7d3e1743-cd34-559d-bad0-d34093686fd6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257281 1145 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{'}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{'}=8y$ together with the initial condition $y(-1)=3.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $3{y}^{''}=4y$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(-1)=0,\\,{y}^{'}(-1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257282 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257283 13194139533864 true] [17592186257282 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257284 13194139533864 true] [17592186257282 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257285 13194139533864 true] [17592186257282 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257286 13194139533864 true] [17592186257282 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257287 13194139533864 true] [17592186257282 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257288 13194139533864 true] [17592186257282 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257289 13194139533864 true] [17592186257282 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257290 13194139533864 true] [17592186257282 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257282 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257282 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257286 13194139533864 true] [17592186257282 1110 #uuid "39594d48-3439-593f-beb0-784efbc73181" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257283 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257283 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257283 1111 #uuid "39ee5fc1-03e0-57e5-bbbf-315d46f4703b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257284 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257284 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257284 1111 #uuid "f57d7578-82c4-5967-a523-96974dbf056c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257285 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257285 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257285 1111 #uuid "9d8e1b40-531d-5738-bd3c-ddd411adcbb3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257286 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257286 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257286 1111 #uuid "3dac5233-1cb8-538b-a95f-9c3184be17d0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257287 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257287 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257287 1111 #uuid "c9953a88-e46e-55ac-b3c0-ab066edb021f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257288 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257288 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257288 1111 #uuid "b345a296-774e-57b4-8d0e-503947db627b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257289 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257289 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257289 1111 #uuid "9d54d347-85b6-56fb-b78d-46ec06bb4b9b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257290 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257290 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257290 1111 #uuid "d2471a44-b1ef-5141-9fe5-a87c669720c3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257291 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257292 13194139533864 true] [17592186257291 1089 "q-118839" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257291 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257291 1100 "What is the distance between the points $A(8,-3,-1)$ and $B(12,5,-2)?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257291 1113 #uuid "1ae8950d-1f55-5b0d-b4d8-6562835c999a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257291 1145 "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(8,-3,-1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(12,5,-2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(12-8)^{2}+(5-(-3))^{2}+(-2-(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{8}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+64+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{81} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257292 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257293 13194139533864 true] [17592186257292 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257292 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257292 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257293 13194139533864 true] [17592186257292 1110 #uuid "7ebf1136-0ad1-5236-b788-da4efd706743" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257293 1034 "9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257293 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257293 1111 #uuid "db0f4fee-52ce-59ea-a878-1cc3251e9c31" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257294 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257295 13194139533864 true] [17592186257294 1089 "q-118863" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257294 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257294 1100 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - ${y}^{'}-8{e}^{y}=2$\nII. - $y{y}^{'}+3=\\operatorname{cos}y$\nIII. - $x{y}^{'}-8={e}^{y}$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257294 1113 #uuid "a3b6aeaa-a451-5b80-8bb6-433e2232b048" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257294 1145 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}-8{e}^{y} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{'} & =8{e}^{y}+2 \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=8{e}^{y}+2.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{y}^{'}+3 & =\\operatorname{cos}y \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}y-3}{y} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}y-3}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{'}-8 & ={e}^{y} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\frac{{e}^{y}+8}{x} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{y}+8}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257295 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257296 13194139533864 true] [17592186257295 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257297 13194139533864 true] [17592186257295 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257298 13194139533864 true] [17592186257295 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257299 13194139533864 true] [17592186257295 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257300 13194139533864 true] [17592186257295 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257301 13194139533864 true] [17592186257295 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257302 13194139533864 true] [17592186257295 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257303 13194139533864 true] [17592186257295 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257295 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257295 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257301 13194139533864 true] [17592186257295 1110 #uuid "d629f59a-43b8-52d9-9357-8e959cb4368d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257296 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257296 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257296 1111 #uuid "3235ef37-0ffb-5843-b119-69f7fbde2c58" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257297 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257297 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257297 1111 #uuid "df5f3e74-890b-5156-ba8c-31602fb54a0f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257298 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257298 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257298 1111 #uuid "258eb886-5afd-548a-957e-221d02dc85f3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257299 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257299 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257299 1111 #uuid "4e023746-ca48-5ec4-98d8-26a7b685ac4d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257300 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257300 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257300 1111 #uuid "ee38a5c3-7fe8-556b-bb81-d972d9343ec4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257301 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257301 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257301 1111 #uuid "471dddbc-f346-5688-a28d-86d6ac2d38da" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257302 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257302 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257302 1111 #uuid "5fd83e73-697a-5565-aede-9bf0a4362b1c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257303 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257303 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257303 1111 #uuid "d79bd66a-a044-5058-9373-524b6b028706" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257304 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257305 13194139533864 true] [17592186257304 1089 "q-118874" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257304 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257304 1100 "A pedestal for a statue consists of eight identical stone blocks in the form of a rectangular solid. Each block is $20\\,\\text{ft}$ long, $15\\,\\text{ft}$ wide, and $2\\,\\text{ft}$ high. What is the total combined volume of the pedestal?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257304 1113 #uuid "4787160a-e9d5-53b7-b357-952fd5a6eafd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257304 1145 "First, we need to find the volume of one block.\n\nWe can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=20,\\,w=15,\\,h=2\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=20\\cdot 15\\cdot 2=600\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total volume of the eight blocks is\n\n$$\n600\\cdot 8=4\\,800\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257305 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257306 13194139533864 true] [17592186257305 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257305 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257305 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257306 13194139533864 true] [17592186257305 1110 #uuid "52c403d0-7af2-5a98-b9fa-393bf6dd87a1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257306 1034 "4\\,800\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257306 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257306 1111 #uuid "23595869-514b-58aa-b864-2a4701504c7a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257307 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257308 13194139533864 true] [17592186257307 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257310 13194139533864 true] [17592186257307 1089 "q-118955" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257307 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257307 1100 "Consider the points $A(12w,2w,-4w)$ and $B(7w,-6w,2w).$ Given that the distance between $A$ and $B$ is $30\\sqrt{5},$ find the possible values of $w.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257307 1113 #uuid "a478ded4-a3e7-57e8-9ba2-a2eb8cb92b12" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257307 1145 "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(12w,2w,-4w)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(7w,-6w,2w)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(7w-12w)^{2}+(-6w-2w)^{2}+(2w-(-4w))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5w)^{2}+(-8w)^{2}+(6w)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{w}^{2}+64{w}^{2}+36{w}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{125{w}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{125}\\,\\sqrt{{w}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{5}\\,|w|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $d=30\\sqrt{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\sqrt{5}\\,|w| & =30\\sqrt{5} \\\\ |w| & =6 \\\\ w & =\\pm 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257308 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257309 13194139533864 true] [17592186257308 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257308 1041 "root-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257308 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257309 13194139533864 true] [17592186257308 1110 #uuid "47bec473-b654-5bf5-8f7f-7ce0b93b538f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257309 1034 "-6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257309 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257309 1111 #uuid "0df561d3-5704-567e-9bea-30eaabf51a64" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257310 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257311 13194139533864 true] [17592186257310 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257310 1041 "root-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257310 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257311 13194139533864 true] [17592186257310 1110 #uuid "0f8f41b8-b188-56fc-ab1f-eefeefda3503" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257311 1034 "6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257311 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257311 1111 #uuid "ee79302d-cad7-5427-88cd-f0908fb97959" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257312 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257313 13194139533864 true] [17592186257312 1089 "q-119029" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257312 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257312 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/42ba6528b1963aa50a80af060fef6331fffe51a564830e35ac10b71782d49e94.png)\n\nWhat is the length of $\\overset{―}{AB}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257312 1113 #uuid "5ca0e9cb-003a-561c-aa77-aa39bda7e844" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257312 1145 "From the figure, we note that the points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates ${{(1,5)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(6,2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})},$ respectively. \n\nTo find the distance $AB,$ we can use the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(6-1)^{2}+(2-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257313 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257314 13194139533864 true] [17592186257313 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257313 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257313 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257314 13194139533864 true] [17592186257313 1110 #uuid "8b67527f-2264-5486-ac15-f6cf6d91ada5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257314 1034 "\\sqrt{34}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257314 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257314 1111 #uuid "9c4a05c2-d5ba-581c-b85c-bd4093180c09" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257315 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257316 13194139533864 true] [17592186257315 1089 "q-119100" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257315 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257315 1100 "Simplify the expression $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257315 1113 #uuid "de7d1c80-2055-515b-a4b8-9b8892573c46" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257315 1145 "First, let's interpret the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }=\\operatorname{tan}\\theta ÷\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \n$$\n\nThen, we recall the identity $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ and we substitute it into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}\\theta ÷\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta })÷\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we turn the division into multiplication, and simplify the expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta })÷\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot 1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\operatorname{sec}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{sec}\\theta .$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257316 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257317 13194139533864 true] [17592186257316 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257316 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257316 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257317 13194139533864 true] [17592186257316 1110 #uuid "45440abb-f97d-5550-b0db-29b920e9a580" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257317 1034 "2\\operatorname{sec}\\theta" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257317 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257317 1111 #uuid "853842e3-487a-5e38-9c0d-543bc62c698b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257318 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257319 13194139533864 true] [17592186257318 1089 "q-119102" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257318 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257318 1100 "Simplify the expression $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{csc}\\theta .$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257318 1113 #uuid "38ea29bc-c897-5316-8be5-65752445ef8f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257318 1145 "First, we recall the identities $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }$ and $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ and we substitute them into the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the three quantities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{csc}\\theta =1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257319 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257320 13194139533864 true] [17592186257319 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257319 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257319 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257320 13194139533864 true] [17592186257319 1110 #uuid "4afbf64c-d695-5ed2-b0e4-5d1a24b3779d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257320 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257320 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257320 1111 #uuid "d9b4e031-b7be-5d38-8cf4-7e4d0443b15d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257321 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257322 13194139533864 true] [17592186257321 1089 "q-11914" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257321 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257321 1100 "Given that $y=5\\operatorname{ln}x-3{x}^{2}+2x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257321 1113 #uuid "6efa523e-e3d2-57fc-85c3-4bc45ebc82f5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257321 1145 "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5\\operatorname{ln}x-3{x}^{2}+2x) \\\\  & =5(\\frac{1}{x})-3\\cdot 2x+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{x}-6x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257322 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257323 13194139533864 true] [17592186257322 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257322 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257322 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257323 13194139533864 true] [17592186257322 1110 #uuid "ad78b57a-f611-5b1d-8314-fee4bd97b9e0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257323 1034 "\\frac{5}{x}-6x+2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257323 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257323 1111 #uuid "2b8b3d32-5a1c-5867-b4c3-7f179546897c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257324 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257325 13194139533864 true] [17592186257324 1089 "q-119215" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257324 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257324 1100 "Given that  $7\\operatorname{sin}\\theta =17\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,$ calculate $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta .$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257324 1113 #uuid "8371e6c3-10d4-531f-8a1a-633dab884727" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257324 1145 "First, we recall the identity $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }.$\n\nTo write the equation in terms of $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta $ only, we divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,$ and apply the above identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =17\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ 7\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } & =17\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\ 7\\operatorname{tan}\\theta  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we isolate $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta $ by dividing both sides of the equation by $7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7\\operatorname{tan}\\theta }{7} & =\\frac{17}{7} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}\\theta  & =\\frac{17}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257325 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257326 13194139533864 true] [17592186257325 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257325 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257325 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257326 13194139533864 true] [17592186257325 1110 #uuid "ed06f08f-7818-53e6-8631-641b2ed08fa9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257326 1034 "\\frac{17}{7}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257326 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257326 1111 #uuid "b08b20f9-5f5e-5aff-93b5-773bfab8b758" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257327 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257328 13194139533864 true] [17592186257327 1089 "q-119266" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257327 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257327 1100 "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257327 1113 #uuid "5a14c4ac-9ae0-5be9-b8ff-f6429667be79" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257327 1145 "First, let's interpret the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}=(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)\n$$\n\nThen, we recall the identities $\\operatorname{cot}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }$ and $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ and we substitute them into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1) & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify, turn the division into multiplication, and then simplify again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1) & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta })÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }})÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}=1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257328 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257329 13194139533864 true] [17592186257328 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257328 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257328 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257329 13194139533864 true] [17592186257328 1110 #uuid "5ae3881d-9f37-5d41-87ad-00d04a4f2741" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257329 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257329 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257329 1111 #uuid "d4544c65-d138-5ebf-8267-be0c4b37fc56" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257330 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257331 13194139533864 true] [17592186257330 1089 "q-119346" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257330 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257330 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/a058d9b800decdb32fdd09715f1bebab03b4a12c7e6119d6c45bbb170ce640b0.png)\n\nWhat is the length of $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ in the diagram above?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257330 1113 #uuid "0f19f56b-b91e-5f5c-acd5-5bfff04f8d01" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257330 1145 "The length of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is equal to the distance between $P$ and $Q.$\n\nThe distance $d$ between two points $P({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $Q({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince $P$ and $Q$ have coordinates ${{(6,\\,0,\\,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and ${{(-1,4,2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}},$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ & =\\sqrt{(-1-6)^{2}+(4-0)^{2}+(2-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-7)^{2}+{4}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{66}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257331 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257332 13194139533864 true] [17592186257331 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257331 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257331 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257332 13194139533864 true] [17592186257331 1110 #uuid "ed79f543-a4c1-5874-b52f-ef24cdd5a6f3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257332 1034 "\\sqrt{66}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257332 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257332 1111 #uuid "93539bab-ab03-5a6a-8270-eae92298c289" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257333 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257334 13194139533864 true] [17592186257333 1089 "q-119376" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257333 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257333 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ff6cdd3a9cc650842bba6e546cc91fca3206279cb6d8f9d835357c3cdcb597ce.png)\n\nConsider the sample space $S$ with the Venn diagram given above. What is the value of a constant $b?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257333 1113 #uuid "d531bcc0-5aad-57d1-a7e6-bdcbfb0a5d04" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257333 1145 "From the given Venn diagram, we have $P(X)=\\frac{1}{b}$ and $P({X}^{'})=\\frac{2}{b}.$\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X)+P({X}^{'}) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{b}+\\frac{2}{b} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{b} & =1 \\\\ b & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257334 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257335 13194139533864 true] [17592186257334 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257334 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257334 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257335 13194139533864 true] [17592186257334 1110 #uuid "9a980036-158c-5b0c-a902-64f2cba3f24e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257335 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257335 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257335 1111 #uuid "bb8457db-f6e5-5ddd-b57d-62da772ce615" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257336 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257337 13194139533864 true] [17592186257336 1089 "q-11939" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257336 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257336 1100 "Given that $y=x\\operatorname{ln}3x,$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257336 1113 #uuid "dac1407f-85ee-5e7c-bd88-0868af1543b5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257336 1145 "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}3x.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=1,\\,{v}^{'}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =(1)(\\operatorname{ln}3x)+(x)(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}3x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257337 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257338 13194139533864 true] [17592186257337 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257337 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257337 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257338 13194139533864 true] [17592186257337 1110 #uuid "63317a9b-966c-5694-a976-c22ff086d1d3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257338 1034 "\\operatorname{ln}3x+1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257338 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257338 1111 #uuid "e4211aec-a9bd-5be3-80d7-a4777c57bb12" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257339 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257340 13194139533864 true] [17592186257339 1089 "q-119398" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257339 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257339 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/791a76ebbb34751b2ce345243c5de4baf95a32357ce48830e3ea43043011b5e1.png)\n\nGiven the above sample space, find $P({X}^{'}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257339 1113 #uuid "307826c5-d518-52f5-8812-84ccd5425547" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257339 1145 "The Venn diagram tells us that $P(X)=\\frac{2}{9}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({X}^{'}) & =1-P(X) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257340 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257341 13194139533864 true] [17592186257340 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257340 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257340 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257341 13194139533864 true] [17592186257340 1110 #uuid "a9a4000a-94f8-5b97-adfe-9613736db240" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257341 1034 "\\frac{7}{9}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257341 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257341 1111 #uuid "a7449b96-505f-5add-86a5-d67ebf511a99" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257342 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257343 13194139533864 true] [17592186257342 1089 "q-119406" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257342 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257342 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/c700c7a205ab674275e2e9f0b2dd79054853dfeaf33f6794d6d7e4196389274b.png)\n\nGiven the above sample space, find $P(X).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257342 1113 #uuid "63041fbf-1c6b-5528-a9b1-540c3e1c23bb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257342 1145 "The Venn diagram tells us that $P({X}^{'})=0.1234.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X) & =1-P({X}^{'}) \\\\  & =1-0.1234 \\\\  & =0.8766.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257343 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257344 13194139533864 true] [17592186257343 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257343 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257343 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257344 13194139533864 true] [17592186257343 1110 #uuid "2a4b6805-4f60-5a64-b64d-a1da46c28340" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257344 1034 "0.8766" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257344 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257344 1111 #uuid "695bb784-c889-5b24-b8b4-10b5a10508ad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257345 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257346 13194139533864 true] [17592186257345 1089 "q-119481" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257345 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257345 1100 "Find the exact value of $\\operatorname{cos}(-{75}^{∘}).$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $-{75}^{∘}={60}^{∘}-{135}^{∘}.$*\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257345 1113 #uuid "dca028c6-39fc-5c90-b60c-c0a6062705b3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257345 1145 "Recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(x-y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $x={60}^{∘}$ and $y={135}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(-{75}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}({60}^{∘}-{135}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}{60}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}{135}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}{60}^{∘}\\operatorname{sin}{135}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257346 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257347 13194139533864 true] [17592186257346 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257346 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257346 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257347 13194139533864 true] [17592186257346 1110 #uuid "47fcc8cc-fc22-5cf8-8870-a191a5c947f2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257347 1034 "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257347 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257347 1111 #uuid "a3974794-7f69-529e-88a6-c77f4d353d57" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257348 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257349 13194139533864 true] [17592186257348 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257352 13194139533864 true] [17592186257348 1089 "q-119652" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257348 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257348 1100 "Of $12$ people attending a seminar, $4$ participants have black hair, and the rest have brown hair. Define $A$ to be the event \"a participant has black hair.\" What is ${A}^{'}$ described in natural language, and what is $P({A}^{'})?$\n\nComplement event: {{complement-event}}\n\nComplement probability: {{complement-probability}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257348 1113 #uuid "e8e1a657-7b1c-5430-8f0a-97245cf198c9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257348 1145 "The **complement** of an event $A,$ denoted ${A}^{'},$ is the *opposite* of $A.$ That is to say, ${A}^{'}$  is the set of outcomes in which $A$ *does not* happen.\n\nHere, ${A}^{'}$ is the opposite of the event \"a participant has black hair.\" So ${A}^{'}$ is the event \"a participant doesn't have black hair.\" In other words, the event ${A}^{'}$ is \"a participant has brown hair.\"\n\nNow, let's calculate the probability $P({A}^{'}).$ The sample space is \n\n$$\nS={\\text{black hair, brown hair}},\n$$\n\nand since there is a different number of participants with of each hair color, we have different probabilities of a participant having \"black hair\" or \"brown hair.\"\n\nOf $12$ people, $8$ of them have brown hair. Therefore, \n\n$$\nP({A}^{'})=\\frac{8}{12}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257349 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257350 13194139533864 true] [17592186257349 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257351 13194139533864 true] [17592186257349 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139533864 true] [17592186257349 1041 "complement-event" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257349 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257351 13194139533864 true] [17592186257349 1110 #uuid "23b2594d-cab3-5737-9737-16ecb75f11d2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257350 1034 "The participant has black hair" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257350 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257350 1111 #uuid "d002352a-cabf-5b87-813c-d520928bb3a5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257351 1034 "The participant has brown hair" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257351 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257351 1111 #uuid "6d28ce5d-3252-5bfc-8b9d-30c90d229c50" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257352 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257353 13194139533864 true] [17592186257352 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257352 1041 "complement-probability" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257352 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257353 13194139533864 true] [17592186257352 1110 #uuid "8d0a21ef-d89d-5675-a3c4-6cd3830ce411" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257353 1034 "\\frac23" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257353 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257353 1111 #uuid "6e683226-0127-5948-9917-9e280c6dfe37" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257354 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257355 13194139533864 true] [17592186257354 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257358 13194139533864 true] [17592186257354 1089 "q-119653" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257354 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257354 1100 "Mary owns $5$ orange shirts, and $15$ shirts in other colors. Define $A$ to be the event \"Mary wears an orange shirt.\" What is ${A}^{'}$ described in natural language, and what is $P({A}^{'})?$\n\nComplement event: {{complement-event}}\n\nComplement probability: {{complement-probability}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257354 1113 #uuid "c05ffc72-f224-50a5-b7b1-6c15fbd2efb9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257354 1145 "The **complement** of an event $A,$ denoted ${A}^{'},$ is the *opposite* of $A.$ That is to say, ${A}^{'}$  is the set of outcomes in which $A$ *does not* happen.\n\nHere, ${A}^{'}$ is the opposite of the event \"Mary wears an orange shirt.\" So ${A}^{'}$ is the event \"Mary doesn't wear an orange shirt.\" In other words, the event ${A}^{'}$ is \"Mary wears a non-orange shirt.\"\n\nNow, let's calculate the probability $P({A}^{'}).$ The sample space is \n\n$$\nS={\\text{orange shirt, non-orange shirt}},\n$$\n\nand since there is a different number of shirts of each color, we have different probabilities of having an \"orange\" or \"non-orange\" shirt.\n\nMary owns $20$ shirts, and $15$ of them are non-orange. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP({A}^{'})=\\frac{15}{20}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257355 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257356 13194139533864 true] [17592186257355 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257357 13194139533864 true] [17592186257355 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139533864 true] [17592186257355 1041 "complement-event" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257355 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257357 13194139533864 true] [17592186257355 1110 #uuid "1085e064-35c0-5fd5-ab8e-1c897de5de71" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257356 1034 "Mary wears an orange shirt" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257356 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257356 1111 #uuid "716804fe-fe83-5eb6-97aa-e9f5d1111d2d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257357 1034 "Mary does not wear an orange shirt" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257357 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257357 1111 #uuid "5d585645-c63f-5b50-a0ed-ac4876f0498b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257358 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257359 13194139533864 true] [17592186257358 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257358 1041 "complement-probability" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257358 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257359 13194139533864 true] [17592186257358 1110 #uuid "8e40d33c-09bb-5da4-9ca6-86df1b6bc0c5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257359 1034 "\\frac34" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257359 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257359 1111 #uuid "b12f6170-556d-5008-b51e-bdf278ffac3e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257360 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257361 13194139533864 true] [17592186257360 1089 "q-119661" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257360 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257360 1100 "Two fair $6$-sided dice are rolled. Define $A$ to be the event \"the sum of outcomes is a prime number\" Which of the following statements are true regarding this event?\n\nI. - $P(A)=0.42$ to $2$ decimal places\nII. - ${A}^{'}$ is the event \"the sum of the outcomes is an even number\"\nIII. - $P({A}^{'})=0.58$ to $2$ decimal places" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257360 1113 #uuid "ccb354f9-ccd4-5d1d-9d93-05b8eecb5e81" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257360 1145 "Let's inspect each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. There are $36$ possible outcomes and $15$ of these have the sum of the outcomes equal to a prime, namely \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,4),(4,1),(2,3),(3,2),(1,6), \\\\  & (6,1),(3,4),(4,3),(2,5),(5,2),(5,6),(6,5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(A)=\\frac{15}{36}=0.42\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places.\n\n- Statement II is false. The complement of $A$ is the event ${A}^{'},$ \"the sum of the outcomes is a composite number\".\n\n- Statement III is true. Since we know that $P(A)=0.42$ to $2$ decimal places, we can calculate $P({A}^{'})$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A)+P({A}^{'}) & =1 \\\\ P({A}^{'}) & =1-P(A) \\\\  & =1-0.42 \\\\  & =0.58.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257362 13194139533864 true] [17592186257361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257363 13194139533864 true] [17592186257361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257364 13194139533864 true] [17592186257361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257365 13194139533864 true] [17592186257361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257366 13194139533864 true] [17592186257361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257367 13194139533864 true] [17592186257361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257368 13194139533864 true] [17592186257361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257369 13194139533864 true] [17592186257361 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257361 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257361 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257364 13194139533864 true] [17592186257361 1110 #uuid "4b17352a-c753-5c59-820d-fe9d7e06eb41" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257362 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257362 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257362 1111 #uuid "59ad92fe-06ee-57cb-aec2-77491ac1bb6e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257363 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257363 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257363 1111 #uuid "940c2463-620e-5c9c-b7fe-91995fe351c5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257364 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257364 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257364 1111 #uuid "3d67ee63-54ab-5bf4-8076-feb68e3aa3e2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257365 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257365 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257365 1111 #uuid "f70090e3-98ef-5f3f-b28c-00eb47513251" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257366 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257366 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257366 1111 #uuid "bce7f2da-3789-5c2b-8e94-19ed4626c645" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257367 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257367 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257367 1111 #uuid "226a80ab-c24c-5168-9995-96b4cdccd0e2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257368 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257368 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257368 1111 #uuid "653dfee8-c0bc-5bea-86ab-851d707ca336" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257369 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257369 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257369 1111 #uuid "e1c0e67e-d808-53cd-9858-cc01631827c5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257370 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257371 13194139533864 true] [17592186257370 1089 "q-119699" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257370 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257370 1100 "Three balls are drawn from a bag that contains $5$ white balls and $4$ black balls. Define $A$ to be the event \"at least one white ball is drawn.\" It is known that $P(A)=0.95$ to $2$ decimal places. To two decimal places, what is the probability of drawing three black balls?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257370 1113 #uuid "76002dd4-3fa5-5b97-999e-21ec06af100c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257370 1145 "The event \"three black balls are drawn\" is the opposite of the event \"at least one white ball is drawn.\"\n\nSo, the event \"three black balls are drawn\" is the complement of $A,$ denoted ${A}^{'}.$\n\nSince we know that $P(A)=0.95$ to $2$ decimal places, we can calculate $P({A}^{'})$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A)+P({A}^{'}) & =1 \\\\ P({A}^{'}) & =1-P(A) \\\\  & =1-0.95 \\\\  & =0.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257371 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257372 13194139533864 true] [17592186257371 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257371 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257371 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257372 13194139533864 true] [17592186257371 1110 #uuid "4d625369-3894-5861-8ca4-9400a3db1aba" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257372 1034 "0.05" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257372 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257372 1111 #uuid "c0c7da23-01a0-51be-9e7e-72e594b04dd3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257373 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257374 13194139533864 true] [17592186257373 1089 "q-119781" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257373 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257373 1100 "Which of the following statements must be true regarding any tetrahedron? \n\nI. - It has $4$ congruent faces, each in the form of a right triangle\nII. - Exactly $3$ faces meet at each vertex\nIII. - It has $4$ edges" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257373 1113 #uuid "d8bee717-f3b4-52d8-bbff-9f569aa6d04c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257373 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/adcc202ad86b782fd153566d6454269dcd50e8f1d9aca37bc42d2266c142ced8.png)\n\nLet's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. A tetrahedron is a regular polyhedron that \n\n- consists of $4$ congruent faces, each in the form of an equilateral triangle (not a right triangle), and\n\n- exactly $3$ faces meet at each vertex.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since there are $F=4$ faces, each in the form of an equilateral triangle, we have\n\n$$\nS=4\\cdot 3=12\n$$\n\nsides combined. Now, each edge of the polyhedron is created when two face sides coincide. So, the number of edges of the tetrahedron must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{S}{2}=\\frac{12}{2}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257375 13194139533864 true] [17592186257374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257376 13194139533864 true] [17592186257374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257377 13194139533864 true] [17592186257374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257378 13194139533864 true] [17592186257374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257379 13194139533864 true] [17592186257374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257380 13194139533864 true] [17592186257374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257381 13194139533864 true] [17592186257374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257382 13194139533864 true] [17592186257374 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257374 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257374 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257382 13194139533864 true] [17592186257374 1110 #uuid "9ca5971d-3642-5e78-b611-8e6d7d3ff936" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257375 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257375 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257375 1111 #uuid "69302e63-a57e-5396-acc9-6dc6cea005b7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257376 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257376 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257376 1111 #uuid "89efa4a6-0420-51af-a849-84e77d8affb5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257377 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257377 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257377 1111 #uuid "9a4e067d-c9c5-5fa9-be76-b089673d2924" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257378 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257378 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257378 1111 #uuid "1e257669-4e9a-5bdf-ac9d-4e4a6c397131" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257379 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257379 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257379 1111 #uuid "e0ea8bdd-632d-58e0-af4b-0c93e0683c00" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257380 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257380 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257380 1111 #uuid "9e738797-cf84-5fab-a373-b7d0a87b64c5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257381 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257381 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257381 1111 #uuid "60c0e146-2c9a-5ce1-aaac-193318cd408e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257382 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257382 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257382 1111 #uuid "921f2784-76d0-5de5-864d-763447c6080c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257383 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257384 13194139533864 true] [17592186257383 1089 "q-119805" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257383 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257383 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/95c30eab094a24beb6976262167401efa973dbac12781c69d074eb14e12e2743.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. What is a unit vector that is parallel to $m+n?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257383 1113 #uuid "6e8f6481-88a7-5c1f-b12c-d389c514d403" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257383 1145 "First, we have to find $m+n.$ According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $m+n$ goes from the tail of $m$ to the head of $n:$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/87647211f77776c5f5f740c205fafb150eef1b9d3ebefb82df73a9fdcd953bdb.png)\n\nIn $△AHB$, we have $∠AHB={90}^{∘}$, $AH=0.5=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $BH=2.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|m+n| & =AB \\\\  & =\\sqrt{A{H}^{2}+B{H}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $m+n$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}.$ Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{1}{|m+n|}\\cdot (m+n) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}(m+n).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257384 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257385 13194139533864 true] [17592186257384 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257384 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257384 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257385 13194139533864 true] [17592186257384 1110 #uuid "75545e5f-12e8-57eb-b949-d8350ed15577" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257385 1034 "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}(m+n)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257385 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257385 1111 #uuid "a8526630-4d0a-5673-a018-d26d3e392511" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257386 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257387 13194139533864 true] [17592186257386 1089 "q-119865" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257386 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257386 1100 "Simplify $\\frac{{sin}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257386 1113 #uuid "5a918518-91f1-50bb-bef6-5c2c5dd04e5a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257386 1145 "First, we rewrite the numerator of the first term using $1-{cos}^{2}x={sin}^{2}x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1=\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the numerator of the first term as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1 & =\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}x)(1+\\operatorname{cos}x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{cos}x)}(1+\\operatorname{cos}x)}{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}x}{1}-1 \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{cos}x-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257387 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257388 13194139533864 true] [17592186257387 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257387 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257387 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257388 13194139533864 true] [17592186257387 1110 #uuid "d0c2566c-62b1-5822-b528-94b1c53921a7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257388 1034 "\\operatorname{cos}x" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257388 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257388 1111 #uuid "21e66950-5962-5b82-a7a0-6b569173657e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257389 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257390 13194139533864 true] [17592186257389 1089 "q-119947" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257389 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257389 1100 "Simplify ${(\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))}^{2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257389 1113 #uuid "5f30800d-77df-5a61-9a68-2afbf867dda1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257389 1145 "First, we expand the parenthesis and use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+{cos}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =[{sin}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+{cos}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})]+2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =1+2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))}^{2}=1+2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257390 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257391 13194139533864 true] [17592186257390 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257390 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257390 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257391 13194139533864 true] [17592186257390 1110 #uuid "ae21b87a-8faf-5500-8005-d41884c80814" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257391 1034 "1+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257391 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257391 1111 #uuid "4f5d5009-47b4-5e9b-a6bb-e3fcfec21e83" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257392 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257393 13194139533864 true] [17592186257392 1089 "q-120026" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257392 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257392 1100 "$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}{80}^{∘}\\operatorname{tan}{40}^{∘}-1}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257392 1113 #uuid "b566fa84-3938-5596-bfbc-9b6e5426b71c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257392 1145 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}2x=2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={40}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot {40}^{∘}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}({80}^{∘}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}{80}^{∘}\\operatorname{tan}{40}^{∘}-1}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}}-1}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{sin}^{2}{40}^{∘}-1}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}{40}^{∘}-1+{sin}^{2}{40}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}{40}^{∘}-(1-{sin}^{2}{40}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing  the Pythagorean identity in the form ${cos}^{2}{40}^{∘}=1-{sin}^{2}{40}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}{40}^{∘}-(1-{sin}^{2}{40}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}} & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}{40}^{∘}-{cos}^{2}{40}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘})(\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘})}(\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘})}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257393 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257394 13194139533864 true] [17592186257393 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257393 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257393 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257394 13194139533864 true] [17592186257393 1110 #uuid "aa6bc17e-27f5-5be0-8522-dd8f97b112b0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257394 1034 "\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257394 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257394 1111 #uuid "45a4cfcd-2df2-5469-bc80-8639d0f34a61" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257395 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257396 13194139533864 true] [17592186257395 1089 "q-120033" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257395 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257395 1100 "Find the exact value of $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257395 1113 #uuid "766de5e3-4cf3-5599-8f12-d24d0a317252" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257395 1145 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257396 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257397 13194139533864 true] [17592186257396 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257396 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257396 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257397 13194139533864 true] [17592186257396 1110 #uuid "ff46dd56-42d2-5fb6-b021-9f93b364efd6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257397 1034 "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257397 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257397 1111 #uuid "04478505-52f9-5abc-a41b-d38c1d9667ae" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257398 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257399 13194139533864 true] [17592186257398 1089 "q-120036" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257398 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257398 1100 "Find the exact value of ${(\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})-\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}))}^{2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257398 1113 #uuid "8d593e11-f74c-57da-a9e4-9637db488d53" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257398 1145 "First, we expand the parenthesis and use the Pythagorean identity.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})-\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}))}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}({165}^{∘})-2\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘})+{cos}^{2}({165}^{∘}) \\\\  & =[{sin}^{2}({165}^{∘})+{cos}^{2}({165}^{∘})]-2\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1-2\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={165}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot {165}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}({330}^{∘}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n1-2\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘})=1-(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{(\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})+\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}))}^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257399 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257400 13194139533864 true] [17592186257399 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257399 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257399 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257400 13194139533864 true] [17592186257399 1110 #uuid "df3ccf03-d2f1-511e-9dd1-7c82faa09dd6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257400 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257400 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257400 1111 #uuid "c946f316-e0f1-528f-b04f-487fc62bc3a1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257401 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257402 13194139533864 true] [17592186257401 1089 "q-120087" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257401 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257401 1100 "$10\\operatorname{sin}({75}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({75}^{∘})\\operatorname{csc}({150}^{∘})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257401 1113 #uuid "72bde4a1-d04b-50b4-bd0e-183a00744709" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257401 1145 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={75}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}({75}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({75}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot {75}^{∘})=\\operatorname{sin}({150}^{∘})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\operatorname{sin}({75}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({75}^{∘})\\operatorname{csc}({150}^{∘}) & =5\\cdot [2\\operatorname{sin}({75}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({75}^{∘})]\\operatorname{csc}({150}^{∘}) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}({150}^{∘})\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}({150}^{∘})} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257402 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257403 13194139533864 true] [17592186257402 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257402 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257402 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257403 13194139533864 true] [17592186257402 1110 #uuid "127575f2-8f92-59b9-afdf-8307dff9b6e4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257403 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257403 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257403 1111 #uuid "88ed0792-cb18-51e5-a9a3-54d22711fc17" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257404 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257405 13194139533864 true] [17592186257404 1089 "q-120094" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257404 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257404 1100 "Find the exact value of $\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257404 1113 #uuid "07ab80ff-a6ba-5457-8088-8c6b6073aa0b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257404 1145 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})=\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{16})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})=\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{8}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$, we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} & =\\frac{4}{2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257405 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257406 13194139533864 true] [17592186257405 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257405 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257405 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257406 13194139533864 true] [17592186257405 1110 #uuid "703becf2-8ebd-59db-8678-340435930cf0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257406 1034 "4\\sqrt{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257406 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257406 1111 #uuid "6cf796a4-4c3a-534e-b51a-7e3a28c8b17f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257407 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257408 13194139533864 true] [17592186257407 1089 "q-120211" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257407 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257407 1100 "Some drinks of different sizes and flavors were sold during a school party. The two-way frequency table below summarizes the sold drinks.\n\n|  | Soda | Water | Coca-Cola | Fanta | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Small | $6$ | $7$ | $9$ | $8$ | $30$ |\n| Large | $5$ | $1$ | $7$ | $7$ | $20$ |\n| Totals | $11$ | $8$ | $16$ | $15$ | $50$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly selected sold drink is a small Fanta.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257407 1113 #uuid "02046115-abbf-5f23-b026-4fef673ef48b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257407 1145 "To estimate the probability that a randomly selected drink is a small Fanta, we\n\n- find the number at the intersection of the row **\"Small\"** and the column **\"Fanta\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe number at the intersection of the row **\"Small\"** and the column **\"Fanta\"** is highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Soda | Water | Coca-Cola | Fanta | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Small | $6$ | $7$ | $9$ | $8$ | $30$ |\n| Large | $5$ | $1$ | $7$ | $7$ | $20$ |\n| Totals | $11$ | $8$ | $16$ | $15$ | $50$ |\n\nDividing by the total number of data points in the table $(50),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{8}{50}=0.16.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257408 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257409 13194139533864 true] [17592186257408 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257408 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257408 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257409 13194139533864 true] [17592186257408 1110 #uuid "ea8f1de7-1eee-58e2-b14a-6b1290a784d1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257409 1034 "0.16" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257409 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257409 1111 #uuid "2404a8fb-8cf7-5292-8507-88750527799b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257410 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257411 13194139533864 true] [17592186257410 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257413 13194139533864 true] [17592186257410 1089 "q-120300" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257410 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257410 1100 "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(0,-11).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257410 1113 #uuid "b65dbd4c-66de-5fa9-bf68-86d78bdb1d13" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257410 1145 "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-11)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{121} \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies on the negative half of $y$-axis, we have $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(11,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257411 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257412 13194139533864 true] [17592186257411 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257411 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257411 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257412 13194139533864 true] [17592186257411 1110 #uuid "bdcef4a3-0af8-5150-93fe-2db0d581e203" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257412 1034 "11" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257412 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257412 1111 #uuid "edfcc133-8b6e-5eb1-9ec7-b30dc4894296" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257413 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257414 13194139533864 true] [17592186257413 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257413 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257413 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257414 13194139533864 true] [17592186257413 1110 #uuid "7d02f2f9-f771-5b05-b5f5-789f22e55ceb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257414 1034 "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257414 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257414 1111 #uuid "9b74e706-500e-512a-b8da-4eca1cba7d44" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257415 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257416 13194139533864 true] [17592186257415 1089 "q-120875" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257415 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257415 1100 "Researchers surveyed students in School A on which superpower they would most like to have. The results of this survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Flying | Invisibility | Other | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Female | $16$ | $18$ | $12$ | $46$ |\n| Male | $28$ | $12$ | $14$ | $54$ |\n| Totals | $44$ | $30$ | $26$ | $100$ |\n\nSchool B has $300$ students. Assuming the distribution of results is the same for both schools, estimate the number of School B students who are male and would choose to fly as their superpower.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257415 1113 #uuid "2a26858a-19aa-5552-b214-8bb2060beda5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257415 1145 "To estimate the number of students who are male and would choose to fly as their superpower, we\n\n- find the number at the intersection of the row **\"Male\"** and the column **\"Flying\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points at the table.\n\nThe number at the intersection of the row **\"Male\"** and the column **\"Flying\"** is highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Flying | Invisibility | Other | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Female | $16$ | $18$ | $12$ | $46$ |\n| Male | $28$ | $12$ | $14$ | $54$ |\n| Totals | $44$ | $30$ | $26$ | $100$ |\n\nDividing this number by the total number of data points in the table $(100),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{28}{100}=0.28.\n$$\n\nThis represents the estimated probability that a randomly selected School A student is a male and chooses to fly as his superpower.\n\nFinally, to find the estimated number of School B students who are male and would choose to fly as their superpower, we multiply the total number of School B students $(300)$ by the probability above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =300\\cdot p \\\\  & =300\\cdot 0.28 \\\\  & =84\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257416 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257417 13194139533864 true] [17592186257416 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257416 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257416 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257417 13194139533864 true] [17592186257416 1110 #uuid "e8f6fb75-fc6d-5913-93b2-5261fe6c7f00" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257417 1034 "84" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257417 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257417 1111 #uuid "4500f0e5-fb34-5b1e-9aa7-f00bfd7572a9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257418 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257419 13194139533864 true] [17592186257418 1089 "q-121065" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257418 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257418 1100 "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}(2x)=\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx={a}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. What could be the value of $a?$\n\n$a =$: {{a}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257418 1113 #uuid "7be751c5-5761-5b27-a477-d52010e0eb74" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257418 1145 "The given equation is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}(2x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the above equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{30}^{∘}}{2}+\\frac{n\\cdot {180}^{∘}}{2} \\\\ x & ={15}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a=15.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257419 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257420 13194139533864 true] [17592186257419 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257419 1041 "a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257419 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257420 13194139533864 true] [17592186257419 1110 #uuid "0f5b13d7-90f7-5557-ad9b-bb080e3046c5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257420 1034 "15" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257420 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257420 1111 #uuid "8bf506eb-2b5f-58df-abc0-fd517d134b8a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257421 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257422 13194139533864 true] [17592186257421 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257424 13194139533864 true] [17592186257421 1089 "q-121381" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257421 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257421 1100 "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}.$ What points **must** lie on $y=\\sqrt{x-1}-1?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257421 1113 #uuid "c0fef01f-2213-54a9-b56d-2a6ffe24af59" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257421 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=\\sqrt{x-1}-1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the right and get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x-1}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+1,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit down and obtain the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x-1}-1.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+1,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+1,b-1).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}$, we have that $\\sqrt{a}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x-1}-1.$ Substituting $x=a+1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{a+1-1}-1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{a}-1 \\\\  & =b-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a+1,b-1)$ must lie on $y=\\sqrt{x-1}-1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257422 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257423 13194139533864 true] [17592186257422 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257422 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257422 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257423 13194139533864 true] [17592186257422 1110 #uuid "01abc940-e8fa-5b9f-9dfd-03ec9a1369b8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257423 1034 "a+1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257423 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257423 1111 #uuid "d45faf36-f6c6-595d-8807-a8261845f66e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257424 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257425 13194139533864 true] [17592186257424 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257424 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257424 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257425 13194139533864 true] [17592186257424 1110 #uuid "28235b12-ac31-57ff-961f-dd84f98b98c5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257425 1034 "b-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257425 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257425 1111 #uuid "5acff8fa-3ac3-5926-8653-c273e47dfb38" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257426 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257427 13194139533864 true] [17592186257426 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257429 13194139533864 true] [17592186257426 1089 "q-121393" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257426 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257426 1100 "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y={x}^{3}.$ What points **must** lie on $y=2(x+2)^{3}?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257426 1113 #uuid "0f1936c8-1899-5449-bbe4-ab64eec00d6a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257426 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=2(x+2)^{3},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y={x}^{3}.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the left and get the graph of $y=(x+2)^{3}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a-2,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of  $2$ parallel to the $y$ -axis and get the graph of  $y=2(x+2)^{3}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a-2,b)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(a-2,2b).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y={x}^{3}$, we have that ${a}^{3}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=2(x+2)^{3}.$ Substituting $x=a-2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2(a-2+2)^{3} \\\\  & =2{a}^{3} \\\\  & =2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a-2,2b)$ must lie on $y=2(x+2)^{3}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257427 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257428 13194139533864 true] [17592186257427 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257427 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257427 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257428 13194139533864 true] [17592186257427 1110 #uuid "917a2fd6-e5fe-5455-9f16-9e9a6915e3c8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257428 1034 "a-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257428 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257428 1111 #uuid "f7313299-63ee-5e14-86f9-1642a1e89e22" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257429 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257430 13194139533864 true] [17592186257429 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257429 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257429 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257430 13194139533864 true] [17592186257429 1110 #uuid "c9113eda-8a00-5aef-8c24-0b19868e6613" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257430 1034 "2b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257430 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257430 1111 #uuid "e949d938-cc8b-57be-904b-0e1503b60994" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257431 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257432 13194139533864 true] [17592186257431 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257434 13194139533864 true] [17592186257431 1089 "q-121431" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257431 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257431 1100 "The point  $(0,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x+2).$ What points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+2?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257431 1113 #uuid "cf204f4e-b758-5c5a-b337-1b3550b1b5b3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257431 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(0,1)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+2:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x+2).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $2$ unit to the right  and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(0+2,1)=(2,1).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,1)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(2,-(1))=(2,-1).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(2,-1)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(2,-1+2)=(2,1).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(0,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x+2)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(0+2)=1\\,⟹\\,f(2)=1.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(2)+2 \\\\  & =-(1)+2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,1)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+2.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257432 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257433 13194139533864 true] [17592186257432 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257432 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257432 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257433 13194139533864 true] [17592186257432 1110 #uuid "2c7eeb88-bccb-5e85-9269-bef59e4f4989" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257433 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257433 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257433 1111 #uuid "8a4290b4-1179-5391-8878-df28a5c914bf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257434 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257435 13194139533864 true] [17592186257434 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257434 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257434 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257435 13194139533864 true] [17592186257434 1110 #uuid "ad0cdf20-74e2-5ba8-bf91-ba3ecd430859" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257435 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257435 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257435 1111 #uuid "da777ae6-b120-55f4-8b2d-e46ca4e39c92" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257436 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257437 13194139533864 true] [17592186257436 1089 "q-121553" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257436 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257436 1100 "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{tan}2x=1$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ If the exact solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{4}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257436 1113 #uuid "0e822166-fc11-5d31-b0db-f193a2f4ca2e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257436 1145 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{4n\\pi }{8} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(1+4n)\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{9\\pi }{8}$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\frac{13\\pi }{8}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{5\\pi }{8},\\frac{9\\pi }{8},\\frac{13\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{13\\pi }{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{14\\pi }{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257437 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257438 13194139533864 true] [17592186257437 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257437 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257437 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257438 13194139533864 true] [17592186257437 1110 #uuid "df1d64ac-fbf7-5d22-828c-e26cd89a1be4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257438 1034 "2\\pi" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257438 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257438 1111 #uuid "9cbdbbd9-a40e-523f-a377-485da5ca9a1f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257439 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257440 13194139533864 true] [17592186257439 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257442 13194139533864 true] [17592186257439 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257444 13194139533864 true] [17592186257439 1089 "q-121583" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257439 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257439 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/2b09fee84a9408526d985220a79ede00b38f811f5439ec74bbfa398c542223a7.png)\n\nRelative to $∠TVU,$ what are the lengths of the opposite, adjacent, and hypotenuse, respectively?\n\nOpposite: {{opposite}}\n\nAdjacent: {{adjacent}}\n\nHypotenuse: {{hypotenuse}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257439 1113 #uuid "bda30b07-0b71-5ff3-8e7f-c4c74b56e446" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257439 1145 "Relative to $∠TVU,$ \n\n- the opposite side is $\\overset{―}{TU},$ and its length is $1,$\n\n- the adjacent side is $\\overset{―}{UV},$ and its length is $2,$ and\n\n- the hypotenuse is $\\overset{―}{TV},$ and its length is $\\sqrt{5}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257440 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257441 13194139533864 true] [17592186257440 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257440 1041 "adjacent" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257440 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257441 13194139533864 true] [17592186257440 1110 #uuid "a0f31e72-927e-5575-94be-e193af9d2229" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257441 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257441 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257441 1111 #uuid "989b3bf6-5a07-5ab4-b5d7-dbdc45571ab7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257442 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257443 13194139533864 true] [17592186257442 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257442 1041 "hypotenuse" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257442 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257443 13194139533864 true] [17592186257442 1110 #uuid "1d4a8e47-9d3c-5924-9054-1e6c41cc81fa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257443 1034 "\\sqrt5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257443 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257443 1111 #uuid "854e0318-6469-5b80-b266-bdb9813587bd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257444 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257445 13194139533864 true] [17592186257444 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257444 1041 "opposite" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257444 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257445 13194139533864 true] [17592186257444 1110 #uuid "c20bad86-5a8c-51ea-8330-f45e1fa747e7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257445 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257445 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257445 1111 #uuid "9bf89322-8a22-55aa-8e94-7f3138e8edcb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257446 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257447 13194139533864 true] [17592186257446 1089 "q-121632" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257446 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257446 1100 "$\\operatorname{sin}(\\theta +{62}^{∘})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257446 1113 #uuid "41c2beb3-8815-57ec-b27b-822e6b76aa4f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257446 1145 "We know that the cofunction of $\\operatorname{sin}\\theta $ is $\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,$ and the angle complementary to $\\theta +{62}^{∘}$ is\n\n$$\n{90}^{∘}-(\\theta +{62}^{∘})={90}^{∘}-\\theta -{62}^{∘}={28}^{∘}-\\theta .\n$$\n\n So we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(\\theta +{62}^{∘})=\\operatorname{cos}({90}^{∘}-(\\theta +{62}^{∘}))=\\operatorname{cos}({28}^{∘}-\\theta ).\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257447 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257448 13194139533864 true] [17592186257447 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257447 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257447 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257448 13194139533864 true] [17592186257447 1110 #uuid "a769566e-64d9-57b3-a1c8-d3ce68ef49f7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257448 1034 "\\operatorname{cos}({28}^{∘}-\\theta )" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257448 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257448 1111 #uuid "2b5a2742-7938-5c7f-ad68-d440c7972e83" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257449 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257449 1089 "q-121912" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257449 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257449 1100 "Consider the equation $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{3x}{2})+1=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ If the exact solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are written in ascending order, what is the numerical value of ${x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257449 1113 #uuid "18ed24b1-d375-51e8-ad2b-0848def589e7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257449 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{3x}{2})+1 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{3x}{2}) & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{3x}{2}) & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{3x}{2}) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x}{2} & =-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =-{60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{{60}^{∘}}{3}+\\frac{n\\cdot {360}^{∘}}{3} \\\\ x & =-{20}^{∘}+n\\cdot {120}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={100}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x={220}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x={340}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx={100}^{∘},{220}^{∘},{340}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{2}+{x}_{3}$ is $220+340=560.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257450 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257451 13194139533864 true] [17592186257450 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257450 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257450 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257451 13194139533864 true] [17592186257450 1110 #uuid "b9536be4-f080-5106-b857-a93e80b63cc1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257451 1034 "560" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257451 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257451 1111 #uuid "651f2754-926f-5093-8c02-3934d8e51f2a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257452 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257452 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257455 13194139533864 true] [17592186257452 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257457 13194139533864 true] [17592186257452 1089 "q-121937" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257452 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257452 1100 "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$, $b=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $c=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].$ Find $a-(b+c).$\n\nRow 1, column 1: {{r1c1}}\n\nRow 2, column 1: {{r2c1}}\n\nRow 3, column 1: {{r3c1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257452 1113 #uuid "f65102c8-6fda-5ced-885c-0d555e3ecc9f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257452 1145 "To compute $a-(b+c),$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-(b+c) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-([\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257453 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257453 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257453 1041 "r1c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257453 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257453 1110 #uuid "96feac81-6330-5421-8ed4-439f33a296e9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257454 1034 "-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257454 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257454 1111 #uuid "f5e1e700-f53a-5f31-b1e5-83e7775f2629" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257455 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257456 13194139533864 true] [17592186257455 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257455 1041 "r2c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257455 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257456 13194139533864 true] [17592186257455 1110 #uuid "5eb92b71-ff02-5352-8087-3a7d6c5372bc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257456 1034 "-7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257456 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257456 1111 #uuid "20ab074f-0118-56c4-9b44-4bafeeac1c74" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257457 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257458 13194139533864 true] [17592186257457 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257457 1041 "r3c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257457 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257458 13194139533864 true] [17592186257457 1110 #uuid "77e61a13-55e4-51b6-9867-a32cbbee6a22" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257458 1034 "-7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257458 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257458 1111 #uuid "a1a9057e-1899-5295-a74f-26c5f9dd16c0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257459 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257460 13194139533864 true] [17592186257459 1089 "q-122052" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257459 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257459 1100 "Given that $k\\ge 1$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(k+2)!}{(k-1)!}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257459 1113 #uuid "3757b1fa-bff3-5ebf-aae8-87bbb8f2ea11" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257459 1145 "First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n(k+2)!=(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)\\cdot k\\cdot (k-1)!\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel the $(k-1)!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(k+2)!}{(k-1)!} & =\\frac{(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)\\cdot k\\cdot (k-1)!}{(k-1)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)\\cdot k\\cdot \\cancel{(k-1)!}}{\\cancel{(k-1)!}} \\\\  & =k(k+1)(k+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257460 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257461 13194139533864 true] [17592186257460 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257462 13194139533864 true] [17592186257460 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257460 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257460 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257460 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257460 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257460 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257462 13194139533864 true] [17592186257460 1110 #uuid "82f048d7-bf0c-5242-b17c-f6e97018bc83" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257461 1034 "k(k+1)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257461 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257461 1111 #uuid "bca3db6d-c5c1-5f7d-92fc-cc4ceb7586cf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257462 1034 "k(k+1)(k+2)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257462 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257462 1111 #uuid "d08aad34-ac2d-5fdd-9ff8-ea9e5d1ceb98" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257463 1034 "(k+1)(k+2)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257463 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257463 1111 #uuid "da27039f-cd4a-5922-b5be-9f0c9a1ec7b4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257464 1034 "\\frac{1}{k(k+1)(k+2)}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257464 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257464 1111 #uuid "5b3a2ccb-3f1f-546f-ad54-e9b6ad017387" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257465 1034 "\\frac{1}{(k+1)(k+2)}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257465 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257465 1111 #uuid "1c6d7373-1583-55db-bb75-706c41837842" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257466 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257467 13194139533864 true] [17592186257466 1089 "q-122063" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257466 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257466 1100 "Given that $n\\ge 2$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(n-1)^{2}\\,(n-1)!}{(n-2)!}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257466 1113 #uuid "3768b9a9-d6ba-5788-9af2-24549ab90243" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257466 1145 "First, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n-1)! & =(n-1)(n-2)!\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can cancel the $(n-2)!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(n-1)^{2}\\cdot (n-1)!}{(n-2)!} & =\\frac{(n-1)^{2}\\cdot (n-1)\\cdot (n-2)!}{(n-2)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n-1)^{2}\\cdot (n-1)\\cancel{(n-2)!}}{\\cancel{(n-2)!}} \\\\  & =(n-1)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257467 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257468 13194139533864 true] [17592186257467 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257469 13194139533864 true] [17592186257467 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257470 13194139533864 true] [17592186257467 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257471 13194139533864 true] [17592186257467 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257472 13194139533864 true] [17592186257467 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257467 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257467 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257470 13194139533864 true] [17592186257467 1110 #uuid "e312be77-589a-5e40-87c8-4626331f81a9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257468 1034 "(n-1)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257468 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257468 1111 #uuid "3e209561-4b0a-557c-802f-0a5efd206604" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257469 1034 "(n-1)^{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257469 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257469 1111 #uuid "0a472835-c516-5910-805b-f7805cb08c00" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257470 1034 "(n-1)^{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257470 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257470 1111 #uuid "aa2ee58b-07ea-5f37-926f-1986ccb81d64" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257471 1034 "n(n-1)^{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257471 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257471 1111 #uuid "629819e6-148e-5cb1-adf8-947e6d73b7dc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257472 1034 "(n-1)^{2}(n-2)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257472 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257472 1111 #uuid "ed37c071-0dab-5bae-9533-39575192858a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257473 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257474 13194139533864 true] [17592186257473 1089 "q-122079" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257473 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257473 1100 "A shop sells $10$ fruit flavors and $12$ chocolate flavors of ice cream. How many choices of ice cream are there?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257473 1113 #uuid "d8e036e3-cd37-5ffb-87e6-664a7ade6560" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257473 1145 "In order to find the total number of choices, we can use the **rule of sum**:\n\n$$\n10+12=22\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $22$ choices of ice cream." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257474 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257475 13194139533864 true] [17592186257474 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257474 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257474 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257475 13194139533864 true] [17592186257474 1110 #uuid "ab2ca614-b982-5185-ba23-40e168a7197a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257475 1034 "22" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257475 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257475 1111 #uuid "c7864dc5-1f1c-5878-8a1f-a85e3b704e18" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257476 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257477 13194139533864 true] [17592186257476 1089 "q-122144" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257476 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257476 1100 "How many possible outcomes are there if a coin is flipped twice, a six-sided die is rolled three times, and a tetrahedral (four-sided) die is rolled once?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257476 1113 #uuid "bfa71c25-63e9-530b-b8d9-c3e3f8a5bfb2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257476 1145 "For the coin, there are $2$ outcomes: \"Heads\" and \"Tails\".\n\nFor the six-sided die, there is a total of $6$ outcomes: $1,2,3,4,5,6.$\n\nFor the tetrahedral die, there is a total of $4$ outcomes: $1,2,3,4.$\n\nUsing the **rule of product**, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total Possible Outcomes} & =\\text{Number of outcomes when the coin is flipped the first time} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of outcomes when the coin is flipped the second time} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of outcomes when the six-sided die is rolled the first time} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of outcomes when the six-sided die is rolled the second time} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of outcomes when the six-sided die is rolled the third time} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of outcomes when the tetrahedral die is rolled the one time} \\\\  & =2\\times 2\\times 6\\times 6\\times 6\\times 4 \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\times {6}^{3}\\times 4 \\\\  & =4\\times 216\\times 4 \\\\  & =3\\,456.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are $3\\,456$ possible outcomes." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257477 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257478 13194139533864 true] [17592186257477 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257477 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257477 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257478 13194139533864 true] [17592186257477 1110 #uuid "2fac9a33-1beb-5d7f-94e8-9a82cdb6858d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257478 1034 "3\\,456" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257478 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257478 1111 #uuid "d4240970-1c4e-5367-8994-1981ec63f032" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257479 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257480 13194139533864 true] [17592186257479 1089 "q-122146" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257479 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257479 1100 "In an ice cream shop offering twelve flavors of ice cream, half a dozen toppings, and half a dozen syrups, how many combinations are possible when selecting one flavor, one topping, and one syrup?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257479 1113 #uuid "7e6b5ed9-cae5-5916-92c5-34e0446c4eac" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257479 1145 "For the ice cream flavor, there are $12$ possible choices (a dozen).\n\nFor the toppings, there are $6$ possible choices (half a dozen).\n\nFor the syrups, there are also $6$ possible choices (half a dozen).\n\nUsing the **rule of product**, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total Possible Choices} & =\\text{Choices for the ice cream flavor} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Choices for the topping} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Choices for the syrup} \\\\  & =12\\times 6\\times 6 \\\\  & =12\\times {6}^{2} \\\\  & =432.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are $432$ possible choices." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257480 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257481 13194139533864 true] [17592186257480 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257480 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257480 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257481 13194139533864 true] [17592186257480 1110 #uuid "4c6dab44-269c-589b-89de-15a0d3593c80" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257481 1034 "432" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257481 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257481 1111 #uuid "c7fa564c-942c-5555-8647-79e6cd11d597" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257482 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257483 13194139533864 true] [17592186257482 1089 "q-12218" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257482 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257482 1100 "Find the values of $k$ for which the quadratic equation $5{x}^{2}+15x+k=0$ has one distinct real solution.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257482 1113 #uuid "3f53a0d5-edce-5e43-88e9-e77b8a039ddf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257482 1145 "The equation has one distinct real solution if its discriminant is zero. So, we need to find the values of $k$ so that the discriminant is zero.\n\nFirst, we calculate the discriminant. We have $a=5,$ $b=15,$ and $c=k,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(15)^{2}-4(5)(k) \\\\  & =225-20k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the values of $k$ such that the discriminant is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =0 \\\\ 225-20k & =0 \\\\ 20k & =225 \\\\ k & =\\frac{225}{20} \\\\ k & =\\frac{45}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation will have one distinct real solution if $k=\\frac{45}{4}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257483 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257484 13194139533864 true] [17592186257483 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257483 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257483 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257484 13194139533864 true] [17592186257483 1110 #uuid "902ad4d0-cf99-5344-b4d5-6eeec1833710" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257484 1034 "\\frac{45}{4}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257484 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257484 1111 #uuid "8524199f-2925-5235-89c3-8465d5e71310" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257485 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257486 13194139533864 true] [17592186257485 1089 "q-122326" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257485 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257485 1100 "What is the diameter of a circle if its area is $\\frac{9}{25}\\pi ?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257485 1113 #uuid "f2d62cce-a90d-5450-806d-43401c6ea8b1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257485 1145 "The formula for the area of a circle is $A=\\pi {r}^{2}$, where $r$ is the radius. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{9}{25}\\pi  & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{9}{25}\\cancel{\\pi } & =\\cancel{\\pi }{r}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diameter is\n\n$$\nd=2\\cdot r=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{6}{5}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257486 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257487 13194139533864 true] [17592186257486 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257486 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257486 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257487 13194139533864 true] [17592186257486 1110 #uuid "5974ae97-1a06-5e01-a932-830915741e4c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257487 1034 "\\frac{6}{5}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257487 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257487 1111 #uuid "112acee7-20bc-5d00-bf2d-194b800fa616" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257488 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257489 13194139533864 true] [17592186257488 1089 "q-123542" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257488 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257488 1100 "Find the value of $\\operatorname{csc}({90}^{∘}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257488 1113 #uuid "966df727-409a-5141-bd11-436c96619e19" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257488 1145 "The angle ${90}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${90}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}({90}^{∘})=1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}({90}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}({90}^{∘})}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{csc}({90}^{∘})=1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257489 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257490 13194139533864 true] [17592186257489 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257489 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257489 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257490 13194139533864 true] [17592186257489 1110 #uuid "e5d81087-399c-58da-b648-55b9f8dd1e58" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257490 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257490 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257490 1111 #uuid "bc14ec51-2320-5036-9a6b-aa529fd9a71a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257491 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257492 13194139533864 true] [17592186257491 1089 "q-123544" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257491 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257491 1100 "Find the value of $\\operatorname{csc}({0}^{∘}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf the value is undefined, enter \"Undefined\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257491 1113 #uuid "147149ab-0381-536f-a9c7-1b31461b25be" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257491 1145 "The angle ${0}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${0}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(1,0).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}({0}^{∘})=0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}({0}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}({0}^{∘})}=\\frac{1}{0}.\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $\\operatorname{csc}({0}^{∘})$ is undefined." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257492 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257493 13194139533864 true] [17592186257492 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257492 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257492 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257493 13194139533864 true] [17592186257492 1110 #uuid "68fb5449-9e08-575e-bf36-b120ccb0ea69" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257493 1034 "Undefined" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257493 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257493 1111 #uuid "fc8cdc6f-a69d-584f-b0fc-dabae251979e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257494 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257495 13194139533864 true] [17592186257494 1089 "q-123583" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257494 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257494 1100 "Find the value of $\\operatorname{tan}\\pi +\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257494 1113 #uuid "606b2a8d-a2fe-5af3-a7b5-53405c89d3ce" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257494 1145 "The angles $\\pi $ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nLet's first consider the angle $\\pi .$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\pi $ corresponds to the point $(-1,0).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}\\pi =-1.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\\pi =0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\\pi =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\pi }{\\operatorname{cos}\\pi }=\\frac{0}{-1}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\\pi +\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0+0=0.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257495 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257496 13194139533864 true] [17592186257495 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257495 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257495 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257496 13194139533864 true] [17592186257495 1110 #uuid "45720d87-6b35-533d-ae59-ce362ab3b985" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257496 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257496 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257496 1111 #uuid "a90d3cb0-00ad-5297-8b6e-e1655f932b2f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257497 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257498 13194139533864 true] [17592186257497 1089 "q-123586" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257497 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257497 1100 "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sec}0+\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257497 1113 #uuid "133173ff-502a-5d88-b605-7da6031fa128" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257497 1145 "The angles $0$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\n- Let's first consider the angle $0.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $0$ corresponds to the point $(1,0).$  Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}0=1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}0=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}0}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\n- Now, let's consider the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$  Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}0+\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1+1=2.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257498 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257499 13194139533864 true] [17592186257498 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257498 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257498 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257499 13194139533864 true] [17592186257498 1110 #uuid "a4bd8137-d637-56bb-a703-1a13466d8298" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257499 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257499 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257499 1111 #uuid "7a15362f-2b20-5913-95e9-c768b333513e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257500 1017 #edb/ref 17592186