\n\nComparing our series to the $p$-series, we have\n\n$$\np=q-2.\n$$\n\nAccording to the $p$-series test, the given series converges if\n\n$$\nq-2>1\\,⟹\\,q>3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent for all $q>3.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257797 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257798 13194139533864 true] [17592186257797 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257797 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257797 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257798 13194139533864 true] [17592186257797 1110 #uuid "51328e83-e134-5598-8043-8d926c65281a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257798 1034 "q>3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257798 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257798 1111 #uuid "6f261c3b-6f2b-5baa-98fb-0cb62c8fc8a2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257799 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257800 13194139533864 true] [17592186257799 1089 "q-125404" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257799 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257799 1100 "For which values of $q$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2-3q}}$ converge?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257799 1113 #uuid "20934715-7c64-505f-835c-e42219a4ae63" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257799 1145 "A $p$-series is any series that takes the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{p}},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. The $p$-series test states that\n\n- if $p>1,$ then the series converges, while\n\n- if $p\\le 1,$ then the series diverges.\n\nComparing our series to the $p$-series, we have\n\n$$\np=2-3q.\n$$\n\nAccording to the $p$-series test, the given series converges if\n\n$$\n2-3q>1\\,⟹\\,q<\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent for all $q<\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257800 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257801 13194139533864 true] [17592186257800 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257800 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257800 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257801 13194139533864 true] [17592186257800 1110 #uuid "b97c1c41-6d2f-53b6-8803-763609ddca4c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257801 1034 "q<\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257801 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257801 1111 #uuid "82d171de-c05b-58a0-88e7-3bdf0b6ccb39" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257802 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257803 13194139533864 true] [17592186257802 1089 "q-125550" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257802 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257802 1100 "What is a $3\\times 2$ matrix?\n\n$$\nE=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,F=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 8 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,G=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257802 1113 #uuid "11d30a39-1dc6-581f-aa48-ba6b816cb779" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257802 1145 "- The matrix $E$ has $2$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $F$ has $3$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 2.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- The matrix $G$ has $3$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, only $F$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257803 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257804 13194139533864 true] [17592186257803 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257803 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257803 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257804 13194139533864 true] [17592186257803 1110 #uuid "a38e78ed-df9c-58d1-90b8-7b6526913134" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257804 1034 "F" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257804 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257804 1111 #uuid "91cbf96f-9d5a-5942-afcf-44040736fccc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257805 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257806 13194139533864 true] [17592186257805 1089 "q-12669" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257805 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257805 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/e92694d3fc8d6be220fc4d45c0d210a53076db72daad477c3199ece5c4460627.png)\n\nIdentify the equation that generated the graph above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257805 1113 #uuid "af276f5b-4246-5be5-bc99-ae92802b70cd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257805 1145 "To plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-\\operatorname{cos}x.$\n\n- Finally, translate it by $3$ units **down** to give $y=-\\operatorname{cos}x-3$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-\\operatorname{cos}x-3.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257806 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257807 13194139533864 true] [17592186257806 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257806 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257806 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257807 13194139533864 true] [17592186257806 1110 #uuid "8aeac9b3-1f57-55e5-93b8-119ce7e31478" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257807 1034 "-\\operatorname{cos}x-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257807 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257807 1111 #uuid "15d0a0c8-61e7-511c-8ae9-44d1ee078f8a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257808 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257809 13194139533864 true] [17592186257808 1089 "q-126989" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257808 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257808 1100 "${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{n})}^{n/x}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257808 1113 #uuid "f5f988bd-3bbf-5601-9c67-d713b7d993de" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257808 1145 "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\n{e}^{u}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{u}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nHence, with $u={x}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n{e}^{{x}^{2}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{n})}^{n/x} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/x} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/x} \\\\  & ={({e}^{{x}^{2}})}^{1/x} \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}/x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257809 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257810 13194139533864 true] [17592186257809 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257809 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257809 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257810 13194139533864 true] [17592186257809 1110 #uuid "c172dac0-13f1-5511-a417-f0c192f117e4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257810 1034 "{e}^{x}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257810 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257810 1111 #uuid "704724ba-430f-55aa-a1a9-5de0b0cb597d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257811 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257812 13194139533864 true] [17592186257811 1089 "q-127614" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257811 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257811 1100 "If\n$D=[\\begin{aligned}2x & 6 \\\\ 2x & x-2\\end{aligned}]$\nand $det(D)=-32$, what is the value of $x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257811 1113 #uuid "4b0686b1-334e-545d-8566-76f487f19d14" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257811 1145 "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}|=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =|\\begin{aligned}2x & 6 \\\\ 2x & x-2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2x\\cdot (x-2)-6\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-16x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=-32$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =-32 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-16x & =-32 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-16x+32 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+16 & =0 \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257812 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257813 13194139533864 true] [17592186257812 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257812 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257812 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257813 13194139533864 true] [17592186257812 1110 #uuid "71885dd8-5771-5616-abe8-685fda47e1b8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257813 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257813 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257813 1111 #uuid "18c6267b-b6e1-588e-a95e-c1c0f10bdcac" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257814 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257815 13194139533864 true] [17592186257814 1089 "q-127701" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257814 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257814 1100 "Calculate the minor of the entry ${p}_{32}$ in the matrix\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 8 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 10 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257814 1113 #uuid "f7eee920-abd1-5e92-872a-5f2d162a508d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257814 1145 "The minor of ${p}_{32}=3$ (third row, second column) is the determinant of the remaining entries when the column and row containing $3$ are removed: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}8 & ∗ & 4 \\\\ -5 & ∗ & 6 \\\\ ∗ & 3 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${p}_{32}=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =8\\cdot 6-4\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =48+20 \\\\  & =68.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257815 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257816 13194139533864 true] [17592186257815 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257815 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257815 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257816 13194139533864 true] [17592186257815 1110 #uuid "0f71b00e-de6c-5483-9764-a413e5bd29b4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257816 1034 "68" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257816 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257816 1111 #uuid "cedb97a5-a477-5288-a35c-c5df85b5d92d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257817 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257818 13194139533864 true] [17592186257817 1089 "q-129471" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257817 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257817 1100 "$\\sqrt{\\frac{{z}^{2}}{25{y}^{2}}}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257817 1113 #uuid "64bdd06f-725c-53d8-b1bd-371eda2884d9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257817 1145 "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that ${z}^{2},25,$ and ${y}^{2}$ are all perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{{z}^{2}}{25{y}^{2}}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{5}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{z\\cdot z}{5\\cdot 5\\cdot y\\cdot y}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{z\\cdot z}{5\\cdot y\\cdot 5\\cdot y}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{z}{5y}\\cdot \\frac{z}{5y}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{z}{5y})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{z}{5y})}^{2}} & =|\\frac{z}{5y}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|z|}{|5y|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|z|}{|5|\\cdot |y|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|z|}{5|y|}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257818 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257819 13194139533864 true] [17592186257818 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257818 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257818 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257819 13194139533864 true] [17592186257818 1110 #uuid "27e3ae06-b55b-542f-b8fe-0458d29c6d2e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257819 1034 "\\frac{|z|}{5|y|}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257819 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257819 1111 #uuid "56097d18-99a7-5c64-b9ca-26271383889b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257820 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257821 13194139533864 true] [17592186257820 1089 "q-1300" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257820 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257820 1100 "If $\\overset{\\to }{u}=⟨-3,1⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{v}=⟨4,6⟩$, then $\\overset{\\to }{u}+\\overset{\\to }{v}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257820 1113 #uuid "24b4dd48-333d-5e25-aa2e-651c9caeda5d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257820 1145 "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{u}+\\overset{\\to }{v} & =⟨-3,1⟩+⟨4,6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3+4,1+6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257821 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257822 13194139533864 true] [17592186257821 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257821 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257821 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257822 13194139533864 true] [17592186257821 1110 #uuid "367b4bb7-ded0-5ab1-83e5-e805999abd93" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257822 1034 "⟨1,7⟩" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257822 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257822 1111 #uuid "40504147-3868-54b4-8ba8-035ff5ce253e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257823 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257824 13194139533864 true] [17592186257823 1089 "q-1301" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257823 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257823 1100 "What is the sum of $\\overset{\\to }{A}=⟨10,5⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{B}=⟨8,-2⟩?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257823 1113 #uuid "43d779a0-07d6-5690-a96a-eab399e84e6a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257823 1145 "To compute $\\overset{\\to }{A}+\\overset{\\to }{B},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{A}+\\overset{\\to }{B} & =⟨10,5⟩+⟨8,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨18,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257824 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257825 13194139533864 true] [17592186257824 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257824 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257824 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257825 13194139533864 true] [17592186257824 1110 #uuid "d1246d23-8c2f-5ec3-b192-4d6d686e3caf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257825 1034 "⟨18,3⟩" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257825 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257825 1111 #uuid "247624a7-df97-533a-a6bd-bacaa26fd01a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257826 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257827 13194139533864 true] [17592186257826 1089 "q-130520" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257826 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257826 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/b9c13f97b40699c11e5b3f4f1c77457177cf83418ce08d7a30420117b816f687.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ is given by the graph above. Calculate $f(-1)+f(3).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257826 1113 #uuid "7c33c986-dc79-5316-96bc-a5c56e151eff" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257826 1145 "We can find the necessary function values using the graph.\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- When $x=-1,$ we have $y=0.$ So, $f(-1)=0.$\n\n- When $x=3,$ we have $y=-2.$ So, $f(3)=-2.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1)+f(3)=0+(-2)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257827 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257828 13194139533864 true] [17592186257827 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257827 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257827 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257828 13194139533864 true] [17592186257827 1110 #uuid "438f7e7f-aba9-5f26-8a88-a8e64341593f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257828 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257828 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257828 1111 #uuid "7eddc1dd-c230-5bc7-af48-2e6fe476b329" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257829 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257830 13194139533864 true] [17592186257829 1089 "q-130569" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257829 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257829 1100 "Evaluate $f(2)$, where $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+7}+{4}^{x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257829 1113 #uuid "13175d1d-ea3b-53e4-8a6a-5d9e08d8c7ad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257829 1145 "We substitute $x=2$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x+7}+{4}^{x} \\\\ f(2) & =\\sqrt{2+7}+{4}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9}+16 \\\\  & =3+16 \\\\  & =19\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257830 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257831 13194139533864 true] [17592186257830 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257830 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257830 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257831 13194139533864 true] [17592186257830 1110 #uuid "d7960ed4-5ae5-5275-b08e-12ab6f8ca413" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257831 1034 "19" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257831 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257831 1111 #uuid "9b977c7e-eb1a-5dec-87f2-788ad6289b79" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257832 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257833 13194139533864 true] [17592186257832 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257835 13194139533864 true] [17592186257832 1089 "q-131072" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257832 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257832 1100 "Solve $\\frac{\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12}}{2}-2=0.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257832 1113 #uuid "b2810621-4a9f-5666-b439-842c3d34a484" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257832 1145 "First, we isolate the term $\\frac{\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12}}{2}$ by adding $2$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12}}{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12}}{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we isolate the square root by multiplying by $2$ on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12}}{2} & =2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12} & =4 \\\\ (\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12})^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ 3{v}^{2}-4v+12 & =16 \\\\ 3{v}^{2}-4v-4 & =0 \\\\ (3v+2)(v-2) & =0 \\\\ v & =-\\frac{2}{3},\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n- For $v=-\\frac{2}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}-4(-\\frac{2}{3})+12}}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}+12}}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{16}}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 2-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $v=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3({2}^{2})-4(2)+12}}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{12-8+12}}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{16}}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 2-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth $v=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $v=2$ satisfy the original equation, so we can accept them both as solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $\\frac{\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12}}{2}-2=0$ are $v=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $v=2.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257833 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257834 13194139533864 true] [17592186257833 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257833 1041 "root-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257833 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257834 13194139533864 true] [17592186257833 1110 #uuid "c623e0f0-67a4-5542-ad44-fac37c1b8f0c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257834 1034 "-\\frac23" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257834 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257834 1111 #uuid "9ce6f026-571d-5730-a17f-97642ece7d60" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257835 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257836 13194139533864 true] [17592186257835 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257835 1041 "root-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257835 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257836 13194139533864 true] [17592186257835 1110 #uuid "a28b87fc-978b-5edd-9ffa-5aa40ef27c4a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257836 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257836 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257836 1111 #uuid "57fdd752-b18e-5774-9009-153f8666ff8f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257837 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257838 13194139533864 true] [17592186257837 1089 "q-131287" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257837 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257837 1100 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257837 1113 #uuid "3d19938b-2667-5667-8ce7-7a27acfa3c8e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257837 1145 "From the graph, we see that as we approach $x=2$ from the left, the $y$-value approaches $0.$ So the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $x=2$ from the right, the $y$-value again approaches $0.$ So the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $0,$ we conclude that\n\n \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)=0.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257838 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257839 13194139533864 true] [17592186257838 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257838 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257838 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257839 13194139533864 true] [17592186257838 1110 #uuid "994bc45e-12a8-51c1-a1ea-6029cb1bf4be" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257839 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257839 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257839 1111 #uuid "b50f64b3-2e2a-557c-bffc-304beb447550" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257840 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257841 13194139533864 true] [17592186257840 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257843 13194139533864 true] [17592186257840 1089 "q-131292" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257840 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257840 1100 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257840 1113 #uuid "2ff2b93a-a0bc-53af-ab2c-ee40e773dd3a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257840 1145 "As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function $g(x)$ approaches $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the function $g(x)$ approaches $0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257841 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257842 13194139533864 true] [17592186257841 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257841 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257841 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257842 13194139533864 true] [17592186257841 1110 #uuid "27dad36a-52c3-50b4-a3c5-7ee3df9f5369" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257842 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257842 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257842 1111 #uuid "e334ffef-75e9-5487-9f3a-77355634a401" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257843 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257844 13194139533864 true] [17592186257843 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257843 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257843 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257844 13194139533864 true] [17592186257843 1110 #uuid "8f502a90-720e-5765-9320-6a3936f57944" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257844 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257844 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257844 1111 #uuid "c598337d-157e-5bbe-8042-0760485016e9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257845 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257846 13194139533864 true] [17592186257845 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257848 13194139533864 true] [17592186257845 1089 "q-131293" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257845 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257845 1100 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257845 1113 #uuid "4561523f-428e-5eae-b015-b8c44c4fd383" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257845 1145 "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty ,$ the graph of the function $g(x)$ moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=4.$\n\nConsequently, $g(x)$ approaches the value $4$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=4.\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$ the graph of the function grows without bound to $\\infty $ as well. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257846 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257847 13194139533864 true] [17592186257846 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257846 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257846 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257847 13194139533864 true] [17592186257846 1110 #uuid "bea32915-546d-54ed-99cc-98c0d5b089df" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257847 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257847 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257847 1111 #uuid "08dffbcc-a431-5f43-ba88-17d3f5929f82" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257848 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257849 13194139533864 true] [17592186257848 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257848 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257848 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257849 13194139533864 true] [17592186257848 1110 #uuid "0d428796-dbd3-5a4b-93fb-06bdd0b671ac" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257849 1034 "\\infty " 13194139533864 true] [17592186257849 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257849 1111 #uuid "808f1015-6891-5a09-93ab-404c85659190" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257850 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257851 13194139533864 true] [17592186257850 1089 "q-131295" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257850 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257850 1100 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(2f(x)-3g(x)+{x}^{2}).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(2f(x)-3g(x)+{x}^{2})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257850 1113 #uuid "623629e1-214d-5c86-b00a-3914400f8f18" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257850 1145 "According to the sum and constant multiple rules, we can compute the required limit as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(2f(x)-3g(x)+{x}^{2})=2{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)-3{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)+{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe above equation assumes that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)$ both exist.\n\nIn part 5, we found that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=2,$ and from part 3, we found ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(2f(x)-3g(x)+{x}^{2}) & =2{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)-3{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)+{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 0+{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4-0+4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257851 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257852 13194139533864 true] [17592186257851 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257851 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257851 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257852 13194139533864 true] [17592186257851 1110 #uuid "ae9fd745-e806-5ad6-895e-42e00c4eb393" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257852 1034 "8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257852 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257852 1111 #uuid "c581f746-f4b4-565e-828e-9a9c760c5c80" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257853 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257854 13194139533864 true] [17592186257853 1089 "q-131296" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257853 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257853 1100 "Given that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}Cf(x)=6,$ where $C$ is a real constant, what is the value of $C?$\n\n$C=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257853 1113 #uuid "ad6f060b-5cea-5986-996d-da86ba31d49c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257853 1145 "From the graph, we see ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=2.$\n\nWe factor the constant out of the limit and evaluate the remaining limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}Cf(x) & =6 \\\\ C\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x) & =6 \\\\ C\\cdot 2 & =6 \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257854 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257855 13194139533864 true] [17592186257854 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257854 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257854 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257855 13194139533864 true] [17592186257854 1110 #uuid "81345881-d6c3-5516-a270-9dc397aaefa6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257855 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257855 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257855 1111 #uuid "d315cf83-135e-579e-a342-0047cef82766" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257856 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257857 13194139533864 true] [17592186257856 1089 "q-131299" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257856 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257856 1100 "What are the points of discontinuity of $f(x)?$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257856 1113 #uuid "40aecb4c-2e61-5d25-88a2-3f0a2a1cdd82" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257856 1145 "The point of discontinuity of $f(x)$ is $x=2.$ It is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of this point without taking our pencil off of the page." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257857 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257858 13194139533864 true] [17592186257857 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257857 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257857 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257858 13194139533864 true] [17592186257857 1110 #uuid "111b6e45-7881-53ea-9f81-b27ffe1c218f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257858 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257858 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257858 1111 #uuid "345f47e2-55a9-53ee-b1e4-65537b163cf4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257859 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257860 13194139533864 true] [17592186257859 1089 "q-131320" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257859 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257859 1100 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257859 1113 #uuid "e174c22e-176b-564d-874b-bf6323795bef" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257859 1145 "In part 1, we showed that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x),$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257860 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257861 13194139533864 true] [17592186257860 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257860 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257860 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257861 13194139533864 true] [17592186257860 1110 #uuid "8232e659-27a5-507f-bd15-b8a46dc6e6cb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257861 1034 "DNE" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257861 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257861 1111 #uuid "8468ab8a-35f5-5465-92dd-649276399934" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257862 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257863 13194139533864 true] [17592186257862 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257865 13194139533864 true] [17592186257862 1089 "q-131334" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257862 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257862 1100 "**In ascending order**, what are the points of discontinuity of $g(x)?$\n\n$g(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257862 1113 #uuid "b49288e9-8609-57aa-a4ab-46cd78301ba3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257862 1145 "The points of discontinuity of $g(x)$ are $x=-1$ and $x=2.$ It is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of these points without taking our pencil off of the page." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257863 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257864 13194139533864 true] [17592186257863 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257863 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257863 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257864 13194139533864 true] [17592186257863 1110 #uuid "f7438fd0-3b41-5961-8782-b5d7f5ecf71d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257864 1034 "-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257864 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257864 1111 #uuid "b34b8521-9fd5-518c-8ea6-3f5b18e43f35" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257865 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257866 13194139533864 true] [17592186257865 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257865 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257865 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257866 13194139533864 true] [17592186257865 1110 #uuid "7ee8d153-f1d7-52a2-bfdf-7f602f427519" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257866 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257866 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257866 1111 #uuid "99802942-acec-5b70-82d3-4643460a87e3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257867 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257868 13194139533864 true] [17592186257867 1089 "q-1324" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257867 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257867 1100 "If $\\overset{\\to }{U}=⟨1,3⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{V}=⟨6,3⟩$, then $\\overset{\\to }{U}+\\overset{\\to }{V}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257867 1113 #uuid "97a85d1a-b43c-5d17-b735-55bbb45a8cd6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257867 1145 "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{U}+\\overset{\\to }{V} & =⟨1,3⟩+⟨6,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+6,3+3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨7,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257868 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257869 13194139533864 true] [17592186257868 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257868 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257868 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257869 13194139533864 true] [17592186257868 1110 #uuid "3d9000c4-d90d-5ca4-9f3f-708690172b37" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257869 1034 "⟨7,6⟩" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257869 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257869 1111 #uuid "9f071f55-37a1-5cf3-a329-6c9003e2b725" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257870 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257871 13194139533864 true] [17592186257870 1089 "q-133024" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257870 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257870 1100 "Find the value of $f(x)$ at $x=4.$\n\n$f(4)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257870 1113 #uuid "e6095b63-ddc6-568b-9380-729c9ade9f90" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257870 1145 "We substitute $4$ for $x$ in the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =4\\sqrt{x}-2 \\\\ f(4) & =4\\sqrt{4}-2 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2-2 \\\\  & =8-2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257871 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257872 13194139533864 true] [17592186257871 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257871 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257871 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257872 13194139533864 true] [17592186257871 1110 #uuid "948fdae7-05ca-5624-a16e-022fb33b1775" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257872 1034 "6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257872 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257872 1111 #uuid "513a423d-a88f-5e18-9d15-f24ca8887917" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257873 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257874 13194139533864 true] [17592186257873 1089 "q-133025" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257873 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257873 1100 "$h(4)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257873 1113 #uuid "f1754b32-47e1-5590-8a91-bbc36a0323fa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257873 1145 "Since $x=4$ is in the interval $x>2,$ we use the second branch of the piecewise function, namely $h(x)=5-x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(4) & =5-4 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257874 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257875 13194139533864 true] [17592186257874 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257874 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257874 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257875 13194139533864 true] [17592186257874 1110 #uuid "72b4136f-4387-5525-beff-16f19e2b0713" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257875 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257875 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257875 1111 #uuid "238dbe8d-4db0-537e-aa65-e66cdd944e03" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257876 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257877 13194139533864 true] [17592186257876 1089 "q-133026" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257876 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257876 1100 "What is the root of the function $g(x)?$\n\nThe root is at $x\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257876 1113 #uuid "6c8691c2-0337-5d26-bf63-fbc25bd0f7e7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257876 1145 "The roots are the solutions to the equation $g(x)=0,$ which we solve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =0 \\\\ 3x-1 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =1 \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has the single root $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257877 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257878 13194139533864 true] [17592186257877 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257877 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257877 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257878 13194139533864 true] [17592186257877 1110 #uuid "fe208381-c8c6-5813-a86d-b35b92287858" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257878 1034 "1/3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257878 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257878 1111 #uuid "80f8910e-5b03-5984-9910-47019bc5d244" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257879 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257880 13194139533864 true] [17592186257879 1089 "q-133027" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257879 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257879 1100 "Using the graph of $y=h(x)$ on the interval $[-1,6],$ find the global minimum value of $h(x)$ on this interval.\n\n${h}_{min}\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257879 1113 #uuid "84eeaa13-d68d-5cfc-8888-807b5ced7f65" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257879 1145 "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n* The global minimum of a function is the smallest value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n* A global minimum point of a function is a point $(x,y)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $h(x)=-2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/bc25d9452e02cfd80ae586368897ef3d917ab9bc5c8e7ea1b74c154f1d4ebb19.png)\n\nTherefore, the global minimum is ${h}_{min}=-2.$ The function reaches this value when $x=1,$ so the global minimum point is $(1,-2).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257880 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257881 13194139533864 true] [17592186257880 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257880 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257880 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257881 13194139533864 true] [17592186257880 1110 #uuid "57a6ec67-c027-538e-9233-46de151c33f5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257881 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257881 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257881 1111 #uuid "93e5b92f-ac10-506e-a57a-e16a167dbe6b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257882 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257883 13194139533864 true] [17592186257882 1089 "q-133028" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257882 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257882 1100 "Write the expression for the function $(f+g)(x)$ in terms of $x.$\n\n$(f+g)(x)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257882 1113 #uuid "06310d36-bd4d-5d43-a430-ab4207ec0de1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257882 1145 "We find $(f+g)(x)$ by adding the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(x) & =(4\\sqrt{x}-2)+(3x-1) \\\\  & =3x+4\\sqrt{x}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257883 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257884 13194139533864 true] [17592186257883 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257883 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257883 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257884 13194139533864 true] [17592186257883 1110 #uuid "b2f1c264-55ce-5555-ba73-15e60bb032a2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257884 1034 "3x+4\\sqrt{x}-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257884 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257884 1111 #uuid "35983dd7-a6f6-5f7e-892d-32e5bb3fc827" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257885 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257886 13194139533864 true] [17592186257885 1089 "q-133029" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257885 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257885 1100 "Find the value of $(f/h)(4).$\n\n$(f/h)(4)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257885 1113 #uuid "f6b2a0d8-4028-5a47-a42c-61c14cfc6c36" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257885 1145 "The notation $(f/h)(x)$ means the quotient of the functions $f(x)$ and $h(x).$ \n\nTo evaluate $(f/h)(4),$ we need to divide $f(4)$ by $h(4).$\n\nEarlier, we found the following values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =6 \\\\ h(4) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n(f/h)(4)=\\frac{f(4)}{h(4)}=\\frac{6}{1}=6.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257886 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257887 13194139533864 true] [17592186257886 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257886 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257886 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257887 13194139533864 true] [17592186257886 1110 #uuid "6df0ed28-1b60-5812-9a56-ad326420afaa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257887 1034 "6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257887 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257887 1111 #uuid "d332894f-e277-5c10-8905-e605f713fc58" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257888 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257889 13194139533864 true] [17592186257888 1089 "q-133030" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257888 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257888 1100 "$(f∘h)(4)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257888 1113 #uuid "d2f3209b-4bde-5973-b485-cd4659645aeb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257888 1145 "The expression $(f∘h)(4)$ is equivalent to $f(h(4)).$ \n\nPreviously, we found that \n\n$$\nh(4)=1.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=1$ into $f(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(h(4)) & =f(1) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{1}-2 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257889 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257890 13194139533864 true] [17592186257889 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257889 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257889 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257890 13194139533864 true] [17592186257889 1110 #uuid "731f05d4-c4f7-5886-9532-afcb439d53c7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257890 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257890 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257890 1111 #uuid "945b8d50-f440-5780-8ea7-c9e76f811169" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257891 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257892 13194139533864 true] [17592186257891 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257894 13194139533864 true] [17592186257891 1089 "q-133031" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257891 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257891 1100 "Consider the function $g(x)$ for $x\\in [-1,1].$ What is its range over the given domain?\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\le \\,g(x)\\,\\le $\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257891 1113 #uuid "2339506b-6632-5223-9e01-443389461a63" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257891 1145 "First, we graph the function $g(x)=3x-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/819903d614018e7945117ddd14b26cf669777c1d4f3742e095b1cebc212bf879.png)\n\nThen, we find the lowest and highest $y$-values in the graph over the domain $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n* The lowest $y$ value is $-4.$ This occurs at the point $(-1,-4).$\n\n* The highest $y$ value is $2.$ This occurs at the point $(1,2).$\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-4$ and $2,$ including the endpoints.\n\nTherefore, the range is $-4\\le y\\le 2,$ which can also be written as \n\n$$\n-4\\le g(x)\\le 2.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257892 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257893 13194139533864 true] [17592186257892 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257892 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257892 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257893 13194139533864 true] [17592186257892 1110 #uuid "06a77d84-2e97-5cb1-b50c-242f92a052c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257893 1034 "-4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257893 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257893 1111 #uuid "96ef80c8-2db1-5351-9296-57af895684bd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257894 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257895 13194139533864 true] [17592186257894 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257894 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257894 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257895 13194139533864 true] [17592186257894 1110 #uuid "ca89e50d-c4a3-56d4-a2ff-cc57d8270589" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257895 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257895 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257895 1111 #uuid "8c191908-eb0e-5337-9407-0cca774ce695" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257896 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257897 13194139533864 true] [17592186257896 1089 "q-133032" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257896 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257896 1100 "Suppose that $r(x)=\\frac{1}{3}x+k$ is the inverse of $g(x).$ What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257896 1113 #uuid "6672f6f6-d069-5e08-8c51-22e371f34dc7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257896 1145 "If $r$ is the inverse of $g,$ then \n\n$$\nr(g(x))=x.\n$$\n\nTo find $r(g(x)),$ we write down the function $r$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $g(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}x+k \\\\ r(g(x)) & =\\frac{1}{3}g(x)+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $g(x)=3x-1$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(g(x)) & =\\frac{1}{3}g(x)+k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(3x-1)+k \\\\  & =x-\\frac{1}{3}+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we know that $r(g(x))=x.$ Equating the expression above with $x$ and solving for $k$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(g(x)) & =x \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{3}+k & =x \\\\ \\cancel{x}-\\frac{1}{3}+k & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}+k & =0 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257897 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257898 13194139533864 true] [17592186257897 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257897 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257897 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257898 13194139533864 true] [17592186257897 1110 #uuid "4b320bbe-c8b3-5d1b-b588-89fe83588f77" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257898 1034 "1/3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257898 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257898 1111 #uuid "29abe2ec-1d6d-5b36-9243-f4f5463a1ce6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257899 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257900 13194139533864 true] [17592186257899 1089 "q-133092" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257899 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257899 1100 "Find the value of $f(x)$ at $x=4.$\n\n$f(4)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257899 1113 #uuid "e4dc0446-bdab-566c-a1ea-d739465a89a6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257899 1145 "We substitute $4$ for $x$ in the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3\\sqrt{x+1} \\\\ f(4) & =3\\sqrt{4+1} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257900 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257901 13194139533864 true] [17592186257900 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257900 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257900 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257901 13194139533864 true] [17592186257900 1110 #uuid "3edf62ed-a7f4-5c27-aa57-65f9839c59ba" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257901 1034 "3\\sqrt{5}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257901 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257901 1111 #uuid "1495831c-3fef-5728-be92-747772130cf0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257902 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257903 13194139533864 true] [17592186257902 1089 "q-133093" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257902 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257902 1100 "$h(4)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257902 1113 #uuid "4facbf96-c4c8-5f38-a25b-4b60a9204fda" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257902 1145 "Since $x=4$ is in the interval $x>3,$ we use the second branch of the piecewise function, namely $h(x)=2x-5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(4) & =2(4)-5 \\\\  & =8-5 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257903 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257904 13194139533864 true] [17592186257903 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257903 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257903 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257904 13194139533864 true] [17592186257903 1110 #uuid "d72cf376-ab9d-5294-aa38-2dbf171dad01" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257904 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257904 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257904 1111 #uuid "9875c946-efae-5e29-b405-94de8fc3b770" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257905 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257906 13194139533864 true] [17592186257905 1089 "q-133094" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257905 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257905 1100 "What is the root of the function $g(x)?$\n\nThe root is at $x\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257905 1113 #uuid "dac5550f-7f7a-5ad7-9089-a64688784e6f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257905 1145 "The roots are the solutions to the equation $g(x)=0,$ which we solve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =0 \\\\ 3x-2 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =2 \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has the single root $x=\\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257906 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257907 13194139533864 true] [17592186257906 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257906 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257906 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257907 13194139533864 true] [17592186257906 1110 #uuid "b3867252-3213-5383-8a8b-466b65ee7411" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257907 1034 "\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257907 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257907 1111 #uuid "8de2c845-8e92-58ae-94f4-adea8d283336" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257908 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257909 13194139533864 true] [17592186257908 1089 "q-133095" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257908 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257908 1100 "Using the graph of $y=h(x)$ on the interval $[-1,5],$ find the global minimum value of $h(x)$ on this interval.\n\n${h}_{min}\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257908 1113 #uuid "1d26441c-ac3b-59d3-b427-084701de0ed6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257908 1145 "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n* The global minimum of a function is the smallest value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n* A global minimum point of a function is a point $(x,y)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $h(x)=-4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/f76f7203f1ba17f8148bbc1140420034a631f46e6995a3bda5453ec3fd9a6f55.png)\n\nTherefore, the global minimum is ${h}_{min}=-4.$ The function reaches this value when $x=1,$ so the global minimum point is $(1,-4).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257909 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257910 13194139533864 true] [17592186257909 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257909 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257909 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257910 13194139533864 true] [17592186257909 1110 #uuid "4123edc7-00ac-585c-92fc-0df39a14aa4e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257910 1034 "-4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257910 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257910 1111 #uuid "583857a8-0144-5997-9ea1-7321299750da" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257911 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257912 13194139533864 true] [17592186257911 1089 "q-133096" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257911 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257911 1100 "Write the expression for the function $(f+g)(x)$ in terms of $x.$\n\n$(f+g)(x)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257911 1113 #uuid "722596b3-9815-59b7-8c7b-48c557696a4b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257911 1145 "We find $(f+g)(x)$ by adding the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(x) & =(3\\sqrt{x+1})+(3x-2) \\\\  & =3x+3\\sqrt{x+1}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257912 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257913 13194139533864 true] [17592186257912 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257912 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257912 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257913 13194139533864 true] [17592186257912 1110 #uuid "90d22b92-cbdd-578f-962c-a6f0554738bc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257913 1034 "3x+3\\sqrt{x+1}-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257913 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257913 1111 #uuid "ff8acee5-720e-5a47-8fb9-ed105708b61c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257914 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257915 13194139533864 true] [17592186257914 1089 "q-133097" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257914 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257914 1100 "Find the value of $(f/h)(4).$\n\n$(f/h)(4)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257914 1113 #uuid "94dd89e7-71bf-5758-b5b6-2bb250a0931a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257914 1145 "The notation $(f/h)(x)$ means the quotient of the functions $f(x)$ and $h(x).$ \n\nTo evaluate $(f/h)(4),$ we need to divide $f(4)$ by $h(4).$\n\nEarlier, we found the following values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =3\\sqrt{5} \\\\ h(4) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n(f/h)(4)=\\frac{f(4)}{h(4)}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{3}=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257915 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257916 13194139533864 true] [17592186257915 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257915 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257915 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257916 13194139533864 true] [17592186257915 1110 #uuid "ba7eefcc-e5e0-599e-a5d6-7a86d1ced9d4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257916 1034 "\\sqrt{5}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257916 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257916 1111 #uuid "dd0a4d20-c266-5180-8d8c-aa495464977f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257917 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257918 13194139533864 true] [17592186257917 1089 "q-133098" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257917 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257917 1100 "$(f∘h)(4)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257917 1113 #uuid "db7e3081-13ac-5bac-97f6-3cdfeabac772" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257917 1145 "The expression $(f∘h)(4)$ is equivalent to $f(h(4)).$\n\nPreviously, we found that \n\n$$\nh(4)=3.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=3$ into $f(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(h(4)) & =f(3) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3+1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257918 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257919 13194139533864 true] [17592186257918 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257918 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257918 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257919 13194139533864 true] [17592186257918 1110 #uuid "1ba2c0da-5e9e-5cee-afa1-b64d8b64f6c9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257919 1034 "6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257919 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257919 1111 #uuid "2ec85d45-705f-58f2-9ecb-beeb96887d3c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257920 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257921 13194139533864 true] [17592186257920 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257923 13194139533864 true] [17592186257920 1089 "q-133099" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257920 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257920 1100 "Consider the function $g(x)$ for $x\\in [0,2].$ What is its range over the given domain?\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\le \\,g(x)\\,\\le $\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257920 1113 #uuid "a61a79f3-a45a-5593-b4bc-eb0ce92364fe" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257920 1145 "First, we graph the function $g(x)=3x-2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/47eb01a66773e5cf2b223cc3f8f4ff02caa424170a8e090ec0b1e45ed6d69741.png)\n\nThen, we find the lowest and highest $y$-values in the graph over the domain $x\\in [0,2].$\n\n* The lowest $y$ value is $-2.$ This occurs at the point $(0,-2).$\n\n* The highest $y$ value is $4.$ This occurs at the point $(2,4).$\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-2$ and $4,$ including the endpoints.\n\nTherefore, the range is $-2\\le y\\le 4,$ which can also be written as \n\n$$\n-2\\le g(x)\\le 4.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257921 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257922 13194139533864 true] [17592186257921 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257921 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257921 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257922 13194139533864 true] [17592186257921 1110 #uuid "9082cc46-6619-50c2-b109-27b601c47f11" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257922 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257922 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257922 1111 #uuid "b93ae47e-c9a4-5c74-b810-01aab25b220c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257923 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257924 13194139533864 true] [17592186257923 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257923 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257923 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257924 13194139533864 true] [17592186257923 1110 #uuid "7d698e12-f6f5-591d-a04a-2546aa9ce49f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257924 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257924 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257924 1111 #uuid "59a2bc2f-fe61-5929-96b2-b7033bdcf072" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257925 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257926 13194139533864 true] [17592186257925 1089 "q-133100" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257925 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257925 1100 "Suppose that $r(x)=\\frac{1}{3}x+k$ is the inverse of $g(x).$ What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257925 1113 #uuid "1c61acd1-ade2-5a44-8b2b-c8e5c8ab07c5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257925 1145 "If $r$ is the inverse of $g,$ then \n\n$$\nr(g(x))=x.\n$$\n\nTo find $r(g(x)),$ we write down the function $r$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $g(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}x+k \\\\ r(g(x)) & =\\frac{1}{3}g(x)+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $g(x)=3x-2$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(g(x)) & =\\frac{1}{3}g(x)+k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(3x-2)+k \\\\  & =x-\\frac{2}{3}+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we know that $r(g(x))=x.$ Equating the expression above with $x$ and solving for $k$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(g(x)) & =x \\\\ x-\\frac{2}{3}+k & =x \\\\ \\cancel{x}-\\frac{2}{3}+k & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}+k & =0 \\\\ k & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257926 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257927 13194139533864 true] [17592186257926 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257926 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257926 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257927 13194139533864 true] [17592186257926 1110 #uuid "c06794a5-596c-5119-8f53-ec6c5b564819" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257927 1034 "\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257927 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257927 1111 #uuid "607fe3ff-c70e-5e90-8be3-b217f42489ed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257928 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257929 13194139533864 true] [17592186257928 1089 "q-133321" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257928 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257928 1100 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257928 1113 #uuid "c6346f18-6a05-5cb3-99b6-ef873ed75441" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257928 1145 "Substituting $x=\\pi $ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{x}{3}) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257929 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257930 13194139533864 true] [17592186257929 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257929 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257929 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257930 13194139533864 true] [17592186257929 1110 #uuid "03906845-5b9e-5247-b7d2-a1ef31e58f50" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257930 1034 "3\\sqrt{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257930 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257930 1111 #uuid "b378bd0a-7b2a-59c0-b5a3-7a1f694c97a0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257931 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257932 13194139533864 true] [17592186257931 1089 "q-133322" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257931 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257931 1100 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257931 1113 #uuid "78cb0bb2-b627-5822-b1ef-7eee7e62fe2f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257931 1145 "Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x)=6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{x}{3}).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/72a0459ee5db59562a19961d0304785fb8d46db8be24ad7839d4b8690b0dc835.png)\n\nAs $x$ decreases to $-\\infty $, the graph oscillates between the values $y=-6$ and $y=6$ indefinitely. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{x}{3})=DNE.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257932 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257933 13194139533864 true] [17592186257932 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257932 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257932 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257933 13194139533864 true] [17592186257932 1110 #uuid "1953e4c1-cae1-5fbb-be88-88080280f26b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257933 1034 "DNE" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257933 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257933 1111 #uuid "0d13a6b0-e0fd-57b9-9a8d-db15584f6682" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257934 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257935 13194139533864 true] [17592186257934 1089 "q-133323" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257934 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257934 1100 "What is the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [3\\pi ,3\\pi +h],$ where $h>0$ is a parameter? Express your answer in terms of $h.$\n\n$\\frac{Δf}{Δx}\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257934 1113 #uuid "15d329ae-5740-515c-97ca-1733812dca77" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257934 1145 "We have the function $f(x)=6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{x}{3}).$ Notice that we are given the length $h$ of the interval. So, the average rate of change of $f$ over this interval is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h},\n$$\n\nwith $a=3\\pi .$ Substituting into the formula and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(3\\pi +h)-f(3\\pi )}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{1}{3}(3\\pi +h))-6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3\\pi )}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3})-6\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi )}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3})-6(0)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3})}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{h}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257935 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257936 13194139533864 true] [17592186257935 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257935 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257935 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257936 13194139533864 true] [17592186257935 1110 #uuid "be226e8c-bccd-5fef-9814-1659f67fd685" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257936 1034 "\\frac{6}{h}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3})" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257936 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257936 1111 #uuid "62dc3b45-a815-59c2-b2dc-522571722f92" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257937 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257938 13194139533864 true] [17592186257937 1089 "q-133324" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257937 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257937 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/1186087a359b3065400101c7f436c3e885bf937477bdd9f73fbdaab6771aa48a.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(h)$ is shown above, where\n\n$$\ng(h)=\\frac{6}{h}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3}).\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, what is ${lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)?$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257937 1113 #uuid "7c776c27-a6a4-5d10-97a0-9fb241329ba9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257937 1145 "The graph shows that as we approach $h=0$ from the left, the $y$ value approaches $-2.$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to \\,{0}^{-}}g(h)=-2.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $h=0$ from the right, the $y$ value approaches $-2.$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to \\,{0}^{+}}g(h)=-2.\n$$\n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $-2,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=-2.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257938 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257939 13194139533864 true] [17592186257938 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257938 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257938 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257939 13194139533864 true] [17592186257938 1110 #uuid "b4c911e3-9ea8-5569-8799-f7127763fe8d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257939 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257939 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257939 1111 #uuid "93e8b85a-cbcf-55fd-b21d-724a4d35c312" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257940 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257941 13194139533864 true] [17592186257940 1089 "q-133325" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257940 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257940 1100 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257940 1113 #uuid "578e3510-260f-5bdc-b7f3-7223ae9ac6f4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257940 1145 "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to (3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{x}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{x}{3})}=\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{x}{3}).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/42869c56604cbec85b5e4915f77b42e72444b7d9d117e140d9eba56b58c0f131.png)\n\nFrom the graph, as $x$ approaches $3\\pi $ from the left, the values of $y=\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{x}{3})$ are positive and increase without bound. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}={lim}_{x\\to \\,(3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{x}{3})=\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257941 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257942 13194139533864 true] [17592186257941 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257941 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257941 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257942 13194139533864 true] [17592186257941 1110 #uuid "775c24c3-fadf-52a7-91fb-f17a6c72d16f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257942 1034 "\\infty " 13194139533864 true] [17592186257942 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257942 1111 #uuid "6f1be0dd-6f4a-5f0d-8efe-3d4f02a58b14" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257943 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257944 13194139533864 true] [17592186257943 1089 "q-133326" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257943 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257943 1100 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257943 1113 #uuid "8c3c9278-79a7-5543-b4fd-fdc095dcc49a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257943 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}=\\frac{2-2}{(2)^{2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & =\\frac{x-2}{(x-2)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x-2}}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{1}{x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257944 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257945 13194139533864 true] [17592186257944 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257944 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257944 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257945 13194139533864 true] [17592186257944 1110 #uuid "209517ec-e06e-50a2-b3b4-440b13e55c97" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257945 1034 "1/4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257945 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257945 1111 #uuid "039afb05-3dd1-55c1-8034-3ea673ee4a22" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257946 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257947 13194139533864 true] [17592186257946 1089 "q-133327" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257946 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257946 1100 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{(x-2)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{(x-2)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2}{3})$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257946 1113 #uuid "8555738f-0375-5e38-b16f-5d036c583f49" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257946 1145 "Previously, we found that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the product rule to the original limit, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{(x-2)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}[\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}\\cdot f(x)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{x}{3}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4})\\cdot 6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257947 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257948 13194139533864 true] [17592186257947 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257947 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257947 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257948 13194139533864 true] [17592186257947 1110 #uuid "7e480ad3-4c1f-58fa-acab-2ec945f606c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257948 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257948 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257948 1111 #uuid "25f4ed6e-501b-5b5e-9497-a5e6e518ce62" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257949 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257950 13194139533864 true] [17592186257949 1089 "q-133328" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257949 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257949 1100 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}f(x)}{(2x+3)^{2}}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}f(x)}{(2x+3)^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257949 1113 #uuid "41f31c41-bebf-52ce-b67c-8ed460afd869" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257949 1145 "In part 1, we found that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x)=3\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}f(x)}{(2x+3)^{2}} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }(\\sqrt{3}f(x))}{{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }(2x+3)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, \n\n* applying the constant multiple rule in the numerator, and \n\n* the root rule, followed by the sum rule in the denominator, \n\nwe obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }(\\sqrt{3}f(x))}{{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }(2x+3)^{2}} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x)}{{({lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }(2x+3))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (3\\sqrt{3})}{(2\\pi +3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{(2\\pi +3)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257950 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257951 13194139533864 true] [17592186257950 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257950 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257950 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257951 13194139533864 true] [17592186257950 1110 #uuid "5f031406-cc55-59c4-9a75-ffc70113c109" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257951 1034 "\\frac{9}{(2\\pi +3)^{2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257951 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257951 1111 #uuid "7e6cb36b-ea61-5db1-94ea-22b111e20e8c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257952 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257953 13194139533864 true] [17592186257952 1089 "q-133329" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257952 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257952 1100 "Find the third partial sum ${s}_{3}$ of the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{f(6n\\pi +\\pi )}{(\\sqrt{3})^{n}}.$\n\n${s}_{3}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257952 1113 #uuid "7b891d1c-e8f1-5953-9447-191cffc40004" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257952 1145 "Let's start by calculating the first three terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =\\frac{f(6\\pi \\cdot 1+\\pi )}{(\\sqrt{3})^{1}}=\\frac{f(7\\pi )}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{7\\pi }{3})}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{6\\sqrt{3}}{2\\cdot \\sqrt{3}}=3 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{f(6\\pi \\cdot 2+\\pi )}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}=\\frac{f(13\\pi )}{3}=\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{13\\pi }{3})}{3}=\\frac{6\\sqrt{3}}{2\\cdot 3}=\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{f(6\\pi \\cdot 3+\\pi )}{(\\sqrt{3})^{3}}=\\frac{f(19\\pi )}{3\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{19\\pi }{3})}{3\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{6\\sqrt{3}}{2\\cdot 3\\sqrt{3}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third partial sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{3} & ={a}_{1}+{a}_{2}+{a}_{3} \\\\  & =3+\\sqrt{3}+1 \\\\  & =4+\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257953 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257954 13194139533864 true] [17592186257953 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257953 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257953 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257954 13194139533864 true] [17592186257953 1110 #uuid "ca9b11ac-3c21-557c-a64c-fda1dd8a8667" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257954 1034 "4+\\sqrt{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257954 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257954 1111 #uuid "214cea84-cf05-523f-bebb-a3215775a2d2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257955 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257956 13194139533864 true] [17592186257955 1089 "q-133330" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257955 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257955 1100 "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{f(6n\\pi +\\pi )}{(\\sqrt{3})^{n}}$ is an infinite geometric series, compute its sum.\n\n${S}_{\\infty }=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257955 1113 #uuid "cacbbb3f-0a1d-5dc5-9d6e-88b149a9ccf2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257955 1145 "Previously, we evaluated the first few terms of the series:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=3,\\,{a}_{2}=\\sqrt{3},\\,{a}_{3}=1\n$$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term is ${a}_{1}=3,$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{1-(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{\\sqrt{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257956 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257957 13194139533864 true] [17592186257956 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257956 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257956 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257957 13194139533864 true] [17592186257956 1110 #uuid "6a7c3b23-e791-5048-9fbd-09cfcdbc92e1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257957 1034 "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}-1}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257957 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257957 1111 #uuid "cbf4fa24-4330-573f-9a8d-bbd104e021f5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257958 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257959 13194139533864 true] [17592186257958 1089 "q-133428" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257958 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257958 1100 "Which of the following expressions are **irrational**?\n\nI. - ${8}^{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{36}$\nII. - ${3}^{20}\\cdot 7.0523$\nIII. - $\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {2}^{-15}$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257958 1113 #uuid "9e4d6294-f5f5-58cd-9f5f-15ca164270ca" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257958 1145 "Recall that: \n\n- the product of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}\\times \\text{rational}=\\text{rational}\n$$\n\n- the product of a nonzero rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}\\times \\text{irrational}=\\text{irrational}\n$$\n\n- the product of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements.\n\n- Expression I is rational. Notice that $\\sqrt{36}=6$ is a rational number. The number ${8}^{5}$ is rational too. So, their product must be rational.\n\n- Expression II is rational. Since both ${3}^{20}$ and $7.0523$ are rational, their product must be rational.\n\n- Expression III is irrational. Since $\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational and ${2}^{-15}$ is rational, their product must be irrational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257959 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257960 13194139533864 true] [17592186257959 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257961 13194139533864 true] [17592186257959 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257962 13194139533864 true] [17592186257959 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257963 13194139533864 true] [17592186257959 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257964 13194139533864 true] [17592186257959 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257965 13194139533864 true] [17592186257959 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257966 13194139533864 true] [17592186257959 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257967 13194139533864 true] [17592186257959 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257959 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257959 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257964 13194139533864 true] [17592186257959 1110 #uuid "356dec81-7040-5fb4-a3fe-80097d3f0467" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257960 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257960 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257960 1111 #uuid "ed3b8032-3546-54b7-9d11-2ba01b471b1b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257961 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257961 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257961 1111 #uuid "1df3a48b-57b3-5f2d-941e-363407328668" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257962 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257962 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257962 1111 #uuid "4a90ccd6-f190-51f1-a576-e641b61d1e99" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257963 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257963 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257963 1111 #uuid "81ca6aa0-5893-5596-9769-736c214e24b1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257964 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257964 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257964 1111 #uuid "12cdbc21-5d48-577c-b672-10da61434ad8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257965 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257965 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257965 1111 #uuid "106cd227-8a53-5674-a963-1cd2ccac7aba" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257966 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257966 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257966 1111 #uuid "44a6a700-fdf8-5f65-8367-a9a1f1bc0cd1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257967 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257967 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257967 1111 #uuid "2203de7e-d536-5ffd-945d-3aa3b477d87e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257968 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257969 13194139533864 true] [17592186257968 1089 "q-133759" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257968 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257968 1100 "If $-\\frac{10tx}{7}=\\frac{5}{2},$ then $x=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257968 1113 #uuid "6358fb8a-0bd1-5bf8-b02f-fe7bc8a93f47" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257968 1145 "We need to isolate $x.$ To do this, we first cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{10tx}{7} & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{-10tx}{7} & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 2\\cdot (-10tx) & =7\\cdot 5 \\\\ -20tx & =35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $-20t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-20tx & =35 \\\\ \\frac{-20tx}{-20t} & =\\frac{35}{-20t} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-20t}x}{\\cancel{-20t}} & =\\frac{35}{-20t} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{35}{20t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the right-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{5\\cdot 7}{5\\cdot 4t} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\cancel{5}\\cdot 7}{\\cancel{5}\\cdot 4t} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{7}{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257969 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257970 13194139533864 true] [17592186257969 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257969 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257969 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257970 13194139533864 true] [17592186257969 1110 #uuid "e39826a6-cf7f-51c7-8fc4-99b28f6dabe0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257970 1034 "-\\frac{7}{4t}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257970 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257970 1111 #uuid "24fa1aee-3b12-594c-8f25-4a4ae63dafe7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257971 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257972 13194139533864 true] [17592186257971 1089 "q-133897" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257971 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257971 1100 "If $\\frac{ab-b}{8}=\\frac{a-3}{2},$ then $a=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257971 1113 #uuid "58fae30e-8e99-56e8-af64-b904d4ec79eb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257971 1145 "We need to isolate all terms with $a$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we first cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{ab-b}{8} & =\\frac{a-3}{2} \\\\ 2\\cdot (ab-b) & =8\\cdot (a-3) \\\\ 2ab-2b & =8a-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $8a$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2ab-2b & =8a-24 \\\\ 2ab-2b-8a & =8a-24-8a \\\\ 2ab-2b-8a & =-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we add $2b$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2ab-2b-8a & =-24 \\\\ 2ab-2b-8a+2b & =2b-24 \\\\ 2ab-8a & =2b-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $a$ are on the left-hand side, and all terms independent of $a$ are on the right-hand side.\n\nThen, we factor out $a$ from the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2ab-8a & =2b-24 \\\\ a(2b-8) & =2b-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(2b-8)$ to isolate $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(2b-8) & =2b-24 \\\\ \\frac{a\\cancel{(2b-8)}}{\\cancel{2b-8}} & =\\frac{2b-24}{2b-8} \\\\ a & =\\frac{2b-24}{2b-8} \\\\ a & =\\frac{2(b-12)}{2(b-4)} \\\\ a & =\\frac{b-12}{b-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $b-4\\ne 0.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257972 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257973 13194139533864 true] [17592186257972 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257972 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257972 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257973 13194139533864 true] [17592186257972 1110 #uuid "540038d1-4d47-5665-9113-5292e89fafa9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257973 1034 "\\frac{b-12}{b-4}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257973 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257973 1111 #uuid "f2845182-faa2-54d5-a2aa-67d66eba400b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257974 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257975 13194139533864 true] [17592186257974 1089 "q-134130" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257974 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257974 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/bc3809e55e601738d56be6885475ed442dec1fb3a0d2e19da14be045510f73dc.png)\n\nThe above scatter plot shows the relationship between age and years of experience of a group of professional soccer players. \n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - Only one player has exactly $10$ years of experience.\nII. - There are two players aged $21.$\nIII. - None of the players has fewer than $6$ years of experience." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257974 1113 #uuid "e2ae6b9a-e5a8-56dc-8196-49d72b8c47d7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257974 1145 "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement I is true. The scatter plot has only one dot whose vertical coordinate equals $10,$ namely, $(21,10).$ This means only one of the players has exactly $10$ years of experience.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/209759f5d70fd8587740d1da840f9da838a3c9a055a0d1e962f76c1cd6341fd3.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The scatter plot has two dots whose horizontal coordinate equals $21,$ namely, $(21,10)$ and $(21,12).$ This means that two players are $21$ years old.\n\n- Statement III is false. From the scatter plot, one player has fewer than $6$ years of experience. The dot below the horizontal line represents this player.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257975 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257976 13194139533864 true] [17592186257975 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257977 13194139533864 true] [17592186257975 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257978 13194139533864 true] [17592186257975 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257979 13194139533864 true] [17592186257975 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257980 13194139533864 true] [17592186257975 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257981 13194139533864 true] [17592186257975 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257982 13194139533864 true] [17592186257975 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257983 13194139533864 true] [17592186257975 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257975 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257975 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257981 13194139533864 true] [17592186257975 1110 #uuid "4271acfa-9f19-528d-b7fe-83b00c07c70b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257976 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257976 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257976 1111 #uuid "2ccadedc-ac9d-501f-ac6f-af1d32f00024" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257977 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257977 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257977 1111 #uuid "d3c896c4-9756-5b21-afd7-840dfc0de007" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257978 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257978 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257978 1111 #uuid "58668d09-0f3a-5dc7-9e04-380e43c43f7b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257979 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257979 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257979 1111 #uuid "f10e9224-203c-5056-a43b-1139e16b53e6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257980 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257980 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257980 1111 #uuid "eddc8efc-da25-514e-ae6d-122bd4f49d5a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257981 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257981 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257981 1111 #uuid "935540f8-c858-5bef-8d72-cd50516436da" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257982 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257982 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257982 1111 #uuid "5aee3821-2280-5178-9380-34cbe7e976d6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257983 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257983 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257983 1111 #uuid "5dde2dc4-e38c-5c9e-836a-729f2fd9d7be" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257984 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257985 13194139533864 true] [17592186257984 1089 "q-134527" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257984 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257984 1100 "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(1.1)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to two decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257984 1113 #uuid "fbd05050-bcf2-5bb4-9011-c5d2c51ceabc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257984 1145 "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{5} & =1+5x+\\frac{1}{2!}\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+5x+10{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{5}\\approx 1+5x+10{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=0.1$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.1)^{5} & =(1+0.1)^{5} \\\\  & \\approx 1+5(0.1)+10(0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =1+5(0.1)+10(0.01) \\\\  & =1+0.5+0.1 \\\\  & =1.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(1.1)^{5}-1.6|}{(1.1)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & \\approx 0.65%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257985 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257986 13194139533864 true] [17592186257985 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257985 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257985 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257986 13194139533864 true] [17592186257985 1110 #uuid "ca19158c-3589-51ce-9250-40e2ad38e966" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257986 1034 "0.65%" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257986 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257986 1111 #uuid "429a1aa9-f72c-50b7-a22c-27192a8b7efa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257987 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257988 13194139533864 true] [17592186257987 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257990 13194139533864 true] [17592186257987 1089 "q-13454" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257987 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257987 1100 "Find the $x$-intercepts of $y=-{x}^{2}-4x+2.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257987 1113 #uuid "d5b37049-7913-52ac-bd10-29e98dfac38e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257987 1145 "We need to find the points on the parabola where $y=0.$ So, we have the following quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-4x+2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's multiply by $-1$ to make the leading coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-{x}^{2}-4x+2) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is difficult to factor, but we can solve it using the quadratic formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{{4}^{2}-(4)(1)(-2)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{16+8}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{24}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{4\\cdot 6}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 2\\sqrt{6}}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $x=-2-\\sqrt{6}$ and $x=-2+\\sqrt{6}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257988 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257989 13194139533864 true] [17592186257988 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257988 1041 "root-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257988 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257989 13194139533864 true] [17592186257988 1110 #uuid "b67e56ce-7857-5d94-806c-15b87097d958" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257989 1034 "-2-\\sqrt6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257989 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257989 1111 #uuid "9b2cd4eb-1be9-5824-ba37-dac440a9b9a1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257990 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257991 13194139533864 true] [17592186257990 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257990 1041 "root-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257990 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257991 13194139533864 true] [17592186257990 1110 #uuid "c861b29c-8354-57c8-957d-c7216336afc5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257991 1034 "-2+\\sqrt6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257991 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257991 1111 #uuid "c4030555-5fa1-5b12-945b-819c15276e96" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257992 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257993 13194139533864 true] [17592186257992 1089 "q-134565" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257992 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257992 1100 "Simplify $2\\sqrt[3]{5}+2\\sqrt{6}+7\\sqrt[3]{40}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257992 1113 #uuid "e38c546d-9295-59dc-a5d8-1c4e46ac7fed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257992 1145 "First, we use the product rule for radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{40} & =\\sqrt[3]{8\\cdot 5}=\\sqrt[3]{8}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{5}=2\\sqrt[3]{5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt[3]{5}+2\\sqrt{6}+7\\sqrt[3]{40} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt[3]{5}+2\\sqrt{6}+7(2\\sqrt[3]{5}) & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt[3]{5}+2\\sqrt{6}+14\\sqrt[3]{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can combine the two $\\sqrt[3]{5}$ terms together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt[3]{5}+2\\sqrt{6}+14\\sqrt[3]{5} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{6}+(2\\sqrt[3]{5}+14\\sqrt[3]{5}) & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{6}+(2+14)\\sqrt[3]{5} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{6}+16\\sqrt[3]{5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257993 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257994 13194139533864 true] [17592186257993 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257993 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257993 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257994 13194139533864 true] [17592186257993 1110 #uuid "e8e6a0d8-bcf5-503d-b979-266bd990300a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257994 1034 "2\\sqrt{6}+16\\sqrt[3]{5}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257994 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257994 1111 #uuid "66eebd56-226b-55bb-adcb-d0c87f674a36" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257995 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257996 13194139533864 true] [17592186257995 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257998 13194139533864 true] [17592186257995 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258000 13194139533864 true] [17592186257995 1089 "q-13468" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257995 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257995 1100 "Determine the real roots of the cubic equation $2{x}^{3}-40{x}^{2}+35x=-3{x}^{3}.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}\n\nValue 3 (least to greatest): {{root-3}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257995 1113 #uuid "73b7efc6-6b1f-5e8e-b511-0bc919992361" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257995 1145 "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(x)=0.$ To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}-40{x}^{2}+35x=-3{x}^{3} \\\\ 5{x}^{3}-40{x}^{2}+35x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $5x.$ We can factor out the $5x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{3}-40{x}^{2}+35x & =0 \\\\ 5x({x}^{2}-8x+7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression ${x}^{2}-8x+7$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x({x}^{2}-8x+7) & =0 \\\\ 5x(x-1)(x-7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x=0,$ $x=1,$ and $x=7.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257996 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257997 13194139533864 true] [17592186257996 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257996 1041 "root-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257996 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257997 13194139533864 true] [17592186257996 1110 #uuid "5c144115-70f1-5567-83f5-e0fb71d3f663" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257997 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257997 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257997 1111 #uuid "14655ff6-bf2e-5fab-b23c-0740cbced876" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257998 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257999 13194139533864 true] [17592186257998 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257998 1041 "root-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257998 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257999 13194139533864 true] [17592186257998 1110 #uuid "71df7fd7-7b26-5c2d-8809-b504f56db558" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257999 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257999 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257999 1111 #uuid "35b6d5ca-5b61-555f-a534-35ad85260dc8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258000 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258001 13194139533864 true] [17592186258000 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258000 1041 "root-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258000 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258001 13194139533864 true] [17592186258000 1110 #uuid "442f30a8-7af7-5011-bb14-90b9a46d8c25" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258001 1034 "7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258001 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258001 1111 #uuid "24dc3cdc-0c4d-5ea2-8e04-f8924a82a9d8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258002 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258003 13194139533864 true] [17592186258002 1089 "q-134815" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258002 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258002 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/cec25c6ab182494771824bae8c2182bf38b57e2d1e81d913e3e49916c8cc3d60.png)\n\nThe residual plot above was constructed after fitting a linear regression model on a set of bivariate numerical data $(x,y).$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - When $x=8,$ the regression equation overestimates the value of $y.$\nII. - The residuals appear to be randomly scattered about the horizontal axis (there are no obvious trends).\nIII. - This linear regression is probably a good fit for the data." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258002 1113 #uuid "d5d30017-4bac-5ef9-907f-2a33c3ed66b1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258002 1145 "Let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The residual equals the actual value minus the estimated value:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \nFrom the plot, the residual at $x=8$ is negative. So, we have that $\\text{Actual}<\\text{Estimated},$ and therefore the model overestimates  this data point.\n\n- Statement II is false. The plot shows the residuals tend to decrease as $x$ increases. So, there is a trend, as shown below.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the residuals are *not* randomly scattered above and below the horizontal axis, our regression line is *not* a good fit for the given data.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258003 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258004 13194139533864 true] [17592186258003 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258005 13194139533864 true] [17592186258003 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258006 13194139533864 true] [17592186258003 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258007 13194139533864 true] [17592186258003 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258008 13194139533864 true] [17592186258003 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258009 13194139533864 true] [17592186258003 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258010 13194139533864 true] [17592186258003 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258011 13194139533864 true] [17592186258003 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186258003 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258003 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258007 13194139533864 true] [17592186258003 1110 #uuid "f244f4e1-13b7-5e68-bfa8-3655a8868455" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258004 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258004 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258004 1111 #uuid "1198c783-6d09-59c8-83de-ea02f8aaafbf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258005 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258005 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258005 1111 #uuid "597a71cf-9864-5336-b079-cb365d18d538" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258006 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258006 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258006 1111 #uuid "9eb50253-a975-5059-90ce-f2edaee71701" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258007 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258007 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258007 1111 #uuid "60f285b6-518e-544a-83b3-61a07eb08a67" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258008 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258008 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258008 1111 #uuid "2d51af7a-49e9-5c4e-b237-6a5f7b502cfb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258009 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258009 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258009 1111 #uuid "0909f57e-a765-5887-be02-0912931e4062" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258010 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258010 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258010 1111 #uuid "a50c314e-f8f7-5634-8543-bfcb643fd0f9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258011 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258011 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258011 1111 #uuid "c2c32971-4119-5de3-be91-98e96faa25f3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258012 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258013 13194139533864 true] [17592186258012 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258015 13194139533864 true] [17592186258012 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258017 13194139533864 true] [17592186258012 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258019 13194139533864 true] [17592186258012 1089 "q-13550" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258012 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258012 1100 "Find $K+L-(K+J)$, if\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,K=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,L=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow 1, column 1: {{r1c1}}\n\nRow 1, column 2: {{r1c2}}\n\nRow 2, column 1: {{r2c1}}\n\nRow 2, column 2: {{r2c2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258012 1113 #uuid "eb48a317-e064-5f22-b418-b65055f852ef" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258012 1145 "First, we simplify the expression by distributing the subtraction over the matrices in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K+L-(K+J) & =K+L-K-J \\\\  & =\\cancel{K}+L-\\cancel{K}-J \\\\  & =L-J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left with a single subtraction. So, we subtract the corresponding entries of the two matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L-J & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6-5 & 6-2 \\\\ 6-2 & 6-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258013 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258014 13194139533864 true] [17592186258013 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258013 1041 "r1c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258013 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258014 13194139533864 true] [17592186258013 1110 #uuid "ca8e1263-f3fe-579d-8e20-d7beb1ea8ae8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258014 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258014 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258014 1111 #uuid "19fea0f1-db68-53b8-b64d-2d81bb965cd8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258015 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258016 13194139533864 true] [17592186258015 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258015 1041 "r1c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258015 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258016 13194139533864 true] [17592186258015 1110 #uuid "db11b31c-592b-587b-af27-7145a6680bc1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258016 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258016 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258016 1111 #uuid "87bc8ad6-e689-5792-9a80-b23182d9d445" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258017 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258018 13194139533864 true] [17592186258017 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258017 1041 "r2c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258017 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258018 13194139533864 true] [17592186258017 1110 #uuid "5a458555-43c2-595b-9bea-97070415fc6b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258018 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258018 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258018 1111 #uuid "25e2da5a-b65e-5688-8adf-7728bf6c3d29" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258019 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258020 13194139533864 true] [17592186258019 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258019 1041 "r2c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258019 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258020 13194139533864 true] [17592186258019 1110 #uuid "73f5c65a-f17f-5663-b1ae-b6481c8333c5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258020 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258020 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258020 1111 #uuid "827ee7ec-62cb-5317-8a23-cb58c8957e2a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258021 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258022 13194139533864 true] [17592186258021 1089 "q-13585" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258021 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258021 1100 "If $f(x)=x+4$ and $g(x)=-3x+2,$ then $(f-g)(x)=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258021 1113 #uuid "807e93c4-5bb4-5de5-8fbc-1aec915f7b84" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258021 1145 "The notation $(f-g)(x)$ means the difference of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\nTo find the difference of the two functions, we subtract $g(x)$ from $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f-g)(x) & =f(x)-g(x) \\\\  & =(x+4)-(-3x+2) \\\\  & =(x+3x)+(4-2) \\\\  & =4x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258022 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258023 13194139533864 true] [17592186258022 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258022 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258022 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258023 13194139533864 true] [17592186258022 1110 #uuid "fe9c33e1-f33c-57e4-8739-951e96637668" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258023 1034 "4x+2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258023 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258023 1111 #uuid "23a535cd-451a-5972-81b1-3fd08d261865" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258024 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258025 13194139533864 true] [17592186258024 1089 "q-13586" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258024 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258024 1100 "If $f(x)=3x+8$ and $g(x)=-5x+7,$ then $(f-g)(x)=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258024 1113 #uuid "54cf059d-83d7-54df-aac4-8e7a8a49e274" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258024 1145 "The notation $(f-g)(x)$ means the difference of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\nTo find the difference of the two functions, we subtract $g(x)$ from $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f-g)(x) & =f(x)-g(x) \\\\  & =(3x+8)-(-5x+7) \\\\  & =(3x-(-5x))+(8-7) \\\\  & =8x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258025 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258026 13194139533864 true] [17592186258025 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258025 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258025 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258026 13194139533864 true] [17592186258025 1110 #uuid "79684820-2beb-5b18-be3e-c78099f2c767" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258026 1034 "8x+1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258026 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258026 1111 #uuid "0141126d-4951-54c1-9360-677112b53c4f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258027 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258028 13194139533864 true] [17592186258027 1089 "q-13594" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258027 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258027 1100 "Given $f(x)=7x+3$ and $g(x)=2-5x,$ find $(fg)(1).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258027 1113 #uuid "084cf399-5cc4-5ae8-9e6f-6bbf5eb1b6ef" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258027 1145 "The notation $(fg)(x)$ means  the product of the functions  $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(fg)(1),$ we find $f(1)$ and $g(1),$ and then multiply the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(1)$ and $g(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =7(1)+3=10 \\\\ g(1) & =2-5(1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the results:\n\n$$\n(fg)(1)=f(1)g(1)=10\\cdot (-3)=-30\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(fg)(x)$ by multiplying the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(x) & =(7x+3)(2-5x) \\\\  & =-35{x}^{2}-x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(fg)(1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(1) & =-35(1)^{2}-(1)+6 \\\\  & =-35-1+6 \\\\  & =-30\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258028 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258029 13194139533864 true] [17592186258028 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258028 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258028 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258029 13194139533864 true] [17592186258028 1110 #uuid "7e585b38-6c42-5ef4-ad3e-c2eee41d3fb6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258029 1034 "-30" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258029 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258029 1111 #uuid "2df2d8f5-89b3-57e4-8f4a-d270b42092e2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258030 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258031 13194139533864 true] [17592186258030 1089 "q-13595" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258030 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258030 1100 "Given $f(x)=x+2$ and $g(x)=x-5$, find $(fg)(-1).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258030 1113 #uuid "8c2c26f6-ca4a-50fd-a1f5-f21eba02c0f0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258030 1145 "The notation $(fg)(x)$ means  the product of the functions  $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(fg)(-1),$ we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1),$ and then multiply the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =(-1)+2=1 \\\\ g(-1) & =(-1)-5=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the results:\n\n$$\n(fg)(-1)=f(-1)g(-1)=1\\cdot (-6)=-6\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(fg)(x)$ by multiplying the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(x) & =(x+2)(x-5) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-5x+2x-10 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3x-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(fg)(-1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(-1) & =(-1)^{2}-3(-1)-10 \\\\  & =1+3-10 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258031 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258032 13194139533864 true] [17592186258031 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258031 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258031 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258032 13194139533864 true] [17592186258031 1110 #uuid "60051f1b-1cc5-57b3-9d12-2baaf0da0965" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258032 1034 "-6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258032 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258032 1111 #uuid "50417128-ff7a-5375-8f9b-6380919d7727" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258033 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258034 13194139533864 true] [17592186258033 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258036 13194139533864 true] [17592186258033 1089 "q-13602" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258033 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258033 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ba20fb3746240004550da009e8cefa3437d559bb9f33e9cf42e97800fbddabec.png)\n\nFind the midpoint of the line segment with endpoints shown above.\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258033 1113 #uuid "30fb72b2-8ceb-5d3e-98ff-4e76c71e39e5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258033 1145 "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between the given points ${{(-2,3)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(4,-2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{-2+4}{2},\\frac{3-2}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{2},\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(1,\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258034 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258035 13194139533864 true] [17592186258034 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258034 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258034 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258035 13194139533864 true] [17592186258034 1110 #uuid "24760f79-1c23-51bb-b2df-97129dc228c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258035 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258035 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258035 1111 #uuid "e75d8e1d-4242-5c15-8568-7a957c4a9719" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258036 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258037 13194139533864 true] [17592186258036 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258036 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258036 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258037 13194139533864 true] [17592186258036 1110 #uuid "7cc5099b-40eb-57c9-a42e-d9bb31902824" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258037 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258037 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258037 1111 #uuid "ffb907ea-d641-581c-98cf-1b962e5e9dcc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258038 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258039 13194139533864 true] [17592186258038 1089 "q-13607" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258038 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258038 1100 "Determine whether the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{n\\sqrt{n}}$\nconverges or diverges.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258038 1113 #uuid "f2feeaf1-eaac-5dbf-95f2-bd5007588656" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258038 1145 "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{1+1/2}}=2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Since $p>1$, the series is convergent. \n\nNote that the factor of $2$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence of the series." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258039 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258040 13194139533864 true] [17592186258039 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258039 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258039 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258040 13194139533864 true] [17592186258039 1110 #uuid "f5cdc8b7-6bee-5aa6-a18a-5b3e707ce5bb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258040 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258040 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258040 1111 #uuid "821eea6e-54b0-5192-84d9-7a02c98fde64" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258041 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258042 13194139533864 true] [17592186258041 1089 "q-136439" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258041 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258041 1100 "A particle starts moving from the point with position vector $4i\\,\\text{cm},$ with an acceleration of $(-2i+3j)\\,{\\text{cm/s}}^{2}$ and an initial velocity of $(2i+j)\\,\\text{cm/s}.$ Find the distance from the particle to the origin after $2$ seconds.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258041 1113 #uuid "26bb15fa-4257-5738-94d3-4830ea1602f2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258041 1145 "If a particle moves with constant acceleration $a,$ the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nr={r}_{0}+ut+\\frac{1}{2}a{t}^{2},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${r}_{0}$ is the initial position of the particle and $u$ is its initial velocity.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{0}=4i,\\,u=2i+j,\\,a=-2i+3j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the particle after $t=2\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =(4i)+(2i+j)(2)+\\frac{1}{2}(-2i+3j)(2)^{2} \\\\  & =(4i)+(4i+2j)+2(-2i+3j) \\\\  & =(8i+2j)+(-4i+6j) \\\\  & =(4i+8j)\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance from the particle to the origin at this time is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r| & =|4i+8j| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{8}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258042 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258043 13194139533864 true] [17592186258042 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258042 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258042 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258043 13194139533864 true] [17592186258042 1110 #uuid "9984c246-d964-5b99-9bf3-78bbbb1bbc2a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258043 1034 "4\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{cm}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258043 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258043 1111 #uuid "35f0d802-b28d-57ac-b8d0-10219125f1ee" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258044 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258045 13194139533864 true] [17592186258044 1089 "q-138848" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258044 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258044 1100 "Consider the function $y=\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}x}.$  The derivative of this function for $x\\in (0,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{'}=\\frac{g(x)\\operatorname{sin}x+h(x)\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x)+h(x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258044 1113 #uuid "f846d5c0-2f2b-5199-8370-0b3c4a4f1f0d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258044 1145 "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}\\cdot \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+6x)^{1/2}\\operatorname{sin}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}y & =\\operatorname{ln}(({x}^{2}+6x)^{1/2}\\operatorname{sin}x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}y & =\\operatorname{ln}(({x}^{2}+6x)^{1/2})+\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{sin}x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{2}+6x)+\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{sin}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{2}+6x)+\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{sin}x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{'} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+6x}\\cdot (2x+6)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{'}}{y} & =\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\operatorname{cot}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =y(\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\operatorname{cot}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}x}(\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\operatorname{cot}x) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}x}(\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}x}(\\frac{(x+3)\\operatorname{sin}x+({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}x}{({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{sin}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}x|}{\\operatorname{sin}x}\\frac{(x+3)\\operatorname{sin}x+({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that $x\\in (0,\\pi ),$ then $|\\operatorname{sin}x|=\\operatorname{sin}x,$ so the fraction $\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}x|}{\\operatorname{sin}x}$ simplifies to $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =\\frac{(x+3)\\operatorname{sin}x+({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x)+h(x)=x+3+{x}^{2}+6x={x}^{2}+7x+3.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258045 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258046 13194139533864 true] [17592186258045 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258045 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258045 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258046 13194139533864 true] [17592186258045 1110 #uuid "7c67f3fb-e493-5dd5-b07c-4feec16b9603" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258046 1034 "{x}^{2}+7x+3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258046 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258046 1111 #uuid "1d5346eb-106f-50f7-93c2-2d1493d37036" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258047 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258048 13194139533864 true] [17592186258047 1089 "q-1389" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258047 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258047 1100 "If $\\overset{\\to }{A}=⟨4,\\frac{1}{2}⟩$, then $-4\\overset{\\to }{A}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258047 1113 #uuid "8fecd984-6de7-5785-803b-13a1f0cd73ff" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258047 1145 "To compute $-4\\overset{\\to }{A},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\overset{\\to }{A} & =-4\\cdot ⟨4,\\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(-4)\\cdot 4,(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-16,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258048 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258049 13194139533864 true] [17592186258048 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258048 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258048 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258049 13194139533864 true] [17592186258048 1110 #uuid "a95ee6b3-71c2-5e0f-9189-350c74985842" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258049 1034 "⟨-16,-2⟩" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258049 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258049 1111 #uuid "57b2ee3d-98e3-53db-9419-d7bb962afcb1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258050 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258051 13194139533864 true] [17592186258050 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258053 13194139533864 true] [17592186258050 1089 "q-138998" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258050 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258050 1100 "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{3{n}^{n}}{(1+n)^{n}}.$ According to the root test, does ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge?\n\n$L$: {{L}}\n\nRoot test conclusion: {{root-test-conclusion}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258050 1113 #uuid "166ee2ec-2929-59e4-ac37-3903890f3dc2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258050 1145 "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{3{n}^{n}}{(1+n)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3{n}^{n}}{(1+n)^{n}})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{3}\\,n}{1+n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{3}\\,n}{n(\\frac{1}{n}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{3}}{(\\frac{1}{n}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0+1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{3}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=1,$ the root test gives no conclusion." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258051 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258052 13194139533864 true] [17592186258051 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258051 1041 "L" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258051 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258052 13194139533864 true] [17592186258051 1110 #uuid "c587d7f7-4217-5d39-b98b-a9d169ba528f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258052 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258052 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258052 1111 #uuid "8f537e09-63db-5400-87d5-4d6e62003142" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258053 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258054 13194139533864 true] [17592186258053 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258055 13194139533864 true] [17592186258053 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258056 13194139533864 true] [17592186258053 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139533864 true] [17592186258053 1041 "root-test-conclusion" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258053 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258056 13194139533864 true] [17592186258053 1110 #uuid "97e027c5-fe4d-5d1e-8776-99ade5e46f52" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258054 1034 "Diverges" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258054 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258054 1111 #uuid "dd52aa37-3a3c-5f61-9789-314e2311d72c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258055 1034 "Converges" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258055 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258055 1111 #uuid "7c19157b-eb38-5696-a3ca-5384bdc4700d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258056 1034 "Inconclusive" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258056 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258056 1111 #uuid "c8a60af4-dc48-5f40-a169-b2599e64fc50" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258057 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258058 13194139533864 true] [17592186258057 1089 "q-14060" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258057 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258057 1100 "Calculate the sum to infinity of the geometric series $40+20+10+5+⋯$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258057 1113 #uuid "97aba825-3afc-5081-87e1-01c8ec528803" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258057 1145 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term is ${a}_{1}=40$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{20}{40}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{(1-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258058 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258059 13194139533864 true] [17592186258058 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258058 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258058 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258059 13194139533864 true] [17592186258058 1110 #uuid "5eb399e4-3dec-5639-9a17-a37effe5f77c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258059 1034 "80" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258059 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258059 1111 #uuid "5d59d8b0-4b56-5725-be31-52c36a0cb78c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258060 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258061 13194139533864 true] [17592186258060 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258063 13194139533864 true] [17592186258060 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258065 13194139533864 true] [17592186258060 1089 "q-14077" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258060 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258060 1100 "Solve ${x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-3x-21=0.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}\n\nValue 3 (least to greatest): {{root-3}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258060 1113 #uuid "b72a04f0-5d72-598f-8e4e-47af21a1c978" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258060 1145 "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-3x-21 & = \\\\ ({x}^{3}+7{x}^{2})-(3x+21) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+7)-3(x+7) & = \\\\ (x+7)\\cdot {x}^{2}-(x+7)\\cdot 3 & = \\\\ (x+7)({x}^{2}-3) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(x+7)({x}^{2}-3)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(x+7)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+7 & =0 \\\\ x & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({x}^{2}-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-7,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{3}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258061 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258062 13194139533864 true] [17592186258061 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258061 1041 "root-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258061 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258062 13194139533864 true] [17592186258061 1110 #uuid "d57d861f-3d8d-57dd-8e05-1e213f99e9da" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258062 1034 "-7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258062 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258062 1111 #uuid "93a31339-31a3-5548-bf82-70f72dcd1507" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258063 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258064 13194139533864 true] [17592186258063 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258063 1041 "root-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258063 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258064 13194139533864 true] [17592186258063 1110 #uuid "5d0f6b9d-a073-50a3-a515-a83016733c3a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258064 1034 "-\\sqrt3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258064 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258064 1111 #uuid "185d00bb-bfee-58d5-bff4-ce7e519b0b5a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258065 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258066 13194139533864 true] [17592186258065 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258065 1041 "root-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258065 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258066 13194139533864 true] [17592186258065 1110 #uuid "006449f7-6d61-52f7-9cf8-f904f2c8bc28" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258066 1034 "\\sqrt3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258066 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258066 1111 #uuid "cd1e4715-9c75-571a-84a1-fe4ac2f9cee0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258067 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258068 13194139533864 true] [17592186258067 1089 "q-14088" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258067 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258067 1100 "Determine the value of the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{(-0.2)}^{3n}$  rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258067 1113 #uuid "308c69f7-025f-5fb5-b821-d31e639575ed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258067 1145 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nThe first term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=6(-0.2)^{3(1)}=6(-0.2)^{3}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the second term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=6(-0.2)^{3(2)}=6(-0.2)^{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{6(-0.2)^{6}}{6(-0.2)^{3}}=(-0.2)^{3}=-0.008.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{(-0.2)}^{3n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-0.2)^{3}}{1-(-0.008)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-0.008)}{1.008} \\\\  & \\approx -0.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the final answer has been rounded to two decimal places." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258068 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258069 13194139533864 true] [17592186258068 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258068 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258068 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258069 13194139533864 true] [17592186258068 1110 #uuid "2c94b1fa-56ab-5b4b-9e3b-4d7264d2e3ba" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258069 1034 "-0.05" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258069 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258069 1111 #uuid "00a19c25-9a8a-5f7c-8819-b75bf130ed1a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258070 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258071 13194139533864 true] [17592186258070 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258073 13194139533864 true] [17592186258070 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258075 13194139533864 true] [17592186258070 1089 "q-14102" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258070 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258070 1100 "Solve $6{y}^{3}+4=8{y}^{2}+3y.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}\n\nValue 3 (least to greatest): {{root-3}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258070 1113 #uuid "23a22035-6f86-5f86-abdd-41c57b6c7a46" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258070 1145 "First, we subtract $8{y}^{2}$ and $3y$ from both sides to make the right side $0.$ To prepare for factoring by grouping, we make sure to write the resulting cubic in standard form.\n\n$$\n6{y}^{3}-8{y}^{2}-3y+4=0\n$$\n\nNext, we factor by grouping. Notice that when grouping the smaller terms, we factor out the negative sign.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{y}^{3}-8{y}^{2}-3y+4 & = \\\\ (6{y}^{3}-8{y}^{2})-(3y-4) & = \\\\ 2{y}^{2}(3y-4)-(3y-4) & = \\\\ (3y-4)\\cdot 2{y}^{2}-(3y-4)\\cdot 1 & = \\\\ (3y-4)(2{y}^{2}-1) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set each factor equal to $0$ and solve.\n\n- Setting the factor $(3y-4)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y-4 & =0 \\\\ 3y & =4 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(2{y}^{2}-1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=\\frac{4}{3},\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258072 13194139533864 true] [17592186258071 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258071 1041 "root-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258071 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258072 13194139533864 true] [17592186258071 1110 #uuid "cf6675f8-a4aa-5984-8ccc-e76815c33031" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258072 1034 "-\\sqrt{\\frac12}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258072 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258072 1111 #uuid "bf7f6163-5a66-5876-95e6-e339fb1c5461" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258073 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258074 13194139533864 true] [17592186258073 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258073 1041 "root-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258073 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258074 13194139533864 true] [17592186258073 1110 #uuid "f8c6f6f4-c82a-55b6-9a6c-3baf693504a6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258074 1034 "\\sqrt{\\frac12}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258074 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258074 1111 #uuid "863d4373-f978-5bdf-afc1-eb2ef0d0ba7e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258075 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258076 13194139533864 true] [17592186258075 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258075 1041 "root-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258075 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258076 13194139533864 true] [17592186258075 1110 #uuid "8f8c7ff1-6e87-561c-8ba0-98a20d7cf5f2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258076 1034 "\\frac43" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258076 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258076 1111 #uuid "090c71a3-6149-5cb3-97a9-7eb38432e1dc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258077 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258078 13194139533864 true] [17592186258077 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258080 13194139533864 true] [17592186258077 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258082 13194139533864 true] [17592186258077 1089 "q-14108" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258077 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258077 1100 "Solve $14{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}-6x+3=0.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}\n\nValue 3 (least to greatest): {{root-3}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258077 1113 #uuid "a40984bf-4ba0-5fcb-b3fc-f4dcbc8bd041" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258077 1145 "We proceed to factor the left side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}-6x+3 & = \\\\ (14{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2})-(6x-3) & = \\\\ 7{x}^{2}(2x-1)-3(2x-1) & = \\\\ (2x-1)\\cdot 7{x}^{2}-(2x-1)\\cdot 3 & = \\\\ (2x-1)(7{x}^{2}-3) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(2x-1)(7{x}^{2}-3)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(2x-1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(7{x}^{2}-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{x}^{2}-3 & =0 \\\\ 7{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{7}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{7}}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258079 13194139533864 true] [17592186258078 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258078 1041 "root-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258078 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258079 13194139533864 true] [17592186258078 1110 #uuid "a1ed5062-5f0b-50a3-a428-6b6b4bc3dc03" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258079 1034 "-\\sqrt{\\frac37}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258079 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258079 1111 #uuid "17396678-9a2d-5efb-84e0-167a80500811" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258080 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258081 13194139533864 true] [17592186258080 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258080 1041 "root-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258080 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258081 13194139533864 true] [17592186258080 1110 #uuid "3d3fe96f-e1fa-5548-9950-4dd6d162540c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258081 1034 "\\frac12" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258081 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258081 1111 #uuid "14c5b409-986b-54df-9960-17c7058f6de6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258082 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258083 13194139533864 true] [17592186258082 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258082 1041 "root-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258082 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258083 13194139533864 true] [17592186258082 1110 #uuid "0df97483-d5f1-54c1-aecd-03d377b92d8c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258083 1034 "\\sqrt{\\frac37}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258083 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258083 1111 #uuid "debcef87-92f3-5cd2-8265-3e8c4b8197a7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258084 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258085 13194139533864 true] [17592186258084 1089 "q-141106" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258084 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258084 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/b70f78004ad1239cc6d7fb46086f8b961d587e69ff5c3a7414beba60cbcf74a9.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $-x(x+6)\\le 0.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258084 1113 #uuid "dee958e0-6ef4-559d-9402-576ab9209618" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258084 1145 "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n-x(x+6)\\le 0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*less than or equal to*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-x(x+6)\n$$\n\nis *below* the $x$-axis or *touches* this axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ed78f461be7f5627814b8df3a85bedcbafbb1456c2dda01e825b0706d164b685.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x\\le -6$ or $x\\ge 0.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258085 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258086 13194139533864 true] [17592186258085 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258085 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258085 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258086 13194139533864 true] [17592186258085 1110 #uuid "d6ef7c0b-8167-5f92-b5f2-d39b028bb38f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258086 1034 "x\\ge 0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258086 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258086 1111 #uuid "30eb643b-1b75-5ffb-9cf0-9e7f3f40f301" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258087 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258088 13194139533864 true] [17592186258087 1089 "q-14121" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258087 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258087 1100 "Find all the real solutions to the equation $3{z}^{3}-{z}^{2}+27z-9=0.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258087 1113 #uuid "fe52eb5f-7e5b-51d0-b560-47f98d7ed314" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258087 1145 "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{3}-{z}^{2}+27z-9 & = \\\\ (3{z}^{3}-{z}^{2})+(27z-9) & = \\\\ {z}^{2}(3z-1)+9(3z-1) & = \\\\ (3z-1)\\cdot {z}^{2}+(3z-1)\\cdot 9 & = \\\\ (3z-1)({z}^{2}+9) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(3z-1)({z}^{2}+9)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(3z-1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z-1 & =0 \\\\ 3z & =1 \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({z}^{2}+9)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince a squared real number cannot be negative, the equation ${z}^{2}+9=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore,  $z=\\frac{1}{3}$ is the only real solution." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258088 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258089 13194139533864 true] [17592186258088 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258088 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258088 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258089 13194139533864 true] [17592186258088 1110 #uuid "214026e5-fe18-58f7-8562-0bab5c37ec87" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258089 1034 "\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258089 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258089 1111 #uuid "e0adb4eb-4e10-57f3-a3ac-5158e7bc1653" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258090 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258091 13194139533864 true] [17592186258090 1089 "q-141281" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258090 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258090 1100 "$\\operatorname{sin}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258090 1113 #uuid "4878f13e-2679-5b9e-8cae-9094f9bb2d55" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258090 1145 "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{cos}v+\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x-\\operatorname{sin}x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258091 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258092 13194139533864 true] [17592186258091 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258091 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258091 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258092 13194139533864 true] [17592186258091 1110 #uuid "9e30f645-54b0-5eae-b57e-34f9c4ffffff" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258092 1034 "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x-\\operatorname{sin}x}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258092 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258092 1111 #uuid "9f515b11-ff1f-54cd-bc3a-d7acaa75d91c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258093 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258094 13194139533864 true] [17592186258093 1089 "q-14130" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258093 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258093 1100 "Factor ${n}^{4}-{36}^{2}$ completely.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258093 1113 #uuid "d866302b-c2d5-51c8-89dc-11e9ed67ca2d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258093 1145 "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{n}^{4}-{36}^{2}={({n}^{2})}^{2}-{36}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{({n}^{2})}^{2}-{36}^{2}=({n}^{2}+36)({n}^{2}-36)\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares. Therefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({n}^{2}+36)({n}^{2}-36) & =({n}^{2}+36)({n}^{2}-{6}^{2}) \\\\  & =({n}^{2}+36)(n+6)(n-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258094 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258095 13194139533864 true] [17592186258094 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258094 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258094 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258095 13194139533864 true] [17592186258094 1110 #uuid "9dfc8025-55f3-55e5-ba54-9cc9e891ef17" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258095 1034 "({n}^{2}+36)(n+6)(n-6)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258095 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258095 1111 #uuid "cf3e47f4-a46a-599a-b9cb-8d68865b7590" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258096 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258097 13194139533864 true] [17592186258096 1089 "q-14132" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258096 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258096 1100 "Factor $8{z}^{4}-128$ completely.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258096 1113 #uuid "22734e95-1c9b-50eb-bd94-6cf73f4dd740" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258096 1145 "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that all of the terms in our expression have a common factor of $8.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $8,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n8{z}^{4}-128=8\\cdot {z}^{4}-8\\cdot 16=8({z}^{4}-16)\n$$\n\nThe expression in parentheses can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n8({z}^{4}-16)=8({({z}^{2})}^{2}-{4}^{2})\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n8({({z}^{2})}^{2}-{4}^{2})=8({z}^{2}+4)({z}^{2}-4)\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares. Therefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8({z}^{2}+4)({z}^{2}-4) & =8({z}^{2}+4)({z}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =8({z}^{2}+4)(z+2)(z-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258097 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258098 13194139533864 true] [17592186258097 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258097 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258097 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258098 13194139533864 true] [17592186258097 1110 #uuid "6ceeb43f-62a0-5bdb-9a18-d593fd2190eb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258098 1034 "8({z}^{2}+4)(z+2)(z-2)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258098 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258098 1111 #uuid "c1664f40-d711-57c0-94df-dc786b1f8199" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258099 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258100 13194139533864 true] [17592186258099 1089 "q-14137" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258099 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258099 1100 "Factor $243-3{x}^{4}$ completely.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258099 1113 #uuid "0260538c-f491-56b1-ab37-bac54b1dad98" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258099 1145 "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that all of the terms have a common factor of $3.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n243-3{x}^{4}=3\\cdot 81-3\\cdot {x}^{4}=3(81-{x}^{4})\n$$\n\nThe expression in parentheses can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n3(81-{x}^{4})=3({9}^{2}-{({x}^{2})}^{2})\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n3({9}^{2}-{({x}^{2})}^{2})=3(9+{x}^{2})(9-{x}^{2})\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares. Therefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(9+{x}^{2})(9-{x}^{2}) & =3(9+{x}^{2})({3}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =3(9+{x}^{2})(3+x)(3-x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258100 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258101 13194139533864 true] [17592186258100 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258100 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258100 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258101 13194139533864 true] [17592186258100 1110 #uuid "fc52295b-34ed-5380-8dfe-34b13cdffe21" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258101 1034 "3(9+{x}^{2})(3+x)(3-x)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258101 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258101 1111 #uuid "ea150614-6020-58e8-8084-1b4e7ab56aaf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258102 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258103 13194139533864 true] [17592186258102 1089 "q-141380" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258102 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258102 1100 "$\\operatorname{cos}(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258102 1113 #uuid "4e30af5f-93af-52ef-810a-d66a6247d423" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258102 1145 "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{cos}v-\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot 0-\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258103 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258104 13194139533864 true] [17592186258103 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258103 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258103 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258104 13194139533864 true] [17592186258103 1110 #uuid "55521609-be41-59e3-8855-f2ba75ddf06e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258104 1034 "-\\operatorname{sin}x" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258104 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258104 1111 #uuid "b38492a4-3e16-5cd9-ab86-d08e043362ee" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258105 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258106 13194139533864 true] [17592186258105 1089 "q-14140" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258105 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258105 1100 "For the polynomial $f(x)=5{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-x+12,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258105 1113 #uuid "e2543d42-472f-5dce-a205-70e0b82fc936" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258105 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $12,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6$ and $\\pm 12.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $5,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 5.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{12}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 6, & \\pm \\frac{6}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 12, & \\pm \\frac{12}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. Listing those potential roots in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{4}{5},\\,\\pm 1\\,\\pm \\frac{6}{5},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm \\frac{12}{5},\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 12.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $24$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 12=24.$)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258106 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258107 13194139533864 true] [17592186258106 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258106 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258106 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258107 13194139533864 true] [17592186258106 1110 #uuid "91cda7c9-e9f2-5ab9-99fe-1f74848e6195" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258107 1034 "24" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258107 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258107 1111 #uuid "97261046-84b7-54d0-ae71-920ee699e7e9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258108 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258109 13194139533864 true] [17592186258108 1089 "q-141474" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258108 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258108 1100 "Simplify $\\sqrt{18{v}^{7}}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258108 1113 #uuid "cd088633-afad-5cbc-8832-da4bf83a4756" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258108 1145 "We first write $18{v}^{7}$ as the product $9{v}^{6}\\cdot 2v$ which contains a perfect square $(9{v}^{6}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{18{v}^{7}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{9{v}^{6}\\cdot 2v} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we separate the product into two separate radicals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{9{v}^{6}\\cdot 2v} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{(3{v}^{3})}^{2}\\cdot 2v} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{(3{v}^{3})}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2v} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the radical containing the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(3{v}^{3})}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2v} & = \\\\ |3{v}^{3}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2v} & = \\\\ |3|\\cdot |{v}^{3}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2v} & = \\\\ 3|v{|}^{3}\\sqrt{2v}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258109 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258110 13194139533864 true] [17592186258109 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258109 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258109 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258110 13194139533864 true] [17592186258109 1110 #uuid "318c1c0e-6690-5da8-9133-9a83baf3541c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258110 1034 "3|v{|}^{3}\\sqrt{2v}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258110 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258110 1111 #uuid "d30286ce-9e42-547d-8b36-ceefcd4a7d6d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258111 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258112 13194139533864 true] [17592186258111 1089 "q-14152" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258111 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258111 1100 "What is the distance between the points $(-\\sqrt{3},2\\sqrt{3})$ and $(3\\sqrt{3},5\\sqrt{3})?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258111 1113 #uuid "e6b0a5be-d880-5387-a7aa-c743ad533f16" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258111 1145 "In this case, we have ${{(-\\sqrt{3},2\\sqrt{3})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(3\\sqrt{3},5\\sqrt{3})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\nSo, the distance $d$ can be calculated using the distance formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(3\\sqrt{3}-(-\\sqrt{3}))}^{2}+{(5\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4\\sqrt{3})^{2}+(3\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16(3)+9(3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48+27} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{75} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258112 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258113 13194139533864 true] [17592186258112 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258112 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258112 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258113 13194139533864 true] [17592186258112 1110 #uuid "23c56db0-c487-5ad8-8d42-607d7b438555" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258113 1034 "5\\sqrt{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258113 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258113 1111 #uuid "eb92eab9-fe3e-51ee-9863-781454b2fb5d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258114 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258115 13194139533864 true] [17592186258114 1089 "q-141615" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258114 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258114 1100 "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${x}^{2}-12x+28.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258114 1113 #uuid "ec2a46d4-91b6-5d7c-83d9-c6d69abedfb0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258114 1145 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+28\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2},$ writing the constant term $28$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{6}^{2}+28\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+28\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}-8.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258115 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258116 13194139533864 true] [17592186258115 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258115 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258115 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258116 13194139533864 true] [17592186258115 1110 #uuid "15f5cc2f-a333-5375-b493-ddb51c74c61f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258116 1034 "(x-6)^{2}-8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258116 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258116 1111 #uuid "9a95e18a-ab4f-581f-bed2-a542bf0c822f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258117 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258118 13194139533864 true] [17592186258117 1089 "q-141752" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258117 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258117 1100 "Given the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following transformations in the order given:\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $8$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\n- Translate it to the left by $10$ units\n\nWhat is the final function expression after applying all the transformations?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258117 1113 #uuid "59de6ac0-9ea0-5370-a7b1-5aef5cb4d796" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258117 1145 "To find the expression, let's apply each of the transformations in turn.\n\n- Stretching the graph of $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $8$ parallel to the $y$-axis is equivalent to multiplying the function expression by $8.$ Applying this transformation, we obtain \n\n$$\ny=8f(x).\n$$\n\n- Stretching the graph of $y=8f(x)$ by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $x$-axis is equivalent to replacing every instance of $x$ with $\\frac{1}{5}x.$ Applying this transformation, we obtain \n\n$$\ny=8f(\\frac{1}{5}x)=8f(\\frac{x}{5}).\n$$\n\n- Translating the graph of $y=8f(\\frac{x}{5})$ by $10$ units to the left is equivalent to replacing every instance of $x$ with $(x+10).$ Applying this transformation, we obtain \n\n$$\ny=8f(\\frac{1}{5}(x+10))=8f(\\frac{x}{5}+2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final function expression is $y=8f(\\frac{x}{5}+2).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258118 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258119 13194139533864 true] [17592186258118 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258118 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258118 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258119 13194139533864 true] [17592186258118 1110 #uuid "95786ca4-328f-5f58-a585-2d5cdfd353d4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258119 1034 "8f(\\frac{x}{5}+2)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258119 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258119 1111 #uuid "a65a79cf-98a7-589e-b027-bc617ec84bff" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258120 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258121 13194139533864 true] [17592186258120 1089 "q-14177" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258120 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258120 1100 "Determine whether the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}\\sqrt{n}}$\nconverges or diverges.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258120 1113 #uuid "a6a632e6-465f-5f69-9c0a-6eab5a5d00ca" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258120 1145 "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2+1/2}}=2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/2}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{2}.$ Since $p>1$, the series is convergent. \n\nNote that the factor of $2$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence of the series." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258121 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258122 13194139533864 true] [17592186258121 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258121 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258121 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258122 13194139533864 true] [17592186258121 1110 #uuid "9bdd2145-2ded-52c3-b907-960924b52d8d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258122 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258122 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258122 1111 #uuid "4ccc69ce-cc94-5927-8983-c3352e5d71af" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258123 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258124 13194139533864 true] [17592186258123 1089 "q-14181" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258123 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258123 1100 "Determine whether the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[8]{{n}^{5}}}$\nconverges or diverges.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258123 1113 #uuid "b428fa76-e87c-5e0d-99bc-e0b26ffcdc6e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258123 1145 "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/8}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{8}.$ Since $p\\le 1$, the series is divergent. \n\nNote that the factor of $3$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence \nof the series." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258124 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258125 13194139533864 true] [17592186258124 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258124 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258124 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258125 13194139533864 true] [17592186258124 1110 #uuid "6ce413f5-be82-5903-af44-36b68c1aca65" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258125 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258125 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258125 1111 #uuid "0977bb5b-ffae-5c4b-bba5-09591162b930" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258126 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258127 13194139533864 true] [17592186258126 1089 "q-14183" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258126 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258126 1100 "Determine whether the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{\\sqrt[7]{{n}^{6}}}$\nconverges or diverges.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258126 1113 #uuid "406bddc0-a5c1-5c9c-a58e-9ea56b088d28" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258126 1145 "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n9{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{6/7}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{6}{7}.$ Since $p\\le 1$, the series is divergent. \n\nNote that the factor of $9$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence \nof the series." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258127 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258128 13194139533864 true] [17592186258127 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258127 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258127 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258128 13194139533864 true] [17592186258127 1110 #uuid "c8af9183-c36f-5740-b5e0-1eae179145e4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258128 1034 "9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258128 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258128 1111 #uuid "9fbe7c5f-0d0f-5cb0-8356-4e360240f7cc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258129 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258130 13194139533864 true] [17592186258129 1089 "q-14186" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258129 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258129 1100 "Determine whether the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{n}}$\nconverges or diverges.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258129 1113 #uuid "60fbd2f4-e4f7-5263-9d84-acda6fd29fa9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258129 1145 "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n9{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{1/2}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Since $p\\le 1$, the series is divergent. \n\nNote that the factor of $9$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence \nof the series." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258130 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258131 13194139533864 true] [17592186258130 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258130 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258130 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258131 13194139533864 true] [17592186258130 1110 #uuid "6a118c03-c82c-5554-b440-65048e0ca485" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258131 1034 "9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258131 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258131 1111 #uuid "4f2b368c-30ff-533a-a028-f9520127f7c9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258132 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258133 13194139533864 true] [17592186258132 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258135 13194139533864 true] [17592186258132 1089 "q-142979" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258132 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258132 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/f065f31c629cbd21a6beffe013c8786a4e8165f6dff09bde72cd3e61c8ae8a62.png)\n\nThe function $f$ is part of the exponential curve $y={e}^{x-a}+b$ shown in the figure above. Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,b\\,=\\,$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258132 1113 #uuid "db023f5c-493e-5e6a-b5ba-9c2ad4393c03" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258132 1145 "Recall that the horizontal asymptote of $y={e}^{x-a}+b$ is $y=b.$ \n\nIn the figure above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=3.$ Hence, $b=3.$\n\nNext, we use the point $(5,4)$ to determine the value of $a.$\nSubstituting $(5,4)$ into the equation of the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5-a}+3 & =4 \\\\ {e}^{5-a} & =1 \\\\ 5-a & =0 \\\\ a & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=5$ and $b=3.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258133 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258134 13194139533864 true] [17592186258133 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258133 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258133 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258134 13194139533864 true] [17592186258133 1110 #uuid "f3a192a2-9343-5383-8e4b-3367a8cbe673" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258134 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258134 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258134 1111 #uuid "51c66fe2-55c0-5652-b399-9a83c90d5f1f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258135 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258136 13194139533864 true] [17592186258135 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258135 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258135 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258136 13194139533864 true] [17592186258135 1110 #uuid "85e2c635-2fde-5105-9f91-bec0aaf59d2f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258136 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258136 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258136 1111 #uuid "457b69e5-fe27-5686-a4ce-b0533ec2b17a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258137 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258138 13194139533864 true] [17592186258137 1089 "q-142980" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258137 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258137 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/cced23601cd4ccb10839c503dc8068c7edf9a4f9daa2f83d2263c37abd7fe86d.png)\n\nThe function $g$ is part of the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{sec}x+3.$ Find the exact value of $y$ when $x=\\frac{13\\pi }{6}.$\n\n$y\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258137 1113 #uuid "b8b028ef-fe60-50c8-bb59-4be283218d8c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258137 1145 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{13\\pi }{6}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13\\pi }{6}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{13\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $y=\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+3=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}+3.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258138 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258139 13194139533864 true] [17592186258138 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258138 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258138 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258139 13194139533864 true] [17592186258138 1110 #uuid "3c4d5cb5-6452-5b82-a82b-bf7b5029a030" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258139 1034 "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}+3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258139 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258139 1111 #uuid "b8c984c4-5b8a-578c-9f32-dd7cb5796e74" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258140 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258141 13194139533864 true] [17592186258140 1089 "q-142991" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258140 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258140 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/e1ada2941e9819e75218f9995d1913d535889c71b29efbf39ef73d91f9113205.png)\n\nThe function $h$ is a part of the graph of the function $y=\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}+3,$ as shown in the figure above. Find the number of weeks $x$ at which the stock price given by this function was $$5,000.$\n\n*Hint: Remember that $h$ is measured in thousands of dollars.*\n\n$x\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258140 1113 #uuid "0ad51429-6a62-5eb4-a98f-df87784728a5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258140 1145 "We have to solve $h(x)=5,$ or equivalently, $\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}+3=5.$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term $\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}$ by subtracting $3$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}+3 & =5 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we isolate the square root by multiplying by $\\frac{3}{2}$ on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x} & =2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =3 \\\\ (\\sqrt{x})^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ x & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{9}+3 & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3+3 & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ 2+3 & =5\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=9$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}+3=5$  is $x=9.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258141 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258142 13194139533864 true] [17592186258141 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258141 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258141 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258142 13194139533864 true] [17592186258141 1110 #uuid "d4672edf-63c4-5c05-b468-eef9e9e3cb2a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258142 1034 "9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258142 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258142 1111 #uuid "1128e265-7219-5e31-98f1-7cc944d3f363" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258143 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258144 13194139533864 true] [17592186258143 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258146 13194139533864 true] [17592186258143 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258148 13194139533864 true] [17592186258143 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258150 13194139533864 true] [17592186258143 1089 "q-142992" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258143 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258143 1100 "What is the largest union of intervals on which the function describing the stock price is continuous?\n\n$x\\in [\\,\\,0\\,\\,,$\n{{field-1}}$)\\cup $\n$($\n{{field-2}}$\\,,$\n{{field-3}}$)\\cup $\n$($\n{{field-4}}$\\,,\\,\\infty ).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258143 1113 #uuid "9a1d8e21-fdef-5be3-a0f9-7a1cd257412c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258143 1145 "The function is continuous on the open intervals $(0,5.4)$, $(5.4,7.2)$ and $(7.2,\\infty ).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=0.$ Hence, it is continuous on the interval $[0,5.4).$\n\nTherefore, we can say that the function is continuous on $x\\in [0,5.4)\\cup (5.4,7.2)\\cup (7.2,\\infty ).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258144 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258145 13194139533864 true] [17592186258144 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258144 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258144 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258145 13194139533864 true] [17592186258144 1110 #uuid "9355f3c0-18a6-5b69-80a1-0364e00808cf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258145 1034 "5.4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258145 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258145 1111 #uuid "043022d2-842f-5a0d-a3e6-a7431fabc0f6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258146 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258147 13194139533864 true] [17592186258146 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258146 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258146 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258147 13194139533864 true] [17592186258146 1110 #uuid "1a799249-37a9-557d-b0c5-0430f1083a73" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258147 1034 "5.4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258147 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258147 1111 #uuid "e5bf4fa7-65cf-5ee1-a4bf-f773b42ad1bb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258148 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258149 13194139533864 true] [17592186258148 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258148 1041 "field-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258148 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258149 13194139533864 true] [17592186258148 1110 #uuid "879e8785-e2e8-5bd7-be03-6e8a499faf90" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258149 1034 "7.2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258149 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258149 1111 #uuid "55261f6a-c49b-5feb-8d2c-d4962026c724" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258150 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258151 13194139533864 true] [17592186258150 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258150 1041 "field-4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258150 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258151 13194139533864 true] [17592186258150 1110 #uuid "1298d7e4-33e3-5bbc-94f9-f7a723b0fb25" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258151 1034 "7.2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258151 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258151 1111 #uuid "e76051ca-b880-583d-9253-e1380d6980a3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258152 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258153 13194139533864 true] [17592186258152 1089 "q-142993" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258152 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258152 1100 "Find the derivative of $h(x).$\n\n${h}^{'}(x)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258152 1113 #uuid "757fea48-6784-5515-8c79-d693e955e14d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258152 1145 "We apply the sum, constant multiple, and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{(\\frac{1}{2}-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258153 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258154 13194139533864 true] [17592186258153 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258153 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258153 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258154 13194139533864 true] [17592186258153 1110 #uuid "4617226c-d552-56ab-9756-b1e85f92a465" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258154 1034 "\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{x}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258154 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258154 1111 #uuid "640c48ea-eca5-5f5c-bd9f-649c0461679e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258155 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258156 13194139533864 true] [17592186258155 1089 "q-142994" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258155 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258155 1100 "What was the exact rate of change of the stock's value at week $9?$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ thousand dollars per week." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258155 1113 #uuid "3b81ed99-9a3f-5dfe-84ce-7ea4d4959a5c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258155 1145 "The function ${h}^{'}(x)$ models the rate of change of the value of the stock, applicable in the domain of $h(x).$\n\nThus, the rate of change of the value of the stock at $x=9$ weeks is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{'}(9) & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change in the stock price was $\\frac{1}{9}$ thousand dollars per week." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258156 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258157 13194139533864 true] [17592186258156 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258156 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258156 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258157 13194139533864 true] [17592186258156 1110 #uuid "3f7aaad1-78f8-517c-9f4a-76f17a311294" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258157 1034 "1/9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258157 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258157 1111 #uuid "2e3b4618-9a7e-5db9-979c-581e6614ef24" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258158 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258159 13194139533864 true] [17592186258158 1089 "q-143040" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258158 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258158 1100 "The broker predicts that, in the future, the stock will have an overall behavior similar to the function $\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[3]{x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258158 1113 #uuid "ec42b25b-8544-5fa5-b45c-8cc0ddc4cd34" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258158 1145 "Let $k(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=k(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/9b81a63c0681b10343cf600cdfc17636c797aeb9b15920da4368d26643827ab5.png)\n\nThe graph shows that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }k(x)=\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258159 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258160 13194139533864 true] [17592186258159 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258159 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258159 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258160 13194139533864 true] [17592186258159 1110 #uuid "a0e303ce-07a6-5eec-befe-7f409b2e02a1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258160 1034 "\\infty " 13194139533864 true] [17592186258160 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258160 1111 #uuid "810fcaa7-47c5-5b76-bae0-da51d895db9a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258161 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258162 13194139533864 true] [17592186258161 1089 "q-143041" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258161 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258161 1100 "The antiderivative of $\\sqrt[3]{x}$ equals{{field-1}} $+\\,\\text{ }C$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258161 1113 #uuid "5cd2d74d-d1cb-5152-8963-c0880a0c094a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258161 1145 "Since $\\sqrt[3]{x}={x}^{1/3}$, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/3+1}}{(\\frac{1}{3}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4/3}}{(\\frac{4}{3})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check our answer by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4/3})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}{x}^{4/3-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{1/3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258162 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258163 13194139533864 true] [17592186258162 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258162 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258162 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258163 13194139533864 true] [17592186258162 1110 #uuid "97e4b732-93bd-528c-a1cf-2c1d29dca6b8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258163 1034 "\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258163 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258163 1111 #uuid "fbd3b291-f315-505c-8814-812856363ced" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258164 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258165 13194139533864 true] [17592186258164 1089 "q-143182" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258164 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258164 1100 "Given that  ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(2n)!}{{5}^{n}}$ for $n\\ge 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}\\ge 1$ for all $n>2$\nII. - ${a}_{n}$ is an increasing sequence\nIII. - ${a}_{n}$ is a decreasing sequence" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258164 1113 #uuid "866beddf-cf9f-5dad-a386-56b1a393c58f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258164 1145 "Suppose the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is a positive sequence defined for $n\\ge 1,$ and that\n\n$$\nL=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- if $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *increasing,* and\n\n- if $0<L\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case, ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(2n)!}{{5}^{n}}$ is positive for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the ratio $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}} & =\\frac{(\\frac{(2n+2)!}{{5}^{n+1}})}{(\\frac{(2n)!}{{5}^{n}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{n}(2n+2)!}{{5}^{n+1}(2n)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{n}\\cdot (2n+2)\\cdot (2n+1)\\cdot (2n)!}{{5}^{n}\\cdot 5\\cdot (2n)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{5}^{n}}\\cdot (2n+2)\\cdot (2n+1)\\cdot \\cancel{(2n)!}}{\\cancel{{5}^{n}}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\cancel{(2n)!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2n+2)(2n+1)}{5} \\\\  & \\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement III is false. Since $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ we can conclude that the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258165 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258166 13194139533864 true] [17592186258165 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258167 13194139533864 true] [17592186258165 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258168 13194139533864 true] [17592186258165 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258169 13194139533864 true] [17592186258165 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258170 13194139533864 true] [17592186258165 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258171 13194139533864 true] [17592186258165 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258172 13194139533864 true] [17592186258165 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258173 13194139533864 true] [17592186258165 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186258165 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258165 1104 #edb/ref 17