omputing the ratio $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{n+2}}{{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{n+1}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{n+2-n-1} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & \\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, whereas statement III is true. Since $0<L\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ we can conclude that the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258265 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258266 13194139533864 true] [17592186258265 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258267 13194139533864 true] [17592186258265 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258268 13194139533864 true] [17592186258265 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258269 13194139533864 true] [17592186258265 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258270 13194139533864 true] [17592186258265 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258271 13194139533864 true] [17592186258265 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258272 13194139533864 true] [17592186258265 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258273 13194139533864 true] [17592186258265 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186258265 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258265 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258270 13194139533864 true] [17592186258265 1110 #uuid "7f57ff79-8f14-56a6-af82-5d31973cc519" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258266 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258266 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258266 1111 #uuid "39e0ba58-7aaf-5b8e-8c5a-d2500c58bc54" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258267 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258267 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258267 1111 #uuid "5165feff-7401-53b8-80a0-65318f5ffe09" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258268 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258268 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258268 1111 #uuid "4ec01e9d-e1b1-5873-a02c-c01826ec3679" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258269 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258269 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258269 1111 #uuid "968b1c70-b015-5c48-b573-1869a2dd4975" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258270 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258270 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258270 1111 #uuid "773829ba-691c-5578-99ea-15e910cb4322" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258271 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258271 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258271 1111 #uuid "07217c3b-a2dd-576c-9462-4d8745c86aac" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258272 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258272 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258272 1111 #uuid "90697352-34c8-5f6e-9bc4-731f9cd53c2c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258273 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258273 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258273 1111 #uuid "9d852a96-7a30-5d23-8d03-f302826e638f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258274 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258275 13194139533864 true] [17592186258274 1089 "q-143220" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258274 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258274 1100 "Given that  ${a}_{n}={(\\frac{7}{6})}^{n}$ for $n\\ge 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1$\nII. - ${a}_{n}$ is an increasing sequence\nIII. - ${a}_{n}$ is a decreasing sequence" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258274 1113 #uuid "27707620-ae31-5355-beb9-34dcaa2b3212" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258274 1145 "Suppose the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is a positive sequence defined for $n\\ge 1,$ and that\n\n$$\nL=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- if $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *increasing,* and\n\n- if $0<L\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case, ${a}_{n}={(\\frac{7}{6})}^{n}$ is positive for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the ratio $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{7}{6})}^{n+1}}{{(\\frac{7}{6})}^{n}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{7}{6})}^{n+1-n} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{7}{6})}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6} \\\\  & \\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement III is false. Since $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ we can conclude that the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258275 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258276 13194139533864 true] [17592186258275 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258277 13194139533864 true] [17592186258275 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258278 13194139533864 true] [17592186258275 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258279 13194139533864 true] [17592186258275 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258280 13194139533864 true] [17592186258275 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258281 13194139533864 true] [17592186258275 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258282 13194139533864 true] [17592186258275 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258283 13194139533864 true] [17592186258275 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186258275 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258275 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258281 13194139533864 true] [17592186258275 1110 #uuid "64a9483f-15e7-582a-8f50-d98770d357aa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258276 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258276 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258276 1111 #uuid "1da15512-9141-5658-adc0-2d796eabc0be" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258277 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258277 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258277 1111 #uuid "23ce18da-bf69-52ea-aee1-4c548b5d7805" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258278 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258278 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258278 1111 #uuid "76758739-ba80-5f82-b136-044fff3dbf5b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258279 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258279 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258279 1111 #uuid "816d8401-ff89-562c-ae81-7b16d2a5bfcf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258280 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258280 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258280 1111 #uuid "323b0993-1575-5b72-888c-f46d1ee98748" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258281 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258281 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258281 1111 #uuid "1e01d71b-4a97-5dd2-989a-7f7537d1b14c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258282 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258282 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258282 1111 #uuid "2b4c4a50-ca75-5aaf-8889-0aa4c0f2819e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258283 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258283 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258283 1111 #uuid "d2a2de85-3c1d-53ef-b1a4-3dca83582c5d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258284 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258285 13194139533864 true] [17592186258284 1089 "q-143365" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258284 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258284 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/469d229fba26a06ae27dd6014230124cc4b379c72d5cc8aa8b6ab4d026903eeb.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $-8+6x-{x}^{2}\\ge 0.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258284 1113 #uuid "f22377a6-66a6-5350-ac0e-892b1407ed25" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258284 1145 "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n-8+6x-{x}^{2}\\ge 0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*greater than or equal to*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-8+6x-{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nis *above* the $x$-axis or *touches* this axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/2929d99093fa5c658059ce0470e5d1e6fa293dcad3a8a5eff1aa83fc05d0a4c3.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $2\\le x\\le 4.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258285 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258286 13194139533864 true] [17592186258285 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258285 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258285 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258286 13194139533864 true] [17592186258285 1110 #uuid "2aa6335f-3929-5d9d-8d32-c909af10b54e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258286 1034 "2\\le x\\le 4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258286 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258286 1111 #uuid "2ab9002a-da67-5693-8558-10c77f908601" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258287 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258288 13194139533864 true] [17592186258287 1089 "q-143516" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258287 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258287 1100 "The acceleration $a(t)$ of the particle is given by the derivative of the velocity with respect to time. Find the function $a(t).$\n\n$a(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}t$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258287 1113 #uuid "4df0d1f9-09b6-5d26-902c-aee8403ebe60" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258287 1145 "First, we write\n\n$$\nv(t)=4{csc}^{2}(t).\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $v(t)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nv=4{u}^{2},\\,u=\\operatorname{csc}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}u}=8u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=-\\operatorname{csc}t\\operatorname{cot}t.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & ={v}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =8u\\cdot (-\\operatorname{csc}t\\operatorname{cot}t) \\\\  & =(8\\operatorname{csc}t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{csc}t\\operatorname{cot}t) \\\\  & =-8{csc}^{2}t\\operatorname{cot}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258288 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258289 13194139533864 true] [17592186258288 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258288 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258288 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258289 13194139533864 true] [17592186258288 1110 #uuid "fb6cc1aa-ac01-556d-8d95-93c1477f94a4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258289 1034 "-8{csc}^{2}t" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258289 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258289 1111 #uuid "0dd98825-1fa3-5ba1-a06e-26ffa83da92f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258290 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258291 13194139533864 true] [17592186258290 1089 "q-143518" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258290 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258290 1100 "Find the acceleration function ${a}_{2}(t)$ of the second particle.\n\n${a}_{2}(t)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258290 1113 #uuid "55d75440-a427-575a-b46b-23b4efdc3e82" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258290 1145 "In part 3, we saw that the derivative of a particle's velocity with respect to time gives its acceleration.\n\nTo differentiate ${v}_{2}(t),$ we require the product rule. Let $g(t)={t}^{2}$ and $h(t)=\\operatorname{sin}(3t).$ Then\n\n$$\n{g}^{'}(t)=2t,\\,{h}^{'}(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}(3t).\n$$\n\nWe used the chain rule to calculate ${h}^{'}(t).$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2}(t) & ={v}_{2}^{'}(t) \\\\  & ={g}^{'}(t)\\cdot h(t)+g(t)\\cdot {h}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(2t)(\\operatorname{sin}(3t))+({t}^{2})(3\\operatorname{cos}(3t)) \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{sin}(3t)+3{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258291 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258292 13194139533864 true] [17592186258291 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258291 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258291 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258292 13194139533864 true] [17592186258291 1110 #uuid "173cbd5e-78dc-5814-8e0d-82fe30df3d42" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258292 1034 "2t\\operatorname{sin}(3t)+3{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}(3t)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258292 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258292 1111 #uuid "845a858e-1ff4-54b5-aac1-289eca93d5e5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258293 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258294 13194139533864 true] [17592186258293 1089 "q-143522" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258293 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258293 1100 "$\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\,\\,=\\,${{field-1}} $+\\text{ }C$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258293 1113 #uuid "89c0e22d-f3ea-5464-882b-c9952565de77" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258293 1145 "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int {csc}^{2}t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{cot}t+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{csc}^{2}t\\,\\text{d}t & =4\\int {csc}^{2}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4(-\\operatorname{cot}t)+C \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{cot}t+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258294 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258295 13194139533864 true] [17592186258294 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258294 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258294 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258295 13194139533864 true] [17592186258294 1110 #uuid "2e4da863-25d4-55d1-8bff-f6bed2de2493" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258295 1034 "-4\\operatorname{cot}t" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258295 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258295 1111 #uuid "2dcfe0d3-17ce-5fc9-9e65-220f684bdd89" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258296 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258297 13194139533864 true] [17592186258296 1089 "q-143531" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258296 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258296 1100 "Find the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n$v(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\text{m/s}$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258296 1113 #uuid "6a57fe21-061f-551f-9781-c27e61809783" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258296 1145 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating $v(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ we have\n\n$$\n4{csc}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=4{(2)}^{2}=16.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258297 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258298 13194139533864 true] [17592186258297 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258297 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258297 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258298 13194139533864 true] [17592186258297 1110 #uuid "ca8409c4-5999-5423-bfeb-c04e340e43e8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258298 1034 "16" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258298 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258298 1111 #uuid "b5da6754-0d94-5766-8532-2c9f59a970a5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258299 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258300 13194139533864 true] [17592186258299 1089 "q-143534" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258299 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258299 1100 "Another particle has a velocity function given by ${v}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}(3t).$ Evaluate the velocity of this particle at $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\n${v}_{2}(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\text{m/s}$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258299 1113 #uuid "8a118ade-6d19-5620-b450-e584298e740f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258299 1145 "Evaluating the function ${v}_{2}(t)$ at $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$, we get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})={(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nFirst, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{5\\pi }{2}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\pi }{2}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})=1.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})={(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})=\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258300 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258301 13194139533864 true] [17592186258300 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258300 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258300 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258301 13194139533864 true] [17592186258300 1110 #uuid "49ba34d1-c6aa-52ee-a5b5-4dc69fb147c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258301 1034 "\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258301 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258301 1111 #uuid "64c80601-24aa-52a1-9fc6-11143ee6411a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258302 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258303 13194139533864 true] [17592186258302 1089 "q-143539" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258302 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258302 1100 "Find ${lim}_{t\\to \\pi /6}{v}_{2}(t)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258302 1113 #uuid "3d6ada42-4014-5f9a-a482-4f54294eb213" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258302 1145 "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\pi /6}{v}_{2}(t) & ={lim}_{t\\to \\pi /6}{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}(3t) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{36}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258303 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258304 13194139533864 true] [17592186258303 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258303 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258303 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258304 13194139533864 true] [17592186258303 1110 #uuid "b6e5b8b9-3638-59f2-aa45-251eb4e8fb35" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258304 1034 "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258304 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258304 1111 #uuid "7bd3bb56-f3ce-5393-bd36-fdcff5225992" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258305 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258306 13194139533864 true] [17592186258305 1089 "q-143540" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258305 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258305 1100 "${lim}_{t\\to {\\pi }^{-}}v(t)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258305 1113 #uuid "2fa4ce58-465a-5d0b-b7f5-902a5179526d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258305 1145 "Substituting $t=\\pi $ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to {\\pi }^{-}}v(t) & ={lim}_{t\\to {\\pi }^{-}}4{csc}^{2}(t) \\\\  & =4{csc}^{2}(\\pi ) \\\\  & =4\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}t.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/361e8bf3b622fc2e73be712dae4ec5d189bd15eecbd33c9295db7365bcf71943.png)\n\nAs $t$ approaches $\\pi $ from the left, the values of $y=\\operatorname{csc}t$ are positive and grow without bound. \n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to {\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}t=\\infty \n$$\n\nApplying the product and power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to {\\pi }^{-}}4{csc}^{2}(t) & ={lim}_{t\\to {\\pi }^{-}}4\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to {\\pi }^{-}}{csc}^{2}(t) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {({lim}_{t\\to {\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}(t))}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying by the positive constant $4$ does not change the sign of the limit, so we have\n\n$$\n4\\cdot {({lim}_{t\\to {\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}(t))}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258306 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258307 13194139533864 true] [17592186258306 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258306 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258306 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258307 13194139533864 true] [17592186258306 1110 #uuid "37ed01ef-bd31-587f-b264-6569a62f6cbc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258307 1034 "\\infty " 13194139533864 true] [17592186258307 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258307 1111 #uuid "6586bca5-be1d-57ac-8fe2-c59efbb1f408" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258308 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258309 13194139533864 true] [17592186258308 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258311 13194139533864 true] [17592186258308 1089 "q-14358" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258308 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258308 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/781f3a15f9c0b8ac1d43e72f0911bc810dc9829f37bf64094a9d1d48f8a7e1f1.png)\n\nFind the midpoint of the line segment shown above.\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258308 1113 #uuid "06a9a618-4104-5c38-8036-d75acee7cee4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258308 1145 "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between the given points ${{(7,-4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(-3,6)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{7-3}{2},\\frac{-4+6}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{2},\\frac{2}{2}) \\\\  & =(2,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258309 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258310 13194139533864 true] [17592186258309 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258309 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258309 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258310 13194139533864 true] [17592186258309 1110 #uuid "57926a54-2a90-573e-9bac-f527f0037ebc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258310 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258310 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258310 1111 #uuid "6747ecdd-9afe-5723-ad80-bae5a0753706" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258311 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258312 13194139533864 true] [17592186258311 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258311 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258311 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258312 13194139533864 true] [17592186258311 1110 #uuid "b15efcc2-8aec-5cd7-8978-77caf324cd6d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258312 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258312 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258312 1111 #uuid "b8e9e03c-28e2-5529-876b-ca1c1e0944d0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258313 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258314 13194139533864 true] [17592186258313 1089 "q-14365" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258313 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258313 1100 "If the midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(7,-4)$ and $(a,b)$ is $(2,1),$\nthen $a+b=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258313 1113 #uuid "5d86f908-7737-5796-8d80-bd80a4b73f99" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258313 1145 "The given points are ${{(7,-4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})},$ ${{(a,b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and ${{(2,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}\\,.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\ (2,1) & =(\\frac{7+a}{2},\\frac{-4+b}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding $x$- and $y$-coordinates in the equation above gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2=\\frac{7+a}{2} \\\\ 1=\\frac{-4+b}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\Rightarrow \\,{\\begin{aligned}a=-3 \\\\ b=6.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $(a,b)=(-3,6)$ and $a+b=3.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258314 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258315 13194139533864 true] [17592186258314 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258314 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258314 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258315 13194139533864 true] [17592186258314 1110 #uuid "aec5bcbf-664d-5504-9bb2-d2b854602e61" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258315 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258315 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258315 1111 #uuid "be6ccc92-c990-5058-ada1-3e61078deb9b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258316 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258317 13194139533864 true] [17592186258316 1089 "q-14388" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258316 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258316 1100 "Given the recursive sequence $f(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n)$ with $f(1)=1$ and $f(2)=3$, what is the value of $f(7)?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258316 1113 #uuid "b8589e67-69ad-5bf0-a4d3-99bbd55f2e6c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258316 1145 "The recursion relation is\n\n$$\nf(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1+2) & =f(1+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =f(2)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =3+1 \\\\ f(3) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=2$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2+2) & =f(2+1)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & =f(3)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & =4+3 \\\\ f(4) & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, substituting $n=3,4,5$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5)= & f(3+2) & =f(3+1)+f(3)=f(4)+f(3)=7+4=11, \\\\ f(6)= & f(4+2) & =f(4+1)+f(4)=f(5)+f(4)=11+7=18, \\\\ f(7)= & f(5+2) & =f(5+1)+f(5)=f(6)+f(5)=18+11=29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(7)=29.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258317 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258318 13194139533864 true] [17592186258317 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258317 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258317 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258318 13194139533864 true] [17592186258317 1110 #uuid "41bc5adc-5b0b-54f7-8e1e-d70f29a06994" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258318 1034 "29" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258318 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258318 1111 #uuid "b2799b7b-24b4-5d16-80c2-ac7ae521d926" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258319 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258320 13194139533864 true] [17592186258319 1089 "q-144195" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258319 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258319 1100 "A cube has a side length of $50\\,\\text{yd}.$ What is the volume of the cube?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258319 1113 #uuid "a41776af-70f9-5b80-bd56-808b6173a158" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258319 1145 "To find the volume $V$ of a cube, we cube its side length. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =(50\\,\\text{yd})^{3} \\\\  & =50\\,\\text{yd}\\times 50\\,\\text{yd}\\times 50\\,\\text{yd} \\\\  & =(50\\times 50\\times 50)\\times (\\text{yd}\\times \\text{yd}\\times \\text{yd}) \\\\  & =125\\,000\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258320 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258321 13194139533864 true] [17592186258320 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258320 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258320 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258321 13194139533864 true] [17592186258320 1110 #uuid "24b7b85e-3e22-57e0-bc6a-a83dfb2f8f57" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258321 1034 "125\\,000\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258321 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258321 1111 #uuid "212eb15e-a3d4-5572-9f85-da6667c67885" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258322 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258323 13194139533864 true] [17592186258322 1089 "q-14440" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258322 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258322 1100 "$\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-80}{x-5}÷\\frac{{x}^{2}-12x+32}{x-5}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258322 1113 #uuid "ce404e57-c40c-5213-a85b-c5fc9098182f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258322 1145 "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-80}{x-5}÷\\frac{{x}^{2}-12x+32}{x-5},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-80}{x-5}÷\\frac{{x}^{2}-12x+32}{x-5} & = \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-80}{x-5}\\cdot \\frac{x-5}{{x}^{2}-12x+32} & = \\\\ \\frac{(x+10)(x-8)}{x-5}\\cdot \\frac{x-5}{(x-8)(x-4)} & = \\\\ \\frac{(x+10)\\cancel{(x-8)}}{\\cancel{(x-5)}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{(x-5)}}{(x-4)\\cancel{(x-8)}} & = \\\\ \\frac{x+10}{x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258323 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258324 13194139533864 true] [17592186258323 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258323 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258323 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258324 13194139533864 true] [17592186258323 1110 #uuid "93179367-4737-5a88-99f7-20cb8d3d440e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258324 1034 "\\frac{x+10}{x-4}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258324 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258324 1111 #uuid "5764bb25-d3d2-57bb-98c2-8cc14ad3013f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258325 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258326 13194139533864 true] [17592186258325 1089 "q-144415" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258325 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258325 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/89a11e09dcb47c6f445e4f7921e8d4670453b10b67be3921d5723728ee154381.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $3-2x-{x}^{2}<0.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258325 1113 #uuid "6488e91c-d0d1-5b12-999b-f3e91536690e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258325 1145 "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n3-2x-{x}^{2}<0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*less than*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola  \n\n$$\ny=3-2x-{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nis *below* the $x$-axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/a73a7db40f0469ff37c14b0bf52a35f28b08ebf8224ab5b86c97f72d5d12aae1.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x<-3$ or    $x>1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258326 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258327 13194139533864 true] [17592186258326 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258326 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258326 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258327 13194139533864 true] [17592186258326 1110 #uuid "cb626680-57c4-56f3-96de-36f87f97430c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258327 1034 "x>1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258327 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258327 1111 #uuid "99e84602-1398-5b4d-904a-8318edc97f15" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258328 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258329 13194139533864 true] [17592186258328 1089 "q-14461" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258328 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258328 1100 "$\\frac{16x-20}{3x-6}÷\\frac{28x-35}{3{x}^{2}-12x+12}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258328 1113 #uuid "4d658cd8-ccd0-50fb-b58a-ac8ee83638ac" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258328 1145 "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{16x-20}{3x-6}÷\\frac{28x-35}{3{x}^{2}-12x+12},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{16x-20}{3x-6}÷\\frac{28x-35}{3{x}^{2}-12x+12} & = \\\\ \\frac{16x-20}{3x-6}\\cdot \\frac{3{x}^{2}-12x+12}{28x-35} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(4x-5)}{3x-6}\\cdot \\frac{(3x-6)(x-2)}{7(4x-5)} & = \\\\ \\frac{4\\cancel{(4x-5)}}{\\cancel{(3x-6)}}\\cdot \\frac{(x-2)\\cancel{(3x-6)}}{7\\cancel{(4x-5)}} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(x-2)}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{4x-8}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258329 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258330 13194139533864 true] [17592186258329 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258329 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258329 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258330 13194139533864 true] [17592186258329 1110 #uuid "d03e19d7-ca4f-5fe1-a756-c538949fc63b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258330 1034 "\\frac{4x-8}{7}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258330 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258330 1111 #uuid "6b143f6a-e1f7-55e4-8fe2-01a39a272c0f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258331 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258332 13194139533864 true] [17592186258331 1089 "q-14527" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258331 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258331 1100 "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(0,\\,1)$ for the curve $y\\operatorname{cos}(x)=1.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258331 1113 #uuid "d727380b-a283-52ef-aecd-bfe58ab6a644" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258331 1145 "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $y\\operatorname{cos}(x)$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\operatorname{cos}(x)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}(x))+\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =0 \\\\ y(-\\operatorname{sin}(x))+\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y\\operatorname{sin}(x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y\\operatorname{sin}(x)}{\\operatorname{cos}(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(0,\\,1)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(0,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={\\frac{y\\operatorname{sin}(x)}{\\operatorname{cos}(x)}}_{(0,\\,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(0)}{\\operatorname{cos}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 0}{1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258332 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258333 13194139533864 true] [17592186258332 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258332 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258332 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258333 13194139533864 true] [17592186258332 1110 #uuid "c7dcdf7e-93c7-56a7-998e-b9fdf99520ff" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258333 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258333 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258333 1111 #uuid "2b0b3773-0d4b-5dc7-84da-e4733320c793" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258334 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258335 13194139533864 true] [17592186258334 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258337 13194139533864 true] [17592186258334 1089 "q-14614" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258334 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258334 1100 "The equation of a curve is $xy+{x}^{2}=1.$ The normal line at the point $(1,\\,0)$ intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258334 1113 #uuid "02036e78-1a4a-5e3b-a210-404f9710dd18" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258334 1145 "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(1,\\,0).$   Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{'},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy+{x}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y+2x & =0 \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-(2x+y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2x+y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,0)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(1,\\,0)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{2x+y}{x})}_{(1,\\,0)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\cdot 1+0}{1} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is ${m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{-2}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ We can write the equation of the normal at the point $(1,\\,0)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & ={m}^{'}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-0) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}(0-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258335 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258336 13194139533864 true] [17592186258335 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258335 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258335 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258336 13194139533864 true] [17592186258335 1110 #uuid "58ec7e67-5450-59cc-b81d-20c384c9bf65" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258336 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258336 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258336 1111 #uuid "7755163d-cfe4-5031-9fba-514ea674855d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258337 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258338 13194139533864 true] [17592186258337 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258337 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258337 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258338 13194139533864 true] [17592186258337 1110 #uuid "7c3887d1-2962-5f88-bbeb-3413a3009764" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258338 1034 "\\,-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258338 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258338 1111 #uuid "de3af54e-bd1a-5397-9399-69696aea8758" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258339 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258340 13194139533864 true] [17592186258339 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258342 13194139533864 true] [17592186258339 1089 "q-14697" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258339 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258339 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/dc45107eb076c0dc199718def7bb3a34b73ad61ca00bda61e09472a9ea589c84.png)\n\nThe equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$  represents a circle. The tangent line at the point $(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ on the circle intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258339 1113 #uuid "2cf6e87b-e52d-5fbd-84ff-b23aaed621ad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258339 1145 "First of all, we need to find the slope of the tangent line at the point $(\\sqrt{2},\\,\\sqrt{2}).$ \n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2x+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2x}{2y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\sqrt{2},\\,\\sqrt{2})$  is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(\\sqrt{2},\\,\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{x}{y})}_{(\\sqrt{2},\\,\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the tangent at the point $(\\sqrt{2},\\,\\sqrt{2})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-\\sqrt{2}) & =-1(x-\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ y & =-(x-\\sqrt{2})+\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this tangent line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(x-\\sqrt{2})+\\sqrt{2} \\\\ y & =-(0-\\sqrt{2})+\\sqrt{2} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2} \\\\ y & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,2\\sqrt{2}).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258340 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258341 13194139533864 true] [17592186258340 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258340 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258340 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258341 13194139533864 true] [17592186258340 1110 #uuid "cf936430-fd7e-59e3-b9d8-e58acbfdf8d1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258341 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258341 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258341 1111 #uuid "f5496481-da3a-5232-86a1-a8d68543f8c4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258342 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258343 13194139533864 true] [17592186258342 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258342 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258342 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258343 13194139533864 true] [17592186258342 1110 #uuid "2e8469ce-cf6a-5704-a82a-7c9d2074a871" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258343 1034 "\\,2\\sqrt{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258343 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258343 1111 #uuid "6c9953ff-73b4-518b-b4dc-26aa0d298c5b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258344 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258345 13194139533864 true] [17592186258344 1089 "q-148506" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258344 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258344 1100 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x+7}{5-x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258344 1113 #uuid "ec63a21d-e9a4-5b86-a387-1414787df3c7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258344 1145 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3x+7}{5-x}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3y+7}{5-y}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{3y+7}{5-y} \\\\ x(5-y) & =3y+7 \\\\ 5x-xy & =3y+7 \\\\ 5x-7 & =3y+xy \\\\ 5x-7 & =y(x+3) \\\\ y & =\\frac{5x-7}{x+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{5x-7}{x+3}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258345 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258346 13194139533864 true] [17592186258345 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258345 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258345 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258346 13194139533864 true] [17592186258345 1110 #uuid "8aac026b-957e-5875-a4f3-f0ae365ece68" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258346 1034 "\\frac{5x-7}{x+3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258346 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258346 1111 #uuid "5aff512e-d746-5224-8de6-f2bc4b8dcbfc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258347 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258348 13194139533864 true] [17592186258347 1089 "q-148512" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258347 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258347 1100 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{6-5x}{x+7}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258347 1113 #uuid "76ebff41-4d22-5f4e-ae10-0f3c0f4254bf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258347 1145 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{6-5x}{x+7}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{6-5y}{y+7}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{6-5y}{y+7} \\\\ x(y+7) & =6-5y \\\\ xy+7x & =6-5y \\\\ xy+5y & =6-7x \\\\ y(x+5) & =6-7x \\\\ y & =\\frac{6-7x}{x+5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{6-7x}{x+5}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258348 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258349 13194139533864 true] [17592186258348 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258348 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258348 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258349 13194139533864 true] [17592186258348 1110 #uuid "c4cde231-fd87-59ce-aa12-eb586f071643" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258349 1034 "\\frac{6-7x}{x+5}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258349 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258349 1111 #uuid "6a95c077-c625-57fa-9f6e-37918d6af117" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258350 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258351 13194139533864 true] [17592186258350 1089 "q-148536" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258350 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258350 1100 "The function $f(x)=\\frac{k}{3x+5}$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{2-5x}{3x}.$ What is the value of $k?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258350 1113 #uuid "1ffc5e8a-01bf-55e5-8b55-8aa82dd13af8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258350 1145 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{3x+5}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{k}{3y+5}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{k}{3y+5} \\\\ x(3y+5) & =k \\\\ 3xy+5x & =k \\\\ 3xy & =k-5x \\\\ y & =\\frac{k-5x}{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{k-5x}{3x}.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=2.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258351 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258352 13194139533864 true] [17592186258351 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258351 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258351 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258352 13194139533864 true] [17592186258351 1110 #uuid "d00e7845-0434-54ae-a9c0-5ff550379e82" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258352 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258352 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258352 1111 #uuid "80e0a1d7-1001-5c5f-9b7f-de98f181baed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258353 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258354 13194139533864 true] [17592186258353 1089 "q-148544" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258353 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258353 1100 "The function $f(x)=\\frac{6}{k-4x}$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3x-6}{4x}.$ What is the value of $k?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258353 1113 #uuid "fed8ebe5-6227-5498-b8e6-525c8727e7e9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258353 1145 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{6}{k-4x}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{6}{k-4y}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{6}{k-4y} \\\\ x(k-4y) & =6 \\\\ kx-4xy & =6 \\\\ 4xy & =kx-6 \\\\ y & =\\frac{kx-6}{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{kx-6}{4x}.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=3.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258354 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258355 13194139533864 true] [17592186258354 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258354 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258354 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258355 13194139533864 true] [17592186258354 1110 #uuid "4810fec2-6560-5d2b-876a-62a56b696f00" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258355 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258355 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258355 1111 #uuid "a74f047b-c214-5885-9d49-41a58bebc409" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258356 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258357 13194139533864 true] [17592186258356 1089 "q-152752" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258356 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258356 1100 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}}(\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}(x)).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258356 1113 #uuid "cdcb38c4-f4a4-5b45-b1c7-b6357ee9e213" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258356 1145 "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/fc06a4e63f38a315dd3944f65f871cca2dbdddee26ec268e857f79e5f4ab8848.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\sqrt{3}$ directly into the limit, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}}(\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}(x)) & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258357 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258358 13194139533864 true] [17592186258357 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258357 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258357 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258358 13194139533864 true] [17592186258357 1110 #uuid "055d63e2-69ea-50f6-93e3-0c50ff07bfcd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258358 1034 "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258358 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258358 1111 #uuid "d4119b66-90a8-59cd-81a0-8a9c747bbae2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258359 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258360 13194139533864 true] [17592186258359 1089 "q-152782" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258359 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258359 1100 "What is the value of ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}(x)-5}{x}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf the value is undefined, enter \"Undefined\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258359 1113 #uuid "93b68d6a-209d-5990-a5e0-9593ec931e11" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258359 1145 "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$ Since $5\\notin [-1,1],$ this means that $\\operatorname{arcsin}(5)$ is undefined.\n\nTherefore, our limit is undefined, too." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258360 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258361 13194139533864 true] [17592186258360 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258360 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258360 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258361 13194139533864 true] [17592186258360 1110 #uuid "95f5d36a-0f55-50d1-bd47-29deb8c9696d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258361 1034 "Undefined" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258361 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258361 1111 #uuid "9ad4d3e3-158b-5b02-b228-c155b6dacaad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258362 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258363 13194139533864 true] [17592186258362 1089 "q-152870" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258362 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258362 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{x-3}{3}))=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258362 1113 #uuid "e6ad1b96-17fe-5464-ba79-198e73b68091" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258362 1145 "First, let's sketch the graph of  $y=\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{x-3}{3}):$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ee481869c72bd5858ac5f2c561605b3b164555a4043eb914d49da7506dcf40dd.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{x-3}{3})$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty ,$ the graph tends to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{x-3}{3})) & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258363 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258364 13194139533864 true] [17592186258363 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258363 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258363 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258364 13194139533864 true] [17592186258363 1110 #uuid "5e118b71-f421-5e1a-8569-811f6fb24e37" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258364 1034 "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258364 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258364 1111 #uuid "17b55411-3bd7-5d36-a7b8-257bfa5719e1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258365 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258366 13194139533864 true] [17592186258365 1089 "q-152889" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258365 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258365 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to \\,1/2}\\operatorname{tan}(6\\operatorname{arccos}(x))=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258365 1113 #uuid "324217ae-8df9-50ab-9ad5-02439e7104ed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258365 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$ \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1/2}\\operatorname{tan}(6\\operatorname{arccos}(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}(6\\operatorname{arccos}(\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}(6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}(2\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258366 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258367 13194139533864 true] [17592186258366 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258366 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258366 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258367 13194139533864 true] [17592186258366 1110 #uuid "1efa3dc0-deae-5f56-91c7-0f1843de34fc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258367 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258367 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258367 1111 #uuid "d92eda19-f935-5bfc-b945-ef9c1298c595" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258368 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258369 13194139533864 true] [17592186258368 1089 "q-152912" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258368 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258368 1100 "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-x-3, & x<-3 \\\\ 0, & x=-3 \\\\ x+3, & x>-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)?$\n\nI. - $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-3$\nII. - ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}f(x)$\nIII. - ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)}f(x)=f(-3)$\nIV. - $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-3$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258368 1113 #uuid "02eda226-1684-50b8-a1ed-83b979a1c53a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258368 1145 "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the second branch of the function, we obtain that\n\n$$\nf(-3)=0.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-3.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}(-x-3) \\\\  & =-(-3)-3 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}(x+3) \\\\  & =(-3)+3 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-3$ exists:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-3)}f(x)=0\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have that ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)}f(x)=0=f(-3).$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since all statements I-III are true, according to the continuity test, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258370 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258371 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258372 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258373 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258374 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258375 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258376 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258377 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258378 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258379 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258380 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258381 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258382 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258383 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258384 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258385 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258377 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1110 #uuid "5ae82df4-cd7c-58ff-a25e-011cdd4816bf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258370 1034 "I, III, IV" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258370 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258370 1111 #uuid "1b4545dd-c671-5f4a-882e-fedfb495f197" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258371 1034 "II" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258371