quare.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true.\n\nFirst, notice that our quadratic equation has rational coefficients. And, since the discriminant is not a perfect square, the equation must have two irrational solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258519 13194139533864 true] [17592186258518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258520 13194139533864 true] [17592186258518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258521 13194139533864 true] [17592186258518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258522 13194139533864 true] [17592186258518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258523 13194139533864 true] [17592186258518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258524 13194139533864 true] [17592186258518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258525 13194139533864 true] [17592186258518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258526 13194139533864 true] [17592186258518 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186258518 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258518 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258523 13194139533864 true] [17592186258518 1110 #uuid "371c5200-94ba-5835-973b-4d663d34437a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258519 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258519 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258519 1111 #uuid "1a055fd9-0617-50fb-a775-e3d958e8e16c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258520 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258520 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258520 1111 #uuid "9feb2e74-f747-57d4-8b97-8116d0ee0c75" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258521 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258521 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258521 1111 #uuid "d2e517d8-1f68-5d43-83d2-6e039e6d0634" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258522 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258522 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258522 1111 #uuid "31acbb2d-ad20-5b0f-963a-864f1fdb0720" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258523 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258523 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258523 1111 #uuid "37fa1935-2489-5a76-a52d-a57af05e1c69" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258524 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258524 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258524 1111 #uuid "364d4895-7821-588d-8882-a142b42c777c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258525 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258525 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258525 1111 #uuid "0974579d-ff02-5b3e-ba9b-31d6d8583f06" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258526 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258526 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258526 1111 #uuid "d5702360-c7bf-55fb-bf9c-3597406e0e5e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258527 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258528 13194139533864 true] [17592186258527 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258530 13194139533864 true] [17592186258527 1089 "q-158540" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258527 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258527 1100 "The point $(-1,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ What points **must** lie on $y=3f(x+2)?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258527 1113 #uuid "6721ce60-bfa1-58ad-aee6-895ef577c707" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258527 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=3f(x+2),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, shift the graph horizontally by $2$ unit to the left and get the graph of \n$y=f(x+2).$  After this transformation, the point $(-1,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1-2,1)=(-3,1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the y-axis and get the graph of \n$y=3f(x+2).$ After this transformation, the point $(-3,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-3,3\\cdot 1)=(-3,3).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-1,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(-1)=1.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=3f(x+2).$ Substituting $x=-3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3f(-3+2) \\\\  & =3f(-1) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-3,3)$ must lie on $y=3f(x+2).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258528 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258529 13194139533864 true] [17592186258528 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258528 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258528 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258529 13194139533864 true] [17592186258528 1110 #uuid "e1178a48-950c-5de3-92d7-39990b834f2e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258529 1034 "-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258529 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258529 1111 #uuid "16fa0f68-bcc1-512b-94af-8147b5a24f01" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258530 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258531 13194139533864 true] [17592186258530 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258530 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258530 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258531 13194139533864 true] [17592186258530 1110 #uuid "ef6514f4-4875-53cd-b442-402cf686bbec" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258531 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258531 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258531 1111 #uuid "0ab5f2fb-c4e9-501a-9315-d07cd5d66360" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258532 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258533 13194139533864 true] [17592186258532 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258535 13194139533864 true] [17592186258532 1089 "q-158542" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258532 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258532 1100 "The point  $(0,3)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x-1).$ What points **must** lie on $y=f(x)+2?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258532 1113 #uuid "56d9614f-42ba-58e8-a0d6-4b222638dad4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258532 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(0,3)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=f(x)+2:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x-1).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $1$ unit to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(0-1,3)=(-1,3).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,3)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(-1,3+2)=(-1,5).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(0,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x-1)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(0-1)=3\\,⟹\\,f(-1)=3.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(-1)+2 \\\\  & =3+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,5)$ must lie on $y=f(x)+2.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258533 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258534 13194139533864 true] [17592186258533 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258533 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258533 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258534 13194139533864 true] [17592186258533 1110 #uuid "543cbfb7-1173-5a65-bc37-f5518618feb6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258534 1034 "-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258534 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258534 1111 #uuid "f25075c9-151d-51ec-ad1b-51c290720160" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258535 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258536 13194139533864 true] [17592186258535 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258535 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258535 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258536 13194139533864 true] [17592186258535 1110 #uuid "4dbbd9a8-a3e8-5417-a704-9319b9fef99a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258536 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258536 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258536 1111 #uuid "74696480-27e8-50b8-8e68-1f5bc2c8821e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258537 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258538 13194139533864 true] [17592186258537 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258540 13194139533864 true] [17592186258537 1089 "q-158546" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258537 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258537 1100 "The point  $(2,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x-2).$ What points **must** lie on $y=3-f(x)?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258537 1113 #uuid "9ce49de9-b37c-5069-a547-f0dd26c1d448" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258537 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(2,1)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=3-f(x):$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x-2).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $2$ units to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2-2,1)=(0,1).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=3-f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,1)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(0,-(1))=(0,-1).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=3-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,-1)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(0,-1+3)=(0,2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(2,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x-2)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(2-2)=1\\,⟹\\,f(0)=1.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=3-f(x).$ Substituting $x=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3-f(0) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,2)$ must lie on $y=3-f(x).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258538 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258539 13194139533864 true] [17592186258538 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258538 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258538 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258539 13194139533864 true] [17592186258538 1110 #uuid "39489835-9589-58bd-9bad-441feaac60d4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258539 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258539 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258539 1111 #uuid "757ceea5-8485-572d-98f2-8cb47886efb2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258540 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258541 13194139533864 true] [17592186258540 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258540 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258540 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258541 13194139533864 true] [17592186258540 1110 #uuid "33a2d1a7-1d12-5bad-8745-cad969fa31d3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258541 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258541 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258541 1111 #uuid "bb9424a8-f686-5da8-a145-a91ff0b23144" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258542 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258543 13194139533864 true] [17592186258542 1089 "q-15940" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258542 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258542 1100 "Find the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[10,13]$ given the following table of values for $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $\\,7\\,$ | $\\,10\\,$ | $\\,11\\,$ | $\\,13\\,$ | $\\,15\\,$ | $\\,17\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,-2\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,10\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ | $\\,-5\\,$ |\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258542 1113 #uuid "be5e99bc-6a52-5455-9180-0bb659dfe8a9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258542 1145 "Using the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(13)-f(10)}{13-10} \\\\  & =\\frac{10-1}{13-10} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{3} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258543 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258544 13194139533864 true] [17592186258543 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258543 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258543 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258544 13194139533864 true] [17592186258543 1110 #uuid "5e28de3b-4f3d-59d5-b4c1-13d8631a52fb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258544 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258544 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258544 1111 #uuid "327dfb30-a023-5c23-bba9-91f243856467" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258545 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258546 13194139533864 true] [17592186258545 1089 "q-160400" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258545 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258545 1100 "Which of the following numbers lie in the second quadrant in an Argand diagram?\n\nI. - $3-\\text{i}$\nII. - $-1+2\\text{i}$\nIII. - $-2-4\\text{i}$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258545 1113 #uuid "7049a950-6667-5e99-a83e-6c95051eae41" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258545 1145 "- The complex number $3-\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=3$ and the imaginary part $y=-1.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(3,-1)$, which lies in the fourth quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-1+2\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-1$ and the imaginary part $y=2.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-1,2)$, which lies in the second quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-2-4\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-2$ and the imaginary part $y=-4.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-2,-4)$, which lies in the third quadrant.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/fafede137db90419fd849820668f36610ec675aa7a0e8a77d706872e33a149ef.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258546 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258547 13194139533864 true] [17592186258546 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258548 13194139533864 true] [17592186258546 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258549 13194139533864 true] [17592186258546 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258550 13194139533864 true] [17592186258546 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258551 13194139533864 true] [17592186258546 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258552 13194139533864 true] [17592186258546 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258553 13194139533864 true] [17592186258546 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258554 13194139533864 true] [17592186258546 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186258546 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258546 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258554 13194139533864 true] [17592186258546 1110 #uuid "73392058-1d10-5e8b-90df-46ae072c8bf7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258547 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258547 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258547 1111 #uuid "6f19b5c4-e10c-5c70-8584-1c203dc7d03f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258548 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258548 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258548 1111 #uuid "a9d66bb6-1a71-5651-aa28-5c76ce9e870d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258549 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258549 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258549 1111 #uuid "f0ace6d7-933c-550c-839a-ce6ea37a0a5a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258550 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258550 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258550 1111 #uuid "4cd1cb4e-2125-579f-a82b-a38e052eaddf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258551 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258551 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258551 1111 #uuid "c5c06e8a-a199-5dd2-b929-b6941f609134" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258552 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258552 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258552 1111 #uuid "4c28e77e-db15-5c0d-8069-14e040f64ecc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258553 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258553 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258553 1111 #uuid "3e3412e2-b865-5b94-9b3e-9b235d4aae3e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258554 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258554 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258554 1111 #uuid "252ca2ec-894b-5bb1-89e3-803daa8710f6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258555 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258556 13194139533864 true] [17592186258555 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258558 13194139533864 true] [17592186258555 1089 "q-16149" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258555 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258555 1100 "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+t,\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}-2,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$\n\nX range: {{x-range}}\n\nY range: {{y-range}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258555 1113 #uuid "a4387361-c86a-55f1-abee-652931e3d6d3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258555 1145 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2}+t \\\\  & ={(t+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & ={(t+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${(t+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\ge 0,$ we can conclude that $x\\ge -\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that ${t}^{2}\\ge 0.$ Therefore, we can conclude that $y\\ge -2.$\n\nSo, the ranges are $x\\ge -\\frac{1}{4}$ and $y\\ge -2.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258556 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258557 13194139533864 true] [17592186258556 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258556 1041 "x-range" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258556 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258557 13194139533864 true] [17592186258556 1110 #uuid "9e463822-912d-5d8d-9273-4f346c0c5a2d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258557 1034 "[-\\frac14,\\infty)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258557 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258557 1111 #uuid "ed7518da-2a73-5f44-b43b-c16f91d894a6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258558 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258559 13194139533864 true] [17592186258558 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258558 1041 "y-range" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258558 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258559 13194139533864 true] [17592186258558 1110 #uuid "42d036ae-7bbb-5cf5-8be4-b64c524400e8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258559 1034 "[-2,\\infty)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258559 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258559 1111 #uuid "aea19993-d64a-5538-8260-3c61f7186752" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258560 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258561 13194139533864 true] [17592186258560 1089 "q-16253" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258560 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258560 1100 "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(4x-{60}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}n,\\,x=b+{90}^{∘}n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. What could be the value of $b?$\n\n$b =$: {{b}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258560 1113 #uuid "14f70208-8f58-51a9-8ebd-e8fa8a3ef6f6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258560 1145 "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-{60}^{∘} & =-{60}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}n, & \\, & \\, & 4x-{60}^{∘} & =-{120}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ={60}^{∘}-{60}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}n & \\, & \\, & 4x & ={60}^{∘}-{120}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}n \\\\ 4x & ={360}^{∘}n & \\, & \\, & 4x & =-{60}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}n \\\\ x & ={90}^{∘}n & \\, & \\, & x & =-{15}^{∘}+{90}^{∘}n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $b=-{15}^{∘}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258561 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258562 13194139533864 true] [17592186258561 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258561 1041 "b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258561 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258562 13194139533864 true] [17592186258561 1110 #uuid "c4bdaa2a-68be-5aaa-85d0-dd4f3b18f5a8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258562 1034 "-{15}^{∘}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258562 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258562 1111 #uuid "40ec5b3b-658e-58ce-a37f-7b92341f3447" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258563 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258564 13194139533864 true] [17592186258563 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258566 13194139533864 true] [17592186258563 1089 "q-16284" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258563 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258563 1100 "If $5{z}^{2}+40=0$, then $z=$\n\nNegative imaginary: {{negative-imaginary}}\n\nPositive imaginary: {{positive-imaginary}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258563 1113 #uuid "2b1ccded-1895-50d8-a721-01d26df6beff" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258563 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{z}^{2}+40 & =0 \\\\ 5{z}^{2} & =-40 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking square root, we obtain:\n\n$$\nz=\\pm \\sqrt{-8}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $z$ using the imaginary unit $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\pm \\sqrt{-8} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{8\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{8}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258564 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258565 13194139533864 true] [17592186258564 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258564 1041 "negative-imaginary" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258564 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258565 13194139533864 true] [17592186258564 1110 #uuid "5d2ec563-ce5d-5b08-ab19-97055ef14592" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258565 1034 "-2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258565 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258565 1111 #uuid "f8dfa706-944c-512a-9279-3428fa659033" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258566 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258567 13194139533864 true] [17592186258566 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258566 1041 "positive-imaginary" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258566 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258567 13194139533864 true] [17592186258566 1110 #uuid "ddb27338-b6d5-5fba-a51c-c79519421418" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258567 1034 "2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258567 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258567 1111 #uuid "4ac685ca-4e45-5609-84d7-2d90fb8c80f6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258568 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258569 13194139533864 true] [17592186258568 1089 "q-16295" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258568 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258568 1100 "$(6-9\\,\\text{i})(3+7\\,\\text{i})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258568 1113 #uuid "85a5b817-448b-5b8d-9409-bbab40114a44" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258568 1145 "Multiplying two complex numbers is similar to multiplying two binomials. We also use the fact that ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6-9\\,\\text{i})(3+7\\,\\text{i}) & =6\\cdot 3+6\\cdot 7\\,\\text{i}+(-9\\,\\text{i})\\cdot 3+(-9\\,\\text{i})\\cdot 7\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =18+42\\,\\text{i}-27\\,\\text{i}-63\\,{\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\  & =18+15\\,\\text{i}-63\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =18+15\\,\\text{i}+63 \\\\  & =81+15\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258569 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258570 13194139533864 true] [17592186258569 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258569 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258569 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258570 13194139533864 true] [17592186258569 1110 #uuid "a19b8fdf-9a2e-506f-b0c0-3a0437ac0fe8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258570 1034 "81+15\\,\\text{i}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258570 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258570 1111 #uuid "aa7963fc-3b48-59c9-ba22-9232563e91b4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258571 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258572 13194139533864 true] [17592186258571 1089 "q-16355" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258571 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258571 1100 "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(2x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a\\pi +\\pi n,\\,x=\\frac{11\\pi }{24}+\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. What could be the value of $a?$\n\n$a =$: {{a}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258571 1113 #uuid "83f30889-f878-583f-b150-a995dc476f5d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258571 1145 "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n, & \\, & \\, & 2x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{12}+2\\pi n & \\, & \\, & 2x & =\\frac{11\\pi }{12}+2\\pi n \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{24}+\\pi n & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{11\\pi }{24}+\\pi n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-\\frac{5}{24}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258572 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258573 13194139533864 true] [17592186258572 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258572 1041 "a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258572 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258573 13194139533864 true] [17592186258572 1110 #uuid "dcb349ee-5aff-5464-b3b9-949ef5685eb8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258573 1034 "-\\frac{5}{24}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258573 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258573 1111 #uuid "bcd9ab5e-9ca8-5494-88f9-a90a5a2fef22" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258574 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258575 13194139533864 true] [17592186258574 1089 "q-16364" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258574 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258574 1100 "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}(4x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a\\pi +\\frac{n\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\n \nwhere $n$ is any integer. What could be the value of $a?$\n\n$a =$: {{a}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258574 1113 #uuid "6404a99e-7757-52ba-89dd-f5be5b3def0b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258574 1145 "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the above equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a=\\frac{1}{8}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258575 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258576 13194139533864 true] [17592186258575 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258575 1041 "a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258575 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258576 13194139533864 true] [17592186258575 1110 #uuid "da0790fc-3f61-57e0-ae49-9f30ce6de3f8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258576 1034 "\\frac{1}{8}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258576 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258576 1111 #uuid "53134b9c-2ec9-5e5a-adbc-78b1a7ed6b45" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258577 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258578 13194139533864 true] [17592186258577 1089 "q-166172" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258577 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258577 1100 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258577 1113 #uuid "7e618581-c326-5ad5-9817-b1c392c0f169" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258577 1145 "We identify the leading term of the polynomial and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }(2x-{x}^{2}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }(-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-(\\infty ) \\\\  & =-\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258578 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258579 13194139533864 true] [17592186258578 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258578 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258578 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258579 13194139533864 true] [17592186258578 1110 #uuid "fc5abb14-71a2-5556-b573-44c813a89a31" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258579 1034 "-\\infty " 13194139533864 true] [17592186258579 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258579 1111 #uuid "4a8b5e35-96f2-5fc6-852f-d05f7ae11c09" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258580 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258581 13194139533864 true] [17592186258580 1089 "q-166173" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258580 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258580 1100 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{2}}{f(x)}.$  If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{2}}{f(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258580 1113 #uuid "e7335c37-4d61-5445-b937-b7603f1ff98b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258580 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit directly by substituting $x=2,$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{2}}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{2}}{2x-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{2}}{2(2)-(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since there is a radical in the numerator, we can evaluate the limit by first multiplying the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{2}}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{2}}{2x-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{2})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{2})}{(2x-{x}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}\\frac{x-2}{x(2-x)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}\\frac{-(2-x)}{x(2-x)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}\\frac{\\cancel{2-x}}{x\\cancel{(2-x)}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258581 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258582 13194139533864 true] [17592186258581 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258581 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258581 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258582 13194139533864 true] [17592186258581 1110 #uuid "c4366125-dde0-5e95-b07b-fea97429c985" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258582 1034 "-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258582 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258582 1111 #uuid "a0a893a0-9c1f-588b-8481-c0239d08c817" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258583 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258584 13194139533864 true] [17592186258583 1089 "q-166174" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258583 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258583 1100 "The instantaneous rate of change of $f(x)$ at $x=3$ is given by\n\n${f}^{'}(3)={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3}={lim}_{x\\to 3}(-x-$\n{{field-1}}\n$).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258583 1113 #uuid "5be7f888-8103-5b32-9838-2ee1a31d74a5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258583 1145 "Simplifying the expression under the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(3) & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(\\frac{[2x-{x}^{2}]-[2(3)-(3)^{2}]}{x-3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(\\frac{-{x}^{2}+2x+3}{x-3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(\\frac{-({x}^{2}-2x-3)}{x-3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(\\frac{-(x+1)\\cancel{(x-3)}}{\\cancel{x-3}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(-x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258584 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258585 13194139533864 true] [17592186258584 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258584 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258584 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258585 13194139533864 true] [17592186258584 1110 #uuid "4a784fbb-536a-5971-96a7-7d5e1444c0b9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258585 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258585 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258585 1111 #uuid "88b9dd30-548b-5770-8c2d-ad71a4396953" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258586 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258587 13194139533864 true] [17592186258586 1089 "q-166175" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258586 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258586 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to 3}(-x-1)=${{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258586 1113 #uuid "7c3bccb0-4d19-5e29-b495-d2fd9d30979b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258586 1145 "We first apply the sum rule, and then apply the constant rule and compute the limits of the power functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(-x-1) & =-{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}x-{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}1 \\\\  & =-(3)-1 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258587 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258588 13194139533864 true] [17592186258587 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258587 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258587 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258588 13194139533864 true] [17592186258587 1110 #uuid "1e6539da-3322-59a4-a724-4cfc95dd35f1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258588 1034 "-4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258588 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258588 1111 #uuid "968285cf-0e5f-56f0-b799-6231645ea515" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258589 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258590 13194139533864 true] [17592186258589 1089 "q-166176" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258589 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258589 1100 "The derivative of $f(x)$ is defined by\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\,2\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,h\\,).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258589 1113 #uuid "71c08803-7449-5a1f-967b-740eb77c9f6b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258589 1145 "Simplifying the expression under the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2(x+h)-(x+h)^{2}-(2x-{x}^{2})}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2x+2h-{x}^{2}-2xh-{h}^{2}-2x+{x}^{2}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2h-2hx-{h}^{2}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{h(2-2x-h)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(2-2x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258590 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258591 13194139533864 true] [17592186258590 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258590 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258590 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258591 13194139533864 true] [17592186258590 1110 #uuid "edaee8b1-9c73-58df-9133-2852fecfb66c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258591 1034 "2x" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258591 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258591 1111 #uuid "59a60774-5d9b-5718-807e-d54dbf557901" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258592 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258593 13194139533864 true] [17592186258592 1089 "q-166177" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258592 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258592 1100 "${f}^{'}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}(2-2x-h)=${{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258592 1113 #uuid "fe503c51-2ef0-5949-be80-1be073db8d67" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258592 1145 "We first apply the sum rule, and then apply the constant rule and compute the limits of the power functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{h\\to \\,0}(2-2x-h) & ={lim}_{h\\to \\,0}(2-2x)-{lim}_{h\\to \\,0}h \\\\  & =2-2x-0 \\\\  & =2-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258593 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258594 13194139533864 true] [17592186258593 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258593 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258593 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258594 13194139533864 true] [17592186258593 1110 #uuid "201b11e5-1c48-52a4-9da0-0b9795eaec60" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258594 1034 "2-2x" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258594 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258594 1111 #uuid "e22463b7-3f0f-5292-9a84-6f7573b5b2bf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258595 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258596 13194139533864 true] [17592186258595 1089 "q-166178" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258595 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258595 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/17c57ba55e06207350ab93c8cac79bf22c48ba94e73ce4f914cd809b74b7d862.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above (not drawn to scale). Estimate the area $A$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$\n\n$A\\,\\approx \\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258595 1113 #uuid "a327301f-c4a9-5bc3-8650-35e0d214db46" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258595 1145 "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/41fde96921443be6c1efc28800e921a189fbe32d8a43667f6a85e7cf399eb745.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA\\approx (\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0.5)+f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.75$ | $1$ | $0.75$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & \\approx (0.75+1+0.75+0)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =2.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =1.25\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258596 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258597 13194139533864 true] [17592186258596 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258596 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258596 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258597 13194139533864 true] [17592186258596 1110 #uuid "9705d313-ba96-5e54-999b-f76dc9705e0e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258597 1034 "1.25" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258597 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258597 1111 #uuid "7b9b9b9d-39a0-5ab6-9093-f6af0e0fb006" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258598 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258599 13194139533864 true] [17592186258598 1089 "q-166179" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258598 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258598 1100 "Write the expression for the Riemann sum from part 7 in sigma notation.\n\nThe right Riemann sum $\\,=\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}0.5(i-$\n{{field-1}} ${i}^{2})$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258598 1113 #uuid "ea206d90-e898-5894-9e09-0438d3e9d3d8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258598 1145 "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nIn our case, we have $n=4$ subintervals, $a=0,$ $b=2,$ and $Δx=0.5.$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(a+iΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}f(0+i\\cdot 0.5)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}0.5f(0.5i) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}0.5(2(0.5i)-{(0.5i)}^{2}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}0.5(i-0.25{i}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258599 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258600 13194139533864 true] [17592186258599 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258599 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258599 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258600 13194139533864 true] [17592186258599 1110 #uuid "b4e4b5a3-a5dc-57ea-b36b-782656ca2ec3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258600 1034 "0.25" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258600 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258600 1111 #uuid "14882465-44da-51bd-9828-96748b5d62c9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258601 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258602 13194139533864 true] [17592186258601 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258604 13194139533864 true] [17592186258601 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258606 13194139533864 true] [17592186258601 1089 "q-166180" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258601 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258601 1100 "The exact value of the area $A$ from part 7 can be expressed using definite integral notation as\n\n$A={\\int }_{a}^{b}$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{d}x,\\,\\,$ where  \n$a\\,=\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,b\\,=\\,$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258601 1113 #uuid "e1a45ff5-8464-5ba2-820e-b08ce09f5a2a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258601 1145 "We can express the area $A$ as the definite integral of \n\n$$\nf(x)=2x-{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nbetween \n\n* the lower limit $x=0,$ and \n\n* the upper limit $x=2.$ \n\nThis gives\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{0}^{2}2x-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258602 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258603 13194139533864 true] [17592186258602 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258602 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258602 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258603 13194139533864 true] [17592186258602 1110 #uuid "f6f0467e-235b-5cfb-858b-72cd6471c495" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258603 1034 "2x-{x}^{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258603 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258603 1111 #uuid "6c192d0e-7507-5afd-b661-aa7f8b5845b8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258604 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258605 13194139533864 true] [17592186258604 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258604 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258604 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258605 13194139533864 true] [17592186258604 1110 #uuid "760b48b0-8fc8-58d1-af9c-815eabc68371" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258605 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258605 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258605 1111 #uuid "e57e6f80-98ec-51f0-b4ef-4ce8ebf21cce" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258607 13194139533864 true] [17592186258606 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258606 1041 "field-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258606 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258607 13194139533864 true] [17592186258606 1110 #uuid "3cd4e733-f0a6-58c4-aa97-f25abd24650f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258607 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258607 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258607 1111 #uuid "b9ba5916-8581-5cae-8448-1a538a55cd83" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258608 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258609 13194139533864 true] [17592186258608 1089 "q-166547" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258608 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258608 1100 "Find the equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $f(x)=-{log}_{2}(9-3x)+4.$\n\n$x =$: {{x}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258608 1113 #uuid "540e0beb-27dc-5689-9b70-9acb574ebe65" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258608 1145 "The equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $y={log}_{2}x$ is $x=0.$\n\nTo find the equation of the vertical asymptote of $f(x),$ we set the argument of the logarithmic term equal to zero, and solve for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-3x & =0 \\\\ -3x & =-9 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=3.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258609 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258610 13194139533864 true] [17592186258609 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258609 1041 "x" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258609 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258610 13194139533864 true] [17592186258609 1110 #uuid "2bf3ba6e-8536-5f0d-84b8-ba0c8d98e218" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258610 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258610 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258610 1111 #uuid "3f493eca-3a21-51c3-92f4-40a48a8146a7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258611 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258612 13194139533864 true] [17592186258611 1089 "q-166947" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258611 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258611 1100 "If you deposit $$12,000$ into an account with a $8%$ annual interest rate, compounded quarterly, how many years will it take until there is at least $$18,000$ in the account?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258611 1113 #uuid "c960aee5-3c8c-5166-a2ed-4e95f0b23d9f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258611 1145 "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded quarterly, and there are $4$ quarters in one year, so $n=4.$ We also have an initial amount of ${P}_{0}=12,000$ and annual rate of $r=0.08.$\n\nTo find the number of years it will take until $P\\ge 18,000,$ we substitute these values into the formula and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt} & \\ge 18,000 \\\\ 12,000{(1+\\frac{0.08}{4})}^{4t} & \\ge 18,000 \\\\ (1+0.02)^{4t} & \\ge \\frac{18,000}{12,000} \\\\ (1.02)^{4t} & \\ge 1.5 \\\\ log\\,({1.02}^{4t}) & \\ge log\\,(1.5) \\\\ 4tlog\\,(1.02) & \\ge log\\,(1.5) \\\\ 4t & \\ge \\frac{log\\,(1.5)}{log\\,(1.02)} \\\\ 4t & \\ge 20.475… \\\\ t & \\ge \\frac{20.475…}{4}\\approx 5.12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember, payments are compounded quarterly, so we must choose either $5$ or $5.25$ years.\n\n- After $5$ years, there is still less than $$18,000$ in the account, because $5<5.12.$\n\n- After $5.25$ years, there will be more than $$18,000$ in the account, since $5.25>5.12.$\n\nTherefore, it will take $5.25$ years ($5$ years and $1$ quarter) until there is at least $$18,000$ in the account." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258612 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258613 13194139533864 true] [17592186258612 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258612 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258612 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258613 13194139533864 true] [17592186258612 1110 #uuid "0158252f-0174-5ffd-92f6-829d16529201" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258613 1034 "5.25" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258613 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258613 1111 #uuid "36592917-0e89-59ea-b1c9-ec6fac2a0b0e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258614 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258615 13194139533864 true] [17592186258614 1089 "q-16698" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258614 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258614 1100 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}3x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258614 1113 #uuid "7c0f667a-d499-5b4f-9cda-30ad08f61832" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258614 1145 "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{cos}3x.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{v}^{'}(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}3x.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(\\operatorname{cos}3x)+({e}^{x})(-3\\operatorname{sin}3x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}3x-3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}3x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}3x-3\\operatorname{sin}3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258615 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258616 13194139533864 true] [17592186258615 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258615 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258615 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258616 13194139533864 true] [17592186258615 1110 #uuid "81d365fe-6cf5-561b-a054-3ad8b527bfc9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258616 1034 "{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}3x-3\\operatorname{sin}3x)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258616 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258616 1111 #uuid "115f0f29-ce77-523c-9e5e-b41e648e8037" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258617 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258618 13194139533864 true] [17592186258617 1089 "q-16699" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258617 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258617 1100 "Find ${f}^{'}(1)$ when $f(x)={cos}^{3}(5{x}^{2}+2).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258617 1113 #uuid "cea81d37-e8da-5ed2-8186-38548c31655d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258617 1145 "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{3},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}v,\\,v=5{x}^{2}+2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=3{u}^{2},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=-\\operatorname{sin}v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=10x.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}v)\\cdot 10x \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{cos}(5{x}^{2}+2))^{2}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}(5{x}^{2}+2))\\cdot 10x \\\\  & =-30x{cos}^{2}(5{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}(5{x}^{2}+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we compute ${f}^{'}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(1) & =-30(1){cos}^{2}(5(1)^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}(5(1)^{2}+2) \\\\  & =-30{cos}^{2}7\\operatorname{sin}7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258618 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258619 13194139533864 true] [17592186258618 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258618 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258618 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258619 13194139533864 true] [17592186258618 1110 #uuid "842e9247-ae0a-5719-b90b-9aa641981a7f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258619 1034 "-30{cos}^{2}7\\operatorname{sin}7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258619 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258619 1111 #uuid "102ce3e3-dc0c-5390-abd0-d647367d0d0a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258620 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258621 13194139533864 true] [17592186258620 1089 "q-167192" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258620 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258620 1100 "In terms of $a$ and $b,\\,p(10)=\\,${{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258620 1113 #uuid "3a5c3449-93ad-5a8f-bbf0-17b76476ebff" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258620 1145 "We substitute $x=10$ into the expression for $p(x)$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =a{x}^{2}+bx \\\\ p(10) & =a(10)^{2}+b(10) \\\\  & =100a+10b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p(10)=100a+10b.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258621 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258622 13194139533864 true] [17592186258621 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258621 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258621 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258622 13194139533864 true] [17592186258621 1110 #uuid "4f2d11c4-7300-5965-99c0-2b6e61b8eb70" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258622 1034 "100a+10b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258622 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258622 1111 #uuid "86a99ba6-4c2c-5807-a56e-b249e1918b4e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258623 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258624 13194139533864 true] [17592186258623 1089 "q-167193" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258623 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258623 1100 "If the company sells ten couches in a week, the weekly profit will be $$4\\,800.$ Find an expression for $b$ in terms of $a.$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258623 1113 #uuid "2bd624b7-fb93-5e18-810a-cc1dd71da80a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258623 1145 "In part 1, we found that $p(10)=100a+10b.$ So, we need to solve the equation $100a+10b=4\\,800$ for the parameter $b.$\n\nWe need to isolate the variable $b.$ To do this, we treat $a$ as though it were a constant and apply the addition and multiplication principles as usual.\n\nFirst, we subtract $100a$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100a+10b & =4\\,800 \\\\ 100a+10b-100a & =4\\,800-100a \\\\ 10b & =4\\,800-100a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $10:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10b & =4\\,800-100a \\\\ \\frac{10b}{10} & =\\frac{4\\,800-100a}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{10}b}{\\cancel{10}} & =\\frac{4\\,800}{10}-\\frac{100a}{10} \\\\ b & =480-10a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $b=480-10a.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258624 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258625 13194139533864 true] [17592186258624 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258624 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258624 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258625 13194139533864 true] [17592186258624 1110 #uuid "98c2fbae-67d3-52ab-807c-2446f504b794" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258625 1034 "480-10a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258625 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258625 1111 #uuid "b3d37fcd-d281-5a16-a427-2712f01e728c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258626 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258627 13194139533864 true] [17592186258626 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258629 13194139533864 true] [17592186258626 1089 "q-167194" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258626 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258626 1100 "The coefficients $a$ and $b$ also satisfy the equation $20b+400a=7\\,200.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$b\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258626 1113 #uuid "0afbd217-b5b5-5642-933f-a0e397418911" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258626 1145 "We have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}b=480-10a \\\\ 20b+400a=7\\,200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=480-10a\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $b$ in the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20b+400a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 20(480-10a)+400a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 9\\,600-200a+400a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 9\\,600+200a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 200a & =-2\\,400 \\\\ a & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-12$ into $b=480-10a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =480-10(-12) \\\\ b & =600\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-12,\\,b=600.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258627 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258628 13194139533864 true] [17592186258627 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258627 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258627 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258628 13194139533864 true] [17592186258627 1110 #uuid "0b772ad6-06db-5726-b6ce-d03788093751" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258628 1034 "-12" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258628 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258628 1111 #uuid "825436cb-9673-51cf-85dc-e619d63d520d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258629 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258630 13194139533864 true] [17592186258629 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258629 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258629 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258630 13194139533864 true] [17592186258629 1110 #uuid "c9d309dc-1a7d-576d-82a8-c54ce41c71fa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258630 1034 "600" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258630 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258630 1111 #uuid "99557b4b-cf24-5413-9953-5485c6e07968" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258631 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258632 13194139533864 true] [17592186258631 1089 "q-167195" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258631 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258631 1100 "Determine the weekly profit if the company produces $40$ couches in a week.\n\nThe company will earn$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$dollars." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258631 1113 #uuid "2a5fdcf4-99c8-52a0-adf5-3a386b9372b4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258631 1145 "From part 3, we have the function $p(x)=-12{x}^{2}+600x.$\n\nWe substitute $x=40$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(40) & =-12(40)^{2}+600(40) \\\\  & =-19\\,200+24\\,000 \\\\  & =4\\,800\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the company will earn $$4\\,800.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258632 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258633 13194139533864 true] [17592186258632 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258632 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258632 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258633 13194139533864 true] [17592186258632 1110 #uuid "43acb66d-8004-5c0e-a631-d3d64fd7793b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258633 1034 "4800" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258633 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258633 1111 #uuid "354f5d76-8f03-5e6e-97fa-a25e850f3cf4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258634 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258635 13194139533864 true] [17592186258634 1089 "q-167196" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258634 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258634 1100 "The manager wants to determine the number of couches the company should produce per week to make a weekly profit of $$7\\,200.$ The required number of sofas $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n12{x}^{2}-600x+7\\,200=0.\n$$\n\nThis equation can be factored as $\\,$$12\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}} $)=0.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258634 1113 #uuid "c82903d9-874d-51ce-9030-da4bfd295182" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258634 1145 "We factor $12{x}^{2}-600x+7\\,200$ using the greatest common factor (GCF).\n\nComputing the GCF, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{GCF}(12{x}^{2},600x,7\\,200)=12.\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite each term in the original expression as a product with the $\\text{GCF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}-600x+7\\,200 & = \\\\ 12\\cdot ({x}^{2})+12\\cdot (-50x)+12\\cdot (600)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the $\\text{GCF}$ outside the parentheses and keep the remaining terms inside:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\cdot ({x}^{2})+12\\cdot (-50x)+12\\cdot (600) & = \\\\ 12({x}^{2}-50x+600)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{2}-50x+600.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258635 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258636 13194139533864 true] [17592186258635 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258635 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258635 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258636 13194139533864 true] [17592186258635 1110 #uuid "930038c5-6051-5626-9760-83c150e619a8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258636 1034 "{x}^{2}-50x+600" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258636 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258636 1111 #uuid "e98593c0-a0cd-5b2e-934f-7284597911fd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258637 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258638 13194139533864 true] [17592186258637 1089 "q-167197" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258637 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258637 1100 "Find the minimum number of couches the company needs to produce per week to make a weekly profit of $$7\\,200.$\n\nThe company needs to produce$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ couches per week." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258637 1113 #uuid "9a0fc448-799b-505a-a910-4ee7e12527c9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258637 1145 "From part 5, the required number of sofas $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n12\\cdot ({x}^{2}-50x+600)=0.\n$$\n\nDividing this equation by $12,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-50x+600=0.\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we want to find a factor pair of the constant term $(600)$ that also adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-50).$ The required factors are $(-20)$ and $(-30).$\n\nUsing this factor pair, we write the linear term as $-50x=-20x-30x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-50x+600 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-20x-30x+600 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-20x-30+600 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-20x)+(-30x+600) & =0 \\\\ x(x-20)-30(x-20) & =0 \\\\ (x-30)(x-20) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the solutions are $x=20$ or $x=30.$ Since we require the minimum solution, we conclude that the company needs to produce $20$ couches per week to make a profit of $$7\\,200.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258638 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258639 13194139533864 true] [17592186258638 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258638 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258638 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258639 13194139533864 true] [17592186258638 1110 #uuid "2e13021a-bbe5-5777-8b5e-ea6b09aed017" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258639 1034 "20" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258639 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258639 1111 #uuid "d8069a5e-c350-59af-92e9-2784d9ed4e96" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258640 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258641 13194139533864 true] [17592186258640 1089 "q-167198" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258640 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258640 1100 "The company wants to increase its output by implementing a new production model. The number of couches ${a}_{n}$ produced in the $n$th week since the implementation of the new model is given by the arithmetic sequence\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}+3,\\,{a}_{1}=11,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nWhat is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence?\n\n${a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258640 1113 #uuid "6c2d8939-9cf0-57a6-8fab-47c4fdcfdadf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258640 1145 "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\\,n\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nso we need to determine the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is ${a}_{1}=11,$ and we can use the recursive rule to compute the second term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}+3 \\\\  & =11+3 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the first and second terms, we can compute the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =14-11 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute ${a}_{1}=11$ and $d=3$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =11+(n-1)(3) \\\\  & =11+3n-3 \\\\  & =3n+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=3n+8,\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258641 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258642 13194139533864 true] [17592186258641 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258641 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258641 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258642 13194139533864 true] [17592186258641 1110 #uuid "6a0acb02-2312-5020-b779-83aeb67738c3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258642 1034 "3n+8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258642 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258642 1111 #uuid "ae498387-642f-5cfd-b426-4ac8e5df83a2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258643 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258644 13194139533864 true] [17592186258643 1089 "q-167208" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258643 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258643 1100 "In terms of $a$ and $b,\\,p(20)=\\,${{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258643 1113 #uuid "75ec1bc2-8651-5192-b499-b0ffe6eb2c97" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258643 1145 "We substitute $x=20$ into the expression for $p(x)$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =a{x}^{2}+bx \\\\ p(20) & =a(20)^{2}+b(20) \\\\  & =400a+20b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p(20)=400a+20b.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258644 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258645 13194139533864 true] [17592186258644 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258644 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258644 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258645 13194139533864 true] [17592186258644 1110 #uuid "6b356e3b-1ab5-5b61-8661-26162f98a2e2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258645 1034 "400a+20b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258645 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258645 1111 #uuid "00db8438-15de-5d14-b327-9416a8d364f5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258646 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258647 13194139533864 true] [17592186258646 1089 "q-167209" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258646 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258646 1100 "If the bakery sells twenty cakes in a week, the weekly profit will be $$4\\,800.$ Find an expression for $b$ in terms of $a.$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258646 1113 #uuid "3fc19ec2-9fcf-558b-bcfa-fe08c1b1d5c0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258646 1145 "In part 1, we found that $p(20)=400a+20b.$ So, we need to solve the equation $400a+20b=4\\,800$ for the parameter $b.$\n\nWe need to isolate the variable $b.$ To do this, we treat $a$ as though it were a constant and apply the addition and multiplication principles as usual.\n\nFirst, we subtract $400a$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}400a+20b & =4\\,800 \\\\ 400a+20b-400a & =4\\,800-400a \\\\ 20b & =4\\,800-400a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $20:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20b & =4\\,800-400a \\\\ \\frac{20b}{20} & =\\frac{4\\,800-400a}{20} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{20}b}{\\cancel{20}} & =\\frac{4\\,800}{20}-\\frac{400a}{20} \\\\ b & =240-20a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $b=240-20a.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258647 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258648 13194139533864 true] [17592186258647 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258647 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258647 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258648 13194139533864 true] [17592186258647 1110 #uuid "b707568f-bbe6-532e-bb58-23348cdbf806" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258648 1034 "240-20a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258648 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258648 1111 #uuid "82cd1908-0024-5479-8067-6288c85edcfa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258649 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258650 13194139533864 true] [17592186258649 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258652 13194139533864 true] [17592186258649 1089 "q-167210" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258649 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258649 1100 "The coefficients $a$ and $b$ also satisfy the equation $30b+900a=6\\,000.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$b\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258649 1113 #uuid "3745304a-337c-57fe-9274-0c5590dd8f9c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258649 1145 "We have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}b=240-20a \\\\ 30b+900a=6\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=240-20a\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $b$ in the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30b+900a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 30(240-20a)+900a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 7\\,200-600a+900a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 7\\,200+300a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 300a & =-1\\,200 \\\\ a & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-4$ into $b=240-20a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =240-20(-4) \\\\ b & =320\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-4,\\,b=320.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258650 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258651 13194139533864 true] [17592186258650 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258650 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258650 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258651 13194139533864 true] [17592186258650 1110 #uuid "e2a946c9-fc0f-55fa-b14d-b6d33d7911be" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258651 1034 "-4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258651 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258651 1111 #uuid "708a0248-6941-505b-81cb-fac6b0e13ef0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258652 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258653 13194139533864 true] [17592186258652 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258652 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258652 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258653 13194139533864 true] [17592186258652 1110 #uuid "2a8ef031-bf8e-58d6-9c2c-ab33d25b38e5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258653 1034 "320" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258653 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258653 1111 #uuid "a194f5e9-9385-5cd1-ad1a-5979b68b20d1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258654 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258655 13194139533864 true] [17592186258654 1089 "q-167211" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258654 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258654 1100 "Determine the weekly profit if the bakery produces $35$ cakes in a week.\n\nThe company will earn$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$dollars." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258654 1113 #uuid "1b9d6db3-e650-565d-8f8c-e2de39a23110" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258654 1145 "From part 3, we have the function $p(x)=-4{x}^{2}+320x.$\n\nWe substitute $x=35$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(35) & =-4(35)^{2}+320(35) \\\\  & =-4\\,900+11\\,200 \\\\  & =6\\,300\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the company will earn $$6\\,300.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258655 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258656 13194139533864 true] [17592186258655 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258655 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258655 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258656 13194139533864 true] [17592186258655 1110 #uuid "c4fb357e-085a-5f3b-b537-38769b8780f2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258656 1034 "6300" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258656 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258656 1111 #uuid "5a3e0464-63c9-5eef-bc9e-5c1c2afcd9f4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258657 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258658 13194139533864 true] [17592186258657 1089 "q-167212" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258657 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258657 1100 "The manager wants to determine the number of cakes the bakery should produce per week to make a weekly profit of $$6\\,000.$ The required number of cakes $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-320x+6\\,000=0.\n$$\n\nThis equation can be factored as $\\,$$4\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}} $)=0.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258657 1113 #uuid "a6d32981-6a85-569d-bdb7-7f0b327bbe43" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258657 1145 "We factor $4{x}^{2}-320x+6\\,000$ using the greatest common factor (GCF).\n\nComputing the GCF, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{GCF}(4{x}^{2},320x,6\\,000)=4\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite each term in the original expression as a product with the $\\text{GCF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-320x+6\\,000 & = \\\\ 4\\cdot ({x}^{2})+4\\cdot (-80x)+4\\cdot (1\\,500)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the $\\text{GCF}$ outside the parentheses and keep the remaining terms inside:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot ({x}^{2})+4\\cdot (-80x)+4\\cdot (1\\,500) & = \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-80x+1\\,500)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{2}-80x+1\\,500.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258658 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258659 13194139533864 true] [17592186258658 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258658 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258658 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258659 13194139533864 true] [17592186258658 1110 #uuid "0e8a5cb2-86eb-5315-b18c-bbfd63944604" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258659 1034 "{x}^{2}-80x+1500" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258659 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258659 1111 #uuid "a7b1e001-fc51-5fb9-8408-2afdb4d08019" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258660 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258661 13194139533864 true] [17592186258660 1089 "q-167213" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258660 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258660 1100 "Find the minimum number of cakes the bakery needs to produce per week to make a weekly profit of $$6\\,000.$\n\nThe bakery needs to produce$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ cakes per week." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258660 1113 #uuid "7095629a-fbfb-5e1c-bf46-32aaaf63e2db" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258660 1145 "From part 5, the required number of cakes $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n4\\cdot ({x}^{2}-80x+1\\,500)=0.\n$$\n\nDividing this equation by $4,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-80x+1\\,500=0.\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we want to find a factor pair of the constant term $(1\\,500)$ that also adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-80).$ The required factors are $(-30)$ and $(-50).$\n\nUsing this factor pair, we write the linear term as $-80x=-30x-50x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-80x+1\\,500 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-30x-50x+1\\,500 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-30x-50x+1\\,500 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-30x)+(-50x+1\\,500) & =0 \\\\ x(x-30)-50(x-30) & =0 \\\\ (x-30)(x-50)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the solutions are $x=30$ or $x=50.$ Since we require the minimum solution, we conclude that the bakery needs to produce $30$ cakes per week to make a profit of $$6\\,000.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258661 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258662 13194139533864 true] [17592186258661 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258661 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258661 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258662 13194139533864 true] [17592186258661 1110 #uuid "8edca494-ef17-5230-bf6f-214bfc5a0fd7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258662 1034 "30" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258662 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258662 1111 #uuid "3a1be701-b65f-52a8-b5ad-99d023b13697" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258663 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258664 13194139533864 true] [17592186258663 1089 "q-167214" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258663 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258663 1100 "The bakery wants to increase its output by implementing a new production model. The number of cakes ${a}_{n}$ produced in the $n$th week since the implementation of the new model is given by the arithmetic sequence\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}+4,\\,{a}_{1}=19,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nWhat is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence?\n\n${a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258663 1113 #uuid "977c6875-4975-5233-bc94-e060e1ce6574" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258663 1145 "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\\,n\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nso we need to determine the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is ${a}_{1}=19,$ and we can use the recursive rule to compute the second term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}+4 \\\\  & =19+4 \\\\  & =23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the first and second terms, we can compute the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =23-19 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute ${a}_{1}=19$ and $d=4$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =19+(n-1)(4) \\\\  & =19+4n-4 \\\\  & =4n+15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=4n+15,\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258664 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258665 13194139533864 true] [17592186258664 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258664 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258664 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258665 13194139533864 true] [17592186258664 1110 #uuid "14145251-4438-553d-b8a7-49e37064d2fb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258665 1034 "4n+15" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258665 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258665 1111 #uuid "a711f70d-38d1-50cb-a94d-e15aadec19bd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258666 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258667 13194139533864 true] [17592186258666 1089 "q-167224" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258666 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258666 1100 "In terms of $a$ and $b,\\,p(5)=\\,${{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258666 1113 #uuid "8eb94650-22b8-5af5-b475-545794c486d4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258666 1145 "We substitute $x=5$ into the expression for $p(x)$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =a{x}^{2}+bx \\\\ p(5) & =a(5)^{2}+b(5) \\\\  & =25a+5b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p(5)=25a+5b.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258667 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258668 13194139533864 true] [17592186258667 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258667 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258667 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258668 13194139533864 true] [17592186258667 1110 #uuid "23855951-e788-5651-a8d5-89a39166d9c6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258668 1034 "25a+5b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258668 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258668 1111 #uuid "ae003cb3-9e89-5cf0-87a7-9ae13147cfee" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258669 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258670 13194139533864 true] [17592186258669 1089 "q-167225" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258669 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258669 1100 "If the company sells five luxury white chocolate boxes a day, the daily profit will be $$375.$ Find an expression for $b$ in terms of $a.$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258669 1113 #uuid "1bcc8843-2a3e-5eb9-b40d-96636fdbcbef" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258669 1145 "In part 1, we found that $p(5)=25a+5b.$ So, we need to solve the equation $25a+5b=375$ for the parameter $b.$\n\nWe need to isolate the variable $b.$ To do this, we treat $a$ as though it were a constant and apply the addition and multiplication principles as usual.\n\nFirst, we subtract $25a$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25a+5b & =375 \\\\ 25a+5b-25a & =375-25a \\\\ 5b & =375-25a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5b & =375-25a \\\\ \\frac{5b}{5} & =\\frac{375-25a}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{5}b}{\\cancel{5}} & =\\frac{375}{5}-\\frac{25a}{5} \\\\ b & =75-5a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $b=75-5a.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258670 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258671 13194139533864 true] [17592186258670 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258670 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258670 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258671 13194139533864 true] [17592186258670 1110 #uuid "13417f50-9eb7-586e-bedf-d1ed568f0a31" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258671 1034 "75-5a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258671 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258671 1111 #uuid "e99de851-0e74-57e3-add3-61a2effed24a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258672 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258673 13194139533864 true] [17592186258672 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258675 13194139533864 true] [17592186258672 1089 "q-167226" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258672 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258672 1100 "The coefficients $a$ and $b$ also satisfy the equation $70a+9b=600.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$b\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258672 1113 #uuid "8d63494e-5f74-557b-a601-72890b90758c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258672 1145 "We have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}b=75-5a \\\\ 70a+9b=600\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=75-5a\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $b$ in the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}70a+9b & =600 \\\\ 70a+9(75-5a) & =600 \\\\ 70a+675-45a & =600 \\\\ 25a+675 & =600 \\\\ 25a & =-75 \\\\ a & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-3$ into $b=75-5a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =75-5(-3) \\\\ b & =90\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-3,\\,b=90.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258673 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258674 13194139533864 true] [17592186258673 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258673 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258673 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258674 13194139533864 true] [17592186258673 1110 #uuid "1dbe85ba-bf16-554c-8d3e-5e4bd5b