true] [17592186258679 1111 #uuid "051bf4e8-235e-5157-adbf-b32ce8b74141" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258680 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258681 13194139533864 true] [17592186258680 1089 "q-167228" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258680 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258680 1100 "The manager wants to determine the number of luxury white chocolate boxes the company should produce per day to make a daily profit of $$600.$ The required number of white chocolate boxes $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-90x+600=0.\n$$\n\nThis equation can be factored as $\\,$$3\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}} $)=0.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258680 1113 #uuid "5430a8dc-1f40-5c68-ac3c-f9038cb61afb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258680 1145 "We factor $3{x}^{2}-90x+600$ using the greatest common factor (GCF).\n\nComputing the GCF, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{GCF}(3{x}^{2},90x,600)=3\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite each term in the original expression as a product with the $\\text{GCF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-90x+600 & = \\\\ 3\\cdot ({x}^{2})+3\\cdot (-30x)+3\\cdot (200)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the $\\text{GCF}$ outside the parentheses and keep the remaining terms inside:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot ({x}^{2})+3\\cdot (-30x)+3\\cdot (200) & = \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-30x+200)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{2}-30x+200.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258681 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258682 13194139533864 true] [17592186258681 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258681 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258681 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258682 13194139533864 true] [17592186258681 1110 #uuid "e0e71c8c-1796-56ae-9cd1-cd72e1f8c4a4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258682 1034 "{x}^{2}-30x+200" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258682 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258682 1111 #uuid "bab1c766-505b-5d09-b1f4-328c7c44c3a5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258683 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258684 13194139533864 true] [17592186258683 1089 "q-167229" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258683 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258683 1100 "Find the minimum number of luxury white chocolate boxes the company needs to produce per day to make a daily profit of $$600.$\n\nThe company needs to produce$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ luxury white chocolate boxes per day." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258683 1113 #uuid "dcb05104-1d9d-5307-88a8-33037d9f7de1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258683 1145 "From part 5, the required number of white chocolate boxes $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n3\\cdot ({x}^{2}-30x+200)=0.\n$$\n\nDividing this equation by $3,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-30x+200=0.\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we want to find a factor pair of the constant term $(200)$ that also adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-30).$ The required factors are $(-10)$ and $(-20).$\n\nUsing this factor pair, we write the linear term as $-30x=-10x-20x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-30x+200 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x-20x+200 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10x-20x+200 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-10x)+(-20x+200) & =0 \\\\ x(x-10)-20(x-10) & =0 \\\\ (x-10)(x-20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the solutions are $x=10$ or $x=20.$ Since we require the minimum solution, we conclude that the company needs to produce $10$ luxury white chocolate boxes per day to make a profit of $$600.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258684 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258685 13194139533864 true] [17592186258684 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258684 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258684 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258685 13194139533864 true] [17592186258684 1110 #uuid "7b3cb1c6-fc04-50f8-86d3-976bbe0f949b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258685 1034 "10" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258685 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258685 1111 #uuid "eb1ba20d-813b-5415-993b-3a79e31ed28e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258686 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258687 13194139533864 true] [17592186258686 1089 "q-167230" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258686 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258686 1100 "The company wants to increase its output by implementing a new production model. The number of luxury white chocolate boxes ${a}_{n}$ produced on the $n$th day since the implementation of the new model is given by the arithmetic sequence\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}+5,\\,{a}_{1}=10,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nWhat is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence?\n\n${a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258686 1113 #uuid "3439e283-cebc-5b5c-92b2-6edb9d70656f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258686 1145 "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\\,n\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nso we need to determine the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is ${a}_{1}=10,$ and we can use the recursive rule to compute the second term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}+5 \\\\  & =10+5 \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the first and second terms, we can compute the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =15-10 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute ${a}_{1}=10$ and $d=5$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =10+(n-1)(5) \\\\  & =10+5n-5 \\\\  & =5n+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=5n+5,\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258687 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258688 13194139533864 true] [17592186258687 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258687 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258687 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258688 13194139533864 true] [17592186258687 1110 #uuid "c7fd9160-1049-5716-8152-75e1f972a4e5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258688 1034 "5n+5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258688 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258688 1111 #uuid "be9bb242-d8e0-523e-a10d-ba52a7adb2a7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258689 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258690 13194139533864 true] [17592186258689 1089 "q-1687" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258689 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258689 1100 "Which angle is coterminal with $\\frac{8\\pi }{3}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258689 1113 #uuid "96eea9dc-629b-5788-b0d6-83fae5b2d265" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258689 1145 "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $ (in radians), we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi :$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi $ to $\\frac{8\\pi }{3}$:\n\n- $\\frac{8\\pi }{3}+2\\pi =\\frac{14\\pi }{3}$\n\nThere are no answer choices larger than $\\frac{14\\pi }{3},$ so we can stop here.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi $ from $\\frac{8\\pi }{3}$:\n\n- $\\frac{8\\pi }{3}-2\\pi =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\n- $\\frac{8\\pi }{3}-2\\cdot (2\\pi )=-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$\n\n- $\\frac{8\\pi }{3}-3\\cdot (2\\pi )=-\\frac{10\\pi }{3}$\n\nThere are no answer choices smaller than $-\\frac{10\\pi }{3},$ so we can stop here.\n\nSo the correct answer is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258690 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258691 13194139533864 true] [17592186258690 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258690 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258690 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258691 13194139533864 true] [17592186258690 1110 #uuid "7801f687-43e5-5b78-9ddc-4b65f5614671" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258691 1034 "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258691 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258691 1111 #uuid "7d7b9fe9-363b-5c56-8f86-c8753415720b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258692 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258693 13194139533864 true] [17592186258692 1089 "q-1700" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258692 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258692 1100 "Find the value of $\\operatorname{tan}({270}^{∘})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf the value is undefined, enter \"Undefined\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258692 1113 #uuid "c7e6e397-9e1b-53ed-9539-68c7fd844687" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258692 1145 "The angle ${270}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${270}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(0,-1).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}({270}^{∘})=0.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}({270}^{∘})=-1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}({270}^{∘})=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}({270}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{cos}({270}^{∘})}=\\frac{-1}{0}.\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $\\operatorname{tan}({270}^{∘})$ is undefined." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258693 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258694 13194139533864 true] [17592186258693 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258693 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258693 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258694 13194139533864 true] [17592186258693 1110 #uuid "effc45c7-2b7f-53bd-9606-791d0a6f57ca" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258694 1034 "Undefined" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258694 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258694 1111 #uuid "0ad12b9e-4974-5164-a400-ca66e15d7e60" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258695 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258696 13194139533864 true] [17592186258695 1089 "q-170143" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258695 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258695 1100 "If $x=\\sqrt[3]{y}+1,$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(3,8).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258695 1113 #uuid "c2e8234a-8f1b-5c06-89b8-b14ff16ffd00" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258695 1145 "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt[3]{y}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt[3]{y})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{1/3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{-2/3}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3{y}^{2/3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=3\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(3,8),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(3,8)} & =3\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}}=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258696 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258697 13194139533864 true] [17592186258696 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258696 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258696 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258697 13194139533864 true] [17592186258696 1110 #uuid "ceaa4d8c-4917-537a-a5bb-86d3ce2d66e5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258697 1034 "12" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258697 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258697 1111 #uuid "6f41737a-2e93-52b1-84d7-8ccbd56d94b6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258698 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258699 13194139533864 true] [17592186258698 1089 "q-1702" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258698 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258698 1100 "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf the value is undefined, enter \"Undefined\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258698 1113 #uuid "8c6dfeb5-0fab-5a95-9a4e-38dfe7f97395" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258698 1145 "The angle $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,-1).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{1}{0}.\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})$ is undefined." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258699 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258700 13194139533864 true] [17592186258699 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258699 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258699 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258700 13194139533864 true] [17592186258699 1110 #uuid "5bedce63-0a7b-569f-93e6-21d59ce5641a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258700 1034 "Undefined" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258700 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258700 1111 #uuid "8712dda2-536b-5ec6-bc6c-f50d40c71e4a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258701 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258702 13194139533864 true] [17592186258701 1089 "q-170491" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258701 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258701 1100 "Find, in radians, the argument of $z=-2-2\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258701 1113 #uuid "70bfc310-494e-53ee-86b4-a9541d3696d2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258701 1145 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d1c9cb7e7b00a11e00eafb4b51696c74c2a0cfb6a41c69cf27ecd011c382bfec.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\pi <\\operatorname{arg}(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ If $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\operatorname{arctan}(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z)=\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258702 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258703 13194139533864 true] [17592186258702 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258702 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258702 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258703 13194139533864 true] [17592186258702 1110 #uuid "ed2533ba-03c9-5b80-bda3-73df89b22c4f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258703 1034 "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258703 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258703 1111 #uuid "4fdc6b0a-3041-5128-91c6-15cccec4bf0a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258704 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258705 13194139533864 true] [17592186258704 1089 "q-170493" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258704 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258704 1100 "Find, in radians, the argument of $z=3-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258704 1113 #uuid "82e6558d-3a1c-5d3b-8169-608f8ebc1f57" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258704 1145 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d58ec3abe2b06d39ec4b8f66e785d433f6bfa6b8eb52faab98aae306a63debe5.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}(z)<2\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z)=2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258706 13194139533864 true] [17592186258705 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258705 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258705 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258706 13194139533864 true] [17592186258705 1110 #uuid "0622ec40-59a3-58fc-8295-363ef0b69c8f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258706 1034 "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258706 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258706 1111 #uuid "7d07a788-7c1d-5a77-a267-41ffbdc311fd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258707 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258708 13194139533864 true] [17592186258707 1089 "q-1713" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258707 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258707 1100 "$\\operatorname{sin}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258707 1113 #uuid "71492ad6-b806-5176-adb4-c0ec0f30d422" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258707 1145 "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{cos}v-\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})-\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x-\\operatorname{cos}x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258708 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258709 13194139533864 true] [17592186258708 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258708 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258708 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258709 13194139533864 true] [17592186258708 1110 #uuid "9f723981-0888-5d03-9387-43b25bc51de3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258709 1034 "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x-\\operatorname{cos}x}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258709 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258709 1111 #uuid "3df5731f-8578-5f92-9736-c4f5f2aa734d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258710 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258711 13194139533864 true] [17592186258710 1089 "q-17197" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258710 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258710 1100 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}(\\sqrt{x+2}+1).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258710 1113 #uuid "aa319499-3756-5d68-bea5-48fa20a410aa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258710 1145 "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+2}+1.$ Notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =\\sqrt{-2+2}+1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0}+1. \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d0e6532c8b96d7a3ab85d73a145a79292b8b0101cfa46ebffb08aefdeb06687b.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we notice that for the left-hand limit, we have:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$\n\nFor the right-hand limit, we have:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x)=1\n$$\n\nSince the two limits are not equal, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258711 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258712 13194139533864 true] [17592186258711 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258711 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258711 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258712 13194139533864 true] [17592186258711 1110 #uuid "0cb64419-83b9-5832-bf6e-114291864ca7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258712 1034 "\\text{DNE}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258712 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258712 1111 #uuid "dbb77218-aebe-55ab-af8a-d101023bcaa6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258713 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258714 13194139533864 true] [17592186258713 1089 "q-17271" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258713 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258713 1100 "The arithmetic series $1+10+19+28+37+46$ can be written in sigma notation as\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258713 1113 #uuid "0439465f-c44b-5ef9-840d-c708559f8d6e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258713 1145 "The first term is ${a}_{1}=1$ and the common difference is: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =10-1 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =1+(n-1)(9) \\\\  & =1+9n-9 \\\\  & =9n-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are $6$ terms in total, from $n=1$ to $n=6.$ So the sigma notation for the given sum is:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}(9n-8)\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258714 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258715 13194139533864 true] [17592186258714 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258714 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258714 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258715 13194139533864 true] [17592186258714 1110 #uuid "080aea64-8587-5a83-80aa-5adba4d0f0e8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258715 1034 "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}(9n-8)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258715 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258715 1111 #uuid "f799ae04-d491-5be0-b200-09d1d66f5a2b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258716 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258717 13194139533864 true] [17592186258716 1089 "q-17280" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258716 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258716 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/5e8383b56998815d4c981e72c946fdbf8de77ece15e52ca2ed5c7371c8f2c8af.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 3}{(\\frac{4f(x)-12}{{x}^{2}-2x+2})}^{2}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258716 1113 #uuid "6f7fded4-a7f4-5dbc-a96e-2d5c3e995127" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258716 1145 "Using the power and quotient rules, we can rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}{(\\frac{4f(x)-12}{{x}^{2}-2x+2})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 3}(4f(x)-12)}{{lim}_{x\\to 3}({x}^{2}-2x+2)})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above is true provided that  ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ exists.\n\nWe find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=4.$ We calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 3}(4f(x)-12)=4(4)-12=4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 3}({x}^{2}-2x+2)={3}^{2}-2(3)+2=5\n$$\n\nSo we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}{(\\frac{4f(x)-12}{{x}^{2}-2x+2})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 3}(4f(x)-12)}{{lim}_{x\\to 3}({x}^{2}-2x+2)})}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258717 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258718 13194139533864 true] [17592186258717 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258717 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258717 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258718 13194139533864 true] [17592186258717 1110 #uuid "a7063e60-d887-53de-8f03-68e4e7251e17" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258718 1034 "\\frac{16}{25}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258718 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258718 1111 #uuid "8fbd1f6f-6266-508a-9271-24f3d4543ce2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258719 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258720 13194139533864 true] [17592186258719 1089 "q-178245" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258719 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258719 1100 "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x)=9{x}^{3}+45{x}^{2}$ have multiplicity $1?$\n\n$x =$: {{x}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258719 1113 #uuid "947ba861-dcfa-53a6-bd12-92fc424fca88" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258719 1145 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. \n\nFor this polynomial, we can factor by noting that the greatest common factor of $9{x}^{3}$ and $45{x}^{2}$ is $9{x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =9{x}^{3}+45{x}^{2} \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}(x+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor ${x}^{2}$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- The factor $(x+5)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-5$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only the root $x=-5$ has multiplicity $1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258720 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258721 13194139533864 true] [17592186258720 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258720 1041 "x" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258720 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258721 13194139533864 true] [17592186258720 1110 #uuid "41937ddf-a832-5711-badd-e59e8e2d2e58" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258721 1034 "-5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258721 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258721 1111 #uuid "c6efb5b2-ddf8-578d-834b-dc2f46f5aa6b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258722 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258723 13194139533864 true] [17592186258722 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258725 13194139533864 true] [17592186258722 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258727 13194139533864 true] [17592186258722 1089 "q-178255" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258722 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258722 1100 "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-25x+25$ have multiplicity $1?$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}\n\nValue 3 (least to greatest): {{root-3}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258722 1113 #uuid "d02e2a80-3a55-5677-827d-c57a97e96eb7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258722 1145 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. For this polynomial, we can factor by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & ={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-25x+25 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x-1)-25(x-1) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-25)(x-1) \\\\  & =(x+5)(x-5)(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $(x+5)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-5$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\n- The factor $(x-5)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=5$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\n- The factor $(x-1)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=1$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, the roots $x=-5,1,5$ all have multiplicity $1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258723 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258724 13194139533864 true] [17592186258723 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258723 1041 "root-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258723 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258724 13194139533864 true] [17592186258723 1110 #uuid "12baccc5-b6a0-5e9c-839f-3f5eb5d4b3da" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258724 1034 "-5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258724 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258724 1111 #uuid "0f2efb63-e84b-5311-be2a-fc56be772094" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258725 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258726 13194139533864 true] [17592186258725 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258725 1041 "root-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258725 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258726 13194139533864 true] [17592186258725 1110 #uuid "b097d600-e75a-5994-b87e-5bd7ab4f8ee0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258726 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258726 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258726 1111 #uuid "bf022ec4-e217-5247-875d-f03f80b7942e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258727 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258728 13194139533864 true] [17592186258727 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258727 1041 "root-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258727 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258728 13194139533864 true] [17592186258727 1110 #uuid "4c3cf54e-16d3-5ffb-938d-c284cc0baabd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258728 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258728 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258728 1111 #uuid "d624c35f-739a-5d07-a324-4ddd18d2817f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258729 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258730 13194139533864 true] [17592186258729 1089 "q-1786" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258729 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258729 1100 "If a master combination lock has to be set to $3$ numbers and each number can range between $1$ and $40,$ including $1$ and $40.$ How many possible combinations are there?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258729 1113 #uuid "8507db0d-b50f-5c25-ab7d-8dbff152c32a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258729 1145 "Using the **rule of product**, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Number of combinations} & =\\text{Total number of options in the }1\\text{st position} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Total number of options in the }2\\text{nd position} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Total number of options in the }3\\text{rd position} \\\\  & =40\\times 40\\times 40 \\\\  & =64\\,000.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $64\\,000$ possible combinations." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258730 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258731 13194139533864 true] [17592186258730 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258730 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258730 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258731 13194139533864 true] [17592186258730 1110 #uuid "cda78ebf-0343-5a94-8ac1-a732e55d1311" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258731 1034 "64\\,000" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258731 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258731 1111 #uuid "0b274020-9c0a-590c-987a-a39babffa5e7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258732 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258733 13194139533864 true] [17592186258732 1089 "q-180277" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258732 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258732 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/a61a6232b2041bab3d455a7d765311ddfae25f674f8dbbf79696d2137355b159.png)\n\nThe Venn diagram shown above represents two events $A$ and $B$ and the number of outcomes in each region of the sample space.\n\nGiven that every outcome is equally likely, find $P({A}^{'}\\cup {B}^{'}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258732 1113 #uuid "27fe13ff-12fc-5d42-8d48-6dd6fccb6a52" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258732 1145 "To calculate the probability of an event using a Venn diagram, we count the number of outcomes in the region that represents the event, and divide by the total number of outcomes of the sample space $S.$\n\nIn the Venn diagram, ${A}^{'}\\cup {B}^{'}$ is represented by the area outside the circle $B$ or outside the circle $A$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/6a3bb7b671e45aeb15d3239cae6fcedb7c36e52b15141d66b23e65066ab37edc.png)\n\nWe count up the outcomes:\n\n- The number of outcomes in ${A}^{'}\\cup {B}^{'}$ is \n\n$$\n2+5+1=8\n$$\n\n- The total number of outcomes in $S$ is\n\n$$\n2+9+5+1=17.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}^{'}\\cup {B}^{'})=\\frac{8}{17}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258733 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258734 13194139533864 true] [17592186258733 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258733 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258733 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258734 13194139533864 true] [17592186258733 1110 #uuid "09e6db05-0c98-5a9e-b2fe-47e73fb3b8e9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258734 1034 "\\frac{8}{17}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258734 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258734 1111 #uuid "8707755e-8a7e-5dff-a69b-97c7680a3a1d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258735 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258736 13194139533864 true] [17592186258735 1089 "q-1803" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258735 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258735 1100 "A word consists of $3$ letters, as shown below:\n\n| Letter | Letter | Letter |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n\nHere, each \"Letter\" can be any capital from A-H ($8$ in total). How many possible words are there?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258735 1113 #uuid "9727e37f-7458-5fb2-9794-a48d52770acf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258735 1145 "For each letter, there is a total of $8$ possibilities: A, B, C, D, E, F, G, and H.\n\nUsing the **rule of product**, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Number of words} & =\\text{Number of options in the first slot} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of options in the second slot} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of options in the third slot} \\\\  & =8\\times 8\\times 8 \\\\  & ={8}^{3} \\\\  & =512.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are $512$ possible words." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258736 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258737 13194139533864 true] [17592186258736 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258736 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258736 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258737 13194139533864 true] [17592186258736 1110 #uuid "0196557c-c5ad-5730-928e-ed86abb7f003" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258737 1034 "512" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258737 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258737 1111 #uuid "03404903-6661-5ae8-b691-ba3920cab111" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258738 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258739 13194139533864 true] [17592186258738 1089 "q-18047" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258738 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258738 1100 "Let $a=⟨5,9⟩.$ Find $|\\,a\\,|.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258738 1113 #uuid "8406b4e9-04c6-52fc-af84-020cf7ff3fa4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258738 1145 "Using the formula, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\,| & =\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(5)^{2}+(9)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+81} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{106}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258739 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258740 13194139533864 true] [17592186258739 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258739 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258739 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258740 13194139533864 true] [17592186258739 1110 #uuid "d6c75e0d-a439-53ac-bd9b-8fbde45fa6f9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258740 1034 "\\sqrt{106}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258740 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258740 1111 #uuid "b0c5630e-f5f4-54c8-8584-dcc2aedcb980" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258741 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258742 13194139533864 true] [17592186258741 1089 "q-18048" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258741 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258741 1100 "if $a=⟨-4,8⟩$, then  what is the magnitude of  $a?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258741 1113 #uuid "425402e4-1000-5287-829b-c8c1c029863d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258741 1145 "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\,| & =\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258742 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258743 13194139533864 true] [17592186258742 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258742 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258742 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258743 13194139533864 true] [17592186258742 1110 #uuid "b62aaa6b-f109-5d1c-bc9f-45c53fc6c0e8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258743 1034 "4\\sqrt{5}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258743 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258743 1111 #uuid "5465f7ff-6941-5b43-892e-7db05bc0ea62" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258744 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258745 13194139533864 true] [17592186258744 1089 "q-18049" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258744 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258744 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/1a250499768a91e3e225a05d141e42d7ed89168ba82c5560777f64b8b29fac4a.png)\n\nLet $C(-1,3)$ and $D(1,-2).$ Find $|\\overset{\\to }{CD}|.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258744 1113 #uuid "a5ac1d93-f1e5-5ed9-858c-8c960fa481ce" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258744 1145 "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OC}=c=-i+3j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OD}=d=i-2j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{CD}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CD} & =\\overset{\\to }{OD}-\\overset{\\to }{OC} \\\\  & =d-c \\\\  & =(i-2j)-(-i+3j) \\\\  & =2i-5j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{CD}| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258745 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258746 13194139533864 true] [17592186258745 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258745 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258745 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258746 13194139533864 true] [17592186258745 1110 #uuid "feea1213-d4dc-56ef-94d3-648d9b29c71a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258746 1034 "\\sqrt{29}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258746 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258746 1111 #uuid "b9043654-a6a6-5fda-9fca-448b0e42f6e8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258747 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258748 13194139533864 true] [17592186258747 1089 "q-1805" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258747 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258747 1100 "A word consists of $4$ letters, as shown below:\n\n| Letter | Letter | Letter | Letter |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n\nHere, each \"Letter\" can be any capital from A-Z ($26$ in total). How many possible words are there?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258747 1113 #uuid "89ba8929-6b23-5b4b-b596-3975abc434a8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258747 1145 "For each letter, there is a total of $26$ possibilities.\n\nUsing the **rule of product**, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Number of words} & =\\text{Number of options in the first slot} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of options in the second slot} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of options in the third slot} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of options in the fourth slot} \\\\  & =26\\times 26\\times 26\\times 26 \\\\  & ={26}^{4} \\\\  & =456\\,976.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are $456\\,976$ possible words." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258748 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258749 13194139533864 true] [17592186258748 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258748 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258748 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258749 13194139533864 true] [17592186258748 1110 #uuid "02e71e6a-8a94-5374-a840-cc367c91a48a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258749 1034 "456\\,976" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258749 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258749 1111 #uuid "11e67cf6-278c-59bb-bedf-08afe3f917f2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258750 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258751 13194139533864 true] [17592186258750 1089 "q-18055" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258750 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258750 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/1e3e9c336bf85cf0f19b2326f12c5fd986fee5b0aae65b8d79257ea234efee13.png)\n\nPoints $P$ and $Q$ are shown in the picture above. Find $|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}|.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258750 1113 #uuid "2e284b77-e04c-5fe5-8766-d54dc8a48dab" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258750 1145 "From the picture, we obtain $P(1,1)$ and $Q(2,3).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OP}=p=i+j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OQ}=q=2i+3j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =q-p \\\\  & =(2i+3j)-(i+j) \\\\  & =i+2j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258751 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258752 13194139533864 true] [17592186258751 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258751 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258751 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258752 13194139533864 true] [17592186258751 1110 #uuid "55beb37c-2d2b-5ea3-a917-eaab108a2bd3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258752 1034 "\\sqrt{5}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258752 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258752 1111 #uuid "f28586fc-b338-5f64-812b-f9245dd5f3c5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258753 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258754 13194139533864 true] [17592186258753 1089 "q-1809" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258753 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258753 1100 "Rio's Pizza offers a mix and match pizza on its menu. There are $4$ different meat types to choose from, $5$ different vegetables, $4$ different types of cheese, and $2$ different types of crust. How many different types of pizza can be made by choosing a type of meat, a vegetable, cheese and a crust?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258753 1113 #uuid "013caba4-8d5e-572a-9ea0-5bedf0d0d6bd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258753 1145 "Using the **rule of product**, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Number of pizzas} & =\\text{Number of type of meats} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of type of vegetables} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of type of cheese} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of type of crust} \\\\  & =4\\times 5\\times 4\\times 2 \\\\  & =160.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $160$ types of pizza can be made with the given ingredients." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258754 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258755 13194139533864 true] [17592186258754 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258754 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258754 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258755 13194139533864 true] [17592186258754 1110 #uuid "d535795e-b9e8-5397-b1c7-31bd591797df" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258755 1034 "160" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258755 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258755 1111 #uuid "3d69ac29-79f0-5fcb-975f-beec98dd346e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258756 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258757 13194139533864 true] [17592186258756 1089 "q-1844" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258756 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258756 1100 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=5{x}^{3}+\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258756 1113 #uuid "9434291c-6cfe-5b68-8887-fef55433f9be" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258756 1145 "Here,  we convert the radical into a rational exponent, and we apply the addition, constant multiple, and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{3}+\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 3{x}^{(3-1)}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {x}^{(\\frac{1}{2}-1)} \\\\  & =15{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =15{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258757 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258758 13194139533864 true] [17592186258757 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258757 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258757 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258758 13194139533864 true] [17592186258757 1110 #uuid "5b6dd331-f0eb-5b9f-b97a-db353a640938" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258758 1034 "15{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258758 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258758 1111 #uuid "3ad2fd21-ec76-5bd4-9ee5-8cccb3dcc982" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258759 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258760 13194139533864 true] [17592186258759 1089 "q-1852" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258759 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258759 1100 "Find ${f}^{''}(x)$ for $f(x)=12{x}^{3}-5\\operatorname{sin}x+4\\operatorname{cos}x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258759 1113 #uuid "e0bfad45-7f5d-5462-bf0e-7422f149d562" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258759 1145 "Computing the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & ={(12{x}^{3}-5\\operatorname{sin}x+4\\operatorname{cos}x)}^{'} &  \\\\  & =3\\cdot 12{x}^{2}-5(\\operatorname{cos}x)+4(-\\operatorname{sin}x) \\\\  & =36{x}^{2}-5\\operatorname{cos}x-4\\operatorname{sin}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{''}(x) & ={(36{x}^{2}-5\\operatorname{cos}x-4\\operatorname{sin}x)}^{'} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 36x-5(-\\operatorname{sin}x)-4(\\operatorname{cos}x) \\\\  & =72x+5\\operatorname{sin}x-4\\operatorname{cos}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258760 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258761 13194139533864 true] [17592186258760 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258760 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258760 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258761 13194139533864 true] [17592186258760 1110 #uuid "beb3d33d-5499-5dae-98ba-1cc96b74dff5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258761 1034 "72x+5\\operatorname{sin}x-4\\operatorname{cos}x" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258761 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258761 1111 #uuid "a5f5dedb-d7a1-543c-95dd-30eb2991f8a2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258762 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258763 13194139533864 true] [17592186258762 1089 "q-18687" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258762 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258762 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/80749f47491522bb42ccca5bce0176b69ca03fa5ac6c33df37350392936857c3.png)\n\nGiven the picture above, find the position vector of the point $Q(1,1,2)$ in terms of $i$, $j$, and $k.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258762 1113 #uuid "b6c80cb4-20a6-5229-893e-cb6d3fa7a36d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258762 1145 "Remember that in general, if a point $Q$ has coordinates $({q}_{x},{q}_{y},{q}_{z}),$ then its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OQ}=q={q}_{x}i+{q}_{y}j+{q}_{z}k.\n$$\n\nHere, the point $Q$ has the coordinates $(1,1,2),$ so its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OQ}=q=i+j+2k.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258763 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258764 13194139533864 true] [17592186258763 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258763 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258763 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258764 13194139533864 true] [17592186258763 1110 #uuid "9c4ced30-ab72-5ea5-96f1-9a6336188a0b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258764 1034 "i+j+2k" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258764 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258764 1111 #uuid "d80e21f6-7eeb-5260-8963-0283594391d2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258765 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258766 13194139533864 true] [17592186258765 1089 "q-1873" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258765 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258765 1100 "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $y=-3\\operatorname{sin}x+5{x}^{3}-19.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258765 1113 #uuid "a9bb1b98-cc66-5ed7-8fea-7a253386cbb5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258765 1145 "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3\\operatorname{sin}x+5{x}^{3}-19) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}x+3\\cdot 5{x}^{2}-0 \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}x+15{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3\\operatorname{cos}x+15{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-3(-\\operatorname{sin}x)+2\\cdot 15x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}x+30x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258766 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258767 13194139533864 true] [17592186258766 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258766 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258766 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258767 13194139533864 true] [17592186258766 1110 #uuid "a363de9f-6798-5ba7-b250-28d1adf26f4d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258767 1034 "3\\operatorname{sin}x+30x" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258767 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258767 1111 #uuid "01539f50-14e5-55b4-b9c0-f017053f2d32" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258768 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258769 13194139533864 true] [17592186258768 1089 "q-1883" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258768 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258768 1100 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ if $y=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{cos}x-3\\operatorname{sec}x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258768 1113 #uuid "49490cb9-3ce1-5534-a5d6-1fffc1caa530" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258768 1145 "We take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{-2}+\\operatorname{cos}x-3\\operatorname{sec}x) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 3{x}^{-3}+(-\\operatorname{sin}x)-3\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x \\\\  & =-6{x}^{-3}-\\operatorname{sin}x-3\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}-\\operatorname{sin}x-3\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258769 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258770 13194139533864 true] [17592186258769 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258769 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258769 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258770 13194139533864 true] [17592186258769 1110 #uuid "33139f29-20c3-5e6c-a620-3ab2dd74f087" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258770 1034 "-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}-\\operatorname{sin}x-3\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258770 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258770 1111 #uuid "4aabe493-35ea-5f67-8298-28db0b078074" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258771 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258772 13194139533864 true] [17592186258771 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258774 13194139533864 true] [17592186258771 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258776 13194139533864 true] [17592186258771 1089 "q-19049" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258771 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258771 1100 "If $r=⟨0,-2,4⟩$ and $s=i-6j$, then $r+s=$\n\nRow 1, column 1: {{r1c1}}\n\nRow 2, column 1: {{r2c1}}\n\nRow 3, column 1: {{r3c1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258771 1113 #uuid "92ba06b2-5956-5b83-8cb1-36fb1d3ffbe6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258771 1145 "First, notice that\n\n$$\ns=i-6j=⟨1,-6,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTo compute the sum, we just add each component individually:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r+s & =⟨0,-2,4⟩+⟨1,-6,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0+1,-2-6,4+0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-8,4⟩ \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258772 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258773 13194139533864 true] [17592186258772 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258772 1041 "r1c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258772 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258773 13194139533864 true] [17592186258772 1110 #uuid "e926ece9-9c6a-5930-b891-d76a9c4b2b6a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258773 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258773 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258773 1111 #uuid "9f7b78d4-1cc0-5070-b66f-31145be45620" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258774 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258775 13194139533864 true] [17592186258774 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258774 1041 "r2c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258774 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258775 13194139533864 true] [17592186258774 1110 #uuid "a50714a1-8282-57e5-8d21-2bb9829fc692" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258775 1034 "-8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258775 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258775 1111 #uuid "c838a260-aad2-5399-833d-b5dbda46900c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258776 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258777 13194139533864 true] [17592186258776 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258776 1041 "r3c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258776 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258777 13194139533864 true] [17592186258776 1110 #uuid "019580bf-188b-5cfc-bd21-39b4613134ce" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258777 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258777 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258777 1111 #uuid "e83ad01d-cddb-50a4-9e42-14abe9456bdf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258778 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258779 13194139533864 true] [17592186258778 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258781 13194139533864 true] [17592186258778 1089 "q-19154" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258778 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258778 1100 "Consider the following table of values for $f(x).$ Knowing that the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,23]$ is $1.5,$ find the value of $p.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ | $\\,13\\,$ | $\\,18\\,$ | $\\,21\\,$ | $\\,23\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,3\\,$ | $\\,-{p}^{2}-4\\,$ | $\\,-13\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,9\\,$ | $\\,13\\,$ |\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258778 1113 #uuid "149a5bb5-23b4-56cd-ac8a-edc3c7f35e91" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258778 1145 "Using the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(23)-f(1)}{23-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{13-(-{p}^{2}-4)}{22} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}^{2}+17}{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=1.5$ on the interval $[1,23].$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\frac{{p}^{2}+17}{22}=1.5.\n$$\n\nSolving for $p,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{p}^{2}+17}{22} & =1.5 \\\\ {p}^{2}+17 & =33 \\\\ {p}^{2} & =33-17 \\\\ {p}^{2} & =16 \\\\ p & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $p=-4$ or $p=4.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258779 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258780 13194139533864 true] [17592186258779 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258779 1041 "root-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258779 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258780 13194139533864 true] [17592186258779 1110 #uuid "f53d7482-1cba-564a-9af7-5083dcff0367" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258780 1034 "-4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258780 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258780 1111 #uuid "8877ffb7-f58c-5fbd-ba52-99c8f52a63f4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258781 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258782 13194139533864 true] [17592186258781 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258781 1041 "root-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258781 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258782 13194139533864 true] [17592186258781 1110 #uuid "9f890d1d-8aea-54ba-95ff-94ffdb406cff" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258782 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258782 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258782 1111 #uuid "f11677ac-9262-56f2-8cbc-9d4cbc75e73a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258783 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258784 13194139533864 true] [17592186258783 1089 "q-19160" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258783 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258783 1100 "Consider the following table of values for $f(x).$ Knowing that the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,3]$ is $3,$ find the value of $p.$ \n\n| $x$ | $\\,-5\\,$ | $\\,-2\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,3\\,$ | $\\,5\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,-11\\,$ | $\\,2p-1\\,$ | $\\,-5\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ | $\\,p+10\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ | $\\,8\\,$ |\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258783 1113 #uuid "2e3ab4e9-a63e-5231-88df-c45b46948041" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258783 1145 "Using the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(3)-f(-2)}{3-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(p+10)-(2p-1)}{3+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11-p}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=3$ on the interval $[-2,3].$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\frac{11-p}{5}=3.\n$$\n\nSolving for $p,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11-p}{5} & =3 \\\\ 11-p & =15 \\\\ -p & =15-11 \\\\ -p & =4 \\\\ p & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258784 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258785 13194139533864 true] [17592186258784 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258784 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258784 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258785 13194139533864 true] [17592186258784 1110 #uuid "9a3ef0df-5c83-5dcc-979e-1d960c4c3f11" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258785 1034 "-4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258785 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258785 1111 #uuid "7415d098-c067-5d3a-a6ae-5cd2e605e7b9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258786 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258787 13194139533864 true] [17592186258786 1089 "q-19181" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258786 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258786 1100 "Find the exact solution to $\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{x}{3})=3.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258786 1113 #uuid "95caf8a3-14ba-5322-a4f2-b737554e209c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258786 1145 "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{x}{3}) & =3 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}(x/3)} & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}(x/3)}=\\frac{x}{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{3} & ={e}^{3} \\\\ x & =3{e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258787 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258788 13194139533864 true] [17592186258787 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258787 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258787 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258788 13194139533864 true] [17592186258787 1110 #uuid "e9ffc634-22c1-519a-9294-d4f4148d2588" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258788 1034 "3{e}^{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258788 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258788 1111 #uuid "8c1c140e-0c12-5d28-b011-815bad00ed2b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258789 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258790 13194139533864 true] [17592186258789 1089 "q-19262" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258789 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258789 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/25e78d632f4bbe23ebbf700946b1a05a6b4f9f578c1d8047ddb067842a820fa9.png)\n\nThe point $A$ with coordinates $(6,\\operatorname{ln}6)$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}x,$ and the point ${A}^{'}$ with coordinates $(4,\\operatorname{ln}6)$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}(ax),$ shown above. What is the value of $a?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258789 1113 #uuid "075f5bd8-8f8e-5a44-8865-d55c0e9f72f8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258789 1145 "The graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}(ax)$ can be obtained from $y=\\operatorname{ln}x$ by a horizontal stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{a}.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we deduce that under such a transformation, the point $(6,\\operatorname{ln}6)$ that lies on $y=\\operatorname{ln}x$ becomes \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{a}\\cdot 6,\\operatorname{ln}6)=(4,\\operatorname{ln}6)\n$$\n\nwhich lies on $y=\\operatorname{ln}(ax).$ \n\nTherefore, $a=\\frac{3}{2}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258790 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258791 13194139533864 true] [17592186258790 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258790 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258790 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258791 13194139533864 true] [17592186258790 1110 #uuid "54c917c5-7d48-5935-8b3a-9bf7e3ba9958" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258791 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258791 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258791 1111 #uuid "23d5ee2a-8c82-5e86-92da-fcd172396fdb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258792 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258793 13194139533864 true] [17592186258792 1089 "q-19306" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258792 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258792 1100 "The position $s(t)$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=5{e}^{t}+2\\operatorname{ln}t+{t}^{2},\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity at time $t=2.$ Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258792 1113 #uuid "f100bda5-e5cc-5ecc-9c54-883833c98d6d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258792 1145 "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5{e}^{t}+2\\operatorname{ln}t+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =5{e}^{t}+\\frac{2}{t}+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the velocity at $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the velocity function above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =5{e}^{2}+\\frac{2}{2}+2(2) \\\\  & =5{e}^{2}+5 \\\\  & =41.945… \\\\  & \\approx 42\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258793 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258794 13194139533864 true] [17592186258793 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258793 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258793 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258794 13194139533864 true] [17592186258793 1110 #uuid "e06156ff-7e9a-591e-81cc-e21a3cf5c67c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258794 1034 "42" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258794 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258794 1111 #uuid "98f7b3ef-65a4-52b4-b5ee-e7567637249d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258795 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258796 13194139533864 true] [17592186258795 1089 "q-1938" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258795 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258795 1100 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ if $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}x}{4x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258795 1113 #uuid "124929ec-0b47-501a-8761-14e3174bc1cd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258795 1145 "We calculate the derivative using the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}x}{4x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x)(4x)-(\\operatorname{sec}x)(4)}{(4x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4x\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-4\\operatorname{sec}x}{16{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(x\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-\\operatorname{sec}x)}{16{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-\\operatorname{sec}x}{4{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258796 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258797 13194139533864 true] [17592186258796 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258796 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258796 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258797 13194139533864 true] [17592186258796 1110 #uuid "42ed291c-f172-57c1-945a-5a2c11b77b6b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258797 1034 "\\frac{x\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-\\operatorname{sec}x}{4{x}^{2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258797 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258797 1111 #uuid "6bfad614-2b1c-5d61-a877-1547f550341a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258798 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258799 13194139533864 true] [17592186258798 1089 "q-19452" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258798 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258798 1100 "The displacement (in meters) of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)=10\\operatorname{sin}t+5\\operatorname{cos}t,$ where $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the particle's acceleration at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ seconds.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258798 1113 #uuid "a75b4db1-5076-5b68-9e0a-66e78c347afd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258798 1145 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{sin}t+5\\operatorname{cos}t) \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}t-5\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{cos}t-5\\operatorname{sin}t) \\\\  & =-10\\operatorname{sin}t-5\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-10\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-5\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-10\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-10{\\text{ m/s}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ seconds is $-10\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258799 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258800 13194139533864 true] [17592186258799 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258799 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258799 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258800 13194139533864 true] [17592186258799 1110 #uuid "1bdf3284-1320-5a8b-b09d-06ba8f0f4cd6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258800 1034 "-10\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258800 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258800 1111 #uuid "b7dfa859-75e4-5dd6-a0ed-49fdcb8ae109" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258801 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258802 13194139533864 true] [17592186258801 1089 "q-19455" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258801 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258801 1100 "The displacement of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)=2{e}^{-t}-\\operatorname{ln}t-4t.$ Find the particle's acceleration at time $t.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258801 1113 #uuid "fb087fc5-5c8e-5daa-8c21-50c3794c633b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258801 1145 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{-t}-\\operatorname{ln}t-4t) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-t}-\\frac{1}{t}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2{e}^{-t}-\\frac{1}{t}-4) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-t}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258802 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258803 13194139533864 true] [17592186258802 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258802 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258802 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258803 13194139533864 true] [17592186258802 1110 #uuid "6d0e3494-3f35-5a2b-a913-8b989f1cc752" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258803 1034 "2{e}^{-t}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258803 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258803 1111 #uuid "a4e18b1b-d9d7-56d0-aea6-f9fa6cfa1eac" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258804 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258805 13194139533864 true] [17592186258804 1089 "q-1949" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258804 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258804 1100 "Find ${y}^{'}$ for $y={log}_{5}(5x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258804 1113 #uuid "8315c87a-da4c-5df4-9d92-af7fcfe96632" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258804 1145 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={log}_{5}(u),\\,u=5x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(5)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(5)}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(5x)\\operatorname{ln}(5)}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{5}}{\\cancel{5}x\\operatorname{ln}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258805 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258806 13194139533864 true] [17592186258805 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258805 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258805 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258806 13194139533864 true] [17592186258805 1110 #uuid "55c0c72f-7925-51bc-83e4-6477c1937fde" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258806 1034 "\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}(5)}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258806 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258806 1111 #uuid "2d38e9d8-1eac-5015-82c0-a063566e871e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258807 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258808 13194139533864 true] [17592186258807 1089 "q-19761" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258807 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258807 1100 "Charlie goes to buy a bike. There are $8$ black bikes, $6$ white bikes, and $7$ silver bikes. How many choices of bike does Charlie have?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258807 1113 #uuid "f8cb7c22-3b81-59c8-9e12-cf4c433c9fb7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258807 1145 "In order to find the total number of choices Charlie has, we can use the **rule of sum**:\n\n$$\n8+6+7=21\n$$\n\nTherefore, Charlie has $21$ choices of bike." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258808 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258809 13194139533864 true] [17592186258808 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258808 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258808 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258809 13194139533864 true] [17592186258808 1110 #uuid "b98cecff-487d-58d4-a352-f3f4ae326137" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258809 1034 "21" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258809 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258809 1111 #uuid "5e959508-8320-5502-aa8d-9f88703c7ad1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258810 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258811 13194139533864 true] [17592186258810 1089 "q-19821" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258810 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258810 1100 "Peter has $8$ shirts, $6$ pants, and $6$ pairs of shoes. If Peter wears one shirt, one pant, and one pair of shoes, how many different ways can Peter dress?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258810 1113 #uuid "aaf4d311-c3be-544b-af6a-27bd8d3ca99a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258810 1145 "Using the **rule of product**, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Number of outfits} & =\\text{Number of shirts} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of pants} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of pairs of shoes} \\\\  & =8\\times 6\\times 6 \\\\  & =288.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Peter can dress in $288$ different ways." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258811 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258812 13194139533864 true] [17592186258811 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258811 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258811 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258812 13194139533864 true] [17592186258811 1110 #uuid "d0663bbf-e9d2-5951-a932-2f4da6e5e822" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258812 1034 "288" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258812 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258812 1111 #uuid "8abc9de6-7201-5c0d-9f8d-5b05fad87efa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258813 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258814 13194139533864 true] [17592186258813 1089 "q-19824" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258813 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258813 1100 "Matthew goes to the library to select a book. In the library, there are $12$ fiction books, $15$ science books, $19$ religious books, and $20$ history books. How many choices of book does Matthew have?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258813 1113 #uuid "e8193198-758f-58fd-af4b-ef906d9232ef" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258813 1145 "In order to find the total number of choices Matthew has, we can use the **rule of sum**:\n\n$$\n12+15+19+20=66\n$$\n\nTherefore, Matthew has $66$ choices of book." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258814 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258815 13194139533864 true] [17592186258814 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258814 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258814 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258815 13194139533864 true] [17592186258814 1110 #uuid "d920084c-b257-5056-a680-3d4357e1663f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258815 1034 "66" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258815 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258815 1111 #uuid "b9321b60-2a46-50c5-b364-64bd11aec43d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258816 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258817 13194139533864 true] [17592186258816 1089 "q-19877" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258816 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258816 1100 "Given that $k\\ge 0$ is an integer, $\\frac{{((k+1)!)}^{2}}{k!}=$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258816 1113 #uuid "4ee6a7d7-dbe7-5b40-954a-acea9efee6e8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258816 1145 "First, we notice that\n\n$$\n(k+1)!=(k+1)k!\n$$\n\nThen, we can cancel the $k!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{((k+1)!)}^{2}}{k!} & =\\frac{{((k+1)k!)}^{2}}{k!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(k+1)^{2}\\,\\cdot (k!)^{2}}{k!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(k+1)^{2}\\,\\cdot k!\\cdot \\cancel{k!}}{\\cancel{k!}} \\\\  & =(k+1)^{2}k!\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258817 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258818 13194139533864 true] [17592186258817 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258819 13194139533864 true] [17592186258817 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258820 13194139533864 true] [17592186258817 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258821 13194139533864 true] [17592186258817 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258822 13194139533864 true] [17592186258817 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186258817 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258817 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258821 13194139533864 true] [17592186258817 1110 #uuid "f402311c-4280-5dc9-b696-2f17bc3e00bc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258818 1034 "\\frac{(k+1)^{2}}{k!}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258818 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258818 1111 #uuid "82a50d39-d29f-52e0-85f8-4f3954ebd28a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258819 1034 "(k+1)k!" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258819 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258819 1111 #uuid "b5ddf863-39cd-5d4b-b3a8-b634565b2e09" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258820 1034 "\\frac{k+1}{k!}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258820 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258820 1111 #uuid "53069e24-60e2-55cb-802d-a45c95d6ce5e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258821 1034 "(k+1)^{2}k!" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258821 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258821 1111 #uuid "7390bed0-b01c-5f2a-befd-54ef50e8735e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258822 1034 "(k+1)^{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258822 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258822 1111 #uuid "d9a85205-4c01-59c8-80a2-d25bfd66faee" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258823 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258824 13194139533864 true] [17592186258823 1089 "q-19882" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258823 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258823 1100 "Given that $n\\ge 9$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(n-8)!\\,(n+9)!}{(n-9)!\\,(n+7)!}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258823 1113 #uuid "4a8f7cc8-f392-57e5-b3c9-254ee480518f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258823 1145 "First, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n+9)! & =(n+9)(n+8)(n+7)! \\\\ (n-8)! & =(n-8)(n-9)!\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can cancel the $(n+7)!$ and $(n-9)!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(n-8)!\\,(n+9)!}{(n-9)!\\,(n+7)!} & =\\frac{[(n-8)\\cdot (n-9)!]\\cdot [(n+9)\\cdot (n+8)\\cdot (n+7)!]}{(n-9)!\\cdot (n+7)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n-8)\\cdot \\cancel{(n-9)!}\\cdot (n+9)\\cdot (n+8)\\cdot \\cancel{(n+7)!}}{\\cancel{(n-9)!}\\cdot \\cancel{(n+7)!}} \\\\  & =(n-8)\\cdot (n+8)\\cdot (n+9) \\\\  & =(n-8)(n+8)(n+9)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258824 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258825 13194139533864 true] [17592186258824 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258826 13194139533864 true] [17592186258824 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258827 13194139533864 true] [17592186258824 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258828 13194139533864 true] [17592186258824 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258829 13194139533864 true] [17592186258824 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186258824 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258824 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258826 13194139533864 true] [17592186258824 1110 #uuid "520f903f-cc0f-5087-a651-ef4c311135ef" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258825 1034 "(n+8)(n+9)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258825 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258825 1111 #uuid "6d4b7050-b0ec-506e-8d2d-3de366c66aef" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258826 1034 "(n-8)(n+8)(n+9)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258826 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258826 1111 #uuid "77002e1b-3856-5764-ac6e-3589194b4e01" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258827 1034 "(n+7)(n+8)(n+9)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258827 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258827 1111 #uuid "238bf615-e4f5-5bc6-8891-998d63d57915" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258828 1034 "\\frac{n+9}{n-9}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258828 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258828 1111 #uuid "acbce194-3273-5052-9801-7566a12961bb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258829 1034 "\\frac{(n+8)(n+9)}{n-9}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258829 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258829 1111 #uuid "43d0cf0c-78ed-59b6-80b1-240f71aac532" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258830 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258831 13194139533864 true] [17592186258830 1089 "q-20183" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258830 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258830 1100 "A ball is thrown up in the air. After $t$ seconds, its height is $h(t)=-16{t}^{2}+54t+10$ feet above the ground.\nWhat is the instantaneous rate of change of the height when $t=1.5$ seconds?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258830 1113 #uuid "9a791af4-1cbd-592a-a199-1e3e85cf8cfa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258830 1145 "The instantaneous rate of change of the height when $t=1.5$ seconds is equal to ${h}^{'}(1.5).$ \n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-16{t}^{2}+54t+10) \\\\  & =-32t+54.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=1.5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{'}(1.5) & =-32(1.5)+54 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the height when $t=1.5$ seconds is $6$ feet per second." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258831 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258832 13194139533864 true] [17592186258831 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258831 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258831 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258832 13194139533864 true] [17592186258831 1110 #uuid "d9fbe8e7-03e3-57f1-8d03-b8f737b529ba" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258832 1034 "6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258832 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258832 1111 #uuid "01a087fc-2640-5da9-891d-8d1a3aca9168" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258833 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258834 13194139533864 true] [17592186258833 1089 "q-20276" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258833 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258833 1100 "Frank has a bag with $10$ different balls. In how many ways can Frank select a set of $8$ balls from the bag, considering that the order does not matter?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258833 1113 #uuid "aaff4c14-16ea-5dec-8f92-9c9a5e0030ea" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258833 1145 "In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $10$ balls, so $n=10.$ We want to select $8$ of those balls from the bag, so we have $r=8.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different potential sets of $8$ balls is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{10}{C}_{8} & =\\frac{10!}{(10-8)!\\,8!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10!}{2!8!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 9\\cdot \\cancel{8!}}{2!\\cancel{8!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 9}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{90}{2} \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258834 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258835 13194139533864 true] [17592186258834 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258834 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258834 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258835 13194139533864 true] [17592186258834 1110 #uuid "505379a5-d388-5642-8c53-555ca99c500e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258835 1034 "45" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258835 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258835 1111 #uuid "9903e779-9ba3-5920-8450-53ab46f9cc63" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258836 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258837 13194139533864 true] [17592186258836 1089 "q-2029" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258836 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258836 1100 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{z}{5}\\,\\text{d}z.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258836 1113 #uuid "739af692-e2e6-5444-a61f-f0c4fca9a9b7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258836 1145 "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{z}{5}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{1}{5}\\int z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{{z}^{1+1}}{1+1})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {z}^{2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{z}^{2}}{10}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258837 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258838 13194139533864 true] [17592186258837 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258837 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258837 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258838 13194139533864 true] [17592186258837 1110 #uuid "7dfedbf2-c835-5df4-be1a-365d0d36a169" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258838 1034 "\\frac{{z}^{2}}{10}+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258838 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258838 1111 #uuid "0c7103af-3b5f-5041-971c-4ee88f87e34e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258839 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258840 13194139533864 true] [17592186258839 1089 "q-20823" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258839 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258839 1100 "A curve is defined as $y=2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+5x.$ Find the average rate of change of $y$ over the interval $[1,4].$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258839 1113 #uuid "a2e31816-43ec-559b-a472-2ed0efb972fe" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258839 1145 "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+5x.$  Applying the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δy}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(4)-f(1)}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{[2({4}^{3})-{4}^{2}+5(4)]-[2({1}^{3})-{1}^{2}+5(1)]}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{132-6}{3} \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258840 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258841 13194139533864 true] [17592186258840 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258840 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258840 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258841 13194139533864 true] [17592186258840 1110 #uuid "d69339cc-4899-5ceb-bc91-5dc45b77c967" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258841 1034 "42" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258841 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258841 1111 #uuid "a978ec35-daa3-5475-b451-48874291889c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258842 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258843 13194139533864 true] [17592186258842 1089 "q-21026" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258842 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258842 1100 "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+8$ is strictly increasing.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258842 1113 #uuid "20089cbf-a0a2-56b3-8290-b7c3572f1443" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258842 1145 "The function is strictly increasing where ${f}^{'}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =2\\cdot 3{x}^{2}-3\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-6x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-6x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n6x(x-1)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=6x(x-1)$ has roots at $x=0$ and $x=1,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ec7ea6baa82d60a6cf472c904460190042d889d86cb092b19d9809111bd93b36.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the intervals $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258843 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258844 13194139533864 true] [17592186258843 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258843 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258843 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258844 13194139533864 true] [17592186258843 1110 #uuid "d2b9ab03-2c43-56b7-a9dc-b74729a8471a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258844 1034 "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (1,\\infty )" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258844 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258844 1111 #uuid "2bd9bb88-515f-53cf-9423-91c74e4a8660" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258845 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258846 13194139533864 true] [17592186258845 1089 "q-21123" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258845 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258845 1100 "The number of machines infected by a computer virus is represented by the function $f(t)=4{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+5t+11,$ where $t$ is the time, in days, since the virus was first detected. What is the average rate of growth of $f(t)$ over the first week?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258845 1113 #uuid "95d04696-3038-5931-a2a7-b332a1053193" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258845 1145 "Let $f(t)=4{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+5t+11$ be the number of machines infected by the computer virus after $t$ days. To find the average rate of growth of $f(t)$ over the first week, we use the average rate of change formula on the interval $[0,7]$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δt} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(7)-f(0)}{7-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{[4(7)^{3}-2(7)^{2}+5(7)+11]-[4(0)^{3}-2(0)^{2}+5(0)+11]}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1309}{7} \\\\  & =187\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average rate of growth over the first week is $187$ machines per day." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258846 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258847 13194139533864 true] [17592186258846 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258846 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258846 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258847 13194139533864 true] [17592186258846 1110 #uuid "d381769d-59ee-54e7-87b7-fa734ad44d15" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258847 1034 "187" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258847 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258847 1111 #uuid "4dab1977-579e-50a4-86c6-15428cba7f1a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258848 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258849 13194139533864 true] [17592186258848 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258851 13194139533864 true] [17592186258848 1089 "q-211287" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258848 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258848 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/282e65324ea1e7893da25ead1cc822c7c166df880c3aecc60face9adbbb7279a.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=f(x+3)-2?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258848 1113 #uuid "4e52eb90-51ba-5a17-ba12-4144b8a682da" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258848 1145 "Notice that the graph of $y=f(x+3)-2$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $3$ units to the left, and then\n\n- translating it by $2$ unit down.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=f(x+3)-2.$ So, we need to find the image of $(-2,6),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=f(x+3)-2,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-2,6).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $3$ units to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x+3).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2-3,6)=(-5,6).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units downward and get the graph of $y=f(x+3)-2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-5,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-5,6-2)=(-5,4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=f(x+3)-2$ is $(-5,4).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258849 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258850 13194139533864 true] [17592186258849 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258849 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258849 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258850 13194139533864 true] [17592186258849 1110 #uuid "d8562ea8-b60f-5aab-8f71-8c5a505b05ec" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258850 1034 "-5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258850 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258850 1111 #uuid "c5e182b6-70c4-58ac-8ad9-1fed5941e707" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258851 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258852 13194139533864 true] [17592186258851 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258851 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258851 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258852 13194139533864 true] [17592186258851 1110 #uuid "da23d844-cff0-5c78-a80e-fb76eedea37a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258852 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258852 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258852 1111 #uuid "7471de03-1dd9-5e44-8b88-2757d3b13db9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258853 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258854 13194139533864 true] [17592186258853 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258856 13194139533864 true] [17592186258853 1089 "q-211304" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258853 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258853 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/73da11bf77a8bc10c00f5636176321ed1b44bbf614c8d4658e574e8ae5f85831.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of $y=f(3x)+4?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258853 1113 #uuid "792abd45-247a-55f0-b21d-fe9a605db7a6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258853 1145 "Notice that the graph of $y=f(3x)+4$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis, and then\n\n- translating it by $4$ units up.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global minimum of $y=f(x)$ to the global minimum of $y=f(3x)+4.$ So, we need to find the image of $(15,-5),$ the global minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=f(3x)+4,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(15,-5).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the result along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ and get the graph of $y=f(3x).$ After this transformation, the point $(15,-5)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 15,-5)=(5,-5).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the graph vertically by $4$ units up and get the graph of $y=f(3x)+4.$ After this transformation, the point $(5,-5)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(5,-5+4)=(5,-1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=f(3x)+4$ is $(5,-1).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258854 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258855 13194139533864 true] [17592186258854 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258854 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258854 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258855 13194139533864 true] [17592186258854 1110 #uuid "ce8f3da4-f6ae-5e81-af3b-b85a93e4272f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258855 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258855 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258855 1111 #uuid "8cb2da4c-01a9-5aec-8ed0-4837588db794" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258856 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258857 13194139533864 true] [17592186258856 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258856 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258856 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258857 13194139533864 true] [17592186258856 1110 #uuid "079eb80a-3cfb-5b85-a9f2-6944f89285d6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258857 1034 "-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258857 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258857 1111 #uuid "41938051-cf64-5229-9f63-7c6cf400baea" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258858 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258859 13194139533864 true] [17592186258858 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258861 13194139533864 true] [17592186258858 1089 "q-211305" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258858 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258858 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d8d23cf36bc9fb526c0e6d041b72e6b378638f662b67aae63531c66b4a8cd6c5.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2})?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258858 1113 #uuid "3ec07d68-7e85-5648-b443-8e43087e0d24" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258858 1145 "Notice that the graph of $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2})$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis, and then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2}).$ So, we need to find the image of $(-2,-1),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2}),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-2,-1).$\n\n- Then, we  stretch the graph  along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $2$ and get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{x}{2}).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2\\cdot (-2),-1)=(-4,-1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we stretch the result  along the $y$-axis by a scale factor of $3$ and get the graph of $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2}).$ After this transformation, the point $(-4,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-4,3\\cdot (-1))=(-4,-3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2})$ is $(-4,-3).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258859 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258860 13194139533864 true] [17592186258859 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258859 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258859 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258860 13194139533864 true] [17592186258859 1110 #uuid "7886f806-4b4e-5ec7-9ff0-f5abc6c38247" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258860 1034 "-4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258860 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258860 1111 #uuid "c37f8b76-b2a0-54d0-81ef-dc0d585b9280" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258861 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258862 13194139533864 true] [17592186258861 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258861 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258861 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258862 13194139533864 true] [17592186258861 1110 #uuid "18331c92-2d7a-5990-b3ed-933af437497e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258862 1034 "-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258862 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258862 1111 #uuid "25a0f757-7428-5a0c-9bb3-857b616e17c0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258863 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258864 13194139533864 true] [17592186258863 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258866 13194139533864 true] [17592186258863 1089 "q-211461" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258863 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258863 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/57f2d73dd902d3e0bafec168cfe6ecd46dfc7d7a9e05f3cf33d710ca3f6b1e76.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of $y=2f(x)+5?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258863 1113 #uuid "99887be0-5847-549d-86a8-9ddaeb49436c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258863 1145 "Notice that the graph of $y=2f(x)+5$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, and then\n\n- translating it by $5$ units up.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global minimum of $y=f(x)$ to the global minimum of $y=2f(x)+5.$ So, we need to find the image of $(4,-5),$ the global minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=2f(x)+5,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(4,-5).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph along the $y$-axis by a scale factor of $2$ and get the graph of $y=2f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,-5)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,2\\cdot (-5))=(4,-10).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result parallel to the $y$-axis by $5$ units up and get the graph of $y=2f(x)+5.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,-10)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,-10+5)=(4,-5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=2f(x)+5$ is $(4,-5).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258864 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258865 13194139533864 true] [17592186258864 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258864 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258864 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258865 13194139533864 true] [17592186258864 1110 #uuid "a87437f4-6972-5d12-b08e-6f25c6683f33" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258865 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258865 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258865 1111 #uuid "93da801d-0b72-5360-a947-82b1e7f55971" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258866 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258867 13194139533864 true] [17592186258866 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258866 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258866 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258867 13194139533864 true] [17592186258866 1110 #uuid "5ccb4329-6487-5563-a24d-77de4314a055" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258867 1034 "-5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258867 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258867 1111 #uuid "a3290094-9762-5a7e-9778-bbfbd1085202" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258868 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258869 13194139533864 true] [17592186258868 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258871 13194139533864 true] [17592186258868 1089 "q-212645" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258868 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258868 1100 "The midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(2,5)$ and $(6,1)$ is$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258868 1113 #uuid "73a3f870-37f5-5f13-aa37-93b9fd1e9ec7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258868 1145 "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between ${{(2,5)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(6,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2+6}{2},\\frac{5+1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{2},\\frac{6}{2}) \\\\  & =(4,3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258869 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258870 13194139533864 true] [17592186258869 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258869 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258869 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258870 13194139533864 true] [17592186258869 1110 #uuid "6231c067-b769-524b-a4e9-4623983ecb54" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258870 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258870 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258870 1111 #uuid "c781a724-2216-56d6-8a67-d54c013b0f5e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258871 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258872 13194139533864 true] [17592186258871 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258871 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258871 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258872 13194139533864 true] [17592186258871 1110 #uuid "90897e95-2725-5182-9f51-5b49b378ffe5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258872 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258872 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258872 1111 #uuid "30b2a856-b4c8-5929-aca8-89d91652234b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258873 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258874 13194139533864 true] [17592186258873 1089 "q-214154" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258873 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258873 1100 "Consider the following sequence:\n\n$$\nf(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n)\\,,\\,f(1)=3\\,,\\,f(2)=-1,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nThe fourth term of the sequence is{{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258873 1113 #uuid "3e1a7869-1d36-5605-ac92-b7a41430847f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258873 1145 "The fourth term is represented by $f(4).$\n\nTo compute $f(4),$ we first need to compute $f(3).$ So, we substitute $n=1$ into the recursive formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1+2) & =f(1+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =f(2)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =(-1)+3 \\\\ f(3) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to compute $f(4),$ we substitute $n=2$ into the recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2+2) & =f(2+1)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & =f(3)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & =2+(-1) \\\\ f(4) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fourth term of the sequence is $f(4)=1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258874 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258875 13194139533864 true] [17592186258874 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258874 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258874 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258875 13194139533864 true] [17592186258874 1110 #uuid "00420ee4-74b7-5f21-9e8b-16d3139d38b6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258875 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258875 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258875 1111 #uuid "9f0de88a-188e-520b-92ae-cabf356afd49" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258876 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258877 13194139533864 true] [17592186258876 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258879 13194139533864 true] [17592186258876 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258881 13194139533864 true] [17592186258876 1089 "q-214601" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258876 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258876 1100 "Find the intersection of intervals $(-3,\\infty )\\cap (1,2).$ Express the result as an inequality.\n\n{{field-1}}\n$<x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258876 1113 #uuid "3a0bc755-6ef3-5d48-9db2-a94c7ff5cd90" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258876 1145 "We start by sketching the two intervals $(-3,\\infty )$ and $(1,2)$ separately:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/0e13318d542f265c4e6955710971f22c58a730c849da9dfdf7750667b8008269.png)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/245813c1d2c41fbe3984e244cba87f0fc33f608d1cba55f9e336f58e16ed2c70.png)\n\nThe intersection $(-3,\\infty )\\cap (1,2)$ represents the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/238d86c58f32da3c5bed87ad1ed44d3db1ae2daa00d4cafcb82b83cf482f6e9e.png)\n\nThus, $(-3,\\infty )\\cap (1,2)=(1,2).$ This corresponds to the inequality $1<x<2.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258877 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258878 13194139533864 true] [17592186258877 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258877 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258877 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258878 13194139533864 true] [17592186258877 1110 #uuid "e736af70-aec3-5cfd-8854-8fc88ddd4184" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258878 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258878 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258878 1111 #uuid "41443632-64e8-54e5-abe8-4ce4a5109c16" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258879 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258880 13194139533864 true] [17592186258879 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258879 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258879 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258880 13194139533864 true] [17592186258879 1110 #uuid "098ecdb4-b6be-595b-9324-0622d00f1861" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258880 1034 "<" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258880 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258880 1111 #uuid "3754c365-f3e4-5f46-bf4e-91d2484608c0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258881 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258882 13194139533864 true] [17592186258881 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258881 1041 "field-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258881 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258882 13194139533864 true] [17592186258881 1110 #uuid "a64f3a61-4455-52da-95c2-a103ee70065b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258882 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258882 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258882 1111 #uuid "72680562-7418-5d88-86ce-91e592b71425" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258883 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258884 13194139533864 true] [17592186258883 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258886 13194139533864 true] [17592186258883 1089 "q-216064" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258883 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258883 1100 "Find the solution to the  following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}a-\\frac{1}{2}b=1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}a+\\frac{1}{3}b=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$a$: {{a}}\n\n$b$: {{b}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258883 1113 #uuid "50eece3f-06ca-554e-b56c-6eb5ad4a01ae" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258883 1145 "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $4$ and the second equation by $15,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}a-\\frac{1}{2}b=1 & |\\times 4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}a+\\frac{1}{3}b=3 & |\\times 15\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}a-2b=4 \\\\ 3a+5b=45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-3,$ then we will get a $-3a$ term that can cancel with the $3a$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}a-2b=4 & |\\,\\times (-3) \\\\ 3a+5b=45 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-3a+6b=-12 \\\\ 3a+5b=45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $a$-variable, and allows us to solve for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{-3a}+6b & =-12 \\\\ \\cancel{3a}+5b & =45 \\\\ (6b+5b) & =(-12+45) \\\\ 11b & =33 \\\\ b & =\\frac{33}{11} \\\\ b & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $b=3$ into the first equation to solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-2b & =4 \\\\ a-2(3) & =4 \\\\ a-6 & =4 \\\\ a & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $a=10,\\,b=3.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258884 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258885 13194139533864 true] [17592186258884 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258884 1041 "a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258884 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258885 13194139533864 true] [17592186258884 1110 #uuid "ba7df017-81ad-5529-9346-295e76e57b85" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258885 1034 "10" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258885 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258885 1111 #uuid "38d448d8-75d3-5040-a188-af3eb10d7682" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258886 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258887 13194139533864 true] [17592186258886 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258886 1041 "b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258886 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258887 13194139533864 true] [17592186258886 1110 #uuid "25bdc574-1350-5311-8164-7343ef90bab7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258887 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258887 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258887 1111 #uuid "cfaad01c-ff25-516e-9912-c42b36d3564b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258888 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258889 13194139533864 true] [17592186258888 1089 "q-2162" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258888 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258888 1100 "$\\int (2{sec}^{2}x-\\operatorname{sin}x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258888 1113 #uuid "88825c37-179e-5c8c-8712-b57ce55ee253" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258888 1145 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cos}x+C, \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{tan}x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{sec}^{2}x-\\operatorname{sin}x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2{sec}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\operatorname{sin}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tan}x-(-\\operatorname{cos}x)+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tan}x+\\operatorname{cos}x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258889 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258890 13194139533864 true] [17592186258889 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258889 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258889 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258890 13194139533864 true] [17592186258889 1110 #uuid "788ae84d-4e27-5426-9b13-50d3d5155053" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258890 1034 "2\\operatorname{tan}x+\\operatorname{cos}x+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258890 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258890 1111 #uuid "3ad23426-0f62-516a-94ef-682b0fb55343" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258891 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258892 13194139533864 true] [17592186258891 1089 "q-216292" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258891 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258891 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/84f2aca97deffb3f0fea0b2d5296d817461b21a8546597dc13800ff538d56197.png)\n\nThe exact length of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258891 1113 #uuid "6829f758-793c-533d-99bf-9cbdf9331799" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258891 1145 "From the figure, we note that the points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates ${{(2,0)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(1,4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})},$ respectively. \n\nTo find the distance $AB,$ we can use the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1-2)^{2}+(4-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258892 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258893 13194139533864 true] [17592186258892 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258892 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258892 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258893 13194139533864 true] [17592186258892 1110 #uuid "90178292-4c9b-5240-8fa3-14cfae17e988" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258893 1034 "\\sqrt{17}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258893 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258893 1111 #uuid "64a6e34d-6330-5164-84fa-ccfa658f023a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258894 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258895 13194139533864 true] [17592186258894 1089 "q-2165" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258894 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258894 1100 "$\\int (\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-{sec}^{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258894 1113 #uuid "aae416b9-61a3-522e-b8ee-6ceb101dcd00" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258894 1145 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{sec}x+C, \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{tan}x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-{sec}^{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x\\,\\text{d}x-\\int {sec}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}x-\\operatorname{tan}x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258895 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258896 13194139533864 true] [17592186258895 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258895 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258895 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258896 13194139533864 true] [17592186258895 1110 #uuid "820dd40b-79de-5cee-8864-d595556d86d4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258896 1034 "\\operatorname{sec}x-\\operatorname{tan}x+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258896 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258896 1111 #uuid "40d14c46-87a2-5faa-8fc7-d7e73653576e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258897 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258898 13194139533864 true] [17592186258897 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258900 13194139533864 true] [17592186258897 1089 "q-216926" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258897 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258897 1100 "The midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(a,5b)$ and $(5a,3b)$\n is$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258897 1113 #uuid "871d6756-50c4-5595-9d16-fed69c77d662" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258897 1145 "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between ${{(a,5b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(5a,3b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{a+5a}{2},\\frac{5b+3b}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{6a}{2},\\frac{8b}{2}) \\\\  & =(3a,4b)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258898 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258899 13194139533864 true] [17592186258898 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258898 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258898 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258899 13194139533864 true] [17592186258898 1110 #uuid "12fad7d8-d97f-5f15-9b8f-b3a0c8b205cb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258899 1034 "3a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258899 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258899 1111 #uuid "85c1be10-42cc-578a-baee-e53263d673fa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258900 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258901 13194139533864 true] [17592186258900 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258900 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258900 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258901 13194139533864 true] [17592186258900 1110 #uuid "4856ff9d-45a6-5958-9a9c-82409c6e255c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258901 1034 "4b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258901 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258901 1111 #uuid "d6b01965-c634-5e87-a1e1-6e155c9b5fa5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258902 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258903 13194139533864 true] [17592186258902 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258905 13194139533864 true] [17592186258902 1089 "q-216929" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258902 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258902 1100 "The midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(4a,-2b)$ and $(-2a,2b)$ is$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258902 1113 #uuid "83dcd74b-de03-5c4e-9c79-1cd2ae389c56" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258902 1145 "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between ${{(4a,-2b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(-2a,2b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{4a+(-2a)}{2},\\frac{-2b+2b}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2a}{2},\\frac{0}{2}) \\\\  & =(a,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258903 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258904 13194139533864 true] [17592186258903 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258903 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258903 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258904 13194139533864 true] [17592186258