37ada10391" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259171 1034 "\\le " 13194139533864 true] [17592186259171 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259171 1111 #uuid "59225edb-9d59-5410-98bc-ba353bcc0b13" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259172 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259173 13194139533864 true] [17592186259172 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259172 1041 "field-6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259172 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259173 13194139533864 true] [17592186259172 1110 #uuid "7705288d-bbe4-5294-9e5e-b179b2a5f170" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259173 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259173 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259173 1111 #uuid "b3d87d67-d1c5-525e-850e-227f380a68bb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259174 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259175 13194139533864 true] [17592186259174 1089 "q-276668" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259174 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259174 1100 "The root of the polynomial $P(x)=(x-6)^{6}(x-3)^{2}(x-5)^{4}$ with multiplicity $6$ is $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259174 1113 #uuid "36f45728-04e4-5c7b-91d4-5a848ede5245" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259174 1145 "The multiplicity of a root of a polynomial equals the exponent of its corresponding factor in the factorization of the polynomial.\n\nNote that the polynomial $P(x)$ is fully factored.\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $(x-6)$ has an exponent of $6$ in the factored form of $P(x).$ So the root $x=6$ has multiplicity $6.$\n\n- The factor $(x-3)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $P(x).$ So the root $x=3$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- The factor $(x-5)$ has an exponent of $4$ in the factored form of $P(x).$ So the root $x=5$ has multiplicity $4.$\n\nTherefore, the root with multiplicity $6$ is $x=6$ only." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259175 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259176 13194139533864 true] [17592186259175 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259175 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259175 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259176 13194139533864 true] [17592186259175 1110 #uuid "f565c296-6c0e-5fec-9683-9c897f7513a7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259176 1034 "6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259176 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259176 1111 #uuid "f73af4d0-df3a-51ad-8da6-6d36254d1f02" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259177 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259178 13194139533864 true] [17592186259177 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259180 13194139533864 true] [17592186259177 1089 "q-276670" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259177 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259177 1100 "Arranged in **ascending order**, the roots of the polynomial $P(x)=(x-3)^{6}(x-2)^{8}({x}^{2}-9)^{2}$ with multiplicity $8$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259177 1113 #uuid "2666031d-a6e3-5f69-baa5-02dd18e325bb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259177 1145 "The multiplicity of a root of a polynomial equals the exponent of its corresponding factor in the factorization of the polynomial.\n\nFirst, let's factor $P(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =(x-3)^{6}(x-2)^{8}({x}^{2}-9)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{6}(x-2)^{8}[(x-3)(x+3)]^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{6}(x-2)^{8}(x-3)^{2}(x+3)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{8}(x-2)^{8}(x+3)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $(x-3)$ has an exponent of $8$ in the factored form of $P(x).$ So the root $x=3$ has multiplicity $8.$\n\n- The factor $(x-2)$ has an exponent of $8$ in the factored form of $P(x).$ So the root $x=2$ has multiplicity $8.$\n\n- The factor $(x+3)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $P(x).$ So the root $x=-2$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the roots with multiplicity $8$ are $x=2$ and $x=3.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259178 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259179 13194139533864 true] [17592186259178 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259178 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259178 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259179 13194139533864 true] [17592186259178 1110 #uuid "1bf697a4-7ad5-51e2-b575-f0a5dd658271" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259179 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259179 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259179 1111 #uuid "1c380d4f-a7b7-5878-ac1e-b25ae3c330ff" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259180 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259181 13194139533864 true] [17592186259180 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259180 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259180 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259181 13194139533864 true] [17592186259180 1110 #uuid "b2f8515c-c71b-5e07-b5a0-731f6c4f8ac1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259181 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259181 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259181 1111 #uuid "7573163b-0eee-5516-ae67-3c8f41919108" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259182 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259183 13194139533864 true] [17592186259182 1089 "q-276811" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259182 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259182 1100 "The multiplicity of the root $x=1$ in the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+14x-8$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259182 1113 #uuid "5190425b-3b02-57a4-9aaa-22d1bda225c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259182 1145 "Since $x=1$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x-1)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x-1)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-1)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-7$ | $14$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $-6$ | $8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-6$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x-1}={x}^{2}-6x+8,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\np(x)=(x-1)({x}^{2}-6x+8).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x-1)(x-2)(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x-1)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=1$ has multiplicity $1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259183 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259184 13194139533864 true] [17592186259183 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259183 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259183 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259184 13194139533864 true] [17592186259183 1110 #uuid "d854d1ff-1b21-5db4-a961-c44b8431d7e6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259184 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259184 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259184 1111 #uuid "57b90b02-8cc5-5ac9-b5cf-a35264ad1838" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259185 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259186 13194139533864 true] [17592186259185 1089 "q-276814" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259185 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259185 1100 "The multiplicity of the root $x=1$ in the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-11x+6$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259185 1113 #uuid "d66368db-ce50-599d-b4ed-96dcd1390201" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259185 1145 "Since $x=1$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x-1)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x-1)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-1)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $4$ | $-11$ | $6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $5$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $5$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x-1}={x}^{2}+5x-6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\np(x)=(x-1)({x}^{2}+5x-6).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x-1)(x-1)(x+6) \\\\  & =(x+6)(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x-1)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=1$ has multiplicity $2.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259186 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259187 13194139533864 true] [17592186259186 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259186 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259186 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259187 13194139533864 true] [17592186259186 1110 #uuid "8f987026-e95a-5fcd-a245-183915aeb393" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259187 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259187 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259187 1111 #uuid "8bf469ac-a510-5ce0-9a82-a32665a0ccc2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259188 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259189 13194139533864 true] [17592186259188 1089 "q-276819" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259188 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259188 1100 "The multiplicity of the root $x=6$ in the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}-18{x}^{2}+108x-216$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259188 1113 #uuid "7faee085-bb90-5473-a347-841343c9777c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259188 1145 "Since $x=6$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x-6)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x-6)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-6)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-6)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $6$ | $1$ | $-18$ | $108$ | $-216$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $6$ | $-72$ | $216$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-12$ | $36$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x-6}={x}^{2}-12x+36,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\np(x)=(x-6)({x}^{2}-12x+36).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x-6)(x-6)(x-6) \\\\  & =(x-6)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x-6)$ has an exponent of $3$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=6$ has multiplicity $3.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259189 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259190 13194139533864 true] [17592186259189 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259189 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259189 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259190 13194139533864 true] [17592186259189 1110 #uuid "e4bdfdb3-237c-546c-b7df-e71df4586637" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259190 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259190 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259190 1111 #uuid "5c2ab28a-cae8-5b86-a7ed-0a9f1c8ae204" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259191 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259192 13194139533864 true] [17592186259191 1089 "q-276827" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259191 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259191 1100 "The multiplicity of the root $x=-5$ in the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-15x-100$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259191 1113 #uuid "11643b7e-f172-5ec5-8c9a-009f4edf0ce0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259191 1145 "Since $x=-5$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x+5)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x+5)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x+5)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+5)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-5$ | $1$ | $6$ | $-15$ | $-100$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-5$ | $-5$ | $100$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $-20$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x+5}={x}^{2}+x-20,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\np(x)=(x+5)({x}^{2}+x-20).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x+5)(x-4)(x+5) \\\\  & =(x-4)(x+5)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x+5)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-5$ has multiplicity $2.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259192 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259193 13194139533864 true] [17592186259192 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259192 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259192 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259193 13194139533864 true] [17592186259192 1110 #uuid "c7a0e3d2-a0da-504b-bd42-b8dc646ed762" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259193 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259193 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259193 1111 #uuid "80880acc-6a2f-568b-a091-3b3f5ff8c0bf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259194 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259195 13194139533864 true] [17592186259194 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259197 13194139533864 true] [17592186259194 1089 "q-278249" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259194 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259194 1100 "In **ascending order**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259194 1113 #uuid "af193db4-aced-5259-96b5-34c474664303" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259194 1145 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}(x-3)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(x-3)=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=0$ and $x=3.$ \n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=0$ and $x=3.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259195 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259196 13194139533864 true] [17592186259195 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259195 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259195 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259196 13194139533864 true] [17592186259195 1110 #uuid "ec5da6d3-c4f9-5015-b5e8-9cbbee98e21c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259196 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259196 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259196 1111 #uuid "2d5d60ca-ce6e-5818-80f6-5e1f00a4ea32" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259197 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259198 13194139533864 true] [17592186259197 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259197 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259197 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259198 13194139533864 true] [17592186259197 1110 #uuid "e7d70ad1-2f32-53da-941b-6509c9c8c13c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259198 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259198 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259198 1111 #uuid "983e13cd-944c-5cec-a2ca-63d865a191e0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259199 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259200 13194139533864 true] [17592186259199 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259202 13194139533864 true] [17592186259199 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259204 13194139533864 true] [17592186259199 1089 "q-278308" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259199 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259199 1100 "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}-6x+8>0?$\n\n$x\\in (-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cup ($\n{{field-2}}\n$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259199 1113 #uuid "afea9ca1-a406-5faa-9e2a-dda897028d2b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259199 1145 "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x+8 & >0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-4) & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x-2)(x-4)=0.$ The roots are $x=2$ and $x=4,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,2),$ $(2,4),$ and $(4,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $(2,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x-2)(x-4)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x-2)(x-4)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x-2)(x-4)$ is positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,2)$ and $(4,\\infty ).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}-6x+8>0$ for all \n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (4,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259200 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259201 13194139533864 true] [17592186259200 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259200 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259200 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259201 13194139533864 true] [17592186259200 1110 #uuid "0f43ee4a-f94f-5cde-ad17-a3f76d1effde" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259201 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259201 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259201 1111 #uuid "b147d074-50c4-579f-b791-a85cf421ccbe" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259202 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259203 13194139533864 true] [17592186259202 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259202 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259202 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259203 13194139533864 true] [17592186259202 1110 #uuid "d68d6d82-a6c8-5f79-977b-16b32614d59a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259203 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259203 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259203 1111 #uuid "e9142388-b025-5efb-95cb-753d76095add" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259204 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259205 13194139533864 true] [17592186259204 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259204 1041 "field-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259204 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259205 13194139533864 true] [17592186259204 1110 #uuid "a9c70dbb-3a1d-59ff-9170-ea4bcf75b936" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259205 1034 "\\infty " 13194139533864 true] [17592186259205 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259205 1111 #uuid "7e7529f6-6980-56a6-af05-5ca35dd024d3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259206 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259207 13194139533864 true] [1759218625