rue] [17592186259252 1111 #uuid "36cdcc63-54f6-5ea7-9963-7b4a2ef25625" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259253 1034 "-" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259253 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259253 1111 #uuid "aa2bf3e3-5f95-5505-858e-1164eaee5d70" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259254 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259255 13194139533864 true] [17592186259254 1089 "q-278536" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259254 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259254 1100 "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259254 1113 #uuid "0df3445d-c4fe-56f7-8f72-bac44d3d2fd6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259254 1145 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx-2=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=2.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259255 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259256 13194139533864 true] [17592186259255 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259255 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259255 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259256 13194139533864 true] [17592186259255 1110 #uuid "06303f5e-9a77-5a76-93dc-2cd6a3f05bfe" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259256 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259256 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259256 1111 #uuid "7cf57b73-5ad2-5eab-bf6d-fb4d095f013d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259257 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259258 13194139533864 true] [17592186259257 1089 "q-278543" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259257 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259257 1100 "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-6}{{x}^{2}+x-2}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259257 1113 #uuid "8e85e93c-3cfa-5608-97e9-59cee6be71eb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259257 1145 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-6}{{x}^{2}+x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)(x+2)}{(x-1)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)\\cancel{(x+2)}}{(x-1)\\cancel{(x+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-3}{x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx-1=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259258 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259259 13194139533864 true] [17592186259258 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259258 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259258 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259259 13194139533864 true] [17592186259258 1110 #uuid "e3d0c0c1-1bcd-51f6-8e4a-70dcf5913bcc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259259 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259259 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259259 1111 #uuid "f3b58eef-9b07-5e62-8dfd-06cdd9792dd0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259260 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259261 13194139533864 true] [17592186259260 1089 "q-278551" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259260 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259260 1100 "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{4}{2{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-1}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259260 1113 #uuid "5f2dc1ae-4892-50b9-981f-808f974086b6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259260 1145 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4}{2{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2{x}^{2}(x-1)+(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)(x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n(2{x}^{2}+1)(x-1)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x-1=0$ gives the solution $x=1.$\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}+1=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptote is $x=1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259261 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259262 13194139533864 true] [17592186259261 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259261 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259261 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259262 13194139533864 true] [17592186259261 1110 #uuid "2bbf306f-0153-5b3f-845e-2e452538f0cf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259262 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259262 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259262 1111 #uuid "e6bca57a-bbd7-5135-b4d1-5394e2880a4c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259263 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259264 13194139533864 true] [17592186259263 1089 "q-280142" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259263 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259263 1100 "Evaluate the **exact value** of the following series:\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}4{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259263 1113 #uuid "03feef15-6821-50d8-957a-59df60c1ee42" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259263 1145 "Since the given series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=4,$ $r=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $N=5.$ If we substitute these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}4{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & =4\\cdot \\frac{(1-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{5})}{1-\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{243}{1024})}{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\frac{781}{1024}}{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{3124}{1024} \\\\  & =\\frac{781}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259264 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259265 13194139533864 true] [17592186259264 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259264 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259264 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259265 13194139533864 true] [17592186259264 1110 #uuid "e8086ada-f6fe-58cb-9114-cce7f17fbdd9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259265 1034 "\\frac{781}{64}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259265 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259265 1111 #uuid "85ebfb2c-99c6-5637-b42c-761be5242059" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259266 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259267 13194139533864 true] [17592186259266 1089 "q-280149" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259266 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259266 1100 "Evaluate the following geometric series:\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}\\frac{3}{2}(4)^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259266 1113 #uuid "d76c89d6-e774-5178-8f95-00904170710b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259266 1145 "Since the given series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=4$ and $N=5.$ We need to figure out the value of ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{3}{2}(4)^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore, we can calculate the first term ${a}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{2}(4)^{1} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=6,r=4$ and $N=5$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}\\frac{3}{2}(4)^{n} & =6\\cdot \\frac{(1-{4}^{5})}{1-4} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{(-1023)}{(-3)} \\\\  & =6(341) \\\\  & =2046\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259267 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259268 13194139533864 true] [17592186259267 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259267 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259267 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259268 13194139533864 true] [17592186259267 1110 #uuid "e210c240-5654-5bf6-9793-aef1a6e8b3c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259268 1034 "2046" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259268 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259268 1111 #uuid "791e76a9-3d5f-57c1-b10b-b6d07ab33163" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259269 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259270 13194139533864 true] [17592186259269 1089 "q-280244" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259269 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259269 1100 "Evaluate the **exact value** of the following geometric series:\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{21}{{7}^{n}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259269 1113 #uuid "38d95bd5-b1e1-544e-9f92-f7bc640d7469" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259269 1145 "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{21}{{7}^{n}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}21\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{1}{7}$ and $N=4.$ We need to figure out ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=21\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore the first term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=21\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{1}=3.\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=3,r=\\frac{1}{7}$ and $N=4$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}21\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n} & =3\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{4}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{7})} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{1}{2401})}{\\frac{6}{7}} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{7}{6}\\cdot \\frac{2400}{2401} \\\\  & =\\frac{1200}{343}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259270 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259271 13194139533864 true] [17592186259270 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259270 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259270 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259271 13194139533864 true] [17592186259270 1110 #uuid "ba568ec5-107a-50c2-adc0-af80b846d5b8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259271 1034 "\\frac{1200}{343}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259271 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259271 1111 #uuid "f46d4583-c3dc-5b07-9d0f-232e78e4619d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259272 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259273 13194139533864 true] [17592186259272 1089 "q-280253" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259272 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259272 1100 "Evaluate the **exact value** of the following geometric series:\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}54\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n-1}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259272 1113 #uuid "503b9785-4d55-5d08-a435-b657b276f67e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259272 1145 "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}54\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n-1}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n-1}$, it is a geometric series starting at $n=1$, with the exponent matching the standard form for partial sums.\n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{1}{6}$, $N=4$, and the first term ${a}_{1}=54.$\n\nSubstituting into the formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}54\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n-1} & =54\\cdot \\frac{1-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{4}}{1-\\frac{1}{6}} \\\\  & =54\\cdot \\frac{1295}{1296}\\cdot \\frac{6}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{259}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259273 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259274 13194139533864 true] [17592186259273 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259273 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259273 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259274 13194139533864 true] [17592186259273 1110 #uuid "b8031aa4-9ef9-5d3c-b2c0-4c90c083fca1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259274 1034 "\\frac{259}{4}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259274 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259274 1111 #uuid "e8824b1b-d8cd-591a-ab89-55dc9fbbc2c8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259275 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259276 13194139533864 true] [17592186259275 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259278 13194139533864 true] [17592186259275 1089 "q-281598" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259275 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259275 1100 "Given that $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$, find a unit vector that is parallel to $v.$\n\n|  |  | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{8}}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $u=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |  |" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259275 1113 #uuid "8bd03f4e-4cf4-5f9e-99a0-8445e7cf14c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259275 1145 "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{|\\,v\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,v\\,| & =\\sqrt{{v}_{x}^{2}+{v}_{y}^{2}+{v}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+0+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $v$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{|\\,v\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{8}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{8}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259276 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259277 13194139533864 true] [17592186259276 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259276 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259276 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259277 13194139533864 true] [17592186259276 1110 #uuid "c38b5a73-a839-59b5-814e-abb5f625b64d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259277 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259277 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259277 1111 #uuid "09d5a123-4fb6-521a-bcac-bb274ffdc3d8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259278 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259279 13194139533864 true] [17592186259278 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259278 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259278 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259279 13194139533864 true] [17592186259278 1110 #uuid "f6d5b760-60d5-5012-bfa5-8667189279b1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259279 1034 "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{8}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259279 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259279 1111 #uuid "d9b0dcd3-9de6-5cc0-ba38-d69ec3de0ab2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259280 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259281 13194139533864 true] [17592186259280 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259283 13194139533864 true] [17592186259280 1089 "q-281604" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259280 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259280 1100 "Given that $w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$, find a unit vector that is parallel to $w.$\n\n|  |  | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{65}}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $u=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |  |" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259280 1113 #uuid "74cf4bef-d896-5bbd-b1b1-0cf31a04a44f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259280 1145 "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{w}{|\\,w\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,w\\,| & =\\sqrt{{w}_{x}^{2}+{w}_{y}^{2}+{w}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+25+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{65}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $w$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{w}{|\\,w\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{65}}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{65}}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{65}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{65}} \\\\ \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{65}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259281 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259282 13194139533864 true] [17592186259281 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259281 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259281 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259282 13194139533864 true] [17592186259281 1110 #uuid "6e02b4ab-23c6-5efe-a930-f01f07fd748f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259282 1034 "-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{65}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259282 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259282 1111 #uuid "cd6a2755-76c0-560b-8aec-2a7c46d85ca9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259283 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259284 13194139533864 true] [17592186259283 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259283 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259283 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259284 13194139533864 true] [17592186259283 1110 #uuid "95498013-eef1-5685-805c-80ad36dd854e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259284 1034 "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{65}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259284 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259284 1111 #uuid "eada3912-a2e9-5c0b-b04e-ba218d303a43" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259285 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259286 13194139533864 true] [17592186259285 1089 "q-281692" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259285 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259285 1100 "Let $R(0,6,-2)$ and $S(-5,1,1).$ Find $|\\overset{\\to }{SR}|.$\n\n$|\\overset{\\to }{SR}|=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259285 1113 #uuid "1a3261c4-18b3-5911-b162-0718243641dd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259285 1145 "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OR} & =r=6j-2k, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{OS} & =s=-5i+j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{SR}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{SR} & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}-\\overset{\\to }{OS} \\\\  & =r-s \\\\  & =(6j-2k)-(-5i+j+k) \\\\  & =5i+5j-3k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{SR}| & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{5}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+25+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{59}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259286 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259287 13194139533864 true] [17592186259286 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259286 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259286 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259287 13194139533864 true] [17592186259286 1110 #uuid "a5a1c15f-128f-5942-80b0-932025fc61d5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259287 1034 "\\sqrt{59}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259287 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259287 1111 #uuid "5225462e-ca00-5ed8-a0f7-7975997fdf05" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259288 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259289 13194139533864 true] [17592186259288 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259291 13194139533864 true] [17592186259288 1089 "q-28178" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259288 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259288 1100 "Solve the equation $\\,-6x=-{x}^{2}-13.$\n\nNegative imaginary: {{negative-imaginary}}\n\nPositive imaginary: {{positive-imaginary}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259288 1113 #uuid "8d48e2ba-01ce-5260-8b5c-13f37e1e6682" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259288 1145 "First, we write the equation in standard form by bringing all of the terms to the left-hand side. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-6x+13=0.\n$$\n\nWe can find the roots of a quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the coefficients are $a=1,\\,b=-6,$ and $c=13.$ Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-6)\\pm \\sqrt{(-6)^{2}-4(1)(13)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pm \\sqrt{36-52}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pm \\sqrt{-16}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pm 4\\,\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =3\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=3+2\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=3-2\\,\\text{i}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259289 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259290 13194139533864 true] [17592186259289 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259289 1041 "negative-imaginary" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259289 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259290 13194139533864 true] [17592186259289 1110 #uuid "a1e7b2d0-75e8-57df-bb5c-be7d6ceb344f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259290 1034 "3-2\\text{i}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259290 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259290 1111 #uuid "80670920-6ea4-5513-9ff8-0666a5ab5a0e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259291 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259292 13194139533864 true] [17592186259291 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259291 1041 "positive-imaginary" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259291 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259292 13194139533864 true] [17592186259291 1110 #uuid "a3f7c406-dba3-5e9d-b52e-eb2f71f3a818" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259292 1034 "3+2\\text{i}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259292 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259292 1111 #uuid "ce5d334d-56cb-50ab-9973-123e41f4dbd6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259293 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259294 13194139533864 true] [17592186259293 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259296 13194139533864 true] [17592186259293 1089 "q-28183" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259293 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259293 1100 "Solve the equation $\\,{x}^{2}+2x=-50.$\n\nNegative imaginary: {{negative-imaginary}}\n\nPositive imaginary: {{positive-imaginary}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259293 1113 #uuid "cce1890b-f4b6-5a39-8f00-affe971dd43b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259293 1145 "First, we write the equation in standard form by bringing all of the terms to the left-hand side. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2x+50=0.\n$$\n\nWe can find the roots of a quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the coefficients are $a=1,\\,b=2,$ and $c=50.$ Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{{2}^{2}-4(1)(50)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{4-200}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{-196}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm 14\\,\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-1\\pm 7\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=-1+7\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=-1-7\\,\\text{i}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259294 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259295 13194139533864 true] [17592186259294 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259294 1041 "negative-imaginary" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259294 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259295 13194139533864 true] [17592186259294 1110 #uuid "9bb0fe08-1cd4-5f26-b858-4fed365eb78e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259295 1034 "-1-7\\text{i}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259295 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259295 1111 #uuid "855e59cf-f2dc-54f0-a4c9-fda962fc6e46" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259296 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259297 13194139533864 true] [17592186259296 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259296 1041 "positive-imaginary" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259296 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259297 13194139533864 true] [17592186259296 1110 #uuid "29b7bbcf-7bd1-562a-a3c0-9d7dcd78211e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259297 1034 "-1+7\\text{i}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259297 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259297 1111 #uuid "b6d028d3-e3cb-5ca9-b1fa-bd692712e892" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259298 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259299 13194139533864 true] [17592186259298 1089 "q-28551" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259298 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259298 1100 "Given $y=7{x}^{2}-1+\\frac{1}{2x}$, find $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259298 1113 #uuid "f5dd3c3b-72ac-507f-a820-ce546a04182e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259298 1145 "First, notice that we can rewrite the function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(x) & =7{x}^{2}-1+\\frac{1}{2x} \\\\  & =7{x}^{2}-1+\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{x}^{2}-1+\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{2}) \\\\  & =7(2)x-0+\\frac{(-1){x}^{-2}}{2} \\\\  & =14x-\\frac{{x}^{-2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(14x-\\frac{{x}^{-2}}{2}) \\\\  & =14-\\frac{(-2){x}^{-3}}{2} \\\\  & =14+{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(14+{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =0+(-3){x}^{-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259299 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259300 13194139533864 true] [17592186259299 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259299 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259299 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259300 13194139533864 true] [17592186259299 1110 #uuid "66cffc73-4a43-5edd-b66b-ade39f8bc170" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259300 1034 "-\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259300 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259300 1111 #uuid "9aa92c0b-20de-5413-9cd6-e2eed743df17" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259301 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259302 13194139533864 true] [17592186259301 1089 "q-288778" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259301 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259301 1100 "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n7{x}^{2}-9x+2=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the sum of its solutions.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259301 1113 #uuid "0273f815-5b6c-5f01-94fb-31a1e3f74724" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259301 1145 "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=7,\\,b=-9,\\,c=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{b}{a} & =-\\frac{(-9)}{7}=\\frac{9}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259302 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259303 13194139533864 true] [17592186259302 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259302 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259302 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259303 13194139533864 true] [17592186259302 1110 #uuid "8791452b-9f5e-5c66-a6eb-fc62a1552d1c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259303 1034 "\\frac{9}{7}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259303 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259303 1111 #uuid "5c5e0cbf-ada9-577c-acdb-c94f01edda6b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259304 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259305 13194139533864 true] [17592186259304 1089 "q-288810" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259304 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259304 1100 "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+172x-15\\,300=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the sum of its solutions.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259304 1113 #uuid "e0b72192-2fad-5822-a291-598e7afc9350" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259304 1145 "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=1,\\,b=172,\\,c=-15\\,300.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{b}{a} & =-\\frac{172}{1}=-172.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259305 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259306 13194139533864 true] [17592186259305 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259305 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259305 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259306 13194139533864 true] [17592186259305 1110 #uuid "3efb9e91-8a8a-5d4a-b7c3-c9aea8cd6ba0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259306 1034 "-172" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259306 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259306 1111 #uuid "22a949e2-f067-5d66-ab20-dc59a15d6625" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259307 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259308 13194139533864 true] [17592186259307 1089 "q-288813" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259307 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259307 1100 "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+11x-20=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the product of its solutions.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259307 1113 #uuid "aaca8727-279c-5e4c-b712-d066e6241ce5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259307 1145 "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=4,\\,b=11,\\,c=-20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the product of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{a} & =\\frac{(-20)}{4}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259308 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259309 13194139533864 true] [17592186259308 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259308 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259308 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259309 13194139533864 true] [17592186259308 1110 #uuid "263e2c7c-5f78-50d0-9f40-04493a057cdb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259309 1034 "-5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259309 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259309 1111 #uuid "f2044120-320b-5665-8071-29b32ad9ca08" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259310 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259311 13194139533864 true] [17592186259310 1089 "q-288814" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259310 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259310 1100 "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n7{x}^{2}-5x-14=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the product of its solutions.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259310 1113 #uuid "939510b6-5bc7-5d08-b915-1376d3819fba" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259310 1145 "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=7,\\,b=-5,\\,c=-14.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the product of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{a} & =\\frac{(-14)}{7}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259311 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259312 13194139533864 true] [17592186259311 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259311 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259311 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259312 13194139533864 true] [17592186259311 1110 #uuid "60ce2755-627d-5f6b-adb2-bb7f7f424e14" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259312 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259312 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259312 1111 #uuid "f16923e8-81d9-586a-aba2-f5c13f77c6c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259313 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259314 13194139533864 true] [17592186259313 1089 "q-288826" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259313 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259313 1100 "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+68x-1\\,041=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the product of its solutions.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259313 1113 #uuid "ccac1a80-68f8-53f5-b849-c8a540e70082" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259313 1145 "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=1,\\,b=68,\\,c=-1\\,041.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the product of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{a} & =\\frac{(-1\\,041)}{1}=-1\\,041.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259314 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259315 13194139533864 true] [17592186259314 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259314 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259314 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259315 13194139533864 true] [17592186259314 1110 #uuid "5adcd2ec-d9e3-5e8d-8f85-09672d3a2271" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259315 1034 "-1\\,041" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259315 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259315 1111 #uuid "367a65ee-1249-516a-9dfb-c4291f663555" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259316 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259317 13194139533864 true] [17592186259316 1089 "q-288849" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259316 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259316 1100 "The quadratic coefficient of a particular quadratic equation equals $1.$ The sum of the solutions equals $-11,$ and the product of the solutions equals $-28.$ Find the equation.\n\nThe equation is\n{{field-1}}\n$=0.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259316 1113 #uuid "ff802175-3c27-5ff7-bdc0-5910dfdc76cf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259316 1145 "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's use the given information to determine the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ in this case:\n\n- We're told that the quadratic coefficient equals $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\na=1.\n$$\n\n- We're told that the sum of the roots equals $-11.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-11 & =-\\frac{b}{a} \\\\ -11 & =-\\frac{b}{1} \\\\ 11 & =b \\\\ b & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- We're told that the product of the roots equals $-28.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-28 & =\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ -28 & =\\frac{c}{1} \\\\ c & =-28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation has coefficients\n\n$$\na=1,\\,b=11,\\,c=-28\n$$\n\nand the equation is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+11x-28=0.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259317 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259318 13194139533864 true] [17592186259317 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259317 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259317 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259318 13194139533864 true] [17592186259317 1110 #uuid "3ffe486b-d471-5767-99f6-442998821574" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259318 1034 "{x}^{2}+11x-28" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259318 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259318 1111 #uuid "15dca4bb-ede0-5492-bd4c-7fc87173d3c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259319 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259320 13194139533864 true] [17592186259319 1089 "q-288850" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259319 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259319 1100 "The quadratic coefficient of a particular quadratic equation equals $1.$ The sum of the solutions equals $5,$ and the product of the solutions equals $-50.$ Find the equation.\n\nThe equation is\n{{field-1}}\n$=0.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259319 1113 #uuid "8e950950-a915-54b2-8881-c84ae2ce175e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259319 1145 "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's use the given information to determine the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ in this case:\n\n- We're told that the quadratic coefficient equals $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\na=1.\n$$\n\n- We're told that the sum of the roots equals $5.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =-\\frac{b}{a} \\\\ 5 & =-\\frac{b}{1} \\\\ -5 & =b \\\\ b & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- We're told that the product of the roots equals $-50.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-50 & =\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ -50 & =\\frac{c}{1} \\\\ c & =-50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation has coefficients\n\n$$\na=1,\\,b=-5,\\,c=-50\n$$\n\nand the equation is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-5x-50=0.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259320 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259321 13194139533864 true] [17592186259320 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259320 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259320 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259321 13194139533864 true] [17592186259320 1110 #uuid "947f4549-fff6-56ca-b591-9ccc9b602b66" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259321 1034 "{x}^{2}-5x-50" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259321 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259321 1111 #uuid "8e1bdaf3-4a29-5c1b-b2bd-a11780cc53a4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259322 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259323 13194139533864 true] [17592186259322 1089 "q-28910" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259322 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259322 1100 "Given the inequalities $-3\\le x\\le 3,$ $\\,x<-1,$ and $\\,x>\\frac{1}{2}$, What sets, expressed in interval notation, represents their common set of values?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259322 1113 #uuid "28246b2b-cdc4-5900-874e-a9bf17a69498" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259322 1145 "To find the common set of values, we graph the inequalities on a number line and find where they overlap:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/6b3676a8f1a489200f861b7e5496b5ae98312a424b3341e120cd0200fdbbdb92.png)\n\nFrom the graph above, we determine that there are no common points." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259323 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259324 13194139533864 true] [17592186259323 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259323 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259323 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259324 13194139533864 true] [17592186259323 1110 #uuid "5cee0f86-5317-5631-a0c3-945ea1ae0623" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259324 1034 "\\emptyset" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259324 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259324 1111 #uuid "92079d22-6708-52f2-9272-039ace1db573" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259325 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259326 13194139533864 true] [17592186259325 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259328 13194139533864 true] [17592186259325 1089 "q-29285" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259325 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259325 1100 "What is the range of the curve $y={2}^{x}-1?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259325 1113 #uuid "70862643-7250-5cd2-b843-a31a73133e34" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259325 1145 "The range of ${2}^{x}$ is\n\n$$\n{2}^{x}>0.\n$$\n\nsubtracting $1,$ in the above inequality  we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x} & >0 \\\\ {2}^{x}-1 & >-1 \\\\ y & >-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $y\\in (-1,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $f(x)={2}^{x}-1$ is shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/50dd789f987d02d264da339ab9cceb210b973a9b6d1af3c14c1d6916980a2de9.png)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259326 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259327 13194139533864 true] [17592186259326 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259326 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259326 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259327 13194139533864 true] [17592186259326 1110 #uuid "463dfe74-402b-5bd5-a5c2-aafda9046df6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259327 1034 "-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259327 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259327 1111 #uuid "2a48e722-1c13-5861-8d36-8d50ad117469" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259328 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259329 13194139533864 true] [17592186259328 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259328 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259328 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259329 13194139533864 true] [17592186259328 1110 #uuid "0ab8c69a-879f-5cba-af78-5035ae35775c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259329 1034 "\\infty" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259329 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259329 1111 #uuid "e68ccfbe-1e0d-5f6c-8d90-5c105f6c816b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259330 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259331 13194139533864 true] [17592186259330 1089 "q-29440" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259330 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259330 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/51a05d6b2fe04eb825b8048281b6d1e94b65df9f6f80c6e8965f9e414c5b360c.png)\n\nFind the inequality that describes the shaded region shown above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259330 1113 #uuid "3410dcfc-f781-556b-b8a2-eb3039fbcda3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259330 1145 "First, we find the equation of the corresponding line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$\n\n- The line intersects the $y$-axis at $(0,1)$, so the $y$-intercept is $b=1.$\n\n- The line passes through the points $(0,1)$ and $(1,-1)$, so its slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{-1-1}{1-0}=\\frac{-2}{1}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is $y=-2x+1.$\n\nFinally, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use. Since the line is dashed, we will use a strict inequality. Since the shaded region is *below* the line, the inequality refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* those on the line.\n\nSo, the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny<-2x+1.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259331 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259332 13194139533864 true] [17592186259331 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259331 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259331 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259332 13194139533864 true] [17592186259331 1110 #uuid "64ea8576-917c-5ae9-bfb2-b3b51b23432c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259332 1034 "y<-2x+1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259332 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259332 1111 #uuid "f4ef1f9e-eecd-5ab7-85a9-5d79005f8346" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259333 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259334 13194139533864 true] [17592186259333 1089 "q-29633" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259333 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259333 1100 "What is the antiderivative of $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259333 1113 #uuid "77f6dc16-41fa-5de8-9654-94f8eff9862e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259333 1145 "Note that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}={x}^{-4}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-4$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-4+1}}{-4+1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{-3}}{3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259334 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259335 13194139533864 true] [17592186259334 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259334 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259334 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259335 13194139533864 true] [17592186259334 1110 #uuid "a604736b-4a8c-5606-83c8-0e38d57b830b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259335 1034 "-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{3}}+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259335 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259335 1111 #uuid "050fa364-a0e5-5fed-b6f7-b0d51b22baa0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259336 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259337 13194139533864 true] [17592186259336 1089 "q-29662" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259336 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259336 1100 "Calculate the integral of $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259336 1113 #uuid "1ae1b109-e109-55e7-aa92-fc7bfae9c3eb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259336 1145 "Note that $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{5/2}}={x}^{-5/2}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}} & =\\int {x}^{-5/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-5/2+1}}{-\\frac{5}{2}+1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{-3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{3/2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259337 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259338 13194139533864 true] [17592186259337 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259337 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259337 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259338 13194139533864 true] [17592186259337 1110 #uuid "b67335b5-2406-50cc-9c2c-a22200a36e4a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259338 1034 "-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{3/2}}+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259338 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259338 1111 #uuid "98a607ae-be78-570b-969b-c27e98d60098" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259339 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259340 13194139533864 true] [17592186259339 1089 "q-29745" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259339 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259339 1100 "${\\int }_{-10}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259339 1113 #uuid "ea49163f-eaaf-5d9b-a819-45e18fcd5c81" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259339 1145 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-10}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1+1}}{1+1}{|}_{-10}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-10}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(-10)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2-50 \\\\  & =-48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259340 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259341 13194139533864 true] [17592186259340 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259340 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259340 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259341 13194139533864 true] [17592186259340 1110 #uuid "3e6f63af-0dee-5a8c-9f14-317d1122f8ad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259341 1034 "-48" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259341 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259341 1111 #uuid "225a1fb0-b238-58ad-a91a-d398c30787f7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259342 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259343 13194139533864 true] [17592186259342 1089 "q-29750" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259342 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259342 1100 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259342 1113 #uuid "8ba992bf-a932-50cb-b345-279ad6d9b588" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259342 1145 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/2+1}}{-\\frac{1}{2}+1}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2{x}^{1/2}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2({4}^{1/2}-{1}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =2(\\sqrt{4}-1) \\\\  & =2(2-1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259343 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259344 13194139533864 true] [17592186259343 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259343 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259343 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259344 13194139533864 true] [17592186259343 1110 #uuid "ae8feea0-bd5b-5959-9749-6a3add6e8c28" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259344 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259344 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259344 1111 #uuid "761e4b32-9ff3-5679-b5ba-dc1e463c6577" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259345 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259346 13194139533864 true] [17592186259345 1089 "q-29825" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259345 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259345 1100 "Find the antiderivative of $3{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259345 1113 #uuid "1f2d5bd7-e7c7-50ca-8707-a9c29a9151da" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259345 1145 "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int 2{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int {x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-2(\\frac{{x}^{-2}}{-2})+C \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+{x}^{-2}+C \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259346 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259347 13194139533864 true] [17592186259346 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259346 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259346 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259347 13194139533864 true] [17592186259346 1110 #uuid "67c13983-cf41-5067-8f92-042471b1b008" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259347 1034 "{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259347 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259347 1111 #uuid "af25d7e7-ed8c-5fc7-989e-6efc6599d586" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259348 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259349 13194139533864 true] [17592186259348 1089 "q-29948" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259348 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259348 1100 "Give an expression equivalent to $\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x}{3{y}^{2}}÷\\frac{x+3}{21{y}^{3}}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259348 1113 #uuid "40d4fd15-9ae0-5ded-bd7b-cba46b16d3d0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259348 1145 "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x}{3{y}^{2}}÷\\frac{x+3}{21{y}^{3}},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x}{3{y}^{2}}÷\\frac{x+3}{21{y}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}+3x}{3{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{21{y}^{3}}{x+3} & = \\\\ \\frac{x(x+3)}{3{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{7\\cdot 3{y}^{2}\\cdot y}{x+3} & = \\\\ \\frac{x\\cancel{(x+3)}}{\\cancel{3{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{7\\cdot \\cancel{3{y}^{2}}\\cdot y}{\\cancel{x+3}} & = \\\\ x\\cdot 7y & = \\\\ 7xy\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259349 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259350 13194139533864 true] [17592186259349 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259349 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259349 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259350 13194139533864 true] [17592186259349 1110 #uuid "42d46319-031d-5a47-93e8-39c58ae92d4e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259350 1034 "7xy" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259350 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259350 1111 #uuid "f3efad38-5359-51ba-b86a-d83ee0284d55" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259351 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259352 13194139533864 true] [17592186259351 1089 "q-29966" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259351 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259351 1100 "If $\\frac{24{a}^{3}{b}^{2}}{25{c}^{3}}\\cdot X=\\frac{12a{b}^{3}}{15c},$ then what is $X?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259351 1113 #uuid "1c5939ab-138a-5087-be80-835026f210bd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259351 1145 "Since $\\frac{24{a}^{3}{b}^{2}}{25{c}^{3}}\\cdot X=\\frac{12a{b}^{3}}{15c},$ we have\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{12a{b}^{3}}{15c}÷\\frac{24{a}^{3}{b}^{2}}{25{c}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nTo compute this quotient, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12a{b}^{3}}{15c}÷\\frac{24{a}^{3}{b}^{2}}{25{c}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{12a{b}^{3}}{15c}\\cdot \\frac{25{c}^{3}}{24{a}^{3}{b}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{12\\cdot a{b}^{2}\\cdot b}{3\\cdot 5c}\\cdot \\frac{5\\cdot 5c\\cdot {c}^{2}}{12\\cdot 2{a}^{2}\\cdot a{b}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{12}\\cdot \\cancel{a{b}^{2}}\\cdot b}{3\\cdot \\cancel{5c}}\\cdot \\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{5c}\\cdot {c}^{2}}{\\cancel{12}\\cdot 2{a}^{2}\\cdot \\cancel{a{b}^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{b}{3}\\cdot \\frac{5{c}^{2}}{2{a}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5b{c}^{2}}{6{a}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259352 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259353 13194139533864 true] [17592186259352 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259352 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259352 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259353 13194139533864 true] [17592186259352 1110 #uuid "4e8cab47-62d8-5b89-a9c0-22b792482cd4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259353 1034 "\\frac{5b{c}^{2}}{6{a}^{2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259353 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259353 1111 #uuid "1833f0b2-441a-57ec-ae57-6f9338dd96bf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259354 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259355 13194139533864 true] [17592186259354 1089 "q-300756" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259354 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259354 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-8{x}^{2})=${{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259354 1113 #uuid "b6ef0baa-19f2-5c87-8441-104458b5100c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259354 1145 "The graph $y={x}^{2}$ is an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, multiplying by the negative coefficient $-8$ causes $y=-8{x}^{2}$ to become a downward parabola. This changes the sign of the limit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-8{x}^{2})=-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2})=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259355 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259356 13194139533864 true] [17592186259355 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259355 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259355 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259356 13194139533864 true] [17592186259355 1110 #uuid "721c43ef-685e-5f93-8853-6882618ceaad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259356 1034 "-\\infty " 13194139533864 true] [17592186259356 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259356 1111 #uuid "6e8a2cc0-3064-527c-aa4a-d38a220c6751" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259357 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259358 13194139533864 true] [17592186259357 1089 "q-300767" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259357 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259357 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-3{x}^{8}-6{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}-15)=${{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259357 1113 #uuid "f376207d-e128-5c61-ab06-8cda85b3c269" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259357 1145 "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-3{x}^{8}-6{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}-15) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-3{x}^{8}) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{8}) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =-\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259358 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259359 13194139533864 true] [17592186259358 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259358 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259358 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259359 13194139533864 true] [17592186259358 1110 #uuid "a0467a37-cde6-50ac-8248-b8ad329354c3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259359 1034 "-\\infty " 13194139533864 true] [17592186259359 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259359 1111 #uuid "9b1d6244-8637-5d3b-b306-c756db7494b7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259360 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259361 13194139533864 true] [17592186259360 1089 "q-300969" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259360 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259360 1100 "Find the **exact** value of the limit.\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{2x+8}{\\sqrt[3]{\\,3{x}^{4}+5\\,}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259360 1113 #uuid "384b4b4a-1a21-5de5-b09c-7d8dcb9b51ed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259360 1145 "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{2x+8}{\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{4}+5}}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to -1}(2x+8)}{{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{4}+5}}\\,.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and the denominator separately.\n\nFor the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}(2x+8) & =2{lim}_{x\\to -1}x+{lim}_{x\\to -1}8 \\\\  & =2(-1)+8 \\\\  & =6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the root rule in the denominator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{4}+5} & =\\sqrt[3]{{lim}_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{4}+5)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{3(-1)^{4}+5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{2x+8}{\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{4}+5}} & =\\frac{6}{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259362 13194139533864 true] [17592186259361 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259361 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259361 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259362 13194139533864 true] [17592186259361 1110 #uuid "c85b5b07-734a-5431-a36d-96c50ba5499f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259362 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259362 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259362 1111 #uuid "40674303-c84e-53da-aa07-9a9b9e2be9fc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259363 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259364 13194139533864 true] [17592186259363 1089 "q-302050" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259363 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259363 1100 "If $p(x)=q(x)=-2x,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({p}^{4}(x)\\cdot {q}^{3}(x))=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259363 1113 #uuid "1dd5962e-7f16-511b-9886-c7af08a85022" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259363 1145 "First, we calculate the powers and multiply the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({p}^{4}(x)\\cdot {q}^{3}(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({(p(x))}^{4}\\cdot {(q(x))}^{3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({(-2x)}^{4}\\cdot {(-2x)}^{3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(16{x}^{4}\\cdot -8{x}^{3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }((16\\cdot -8){x}^{4+3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-128\\cdot {x}^{7}) \\\\  & =-128\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{7}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{7}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the negative constant $-128$ changes the sign of the limit. \nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-128\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{7} & =-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{7} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-128{x}^{7})=-\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259364 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259365 13194139533864 true] [17592186259364 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259364 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259364 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259365 13194139533864 true] [17592186259364 1110 #uuid "4ac13cb1-f847-5dfd-b77f-8b30d856c849" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259365 1034 "-\\infty " 13194139533864 true] [17592186259365 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259365 1111 #uuid "e7d113b2-2b28-5f24-ba8b-62b4f9edb7b9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259366 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259367 13194139533864 true] [17592186259366 1089 "q-302152" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259366 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259366 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{log}_{6}(4x-2)}{x+3}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259366 1113 #uuid "03fac39f-7bd4-593c-8143-5d6d29276bf9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259366 1145 "The function ${log}_{6}(4x-2)$ is well defined for $x>\\frac{1}{2},$ and so to find this limit we substitute $x=2$ into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{log}_{6}(4x-2)}{x+3} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 2}{log}_{6}(4x-2)}{{lim}_{x\\to 2}(x+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{6}(4\\cdot 2-2)}{2+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{6}6}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{6}({6}^{1})}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259367 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259368 13194139533864 true] [17592186259367 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259367 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259367 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259368 13194139533864 true] [17592186259367 1110 #uuid "5a167e5e-f8ff-5323-922c-31faa60e4d63" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259368 1034 "\\frac{1}{5}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259368 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259368 1111 #uuid "e5f158ff-5efa-5a7b-be91-b288a1bd7ed3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259369 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259370 13194139533864 true] [17592186259369 1089 "q-302174" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259369 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259369 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{log}_{3}(-4x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259369 1113 #uuid "6a119145-cfea-5bc3-a23b-0c4ed68fe966" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259369 1145 "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{3}(-4x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/bb8d326b427d481c69ad021a831efadf77cb90540dd79d0c3edd29f68cae41ec.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\ge 0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{log}_{3}(-4x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x<0.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{log}_{3}(-4x)=-\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259370 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259371 13194139533864 true] [17592186259370 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259370 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259370 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259371 13194139533864 true] [17592186259370 1110 #uuid "ee443d81-2272-555f-9267-d4c6d32ea69b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259371 1034 "-\\infty " 13194139533864 true] [17592186259371 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259371 1111 #uuid "229701dc-ff3f-5fa5-a496-0eff3b7db0ca" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259372 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259373 13194139533864 true] [17592186259372 1089 "q-302415" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259372 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259372 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\operatorname{csc}(2x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259372 1113 #uuid "83f4f818-d835-5987-9636-e45c64049c02" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259372 1145 "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ directly into the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\operatorname{csc}(2x) & =\\operatorname{csc}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259373 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259374 13194139533864 true] [17592186259373 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259373 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259373 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259374 13194139533864 true] [17592186259373 1110 #uuid "ba39c0e5-abab-533a-9258-7d7166fa507e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259374 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259374 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259374 1111 #uuid "70779499-2c86-54f2-81ee-5504191f9ca3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259375 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259376 13194139533864 true] [17592186259375 1089 "q-302430" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259375 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259375 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\frac{\\pi }{2}}4\\operatorname{cot}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259375 1113 #uuid "001d04f8-b07b-5927-bb3f-243c2de00cc8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259375 1145 "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\frac{\\pi }{2}}4\\operatorname{cot}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =4\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259376 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259377 13194139533864 true] [17592186259376 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259376 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259376 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259377 13194139533864 true] [17592186259376 1110 #uuid "43f94853-1c0e-5c4c-9db2-3cf5d1d70da9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259377 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259377 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259377 1111 #uuid "31bd45ff-27ae-5ca3-a764-8f5b76e15666" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259378 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259379 13194139533864 true] [17592186259378 1089 "q-302976" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259378 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259378 1100 "Given that ${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt{5x-m}=\\sqrt{5},$ what is the value of constant $m?$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259378 1113 #uuid "cf9923c2-2a75-5d31-b240-73c84287294f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259378 1145 "Since we know that the limit of $\\sqrt{5x-m}$ exists, we can substitute $x=2$ into this expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt{5x-m} & =\\sqrt{5(2)-m} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10-m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the above limit is equal to $\\sqrt{5},$ we can equate the above with $\\sqrt{5},$ and then solve for $m:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10-m} & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 10-m & =5 \\\\ m & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259379 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259380 13194139533864 true] [17592186259379 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259379 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259379 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259380 13194139533864 true] [17592186259379 1110 #uuid "656f0b5e-8a20-5b17-b807-c16226a1ba77" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259380 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259380 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259380 1111 #uuid "f8ee5394-b2dd-50d9-b7b0-77c1f5409d4f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259381 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259382 13194139533864 true] [17592186259381 1089 "q-3043" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259381 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259381 1100 "Solve for $a$ where $\\sqrt{\\frac{2a+4}{5}}=2.$\n\n$a$: {{a}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259381 1113 #uuid "2b6e17eb-b41f-58d1-bd2b-5852d16cb44e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259381 1145 "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{2a+4}{5}}=2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{\\frac{2a+4}{5}})}^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{2a+4}{5} & =4 \\\\ 2a+4 & =20 \\\\ a & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{2\\cdot 8+4}{5}} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{16+4}{5}} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $a=8$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $\\sqrt{\\frac{2a+4}{5}}=2$  is $a=8.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259382 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259383 13194139533864 true] [17592186259382 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259382 1041 "a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259382 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259383 13194139533864 true] [17592186259382 1110 #uuid "34d701d3-71f6-53e4-bd4d-224633ad5700" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259383 1034 "8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259383 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259383 1111 #uuid "50506808-de9a-51bb-9395-185ea71aaaad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259384 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259385 13194139533864 true] [17592186259384 1089 "q-30612" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259384 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259384 1100 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y={log}_{2}x?$\n\nI. - The range is $y>0$\nII. - Its vertical asymptote is $x=2$\nIII. - It passes through the point $(2,1)$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259384 1113 #uuid "5418831c-17ec-5431-913b-b0965c88ed86" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259384 1145 "Let's plot the graph of $y={log}_{2}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/fbc441f5915ef0dbe1e65ce1f748675e135ea8a2dc3a7d24dad56d55fca20eaf.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. From the graph, we see that the range is all real numbers.\n\n- Statement II is false. The vertical asymptote is $x=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Substituting $x=2$ into the equation of the curve gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{2}(2) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}({2}^{1}) \\\\  & =1\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259385 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259386 13194139533864 true] [17592186259385 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259387 13194139533864 true] [17592186259385 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259388 13194139533864 true] [17592186259385 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259389 13194139533864 true] [17592186259385 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259390 13194139533864 true] [17592186259385 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259391 13194139533864 true] [17592186259385 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259392 13194139533864 true] [17592186259385 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259393 13194139533864 true] [17592186259385 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186259385 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259385 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259390 13194139533864 true] [17592186259385 1110 #uuid "0816cacf-8922-5aca-afbb-cbc4aa237440" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259386 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259386 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259386 1111 #uuid "620979e0-b1ef-525e-a283-e88692b6e5af" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259387 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259387 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259387 1111 #uuid "de778815-cdbd-57af-b56c-86699d271222" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259388 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259388 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259388 1111 #uuid "c969f090-f90c-5e12-bfcb-3b0f97b43ade" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259389 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259389 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259389 1111 #uuid "92113b13-9afe-5b9c-9dad-982f3ab8fea1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259390 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259390 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259390 1111 #uuid "2f2fa861-14fc-5e57-8227-38e1af00edef" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259391 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259391 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259391 1111 #uuid "7eef7db2-3ce0-5d08-970f-0e4cef942ffe" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259392 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259392 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259392 1111 #uuid "b1be4ee3-a14c-54f0-9e69-0e8a5551cf51" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259393 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259393 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259393 1111 #uuid "209c21c4-7e6b-5776-9119-77f75ea9f42b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259394 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259395 13194139533864 true] [17592186259394 1089 "q-30780" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259394 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259394 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/315b26c0b615d3e9d6e422903b006ddf5eb8394aebbc75776453f46596f3e120.png)\n\nThe equation of the curve above is $y=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-3x+k.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259394 1113 #uuid "0b30ed9d-e1db-5113-aeb4-9d6baba177b2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259394 1145 "Notice that if we substitute $x=0$ into the given equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-3x+k \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}-3(0)+k \\\\ y & =k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nmeaning that the curve intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,k).$ Based on the graph, we can find that $k=-\\frac{3}{2}$ since our parabola intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-\\frac{3}{2}).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259395 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259396 13194139533864 true] [17592186259395 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259395 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259395 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259396 13194139533864 true] [17592186259395 1110 #uuid "de1b8841-efef-53fd-8d2d-8d82fb7aa2a3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259396 1034 "-\\frac32" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259396 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259396 1111 #uuid "e47c6b49-e3ce-5556-8d0d-cb202b1a4356" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259397 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259398 13194139533864 true] [17592186259397 1089 "q-308798" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259397 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259397 1100 "Find the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line to the curve $y={x}^{2}+3x$ at the point $(-1,-2).$\n\n${m}^{'}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259397 1113 #uuid "4147d71c-0780-5d7a-b644-4ac51dc7dac4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259397 1145 "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}+3x.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+3x)=2x+3\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(-1,-2):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(-1)=2(-1)+3=-2+3=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{'} & =-\\frac{1}{m} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259398 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259399 13194139533864 true] [17592186259398 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259398 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259398 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259399 13194139533864 true] [17592186259398 1110 #uuid "5df9d6fb-6be9-590e-a368-cd71009532f8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259399 1034 "-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259399 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259399 1111 #uuid "d6faeda4-9cd8-5fc1-a2cd-ee250d4c774f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259400 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259401 13194139533864 true] [17592186259400 1089 "q-310148" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259400 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259400 1100 "Given that $f(x)={log}_{5}(7-6x),$ find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259400 1113 #uuid "63271a66-41d7-583a-8800-b8588316b76a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259400 1145 "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)={log}_{5}(u),\\,u=7-6x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(5)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-6.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(5)}\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(7-6x)\\operatorname{ln}(5)}\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(6x-7)\\operatorname{ln}(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259401 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259402 13194139533864 true] [17592186259401 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259401 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259401 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259402 13194139533864 true] [17592186259401 1110 #uuid "79303664-97ab-5458-84fe-059bbf54a7cf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259402 1034 "\\frac{6}{(6x-7)\\operatorname{ln}(5)}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259402 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259402 1111 #uuid "75455967-ab5b-5fa9-a900-8596d3bd9923" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259403 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259404 13194139533864 true] [17592186259403 1089 "q-310170" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259403 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259403 1100 "Given $g(x)=4{x}^{2}+6x-\\frac{2}{x},$ we have\n\n${g}^{‴}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259403 1113 #uuid "c6d68558-2aaa-5e27-875e-de85a290b654" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259403 1145 "First, notice that we can rewrite the function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =4{x}^{2}+6x-\\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}+6x-2{x}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2}+6x-2{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =8x+6+2{x}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{''}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8x+6+2{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =8-4{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8-4{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =12{x}^{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259404 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259405 13194139533864 true] [17592186259404 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259404 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259404 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259405 13194139533864 true] [17592186259404 1110 #uuid "928663e7-ec6e-52d7-bce4-edd3e45dbad7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259405 1034 "\\frac{12}{{x}^{4}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259405 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259405 1111 #uuid "9f1fcdbc-a080-57f7-ba71-08ea4d2d53d2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259406 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259407 13194139533864 true] [17592186259406 1089 "q-310182" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259406 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259406 1100 "Find the slope $m$ of $y =\\sin \\left(\\pi \\left(x^{2} + 2x - 2\\right)\\right)$ at $x = 1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259406 1113 #uuid "4007a637-1203-5eab-9c3e-d86e260889f8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259406 1145 "The slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x=1$ is given by $f'(1).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\ny = \\sin{u}, \\qquad u = \\pi \\left(x^{2} +2x - 2\\right).\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\cos{u}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\pi (2x + 2).\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\cos u \\cdot \\pi (2x+2)\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\pi (2x+2)\\cos u\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\pi (2x+2)\\cos \\left(  \\pi \\left(x^{2} +2x - 2\\right) \\right).\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we find\n\n\\begin{align}\nf'(1) & =\\pi (2\\cdot1+2)\\cos \\left(  \\pi \\left(1^{2} +2\\cdot1 - 2\\right) \\right) \\\\[3pt]\n\n& =4\\pi \\cos(\\pi) \\\\[3pt]\n&= 4\\pi \\cdot (-1) \\\\[3pt]\n&= -4\\pi\n\\end{align}\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of $y = f(x)$ at $x = 1$ is \n\\[\nm = f'(1) = \\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{-4\\pi}.\n\\]" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259407 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259408 13194139533864 true] [17592186259407 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259407 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259407 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259408 13194139533864 true] [17592186259407 1110 #uuid "a2a47021-fb80-56ac-9acd-3b46f185f95e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259408 1034 "$-4\\pi$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259408 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259408 1111 #uuid "b03bf73f-e8ce-5f5b-9004-f803d8be9067" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259409 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259410 13194139533864 true] [17592186259409 1089 "q-310188" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259409 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259409 1100 "Differentiate ${3}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}(x).$\n\n$({3}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}(x))^{'}=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,{3}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}(x)$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259409 1113 #uuid "3d49772e-f3e5-5d1d-a237-9dc33783e3e3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259409 1145 "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={3}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({3}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}(x))^{'} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =({3}^{x})^{'}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(x)+{3}^{x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}(x))^{'} \\\\  & =({3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(3))(\\operatorname{sin}(x))+({3}^{x})(\\operatorname{cos}(x)) \\\\  & ={3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(3)\\operatorname{sin}(x)+{3}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259410 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259411 13194139533864 true] [17592186259410 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259410 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259410 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259411 13194139533864 true] [17592186259410 1110 #uuid "c9a0a841-49fd-5c99-8a94-f01b93664851" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259411 1034 "{3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(3)\\operatorname{sin}(x)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259411 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259411 1111 #uuid "c857ebdc-bf0f-5abb-b2fe-658d35af257b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259412 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259413 13194139533864 true] [17592186259412 1089 "q-310788" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259412 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259412 1100 "Given that $-2{x}^{2}-4kx-8k=0,$ where $k$ is a **positive** constant, has exactly one real solution, what is the value of $k?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259412 1113 #uuid "b9df0228-b059-53ad-bb1d-ba5e63872766" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259412 1145 "A quadratic equation has exactly one real root if the discriminant is equal to zero: $D=0.$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na=-2,\\,b=-4k,\\,c=-8k\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-4k)^{2}-4(-2)(-8k) \\\\  & =16{k}^{2}-64k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have exactly one real solution, we need $D=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{k}^{2}-64k & =0 \\\\ 16k(k-4) & =0 \\\\ k & =0,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be positive, we conclude that $k=4.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259413 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259414 13194139533864 true] [17592186259413 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259413 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259413 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259414 13194139533864 true] [17592186259413 1110 #uuid "3e1c629d-9781-51b9-97e1-e229d8be1776" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259414 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259414 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259414 1111 #uuid "bcfe502a-e69f-59b8-bed5-dd062ff1b211" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259415 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259416 13194139533864 true] [17592186259415 1089 "q-31226" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259415 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259415 1100 "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259415 1113 #uuid "5c9eb90c-59cb-57bc-b62b-90c5bc3c0020" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259415 1145 "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=3{x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{tan}x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={sec}^{2}x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2})({sec}^{2}x)+(\\operatorname{tan}x)(6x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{sec}^{2}x+6x\\operatorname{tan}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi $ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=\\pi } & =3{x}^{2}{sec}^{2}x+6x\\operatorname{tan}x{|}_{x=\\pi } \\\\  & =3(\\pi )^{2}{sec}^{2}(\\pi )+6(\\pi )\\operatorname{tan}(\\pi ) \\\\  & =3{\\pi }^{2}\\cdot 1+6\\pi \\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3{\\pi }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $3{\\pi }^{2}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259416 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259417 13194139533864 true] [17592186259416 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259416 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259416 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259417 13194139533864 true] [17592186259416 1110 #uuid "a85f47f0-f0c4-5bf2-a577-9d7ce06f80ad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259417 1034 "3{\\pi }^{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259417 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259417 1111 #uuid "9fea484c-5b7e-5008-92b6-4a3b92ea163e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259418 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259419 13194139533864 true] [17592186259418 1089 "q-31241" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259418 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259418 1100 "Find the slope of $y=\\operatorname{sin}({x}^{2}+2x-1)$ at $x=0.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259418 1113 #uuid "a9a78083-f539-5f87-8b3e-c72bf4128486" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259418 1145 "The slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by ${f}^{'}(0).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{'}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}u,\\,u={x}^{2}+2x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{cos}u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x+2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}u\\cdot (2x+2) \\\\  & =(2x+2)\\operatorname{cos}u \\\\  & =(2x+2)\\operatorname{cos}({x}^{2}+2x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(0) & =(2\\cdot 0+2)\\operatorname{cos}({0}^{2}+2\\cdot 0-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}(-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is $2\\operatorname{cos}1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259419 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259420 13194139533864 true] [17592186259419 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259419 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259419 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259420 13194139533864 true] [17592186259419 1110 #uuid "23224884-d1e7-55e4-b946-c8bc01c96771" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259420 1034 "2\\operatorname{cos}1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259420 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259420 1111 #uuid "e0efb74e-6dc4-52d8-b4f1-9c5676d9a231" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259421 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259422 13194139533864 true] [17592186259421 1089 "q-31261" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259421 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259421 1100 "$\\int ({e}^{x}+{8}^{x}+\\frac{3}{x})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259421 1113 #uuid "8e8b9317-60d7-599c-a6b0-5d0715ef2392" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259421 1145 "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({e}^{x}+{8}^{x}+\\frac{3}{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {8}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{3}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}+\\frac{{8}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}8}+3\\operatorname{ln}|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259422 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259423 13194139533864 true] [17592186259422 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259422 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259422 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259423 13194139533864 true] [17592186259422 1110 #uuid "7136d56c-9293-54dd-9641-7de71427756d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259423 1034 "{e}^{x}+\\frac{{8}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}8}+3\\operatorname{ln}|x|+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259423 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259423 1111 #uuid "bcf6f2d5-f362-5281-9c11-e26e737ab09c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259424 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259425 13194139533864 true] [17592186259424 1089 "q-31266" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259424 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259424 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{x+1}{3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+4}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259424 1113 #uuid "d6d25e57-c27a-5cc5-8667-e7f17ee59ccd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259424 1145 "Note that plugging in $x=-1$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{x+1}{3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+4} & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{[x+1]^{'}}{[3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+4]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{1}{9{x}^{2}-2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9(-1)^{2}-2(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259425 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259426 13194139533864 true] [17592186259425 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259425 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259425 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259426 13194139533864 true] [17592186259425 1110 #uuid "7da96791-d575-5748-9560-bed41a152ebb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259426 1034 "\\frac{1}{11}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259426 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259426 1111 #uuid "abf12427-ef59-522b-a10d-49ebe034b6be" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259427 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259428 13194139533864 true] [17592186259427 1089 "q-31272" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259427 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259427 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\sqrt{x+4}-x-8}{{x}^{2}}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259427 1113 #uuid "5f6b45e4-aad3-52ce-a5c4-dc96f14dd157" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259427 1145 "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\sqrt{x+4}-x-8}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[4\\sqrt{x+4}-x-8]}^{'}}{[{x}^{2}]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+4}}-1]}{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\sqrt{x+4}-x-8}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+4}}-1]}{2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+4}}-1]}^{'}}{[2x]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[-\\frac{1}{(x+4)\\sqrt{x+4}}]}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{[-\\frac{1}{(0+4)\\sqrt{0+4}}]}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{[-\\frac{1}{4(2)}]}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259428 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259429 13194139533864 true] [17592186259428 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259428 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259428 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259429 13194139533864 true] [17592186259428 1110 #uuid "d44d9737-467b-5c51-8ca8-3576f2404f16" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259429 1034 "-\\frac{1}{16}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259429 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259429 1111 #uuid "25ff98e6-667e-58bd-98fc-d22141a55c7f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259430 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259431 13194139533864 true] [17592186259430 1089 "q-31288" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259430 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259430 1100 "Find ${f}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ if $f(x)=-6\\operatorname{cot}x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259430 1113 #uuid "81fa46b9-6e38-5bbb-a82c-d73296a4d0fd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259430 1145 "First, we just take the constant out of the derivative and use the result for the derivative of $\\operatorname{cot}x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-6\\operatorname{cot}x) \\\\  & =-6(-{csc}^{2}x) \\\\  & =6{csc}^{2}x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ in the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =6{csc}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =6{(2)}^{2} \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259431 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259432 13194139533864 true] [17592186259431 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259431 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259431 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259432 13194139533864 true] [17592186259431 1110 #uuid "34136657-7590-59a8-8251-ce13de8a18c7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259432 1034 "24" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259432 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259432 1111 #uuid "fe55bba9-dd10-5bc7-9f24-e71a53b0559b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259433 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259434 13194139533864 true] [17592186259433 1089 "q-312907" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259433 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259433 1100 "Find ${f}^{'}(x)$ when $f(x)={cos}^{8}(5{x}^{3}).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} ${cos}^{7}(5{x}^{3})\\operatorname{sin}(5{x}^{3})$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259433 1113 #uuid "6422b1f8-1755-59dd-a7bc-a54ffd2688c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259433 1145 "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{8},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}v,\\,v=5{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=8{u}^{7},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=-\\operatorname{sin}v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=15{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =8{u}^{7}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}v)\\cdot 15{x}^{2} \\\\  & =8{(\\operatorname{cos}(5{x}^{3}))}^{7}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}(5{x}^{3}))\\cdot 15{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-120{x}^{2}{cos}^{7}(5{x}^{3})\\operatorname{sin}(5{x}^{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259434 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259435 13194139533864 true] [17592186259434 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259434 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259434 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259435 13194139533864 true] [17592186259434 1110 #uuid "36db61fa-c70d-5a2d-8faa-8f54dc214ad6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259435 1034 "-120{x}^{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259435 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259435 1111 #uuid "01a74bc4-19e8-59d6-b3c4-1f7913db2ec0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259436 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259437 13194139533864 true] [17592186259436 1089 "q-313299" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259436 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259436 1100 "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}(2x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259436 1113 #uuid "d32428de-c244-5f32-a35a-7db55d2d68e2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259436 1145 "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nThis question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}2x.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=2x,\\,{v}^{'}(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}2x.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =(2x)(\\operatorname{sin}2x)+({x}^{2})(2\\operatorname{cos}2x) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}2x+2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})|}_{x=\\pi /4} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+2{(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\cdot \\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259437 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259438 13194139533864 true] [17592186259437 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259437 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259437 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259438 13194139533864 true] [17592186259437 1110 #uuid "592c960a-7917-538b-9b05-b6908b3d02b1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259438 1034 "\\frac\\pi2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259438 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259438 1111 #uuid "d20b04ed-9f25-54bb-9a7c-af2f68180637" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259439 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259440 13194139533864 true] [17592186259439 1089 "q-31333" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259439 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259439 1100 "Calculate $\\dfrac {\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ for $y =8\\csc(10x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259439 1113 #uuid "01c41333-1844-5900-ad46-7ce70f99f944" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259439 1145 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\ny = 8\\csc{u}, \\qquad u = 10x\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = -8\\csc u \\cot u \\qquad \\style{font-family:inherit;}{\\text{and}} \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 10.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} & = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n&=-8\\csc u \\cot u \\cdot (10)\\\\[5pt]\n&=-80\\csc u \\cot u \\\\[5pt]\n&=-80\\csc (10x) \\cot (10x).\n\\end{align*}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259440 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259441 13194139533864 true] [17592186259440 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259440 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259440 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259441 13194139533864 true] [17592186259440 1110 #uuid "0c9926bd-6a5b-5790-a025-a5a677b25113" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259441 1034 "-80\\csc(10x)\\cot(10x)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259441 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259441 1111 #uuid "16651160-26ff-5241-9989-b9ee17f48379" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259442 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259443 13194139533864 true] [17592186259442 1089 "q-313362" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259442 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259442 1100 "Given that $y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{(3x+5)^{2}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | ${(3x+5)}^{3}$ |" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259442 1113 #uuid "a22f8807-f06b-5bb5-ba16-a2214209db89" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259442 1145 "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)={x}^{3}$ and the denominator $v(x)=(3x+5)^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=3{x}^{2},\\,{v}^{'}(x)=6(3x+5).\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3{x}^{2}){(3x+5)}^{2}-({x}^{3})(6(3x+5))}{{({(3x+5)}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}{(3x+5)}^{2}-6{x}^{3}(3x+5)}{{(3x+5)}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}(3x+5)((3x+5)-2x)}{{(3x+5)}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}(x+5)}{{(3x+5)}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259443 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259444 13194139533864 true] [17592186259443 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259443 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259443 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259444 13194139533864 true] [17592186259443 1110 #uuid "cd475635-649d-56fe-b8a0-c5c13f9d210c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259444 1034 "3{x}^{2}(x+5)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259444 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259444 1111 #uuid "b5b8f9fb-c244-5126-9181-8847430c5aef" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259445 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259446 13194139533864 true] [17592186259445 1089 "q-31341" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259445 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259445 1100 "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\cot\\left(1-\\dfrac x 5 \\right)$ at the point where $x =1.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259445 1113 #uuid "83184978-b220-5676-82cf-9ec259139917" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259445 1145 "The slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x=1$ is given by $f'(1).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\nf = \\cot{u}, \\qquad u = 1-\\dfrac x 5.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = -\\csc^2 u \\qquad \\text{and}\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -\\dfrac{1}{5}.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x} &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n&=-\\csc^2 u \\cdot  \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{5}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac{1}{5}\\csc^2 \\left(1-\\dfrac{x}{5}\\right).\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we find\n\n\\begin{eqnarray}\n    \\eqalign{\n\nf'(x) & =\\dfrac 1 5\\csc^2\\left(1-\\dfrac 1 5 \\right) \\\\[3pt]\n\t& =\\dfrac 1 5\\csc^2\\left(\\dfrac 4 5 \\right) .\n       \n    }\n\\end{eqnarray}\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve $y = f(x)$ at $x = 1$ is $\\dfrac{1}{5}\\csc^2\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259446 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259447 13194139533864 true] [17592186259446 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259446 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259446 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259447 13194139533864 true] [17592186259446 1110 #uuid "048e41c7-4abd-5f21-a9c0-f0b4e4a99ac2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259447 1034 "\\frac15\\csc^2(\\frac45)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259447 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259447 1111 #uuid "937297d6-a133-550c-8cc2-602f4a7f8920" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259448 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259448 1089 "q-31355" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259448 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259448 1100 "Find ${f}^{'}(x)$ if $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}x}{{x}^{2}}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259448 1113 #uuid "e90a75e5-83c6-52f3-9c5a-0f176678967d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259448 1145 "We calculate the derivative using the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}x}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-{csc}^{2}x)\\cdot {x}^{2}-\\operatorname{cot}x\\cdot (2x)}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-x{csc}^{2}x-2\\operatorname{cot}x}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259449 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259450 13194139533864 true] [17592186259449 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259449 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259449 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259450 13194139533864 true] [17592186259449 1110 #uuid "35589321-9f7c-5ff0-87ab-ad7726d91a5b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259450 1034 "\\frac{-x{csc}^{2}x-2\\operatorname{cot}x}{{x}^{3}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259450 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259450 1111 #uuid "07c29bc9-70da-577a-ad45-1709eb9e94ec" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259451 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259452 13194139533864 true] [17592186259451 1089 "q-31366" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259451 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259451 1100 "Factor $81{x}^{4}-{y}^{4}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259451 1113 #uuid "4c7551de-24f5-5c99-ac5b-64920692e68d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259451 1145 "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n81{x}^{4}-{y}^{4}={(9{x}^{2})}^{2}-{({y}^{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{(9{x}^{2})}^{2}-{({y}^{2})}^{2}=(9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(9{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares. Therefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(9{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) & =(9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})((3x)^{2}-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =(9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(3x+y)(3x-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259452 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259452 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259452 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259452 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259452 1110 #uuid "cb915747-92fd-52d0-a4db-6a5681bec3fb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259453 1034 "(9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(3x+y)(3x-y)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259453 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259453 1111 #uuid "292d93ec-64cc-5162-a95a-c9fa7551cfba" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259454 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259455 13194139533864 true] [17592186259454 1089 "q-313722" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259454 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259454 1100 "Find ${h}^{'}(x)$ if $h(x)=4x\\operatorname{csc}(x).$\n\n${h}^{'}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259454 1113 #uuid "4ba46c76-d873-5609-acab-7d3bfa67173b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259454 1145 "We calculate the derivative using the product rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x\\operatorname{csc}(x)) \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{csc}(x))+(4x)(-\\operatorname{csc}(x)\\operatorname{cot}(x)) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{csc}(x)-4x\\operatorname{csc}(x)\\operatorname{cot}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259455 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259456 13194139533864 true] [17592186259455 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259455 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259455 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259456 13194139533864 true] [17592186259455 1110 #uuid "0ad46b0f-dbd5-54af-9929-d948ec59e431" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259456 1034 "4\\operatorname{csc}(x)-4x\\operatorname{csc}(x)\\operatorname{cot}(x)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259456 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259456 1111 #uuid "e4c318c7-19c4-55b9-ad35-06d42b8bf435" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259457 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259458 13194139533864 true] [17592186259457 1089 "q-31383" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259457 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259457 1100 "What is the coefficient of ${x}^{2}y$ in the product $(2x+y)({x}^{2}-2xy+3{y}^{2})?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259457 1113 #uuid "cf4dcdbc-9168-55c3-a5fe-dc072fb8a7ca" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259457 1145 "We distribute both terms of the binomial in the first set of parentheses to the trinomial in the second set of parentheses. Then, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+y)({x}^{2}-2xy+3{y}^{2}) & = \\\\ 2x({x}^{2}-2xy+3{y}^{2})+y({x}^{2}-2xy+3{y}^{2}) & = \\\\ 2{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}y+6x{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}y-2x{y}^{2}+3{y}^{3} & = \\\\ 2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+4x{y}^{2}+3{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{2}y$ is $-3.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259458 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259459 13194139533864 true] [17592186259458 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259458 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259458 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259459 13194139533864 true] [17592186259458 1110 #uuid "a3ac9484-9a16-54ca-ad81-23e24fe0f882" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259459 1034 "-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259459 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259459 1111 #uuid "66d3f27d-31a7-5ebb-a2b6-ef885bbff640" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259460 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259461 13194139533864 true] [17592186259460 1089 "q-31385" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259460 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259460 1100 "Give an expression equivalent to $(3x+4)({x}^{2}-6x-9)?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259460 1113 #uuid "691f1984-3037-52ad-a05f-3666f60ccac0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259460 1145 "We distribute both terms of the binomial in the first set of parentheses to the trinomial in the second set of parentheses. Then, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x+4)({x}^{2}-6x-9) & = \\\\ 3x({x}^{2}-6x-9)+4({x}^{2}-6x-9) & = \\\\ 3{x}^{3}-18{x}^{2}-27x+4{x}^{2}-24x-36 & = \\\\ 3{x}^{3}-14{x}^{2}-51x-36\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259462 13194139533864 true] [17592186259461 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259461 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259461 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259462 13194139533864 true] [17592186259461 1110 #uuid "0c430999-f2b0-5ac6-9075-b351b033d7aa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259462 1034 "3{x}^{3}-14{x}^{2}-51x-36" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259462 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259462 1111 #uuid "17b53bea-f6e2-5283-94c1-cc961dd74399" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259463 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259463 1089 "q-315534" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259463 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259463 1100 "The velocity $v$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $v(t)=3{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}-5t.$ Calculate the acceleration $a$ of the particle at time $t.$\n\n$a(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259463 1113 #uuid "f16010a1-03ac-5174-a9bf-0b8620798263" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259463 1145 "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}-5t) \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}+12t-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259464 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259464 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259464 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259464 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259464 1110 #uuid "f8670335-24d0-58b8-bdc3-947c6ccc38cf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259465 1034 "9t^2+12t-5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259465 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259465 1111 #uuid "2a36061a-89e5-5d38-8a4d-4753f2e1e551" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259466 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259467 13194139533864 true] [17592186259466 1089 "q-315704" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259466 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259466 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ca91f5bd9354efe22a5782fb7aee7c4a147b5ae186a661fbc4f77aa982af28c8.png)\n\nThe area under the curve $y=9-{x}^{2}$ between $x=-1$, $x=2$ and the $x$-axis is approximated using a right Riemann sum, as shown above. What is the expression for the right Riemann sum ${S}_{R}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $i.$*\n\n${S}_{R}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}($\n{{field-1}} $)$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259466 1113 #uuid "e2c521c2-d305-533c-b589-d2a02598a150" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259466 1145 "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{S}_{R}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we see that there are $n=3$ rectangles. The endpoints indicate that $a=-1$ and $b=2,$ so the step size is \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-(-1)}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum ${S}_{R}$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{R}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(a+iΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}f(-1+i\\cdot 1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}f(i-1) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}[9-(i-1)^{2}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}[9-({i}^{2}-2i+1)] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}(8+2i-{i}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259467 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259468 13194139533864 true] [17592186259467 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259467 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259467 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259468 13194139533864 true] [17592186259467 1110 #uuid "e8e4c9b6-7c52-5c61-a079-5e5b766d8862" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259468 1034 "8+2i-{i}^{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259468 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259468 1111 #uuid "74c4b0d3-8dd1-599f-9f63-02f09520a7cd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259469 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259470 13194139533864 true] [17592186259469 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259472 13194139533864 true] [17592186259469 1089 "q-315755" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259469 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259469 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4 \\\\ x={y}^{2}-10y+29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x,y)$ of the system above.\n\n$(x,y)=($ {{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}} $)$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259469 1113 #uuid "5d374218-4fc1-5b1c-b8fc-aa9ccc1df000" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259469 1145 "First, we set the equations equal and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-10y+29 & =4 \\\\ {y}^{2}-10y+25 & =0 \\\\ (y-5)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-5 & =0 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $(4,5).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259470 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259471 13194139533864 true] [17592186259470 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259470 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259470 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259471 13194139533864 true] [17592186259470 1110 #uuid "98a056b0-c3d0-51d6-975b-20443215dccf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259471 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259471 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259471 1111 #uuid "24270c0b-f6b0-5152-ae02-d754880a56f1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259472 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259473 13194139533864 true] [17592186259472 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259472 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259472 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259473 13194139533864 true] [17592186259472 1110 #uuid "f3042e9c-e647-5a95-81fb-10468ab91b70" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259473 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259473 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259473 1111 #uuid "624c7ae5-bd38-54aa-be9a-0a6d0ecf556a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259474 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259475 13194139533864 true] [17592186259474 1089 "q-315758" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259474 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259474 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-{x}^{2}+2x-3 \\\\ y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf pairs $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ with ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the system above, what is the value of ${x}_{2}{y}_{2}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf none exist, enter a phrase such as \"No real solutions\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259474 1113 #uuid "cf7ed733-de8f-586d-bd6c-3546abb19c44" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259474 1145 "First, we set the equations equal and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+2x-3 & =1 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+2x-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the obtained quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=1,\\,b=-1\\cdot 2=-2,\\,c=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the discriminant of the quadratic is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-2)^{2}-4(1)(4) \\\\  & =-12<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system has no real solutions." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259475 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259476 13194139533864 true] [17592186259475 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259475 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259475 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259476 13194139533864 true] [17592186259475 1110 #uuid "b0195630-1fa8-58d5-b20c-3e9474d53208" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259476 1034 "No real solutions" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259476 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259476 1111 #uuid "e134657f-d454-513e-af3b-c71b4ee5796e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259477 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259478 13194139533864 true] [17592186259477 1089 "q-315764" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259477 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259477 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x={y}^{2}-3y \\\\ x=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf pairs $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ with ${y}_{1}<{y}_{2}$ are the solutions of the system above, what is the value of ${x}_{1}{y}_{2}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259477 1113 #uuid "2e2cc1ac-6bdd-51c7-9e50-11f12536a850" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259477 1145 "First, we set the equations equal and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-3y & =10 \\\\ {y}^{2}-3y-10 & =0 \\\\ (y-5)(y+2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are \n\n$$\n({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(10,-2)\\,\\text{and}\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(10,5).\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{y}_{2}=(10)\\cdot (5)=50.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259478 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259479 13194139533864 true] [17592186259478 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259478 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259478 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259479 13194139533864 true] [17592186259478 1110 #uuid "91e13cb0-b368-5ecf-ad50-5a5f174af71b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259479 1034 "50" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259479 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259479 1111 #uuid "beecc13b-b00c-5d98-bd7b-d43e933947ee" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259480 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259481 13194139533864 true] [17592186259480 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259483 13194139533864 true] [17592186259480 1089 "q-315767" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259480 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259480 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=7 \\\\ x={y}^{2}+4y+11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x,y)$ of the system above.\n\n$(x,y)=($ {{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}} $)$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259480 1113 #uuid "68136187-6129-51e6-90fd-f5ffc38cff9b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259480 1145 "First, we set the equations equal and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+4y+11 & =7 \\\\ {y}^{2}+4y+4 & =0 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y+2 & =0 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $(7,-2).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259481 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259482 13194139533864 true] [17592186259481 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259481 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259481 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259482 13194139533864 true] [17592186259481 1110 #uuid "19a2f6e8-62c3-5307-aadb-70d34d8a2037" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259482 1034 "7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259482 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259482 1111 #uuid "8863d29b-8e2a-5286-91cf-0c76e316cc04" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259483 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259484 13194139533864 true] [17592186259483 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259483 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259483 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259484 13194139533864 true] [17592186259483 1110 #uuid "76364189-2634-5706-b8d4-09600bad3673" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259484 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259484 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259484 1111 #uuid "42c10b4f-628c-5218-8d12-3cd70398ba78" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259485 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259486 13194139533864 true] [17592186259485 1089 "q-315776" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259485 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259485 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x={y}^{2}-5y+6 \\\\ x=2y-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf pairs $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ with ${y}_{1}<{y}_{2}$ are the solutions of the system above, what is the value of ${x}_{1}{y}_{2}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259485 1113 #uuid "96fa5c30-270f-5517-92f5-554b6af8cb5c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259485 1145 "First, we set the equations equal and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-5y+6 & =2y-4 \\\\ {y}^{2}-7y+10 & =0 \\\\ (y-5)(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =2,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $y$ in the second equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{1}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2{y}_{1}-4 \\\\  & =2(2)-4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${y}_{2}=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2{y}_{2}-4 \\\\  & =2(5)-4 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are \n\n$$\n({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,2)\\,\\text{and}\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(6,5).\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{y}_{2}=(0)\\cdot (5)=0.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259486 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259487 13194139533864 true] [17592186259486 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259486 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259486 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259487 13194139533864 true] [17592186259486 1110 #uuid "41185ab3-82a6-5e34-ba19-ade16b2c73b6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259487 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259487 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259487 1111 #uuid "68a8f98f-9dff-58da-8426-3f2116ce5153" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259488 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259489 13194139533864 true] [17592186259488 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259491 13194139533864 true] [17592186259488 1089 "q-315782" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259488 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259488 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x={y}^{2}+y+5 \\\\ x=-3y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x,y)$ of the system above.\n\n$(x,y)=($ {{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}} $)$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259488 1113 #uuid "92e73856-4ae4-5727-a5f9-ecbb2cce4a26" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259488 1145 "First, we set the equations equal and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+y+5 & =-3y+1 \\\\ {y}^{2}+4y+4 & =0 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y+2 & =0 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute this into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-3y+1 \\\\  & =-3(-2)+1 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $(7,-2).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259489 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259490 13194139533864 true] [17592186259489 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259489 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259489 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259490 13194139533864 true] [17592186259489 1110 #uuid "6309eafd-2539-5eeb-8a2f-fb33e08e5c13" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259490 1034 "7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259490 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259490 1111 #uuid "16fdb34f-cb52-52b3-8f12-8f1f4fc8cd02" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259491 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259492 13194139533864 true] [17592186259491 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259491 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259491 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259492 13194139533864 true] [17592186259491 1110 #uuid "873d2ca7-9ce3-5d5d-8074-201ffd7e6aa0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259492 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259492 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259492 1111 #uuid "50bdecd5-2ea0-5053-a2c9-36af6feb993a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259493 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259494 13194139533864 true] [17592186259493 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259496 13194139533864 true] [17592186259493 1089 "q-315828" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259493 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259493 1100 "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{\\,1+4k/n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259493 1113 #uuid "34d0f7c4-2abe-5df5-963b-378be7040284" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259493 1145 "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=1.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-1}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=1+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={e}^{\\,{x}_{k}}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{\\,1+4k/n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)={e}^{x}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=5.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259494 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259495 13194139533864 true] [17592186259494 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259494 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259494 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259495 13194139533864 true] [17592186259494 1110 #uuid "2f256b11-f212-5064-a478-a91d70be3eda" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259495 1034 "{e}^{x}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259495 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259495 1111 #uuid "903adbd9-a10f-58fb-8a98-e6e37b7ed681" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259496 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259497 13194139533864 true] [17592186259496 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259496 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259496 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259497 13194139533864 true] [17592186259496 1110 #uuid "1e0a3a4c-f1aa-5bd4-b510-60c3ff25d562" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259497 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259497 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259497 1111 #uuid "3e75c3d7-59f2-5707-90f3-3f9ef53a3d2c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259498 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259499 13194139533864 true] [17592186259498 1089 "q-315849" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259498 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259498 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}-6x \\\\ 5{x}^{2}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $(x,y)$ is a solution of the system above such that $x\\ne 0$, what is the value of $x+y?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259498 1113 #uuid "d43a11ff-b10a-5b65-9970-066e56ae225b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259498 1145 "From the first equation, we have\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}-6x.\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2} & =2y \\\\ 5{x}^{2} & =2({x}^{2}-6x) \\\\ 5{x}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}-12x \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+12x & =0 \\\\ x(3x+12) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x$ in the first equation.\n\n- For ${x}_{1}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1} \\\\  & =(0)^{2}-6(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${x}_{2}=-4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & ={x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{2} \\\\  & =(-4)^{2}-6(-4) \\\\  & =16+24 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are \n\n$$\n({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,0)\\,\\text{and}\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(-4,40).\n$$\n\nFinally, since we require the solution such that $x\\ne 0,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & ={x}_{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-4+40 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259499 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259500 13194139533864 true] [17592186259499 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259499 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259499 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259500 13194139533864 true] [17592186259499 1110 #uuid "9ca2b466-3788-5505-b7ed-22b8c756e13b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259500 1034 "36" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259500 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259500 1111 #uuid "5d9b33ea-d06b-56b0-adff-af0c3dfb8852" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259501 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259502 13194139533864 true] [17592186259501 1089 "q-315861" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259501 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259501 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3{y}^{2}-4y \\\\ 8x-7{y}^{2}=5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $(x,y)$ is a solution of the system above such that $x\\ne 0$, what is the value of $x+y?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259501 1113 #uuid "7f964985-f603-54e9-95ca-111340622232" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259501 1145 "First, we simplify the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-7{y}^{2} & =5x \\\\ 3x-7{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3x & =7{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, from the first equation, we have\n\n$$\nx=3{y}^{2}-4y.\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =7{y}^{2} \\\\ 3(3{y}^{2}-4y) & =7{y}^{2} \\\\ 9{y}^{2}-12y & =7{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-12y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-6y & =0 \\\\ y(y-6) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $y$ in the first equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{1}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =3{y}_{1}^{2}-4{y}_{1} \\\\  & =3(0)^{2}-4(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${y}_{2}=6,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =3{y}_{2}^{2}-4{y}_{2} \\\\  & =3(6)^{2}-4(6) \\\\  & =108-24 \\\\  & =84.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are \n\n$$\n({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,0)\\,\\text{and}\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(84,6).\n$$\n\nFinally, since we require the solution such that $x\\ne 0,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & ={x}_{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\  & =84+6 \\\\  & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259502 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259503 13194139533864 true] [17592186259502 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259502 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259502 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259503 13194139533864 true] [17592186259502 1110 #uuid "b6f4edf2-6034-5d9e-8266-21ca7ae38846" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259503 1034 "90" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259503 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259503 1111 #uuid "a501b122-c456-5b2c-befa-1a923f6cc418" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259504 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259505 13194139533864 true] [17592186259504 1089 "q-315865" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259504 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259504 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x={y}^{2}+2y \\\\ 4x-3{y}^{2}=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $(x,y)$ is a solution of the system above such that $y\\ne 0$, what is the value of $x-y?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259504 1113 #uuid "e7347537-9022-5098-b0dd-a7d044f3e73e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259504 1145 "First, we simplify the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-3{y}^{2} & =2x \\\\ 2x-3{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2x & =3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, from the first equation, we have\n\n$$\nx={y}^{2}+2y.\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2({y}^{2}+2y) & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2}+4y & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y & =0 \\\\ y(y-4) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $y$ in the first equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{1}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{1} \\\\  & =(0)^{2}+2(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${y}_{2}=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(4)^{2}+2(4) \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are \n\n$$\n({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,0)\\,\\text{and}\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(24,4).\n$$\n\nFinally, since we require the solution such that $y\\ne 0,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & ={x}_{2}-{y}_{2} \\\\  & =24-4 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259505 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259506 13194139533864 true] [17592186259505 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259505 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259505 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259506 13194139533864 true] [17592186259505 1110 #uuid "4dfff635-3e1f-572d-9f57-1ef58d08225b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259506 1034 "20" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259506 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259506 1111 #uuid "22f30778-fb53-5312-8841-e16de75ddb46" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259507 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259508 13194139533864 true] [17592186259507 1089 "q-316010" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259507 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259507 1100 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{32}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{32}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259507 1113 #uuid "b0bffb42-be09-55cb-8b31-881c330b35e8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259507 1145 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{32}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{32}{x}^{-2/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-2/5+1}}{(-\\frac{2}{5}+1)}{|}_{1}^{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\,{x}^{3/5}{|}_{1}^{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\,{(\\sqrt[5]{x})}^{3}{|}_{1}^{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}({(\\sqrt[5]{32})}^{3}-{(\\sqrt[5]{1})}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}({2}^{3}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259508 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259509 13194139533864 true] [17592186259508 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259508 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259508 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259509 13194139533864 true] [17592186259508 1110 #uuid "42e20265-3fc6-5ad1-b14c-f0c396b19a7f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259509 1034 "\\frac{35}3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259509 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259509 1111 #uuid "5bdf05f2-2d0a-5785-8632-d7bb1bd2cd88" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259510 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259511 13194139533864 true] [17592186259510 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259513 13194139533864 true] [17592186259510 1089 "q-316031" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259510 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259510 1100 "The displacement $s$ (in meters) relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by the equation $s(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(t+1)+6}{{t}^{2}+1}$, where $t$ is the time, measured in seconds. Find the position ${s}_{0}$ and velocity ${v}_{0}$ of the particle at the initial moment of time $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n${s}_{0}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\text{m}\\,\\text{and}\\,$\n${v}_{0}=$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{m/s}$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259510 1113 #uuid "d6fb64ab-22ee-5c67-af5e-8451c17bd02b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259510 1145 "To calculate the initial position, we substitute $t=0$ it into the equation $s(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(t+1)+6}{{t}^{2}+1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{0} & =s(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(0+1)+6}{{0}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =6\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(t+1)+6}{{t}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{t+1}\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)-(\\operatorname{ln}(t+1)+6)\\cdot 2t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}+1-2t(t+1)(\\operatorname{ln}(t+1)+6)}{(t+1)({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the velocity at $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ we substitute $t=0$ into the velocity function above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{0}^{2}+1-2(0)(0+1)(\\operatorname{ln}(0+1)+6)}{(0+1)({0}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot {1}^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259511 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259512 13194139533864 true] [17592186259511 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259511 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259511 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259512 13194139533864 true] [17592186259511 1110 #uuid "c6060556-4f2f-528e-867a-92246354b974" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259512 1034 "6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259512 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259512 1111 #uuid "11d4230a-c6eb-5dc5-ad07-58e762322846" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259513 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259514 13194139533864 true] [17592186259513 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259513 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259513 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259514 13194139533864 true] [17592186259513 1110 #uuid "af6761b6-ac37-533b-acfa-de643b510281" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259514 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259514 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259514 1111 #uuid "34f275eb-583c-59aa-91f1-acad4dd146e9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259515 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259516 13194139533864 true] [17592186259515 1089 "q-31712" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259515 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259515 1100 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=3-6\\operatorname{arctan}(2x-1).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259515 1113 #uuid "9aa198d8-6f8b-5468-8360-5a393ea89347" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259515 1145 "The formula for the derivative of arctangent is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}x)=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3-6\\operatorname{arctan}(2x-1)) \\\\  & =0-6\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}(2x-1)) \\\\  & =0-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{1+(2x-1)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-1) \\\\  & =0-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{1+(2x-1)^{2}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0-6\\cdot \\frac{2}{1+(2x-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{2+4{x}^{2}-4x} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{-1+2x-2{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259516 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259517 13194139533864 true] [17592186259516 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259516 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259516 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259517 13194139533864 true] [17592186259516 1110 #uuid "532c760f-464a-5f89-ac61-cb2ed49c7c44" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259517 1034 "\\frac{6}{-1+2x-2{x}^{2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259517 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259517 1111 #uuid "b5cee744-6510-55f6-b1d3-f81d48c58028" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259518 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259519 13194139533864 true] [17592186259518 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259521 13194139533864 true] [17592186259518 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259523 13194139533864 true] [17592186259518 1089 "q-318377" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259518 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259518 1100 "If $c=⟨4,2,6⟩$ and $d=⟨-1,5,5⟩$, then\n\n$c-d=⟨$\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}},\n{{field-3}}$⟩.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259518 1113 #uuid "709b00dd-0479-5892-a6bc-f752585376ff" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259518 1145 "To compute the difference, we subtract each component individually:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c-d & =⟨4,2,6⟩-⟨-1,5,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4-(-1),2-5,6-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,-3,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259519 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259520 13194139533864 true] [17592186259519 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259519 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259519 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259520 13194139533864 true] [17592186259519 1110 #uuid "33603e3b-595e-5033-8240-5f2bd5947655" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259520 1034 "5"