\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =(2-\\operatorname{tan}(x))^{'}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}(x)+(2-\\operatorname{tan}(x))\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}(x))^{'} \\\\  & =(-{sec}^{2}(x))(\\operatorname{cos}(x))+(2-\\operatorname{tan}(x))(-\\operatorname{sin}(x)) \\\\  & =-{sec}^{2}(x)\\operatorname{cos}(x)+(\\operatorname{tan}(x)-2)\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259526 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259527 13194139533864 true] [17592186259526 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259526 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259526 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259527 13194139533864 true] [17592186259526 1110 #uuid "c39ff20e-51e9-56a7-837f-d05eb8d71985" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259527 1034 "(\\operatorname{tan}(x)-2)\\operatorname{sin}(x)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259527 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259527 1111 #uuid "43184841-49d1-5070-bdf5-0a234bfec24b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259528 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259529 13194139533864 true] [17592186259528 1089 "q-319421" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259528 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259528 1100 "Given that $f(x)={5}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}(x),$ find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,{5}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}(x)$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259528 1113 #uuid "458f8052-b021-5a86-a091-16ca938dd05f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259528 1145 "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={5}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & ={({5}^{x})}^{'}\\operatorname{sin}(x)+{5}^{x}{(\\operatorname{sin}(x))}^{'} \\\\  & =({5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(5))\\operatorname{sin}(x)+{5}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}(x)) \\\\  & ={5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(5)\\operatorname{sin}(x)+{5}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259529 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259530 13194139533864 true] [17592186259529 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259529 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259529 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259530 13194139533864 true] [17592186259529 1110 #uuid "d9a3a4c7-b18f-5795-8484-c3728f71e3f5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259530 1034 "{5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(5)\\operatorname{sin}(x)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259530 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259530 1111 #uuid "352d9a06-5c86-5ad5-a31c-625b0ff74a53" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259531 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259532 13194139533864 true] [17592186259531 1089 "q-319487" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259531 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259531 1100 "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=3+8\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}(z)\\approx $\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259531 1113 #uuid "81300025-2eb5-531e-a8ef-a34d25e65f22" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259531 1145 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/fc3602d0d057902e1a510485ae8f02746afd80ea3bb7c2c8efc40ccfaf7b3dd4.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $0<\\operatorname{arg}(z)<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arg}(z)=arctan\\,(\\frac{8}{3})\\approx 1.21,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259532 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259533 13194139533864 true] [17592186259532 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259532 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259532 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259533 13194139533864 true] [17592186259532 1110 #uuid "f998fc59-ebd1-51ac-aad9-bf3636d66256" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259533 1034 "1.21" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259533 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259533 1111 #uuid "47c9d159-d38d-571e-8e23-96fca99598f8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259534 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259535 13194139533864 true] [17592186259534 1089 "q-319495" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259534 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259534 1100 "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=-7+5\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}(z)\\approx $\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259534 1113 #uuid "1a69aadf-c1cd-5b5a-91d9-c39dafc5090a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259534 1145 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/86e7a97f9dcdec8a81d5362c79f15887da8ce13248c4758eafa49e960d8cf128.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}(z)<\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}\\alpha  & =\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{5}{7}) \\\\  & =0.620\\text{ }249\\text{ }…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z)=\\pi -0.620\\text{ }249\\text{ }…\\approx 2.52,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259535 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259536 13194139533864 true] [17592186259535 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259535 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259535 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259536 13194139533864 true] [17592186259535 1110 #uuid "7a0f0d6c-b5f9-5ac1-8827-380546e14c22" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259536 1034 "2.52" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259536 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259536 1111 #uuid "58558700-309c-52c4-af9e-3aa3f5fd3339" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259537 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259538 13194139533864 true] [17592186259537 1089 "q-319554" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259537 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259537 1100 "Find, **in radians**, the **exact value** of the argument of $z=-6\\sqrt{3}-6\\text{i}.$\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}(z)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259537 1113 #uuid "730d6833-84c1-5541-960f-71582df5543b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259537 1145 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ea995ef688b12c8d58076fc832c9a5c735ff5933aff3ac6c347ae27850adbb2e.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\pi <\\operatorname{arg}(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}\\alpha  & =\\frac{6}{6\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\alpha  & =arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z)=\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259538 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259539 13194139533864 true] [17592186259538 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259538 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259538 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259539 13194139533864 true] [17592186259538 1110 #uuid "e44e66c8-5155-5882-9deb-8d3e1978fda2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259539 1034 "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259539 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259539 1111 #uuid "618ba24b-c462-52a8-adae-c106bb4b969a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259540 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259541 13194139533864 true] [17592186259540 1089 "q-319558" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259540 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259540 1100 "Find, **in radians**, the **exact value** of the argument of $z=-3-3\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}(z)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259540 1113 #uuid "9947af32-4016-5a60-b152-40626695bde1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259540 1145 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/f6fb8f3f901796a1bc6320148c9c58295d45fadd4f1e5379588e89072e153f77.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\pi <\\operatorname{arg}(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}\\alpha  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z)=\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259541 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259542 13194139533864 true] [17592186259541 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259541 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259541 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259542 13194139533864 true] [17592186259541 1110 #uuid "2df36b26-9c4b-5f96-adef-b96e9ea08ef9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259542 1034 "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259542 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259542 1111 #uuid "cb8a91ea-8688-5b3e-b0ad-e48bc15c385a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259543 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259544 13194139533864 true] [17592186259543 1089 "q-319825" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259543 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259543 1100 "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(x-1)\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=0$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259543 1113 #uuid "dd0e54ab-23dd-5fbf-99e4-afe94b73a3d3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259543 1145 "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=x-1$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{2}{2x+1}+\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-1)}{2x+1}+\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & =\\frac{2(0-1)}{2(0)+1}+\\operatorname{ln}(2(0)+1) \\\\  & =(-2)+\\operatorname{ln}(1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-2.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259544 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259545 13194139533864 true] [17592186259544 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259544 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259544 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259545 13194139533864 true] [17592186259544 1110 #uuid "8c4079ac-5969-5352-90bf-234b527c5450" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259545 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259545 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259545 1111 #uuid "48cf976f-c492-5116-a478-b5c3d499c7bf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259546 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259547 13194139533864 true] [17592186259546 1089 "q-319909" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259546 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259546 1100 "Given that $f(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{2x},$ find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=2x{e}^{2x}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259546 1113 #uuid "1572c639-23d1-5637-b129-4ba470408d68" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259546 1145 "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)={e}^{2x}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{'}{e}^{2x}+{x}^{2}({e}^{2x})^{'} \\\\  & =(2x)({e}^{2x})+({x}^{2})(2{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2x}+2{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259547 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259548 13194139533864 true] [17592186259547 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259547 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259547 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259548 13194139533864 true] [17592186259547 1110 #uuid "7dc5092f-6845-56f1-ac03-ff53f1048789" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259548 1034 "2{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259548 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259548 1111 #uuid "0c5ff6e9-0b3c-5118-9120-380862efdc1e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259549 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259550 13194139533864 true] [17592186259549 1089 "q-31993" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259549 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259549 1100 "Factor the expression ${x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{z}^{4}{w}^{4}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259549 1113 #uuid "6eebc99b-98a9-5a2d-94e8-405ae6440983" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259549 1145 "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{z}^{4}{w}^{4}=(xy)^{2}-{({z}^{2}{w}^{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n(xy)^{2}-{({z}^{2}{w}^{2})}^{2}=(xy+{z}^{2}{w}^{2})(xy-{z}^{2}{w}^{2})\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259550 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259551 13194139533864 true] [17592186259550 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259550 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259550 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259551 13194139533864 true] [17592186259550 1110 #uuid "ea6a0a19-5a42-53b6-99c0-e368bbf084df" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259551 1034 "(xy+{z}^{2}{w}^{2})(xy-{z}^{2}{w}^{2})" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259551 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259551 1111 #uuid "5d4af6ae-314f-5b48-82a0-bdbce8d91dc6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259552 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259553 13194139533864 true] [17592186259552 1089 "q-320077" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259552 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259552 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/07386bb0e42ae051fadc55f3949150582f31df36b28acee666c1e9583c5cbbf1.png)\n\nWhat complex number is represented in the Argand diagram above? Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259552 1113 #uuid "09b04830-3363-5cf1-8509-ae768a428aac" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259552 1145 "The coordinates of the point are $(0,-6).$\n\n- The real part of this complex number is $x=0,$ and\n\n- the imaginary part is $y=-6.$\n\nTherefore, the number depicted is $z=0-6i,$ which can be simplified to $z=-6i.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259553 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259554 13194139533864 true] [17592186259553 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259553 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259553 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259554 13194139533864 true] [17592186259553 1110 #uuid "4df4fbd9-c930-5449-b653-e838fec21002" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259554 1034 "-6i" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259554 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259554 1111 #uuid "95c00f40-1fa1-5066-bc6d-a527f0363ea4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259555 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259556 13194139533864 true] [17592186259555 1089 "q-320079" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259555 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259555 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/29ec09bdf2f2781a4f7eb