cted is $z=1-5i.$ Finally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =1-5i \\\\  & =-5\\,i+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259559 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259560 13194139533864 true] [17592186259559 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259559 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259559 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259560 13194139533864 true] [17592186259559 1110 #uuid "1fefbc6f-1f19-515b-82ae-260c313e48df" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259560 1034 "-5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259560 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259560 1111 #uuid "407fd3df-d3d3-5c46-a5b2-64c625a711a8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259561 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259562 13194139533864 true] [17592186259561 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259561 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259561 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259562 13194139533864 true] [17592186259561 1110 #uuid "294c3075-7bff-58e6-ac44-1e6f9aa1e66d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259562 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259562 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259562 1111 #uuid "69f31a85-7a99-5202-8211-d6e74c6cb923" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259563 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259564 13194139533864 true] [17592186259563 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259566 13194139533864 true] [17592186259563 1089 "q-320156" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259563 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259563 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/78f2b0b5784aee7237ad7a1912bf54f5371ec88fc504cee594618208f79b499b.png)\n\nExpress the complex number represented in the Argand diagram above in the form $bi+a,$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$i\\,-$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259563 1113 #uuid "f4c8480d-b5e8-5a54-886d-eb152592bdcc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259563 1145 "The coordinates of the point are $(-6,3).$\n\n- The real part of this complex number is $x=-6,$ and\n\n- the imaginary part is $y=3.$\n\nTherefore, the number depicted is $z=-6+3i.$ Finally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-6+3i \\\\  & =3\\,i-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259564 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259565 13194139533864 true] [17592186259564 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259564 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259564 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259565 13194139533864 true] [17592186259564 1110 #uuid "ea0da6aa-3703-5dc3-bd74-3a37024d0860" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259565 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259565 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259565 1111 #uuid "eadc3afc-965d-5497-a19e-5f6d336424a1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259566 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259567 13194139533864 true] [17592186259566 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259566 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259566 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259567 13194139533864 true] [17592186259566 1110 #uuid "af1ea0c3-ae93-594e-b961-0252edf69b5c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259567 1034 "6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259567 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259567 1111 #uuid "9664be6e-b39e-54ab-a099-49eb1d97c714" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259568 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259569 13194139533864 true] [17592186259568 1089 "q-320665" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259568 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259568 1100 "Expressed in degrees, $\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259568 1113 #uuid "e1e3d3bc-82fc-5fa2-90ce-eb77683f685b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259568 1145 "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values in the first quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}({45}^{∘})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259569 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259570 13194139533864 true] [17592186259569 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259569 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259569 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259570 13194139533864 true] [17592186259569 1110 #uuid "1afda0f2-c499-5fb4-a591-efc3a186f139" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259570 1034 "45" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259570 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259570 1111 #uuid "ab2ed601-4938-54fd-9c7a-c1bcd1067fb6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259571 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259572 13194139533864 true] [17592186259571 1089 "q-320723" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259571 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259571 1100 "Expressed in radians, $\\operatorname{arctan}(\\sqrt{3})=${{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259571 1113 #uuid "fadd708b-85a8-59ac-a2d8-87b9003aff34" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259571 1145 "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}x$ is given by:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\sqrt{3}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where the tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259572 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259573 13194139533864 true] [17592186259572 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259572 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259572 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259573 13194139533864 true] [17592186259572 1110 #uuid "67ffd3b3-5c41-5076-8346-8b9088e2acb2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259573 1034 "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259573 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259573 1111 #uuid "d056fb6f-7f25-5012-83fb-2a87bb1466d1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259574 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259575 13194139533864 true] [17592186259574 1089 "q-32102" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259574 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259574 1100 "$-4{y}^{2}+8y+12=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259574 1113 #uuid "0cb8daf7-0f19-5065-b105-2711fc30b94c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259574 1145 "First, notice that we can factor out a $(-4)$ from each of the terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{y}^{2}+8y+12 & =-4({y}^{2}-2y-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the polynomial ${y}^{2}-2y-3$ in parentheses.\n\nWe start by listing the factor pairs of the constant term $(-3).$ The constant term is negative, so that means one of the factors is negative and the other is positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & \\times (-3) \\\\ (-1) & \\times 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+(-3) & =-2 & ✓ \\\\ (-1)+3 & =2 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, we write the linear term as $-2y=y-3y$ and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4({y}^{2}-2y-3) & = \\\\ -4({y}^{2}+y-3y-3) & = \\\\ -4(({y}^{2}+y)+(-3y-3)) & = \\\\ -4(y(y+1)-3(y+1)) & = \\\\ -4(y-3)(y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259575 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259576 13194139533864 true] [17592186259575 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259575 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259575 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259576 13194139533864 true] [17592186259575 1110 #uuid "a22290af-e9be-5113-9bb3-919ee0137c97" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259576 1034 "-4(y-3)(y+1)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259576 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259576 1111 #uuid "38329df4-a6b6-5736-b50b-07bf8b589daa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259577 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259578 13194139533864 true] [17592186259577 1089 "q-321138" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259577 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259577 1100 "Expressed in degrees, $4\\operatorname{arctan}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259577 1113 #uuid "100a789f-1084-51be-ba86-3a2a27edfbe2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259577 1145 "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}<\\theta <{90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}(-{30}^{∘})=-\\operatorname{tan}({30}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $-{30}^{∘}$ lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\narctan\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=-{30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n4arctan\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=4\\cdot (-{30}^{∘})={-120}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259578 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259579 13194139533864 true] [17592186259578 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259578 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259578 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259579 13194139533864 true] [17592186259578 1110 #uuid "3181f7fa-572e-51e1-934e-8f5364b3af9c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259579 1034 "-120" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259579 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259579 1111 #uuid "04ac59b0-6e4f-5dce-ae60-9b5f04dd2348" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259580 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259581 13194139533864 true] [17592186259580 1089 "q-321647" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259580 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259580 1100 "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=25{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-x-5$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259580 1113 #uuid "6d0c7037-a792-5532-8a26-b8019a0ead40" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259580 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-5,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 5.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $25,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, $\\pm 5$, and $\\pm 25.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{5},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{25}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{5}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{5}{5},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{5}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{5},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{25}, \\\\ \\pm 5,\\text{ }\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{25},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{5},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 5.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $8$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 4=8.$)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259581 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259582 13194139533864 true] [17592186259581 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259581 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259581 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259582 13194139533864 true] [17592186259581 1110 #uuid "6be0c4e8-25bd-59ee-ae21-d0ce51fe84b8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259582 1034 "8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259582 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259582 1111 #uuid "427f79d7-8cbe-5fad-9c19-bda31862cb4a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259583 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259584 13194139533864 true] [17592186259583 1089 "q-322676" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259583 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259583 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/2f5f1a6c55e05048d5e62920fd49de1f55075905123a05908594b7915cb46b2e.png)\n\nFind the position vector of $U(-1,-3,0)$ in terms of $i$, $j$, and $k.$ Type $i,$ $j,$ and $k$ to represent the vectors $i,$ $j,$ and $k,$ respectively.\n\nThe position vector of $U$ is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259583 1113 #uuid "71ec538c-6e4e-5430-adc3-249373639151" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259583 1145 "Remember that in general, if a point $U$ has coordinates $({u}_{x},{u}_{y},{u}_{z}),$ then its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u={u}_{x}i+{u}_{y}j+{u}_{z}k.\n$$\n\nHere, the point $U$ has the coordinates $(-1,-3,0),$ so its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OU}=(-1)i+(-3)j+0k=-i-3j.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259584 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259585 13194139533864 true] [17592186259584 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259584 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259584 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259585 13194139533864 true] [17592186259584 1110 #uuid "6d881c1d-7f91-5c21-b1e1-8aa8814dfea8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259585 1034 "-i-3j+0k" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259585 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259585 1111 #uuid "952a2a84-1781-5ffd-936f-6f30ac5adcd6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259586 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259587 13194139533864 true] [17592186259586 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259589 13194139533864 true] [17592186259586 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259591 13194139533864 true] [17592186259586 1089 "q-322683" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259586 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259586 1100 "Write the vector $b=-7i+2j+5k$ in angle bracket notation.\n\n$b=⟨$\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}},\n{{field-3}}$⟩$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259586 1113 #uuid "e95cfe22-0303-57f5-8f01-714df12a8f83" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259586 1145 "Remember that in general, if a point $B$ has coordinates $({b}_{x},{b}_{y},{b}_{z}),$ then its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=⟨{b}_{x},{b}_{y},{b}_{z}⟩=[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{x} \\\\ {b}_{y} \\\\ {b}_{z}\\end{aligned}]={b}_{x}\\,i+{b}_{y}\\,j+{b}_{z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =-7i+2j+5k \\\\  & =-7\\cdot i+2\\cdot j+5\\cdot k \\\\  & =⟨-7,2,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259587 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259588 13194139533864 true] [17592186259587 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259587 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259587 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259588 13194139533864 true] [17592186259587 1110 #uuid "2e2b2d3d-dc10-57bb-b4eb-b441ed7bed32" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259588 1034 "-7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259588 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259588 1111 #uuid "be648c48-4047-584a-b794-e75cad2c3eac" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259589 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259590 13194139533864 true] [17592186259589 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259589 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259589 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259590 13194139533864 true] [17592186259589 1110 #uuid "8681383d-8705-5781-b760-7f881834df21" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259590 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259590 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259590 1111 #uuid "af0d2bc5-d722-511d-9c0b-71a05c581007" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259591 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259592 13194139533864 true] [17592186259591 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259591 1041 "field-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259591 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259592 13194139533864 true] [17592186259591 1110 #uuid "f25949b9-4354-5131-a1b6-3792ad7c0e6f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259592 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259592 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259592 1111 #uuid "4b38906a-c5ec-5e2c-aa82-e74377f9d2b7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259593 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259594 13194139533864 true] [17592186259593 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259596 13194139533864 true] [17592186259593 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259598 13194139533864 true] [17592186259593 1089 "q-323611" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259593 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259593 1100 "The polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-10x-24$ has one of its roots in the interval $(2,4).$ In **ascending order**, the distinct real roots of $f(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259593 1113 #uuid "4acf3abe-b1ce-5c44-b7f4-f5eee1633c9e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259593 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-24$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-24}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 8,\\,\\pm 12,\\,\\pm 24.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(2,4)$ is $3.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $3$ | $-10$ | $-24$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $18$ | $24$ |\n|  | $1$ | $6$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-3)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-3)({x}^{2}+6x+8).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-3)({x}^{2}+6x+8) \\\\  & =(x-3)(x+2)(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find that, in ascending order, the roots of the polynomial are $x=-4,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=3.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259594 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259595 13194139533864 true] [17592186259594 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259594 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259594 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259595 13194139533864 true] [17592186259594 1110 #uuid "aec7e4e7-60c7-5348-97e4-6b83eb41af42" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259595 1034 "-4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259595 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259595 1111 #uuid "052bb3bb-75e5-57ae-8dc4-4d7ca1d25494" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259596 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259597 13194139533864 true] [17592186259596 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259596 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259596 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259597 13194139533864 true] [17592186259596 1110 #uuid "26471634-7f58-5bbf-b440-69354b9bced1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259597 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259597 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259597 1111 #uuid "ef634c1b-9d3d-5d79-a276-a4667af5e630" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259598 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259599 13194139533864 true] [17592186259598 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259598 1041 "field-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259598 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259599 13194139533864 true] [17592186259598 1110 #uuid "ea9f6c1c-9b24-557e-ac8d-62dfc574cbe3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259599 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259599 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259599 1111 #uuid "98b3f5f4-4a66-5a51-91f1-dc474368396a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259600 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259601 13194139533864 true] [17592186259600 1089 "q-323830" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259600 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259600 1100 "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=27{x}^{3}-54{x}^{2}+36x-8$ has a root in the interval $(\\frac{1}{2},1),$ the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x)$ equals{{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259600 1113 #uuid "6dc510f3-7557-5168-b83b-b438937dc73f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259600 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-8$ and the leading coefficient is $27.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-8}{\\text{factor of }27}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\pm \\frac{1}{27},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{27},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{9},\\,\\pm \\frac{4}{27},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{9},\\,\\frac{8}{27},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{4}{9}, \\\\  & \\pm \\frac{2}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{8}{9},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{4}{3},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm \\frac{8}{3},\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ are $\\frac{2}{3}$ and $\\frac{8}{9}.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-\\frac{2}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $27$ | $-54$ | $36$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $18$ | $-24$ | $8$ |\n|  | $27$ | $-36$ | $12$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-\\frac{2}{3})(27{x}^{2}-36x+12).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-\\frac{2}{3})(27{x}^{2}-36x+12) \\\\  & =3(x-\\frac{2}{3})(9{x}^{2}-12x+4) \\\\  & =3(x-\\frac{2}{3})(3x-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(3x-2)(3x-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(3x-2)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only root of $f(x)$ is $x=\\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259601 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259602 13194139533864 true] [17592186259601 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259601 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259601 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259602 13194139533864 true] [17592186259601 1110 #uuid "233e0f41-f09e-5677-a93e-4488081bb7d2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259602 1034 "\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259602 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259602 1111 #uuid "263b2379-ca78-504d-b716-0871ff95c633" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259603 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259604 13194139533864 true] [17592186259603 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259606 13194139533864 true] [17592186259603 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259608 13194139533864 true] [17592186259603 1089 "q-326144" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259603 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259603 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/9e2575f2e20fb3146a92768eeedb32bed1bdb1ddb69f68b55c925a8f9898b695.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $-(x-5)(x+3)\\le 0.$\n\nThe solution to the inequality is $x\\le $\n{{field-1}}\nor $x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259603 1113 #uuid "a1abfe16-544e-536a-be8b-9d5556c70b80" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259603 1145 "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n-(x-5)(x+3)\\le 0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*less than or equal to*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-(x-5)(x+3)\n$$\n\nis *below* the $x$-axis or *touches* this axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/68d77d138842f95fbc055d5f8a0adda2bb7b04f8a133ce02d4813a54265e883b.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x\\le -3$ or $x\\ge 5.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259604 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259605 13194139533864 true] [17592186259604 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259604 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259604 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259605 13194139533864 true] [17592186259604 1110 #uuid "c50dbe77-d135-57d0-9cb9-7fb3abe07433" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259605 1034 "-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259605 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259605 1111 #uuid "9fe08400-ef5a-5e19-86dd-e40b5f494f94" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259607 13194139533864 true] [17592186259606 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259606 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259606 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259607 13194139533864 true] [17592186259606 1110 #uuid "7dba7302-3b20-5a28-be96-eec1f27df504" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259607 1034 "\\ge " 13194139533864 true] [17592186259607 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259607 1111 #uuid "d5ddc88e-e53a-5e20-bf3a-ef5c14a3bacd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259608 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259609 13194139533