4$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ \n\nSo, the solutions to the equation, in ascending order, are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,{x}_{3}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259669 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259670 13194139533864 true] [17592186259669 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259669 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259669 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259670 13194139533864 true] [17592186259669 1110 #uuid "d34873d1-7f49-5e2c-8a8f-c6f66824f2e9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259670 1034 "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259670 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259670 1111 #uuid "f5fcd305-9bf2-539d-a1e2-4403dfbee354" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259671 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259672 13194139533864 true] [17592186259671 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259671 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259671 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259672 13194139533864 true] [17592186259671 1110 #uuid "ac740bb2-2006-5d21-88a9-68fedeb52a80" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259672 1034 "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259672 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259672 1111 #uuid "6dd25731-8ff7-503c-b80c-82c9e8d0849a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259673 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259674 13194139533864 true] [17592186259673 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259673 1041 "field-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259673 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259674 13194139533864 true] [17592186259673 1110 #uuid "d80c21c5-b2dc-5132-8a5b-2e3a1fd62add" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259674 1034 "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259674 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259674 1111 #uuid "771c7e95-7848-525d-8ae7-8fefc48a65ae" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259675 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259676 13194139533864 true] [17592186259675 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259675 1041 "field-4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259675 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259676 13194139533864 true] [17592186259675 1110 #uuid "be1d2089-a2c6-5262-a8ea-40a8fb205738" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259676 1034 "\\frac{7\\pi }{4}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259676 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259676 1111 #uuid "b3ee9484-3b7b-5399-a1b0-d2cfb9b1eca9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259677 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259678 13194139533864 true] [17592186259677 1089 "q-328449" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259677 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259677 1100 "Consider the following trigonometric equation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}(4x)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{24}+\\frac{\\pi n}{2},\\,x=a\\pi +\\frac{\\pi n}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value of $a$ in the range $[0,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259677 1113 #uuid "3b14cf9c-c1ee-5a69-a0d8-213d7f9bcecc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259677 1145 "First, note that the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(4x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n4x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n,\\,4x=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n, & \\,4x & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{24}+\\frac{\\pi n}{2}, & \\,x & =\\frac{11\\pi }{24}+\\frac{\\pi n}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $a=\\frac{11}{24}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259678 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259679 13194139533864 true] [17592186259678 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259678 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259678 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259679 131941