 increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259794 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259795 13194139533864 true] [17592186259794 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259794 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259794 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259795 13194139533864 true] [17592186259794 1110 #uuid "bce0a9de-e9e7-5aa6-9164-a965d01e9c91" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259795 1034 "\\infty " 13194139533864 true] [17592186259795 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259795 1111 #uuid "11e9e953-973a-5e44-8737-3fb8a6082dca" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259796 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259797 13194139533864 true] [17592186259796 1089 "q-338361" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259796 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259796 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/14340a0938b61ef8c71a687bb280f601ade6aa062d6d4dd87d2786113692fac1.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(f(x)+2)^{4}}{(f(x)-5)^{3}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259796 1113 #uuid "4a0d1c8d-bddb-5da2-84fd-afcb22c27644" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259796 1145 "Using the power and quotient rules, we can rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(f(x)+2)^{4}}{(f(x)-5)^{3}} & =\\frac{{({lim}_{x\\to 0}(f(x)+2))}^{4}}{{({lim}_{x\\to 0}(f(x)-5))}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above is true provided that  ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ exists.\n\nWe find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=2.$ We calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(f(x)+2)=2+2=4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(f(x)-5)=2-5=-3\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(f(x)+2)^{4}}{(f(x)-5)^{3}} & =\\frac{{4}^{4}}{(-3)^{3}}=\\frac{256}{-27}=-\\frac{256}{27}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259797 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259798 13194139533864 true] [17592186259797 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259797 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259797 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259798 13194139533864 true] [17592186259797 1110 #uuid "a30acf33-8dde-533b-bd36-a66e5c640b6f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259798 1034 "-\\frac{256}{27}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259798 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259798 1111 #uuid "6a3feeeb-3691-52ea-bd09-ec47e1d1905a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259799 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259800 13194139533864 true] [17592186259799 1089 "q-338377" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259799 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259799 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/a086a1f616aee596a021ee4ef6bdc8723309cf8959719e9f55d6f20b845bd760.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of the function $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}+3)^{2}(5-f(x))^{3}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259799 1113 #uuid "399fc725-0978-537e-bb0c-8bab97a72cc3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259799 1145 "From the graph we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)=4.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}+3)^{2}(5-f(x))^{3}={lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}+3)^{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -2}(5-f(x))^{3}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}+3)^{2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}+3))}^{2} \\\\  & ={((-2)^{2}+3)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(4+3)}^{2} \\\\  & ={7}^{2} \\\\  & =49\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}(5-f(x))^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to -2}(5-f(x)))}^{3} \\\\  & ={(5-4)}^{3} \\\\  & ={1}^{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}+3)^{2}(5-f(x))^{3} & =49\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259800 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259801 13194139533864 true] [17592186259800 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259800 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259800 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259801 13194139533864 true] [17592186259800 1110 #uuid "38611adc-fcfc-57f3-a7f8-c0d50374f43d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259801 1034 "49" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259801 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259801 1111 #uuid "303d6888-a0f9-5d27-9bf3-15b38bc9ebd6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259802 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259803 13194139533864 true] [17592186259802 1089 "q-338382" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259802 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259802 1100 "Find the **exact** value of the limit.\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 25}{x}^{-1/2}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259802 1113 #uuid "58b9aff2-9496-5de6-9055-ee77ce1e5ffe" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259802 1145 "Applying the root rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 25}{x}^{-1/2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to 25}x)}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =(25)^{-1/2} \\\\  & ={({5}^{2})}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ={5}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259803 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259804 13194139533864 true] [17592186259803 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259803 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259803 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259804 13194139533864 true] [17592186259803 1110 #uuid "96e2c7e3-8338-5551-8028-99ce0a4060df" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259804 1034 "\\frac{1}{5}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259804 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259804 1111 #uuid "263f8f83-8f38-59a3-8479-d6660d0fddf4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259805 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259806 13194139533864 true] [17592186259805 1089 "q-338425" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259805 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259805 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/82d5db4c21b908542c17e872bb23db641cd4f66a30735b98f38ca2f3b0f545ea.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\sqrt{\\frac{(x+3)^{3}-f(x)\\,g(x)}{\\,2f(x)+g(x)\\,}}=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259805 1113 #uuid "8c44da89-1286-5f1f-9d2e-1cab9ebd03e6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259805 1145 "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=2$ and ${lim}_{x\\to 0}g(x)=3.$\n\nApplying the root and the quotient rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\sqrt{\\frac{(x+3)^{3}-f(x)\\,g(x)}{\\,2f(x)+g(x)\\,}} & =\\sqrt{\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(x+3)^{3}-f(x)\\,g(x)}{\\,2f(x)+g(x)\\,}\\,} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\,\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 0}((x+3)^{3}-f(x)\\,g(x))}{{lim}_{x\\to 0}(2f(x)+g(x))}\\,}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator separately. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}((x+3)^{3}-f(x)\\,g(x)) & =(0+3)^{3}-(2)(3) \\\\  & =27-6 \\\\  & =21,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}(2f(x)+g(x)) & =2(2)+3 \\\\  & =4+3 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\sqrt{\\frac{(x+3)^{3}-f(x)\\,g(x)}{\\,2f(x)+g(x)\\,}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{21}{7}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259806 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259807 13194139533864 true] [17592186259806 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259806 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259806 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259807 13194139533864 true] [17592186259806 1110 #uuid "440091bb-635f-505d-97b8-00c94a33ea82" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259807 1034 "\\sqrt{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259807 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259807 1111 #uuid "d3c5c7cc-3d68-5cc6-965f-05e885a91cb2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259808 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259809 13194139533864 true] [17592186259808 1089 "q-338440" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259808 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259808 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\pi /6}(\\operatorname{csc}(3x)+6x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259808 1113 #uuid "2ccc6a25-2db7-5706-96d9-17776fd59433" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259808 1145 "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ directly into the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /6}(\\operatorname{csc}(3x)+6x) & =\\operatorname{csc}(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})+6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}+\\pi  \\\\  & =1+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259809 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259810 13194139533864 true] [17592186259809 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259809 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259809 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259810 13194139533864 true] [17592186259809 1110 #uuid "247ff0b5-77b8-534b-9382-37f314bfbdc0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259810 1034 "1+\\pi " 13194139533864 true] [17592186259810 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259810 1111 #uuid "dbfe9c99-a15e-56d3-a022-b2e5b5db6c5c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259811 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259812 13194139533864 true] [17592186259811 1089 "q-338444" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259811 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259811 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\pi /3}csc\\,(\\frac{3x}{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259811 1113 #uuid "7dc64186-1928-5101-a754-746877780e27" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259811 1145 "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ directly into the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /3}csc\\,(\\frac{3x}{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259812 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259813 13194139533864 true] [17592186259812 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259812 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259812 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259813 13194139533864 true] [17592186259812 1110 #uuid "38935977-02b1-5ee5-979d-5436f0462ccf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259813 1034 "\\sqrt{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259813 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259813 1111 #uuid "098095c4-58e8-5d0b-b380-d18f864b68d5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259814 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259815 13194139533864 true] [17592186259814 1089 "q-338473" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259814 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259814 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x}-2}{x-2}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259814 1113 #uuid "c474b397-28c9-5a8f-b7cf-50951877499f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259814 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x}-2}{x-2} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2(2)}-2}{2-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x}-2}{x-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(\\sqrt{2x}-2)(\\sqrt{2x}+2)}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{2x}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{2x})}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{2x}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2x-4}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{2x}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2(x-2)}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{2x}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2\\cancel{(x-2)}}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(\\sqrt{2x}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2x}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2(2)}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259815 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259816 13194139533864 true] [17592186259815 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259815 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259815 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259816 13194139533864 true] [17592186259815 1110 #uuid "3bc45c87-8b02-5cbf-82c3-75765280b0a9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259816 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259816 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259816 1111 #uuid "c1ac8c16-0300-57ea-be9d-c169520d5e13" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259817 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259818 13194139533864 true] [17592186259817 1089 "q-338485" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259817 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259817 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3x+25}}{x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259817 1113 #uuid "5098a209-5e2a-59a9-840f-1d112ceb224b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259817 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3x+25}}{x} & =\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3(0)+25}}{0}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3x+25}}{x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(5-\\sqrt{3x+25})(5+\\sqrt{3x+25})}{x(5+\\sqrt{3x+25})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{3x+25})}^{2}}{x(5+\\sqrt{3x+25})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{25-(3x+25)}{x(5+\\sqrt{3x+25})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-3x}{x(5+\\sqrt{3x+25})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-3}{5+\\sqrt{3x+25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{5+\\sqrt{3(0)+25}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259818 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259819 13194139533864 true] [17592186259818 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259818 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259818 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259819 13194139533864 true] [17592186259818 1110 #uuid "76b70a58-b70f-5293-b8fb-3650a675967b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259819 1034 "-\\frac3{10}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259819 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259819 1111 #uuid "74d73477-f34f-5580-b9b7-c8a24304e0bf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259820 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259821 13194139533864 true] [17592186259820 1089 "q-338487" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259820 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259820 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{3x-2}-\\sqrt{x+2}}{x-2}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259820 1113 #uuid "aa14dfc7-bd15-596f-a1c8-753114c04592" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259820 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{3x-2}-\\sqrt{x+2}}{x-2} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3(2)-2}-\\sqrt{2+2}}{2-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{3x-2}-\\sqrt{x+2}}{x-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(\\sqrt{3x-2}-\\sqrt{x+2})(\\sqrt{3x-2}+\\sqrt{x+2})}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{3x-2}+\\sqrt{x+2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(\\sqrt{3x-2})^{2}-(\\sqrt{x+2})^{2}}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{3x-2}+\\sqrt{x+2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(3x-2)-(x+2)}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{3x-2}+\\sqrt{x+2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2x-4}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{3x-2}+\\sqrt{x+2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2(x-2)}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{3x-2}+\\sqrt{x+2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2\\cancel{(x-2)}}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(\\sqrt{3x-2}+\\sqrt{x+2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3x-2}+\\sqrt{x+2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3(2)-2}+\\sqrt{2+2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259821 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259822 13194139533864 true] [17592186259821 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259821 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259821 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259822 13194139533864 true] [17592186259821 1110 #uuid "d824f055-2a42-57ab-b57c-c2792c42c0d6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259822 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259822 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259822 1111 #uuid "a7a56ba9-d5c8-5edf-b051-a0fcda4a8487" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259823 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259824 13194139533864 true] [17592186259823 1089 "q-338517" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259823 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259823 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259823 1113 #uuid "7c248ff4-4847-5ae9-892d-0c9714b8f260" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259823 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=0$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{x+1}-2} & =\\frac{{3}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{3+1}-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{x+1}-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{({x}^{2}-9)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+1}-2)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+1})^{2}-{2}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{(x+1)-4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{x-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-3)}(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{\\cancel{x-3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\,(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2) \\\\  & =(3+3)(\\sqrt{3+1}+2) \\\\  & =6(4) \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259824 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259825 13194139533864 true] [17592186259824 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259824 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259824 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259825 13194139533864 true] [17592186259824 1110 #uuid "6b5640e3-e135-59a8-88e8-a44d496816e2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259825 1034 "24" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259825 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259825 1111 #uuid "6a3764b5-0eaf-586c-8cf3-8754e6fd6227" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259826 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259827 13194139533864 true] [17592186259826 1089 "q-338671" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259826 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259826 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}(2x){e}^{-3(x+2)})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259826 1113 #uuid "d11fc22e-27dc-5e16-bb71-552dd8da0121" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259826 1145 "Notice that we can rewrite the given expression as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}(2x){e}^{-3(x+2)}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3(x+2)}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{3(x+2)}≫\\operatorname{ln}(2x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3(x+2)}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}(2x){e}^{-3(x+2)}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3(x+2)}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259827 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259828 13194139533864 true] [17592186259827 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259827 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259827 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259828 13194139533864 true] [17592186259827 1110 #uuid "2e8ec972-c67d-5fea-a1b3-1aac198c5c4a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259828 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259828 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259828 1111 #uuid "578b2cf5-183e-5a1f-b8c5-5dba90fd4584" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259829 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259830 13194139533864 true] [17592186259829 1089 "q-338679" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259829 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259829 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3x}}-3)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259829 1113 #uuid "ae096ba8-7305-5c9b-9b5b-b942d8e27c2c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259829 1145 "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{3x}≫\\operatorname{ln}(2x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3x}}-3)=0-3=-3.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259830 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259831 13194139533864 true] [17592186259830 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259830 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259830 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259831 13194139533864 true] [17592186259830 1110 #uuid "9be028ea-240c-5f99-b73d-942fa2a9e307" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259831 1034 "-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259831 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259831 1111 #uuid "ea78c6f0-f62c-57c9-8f0a-9bae1cffea30" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259832 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259833 13194139533864 true] [17592186259832 1089 "q-339133" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259832 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259832 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}\\frac{25\\operatorname{arccos}(x)}{{x}^{2}+\\pi }=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259832 1113 #uuid "fab1224a-731f-5aa7-8ae2-ee992a17f981" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259832 1145 "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/85a182aabfe64c27eaafde94e3bdbede9409b02cfd87395cb2fc5543b09c4e05.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$ \n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}\\frac{25\\operatorname{arccos}(x)}{{x}^{2}+\\pi } & =\\frac{25\\operatorname{arccos}(1)}{{1}^{2}+\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{25\\cdot 0}{1+\\pi } \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259833 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259834 13194139533864 true] [17592186259833 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259833 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259833 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259834 13194139533864 true] [17592186259833 1110 #uuid "a4b9627a-e7d2-5f0e-a6ad-61c7f1aa6553" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259834 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259834 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259834 1111 #uuid "4772e15f-3158-59c7-83b6-3713f61f26b1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259835 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259836 13194139533864 true] [17592186259835 1089 "q-339377" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259835 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259835 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}(5-x))=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259835 1113 #uuid "130e1293-e299-5dab-8944-efcd214f6324" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259835 1145 "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}(5-x):$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ce2078de99b2c037b0ab6d5c5dcd4dab1e8aa7005417d87ca83176bf197fd5dc.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}(5-x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}(5-x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}(5-x))=\\frac{8}{\\pi }\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2})=4.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259836 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259837 13194139533864 true] [17592186259836 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259836 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259836 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259837 13194139533864 true] [17592186259836 1110 #uuid "6954fc0f-4cd6-5486-b67a-aa9af0bf66de" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259837 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259837 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259837 1111 #uuid "0c5cac94-0da9-5a00-a571-d2ac997c806a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259838 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259839 13194139533864 true] [17592186259838 1089 "q-339446" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259838 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259838 1100 "If $y=-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}},$ find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259838 1113 #uuid "e59a730f-20bd-5ba6-bc1c-41bc449c1743" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259838 1145 "We first rewrite the function as\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}}=-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-4/3}.\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the constant multiple and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-4/3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-4/3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}){x}^{(-\\frac{4}{3}-1)} \\\\  & =2{x}^{-7/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{7}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259839 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259840 13194139533864 true] [17592186259839 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259839 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259839 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259840 13194139533864 true] [17592186259839 1110 #uuid "99cdec2c-24ff-54e1-ae77-fb555b33d336" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259840 1034 "\\frac{2}{{x}^{7/3}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259840 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259840 1111 #uuid "abdbd32d-1c31-5654-8c16-551742440db5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259841 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259842 13194139533864 true] [17592186259841 1089 "q-339489" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259841 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259841 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}sin\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}(x))=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259841 1113 #uuid "286490e2-c616-59ab-b79b-2b35e220b7f8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259841 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/68f1ebd0457e0c127f8ed6c979feba58196aa6bb43d97d211efa9b39bc33f637.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}sin\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}(x)) & =sin\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}(1)) \\\\  & =sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259842 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259843 13194139533864 true] [17592186259842 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259842 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259842 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259843 13194139533864 true] [17592186259842 1110 #uuid "8617f523-d884-5387-8b52-b9a7de42f133" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259843 1034 "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259843 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259843 1111 #uuid "b4c23c77-cb0b-559b-8f14-991329134687" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259844 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259845 13194139533864 true] [17592186259844 1089 "q-339584" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259844 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259844 1100 "If $g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=3$ and $g(3)=16,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(4g(x)-3x\\sqrt{g(3)})=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259844 1113 #uuid "63cab282-4c4b-564d-875e-58dade96cb8f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259844 1145 "Since $g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}g(x)=g(3)=16.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(4g(x)-3x\\sqrt{g(3)}) & =4({lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}g(x))-({lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}3x)\\sqrt{g(3)} \\\\  & =4(16)-3(3)\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =64-9(4) \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259845 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259846 13194139533864 true] [17592186259845 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259845 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259845 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259846 13194139533864 true] [17592186259845 1110 #uuid "61daeea4-8a05-5ace-93c2-681e77dda30d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259846 1034 "28" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259846 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259846 1111 #uuid "b7f8b837-aa2a-5d9d-aa9b-832ee54e5b21" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259847 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259848 13194139533864 true] [17592186259847 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259850 13194139533864 true] [17592186259847 1089 "q-339731" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259847 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259847 1100 "Find the left- and right-sided limits at $x=\\pi $ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(x-\\pi +\\sqrt{3})^{2} & \\text{ for }\\,x<\\pi  \\\\ 5+2\\operatorname{cos}(x) & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge \\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,$\n${lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259847 1113 #uuid "77b4e96f-5072-5275-8cf8-c358fd213cd4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259847 1145 "Approaching from the left, we have $x<\\pi ,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=(x-\\pi +\\sqrt{3})^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}(x-\\pi +\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\  & =(\\pi -\\pi +\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x\\ge \\pi ,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=5+2\\operatorname{cos}(x)$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}(5+2\\operatorname{cos}(x)) \\\\  & =5+2\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi ) \\\\  & =5+2(-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=3.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259848 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259849 13194139533864 true] [17592186259848 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259848 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259848 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259849 13194139533864 true] [17592186259848 1110 #uuid "512c6bf0-66f2-5e2b-aebd-63701a637a3f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259849 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259849 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259849 1111 #uuid "7b881693-bfa3-5064-accc-6349d95b1535" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259850 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259851 13194139533864 true] [17592186259850 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259850 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259850 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259851 13194139533864 true] [17592186259850 1110 #uuid "db98dfee-cb1f-54a6-a984-ac6ce65a2605" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259851 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259851 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259851 1111 #uuid "3e8d4895-61e5-563e-9628-0b60ef993e8d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259852 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259853 13194139533864 true] [17592186259852 1089 "q-339827" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259852 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259852 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}{log}_{3}(x+7)\\cdot {log}_{3}(5-x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259852 1113 #uuid "e2bb04bd-6dcb-51fa-87c5-f2822ddf4961" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259852 1145 "The function ${log}_{3}(x+7)\\cdot {log}_{3}(5-x)$ is well defined for $x>-7$ and $x<5,$ and so to find this limit we substitute $x=2$ into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}{log}_{3}(x+7)\\cdot {log}_{3}(5-x) & ={log}_{3}(2+7)\\cdot {log}_{3}(5-2) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}(9)\\cdot {log}_{3}(3) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}({3}^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259853 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259854 13194139533864 true] [17592186259853 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259853 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259853 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259854 13194139533864 true] [17592186259853 1110 #uuid "b52ad5f2-2eaf-5cc7-b3fc-39152ab8f994" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259854 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259854 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259854 1111 #uuid "2e4ec5d3-d8f6-557d-baf7-74248b61e78e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259855 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259856 13194139533864 true] [17592186259855 1089 "q-339846" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259855 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259855 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{log}_{4}(-6x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259855 1113 #uuid "ef5eb0fa-9598-5322-bdda-f9890c3d2532" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259855 1145 "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{4}(-6x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/8a4a1bc79f9d096c3ebe2b4ff37e8682f74ab04583101dfe1d7f1d5f2e8df8c1.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{log}_{4}(-6x)=\\infty \n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259856 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259857 13194139533864 true] [17592186259856 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259856 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259856 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259857 13194139533864 true] [17592186259856 1110 #uuid "e7ff9c97-e39b-5817-82bb-d0588b34b4c4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259857 1034 "\\infty " 13194139533864 true] [17592186259857 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259857 1111 #uuid "fc0ecdff-bf89-5b51-9959-e731f64dd826" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259858 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259859 13194139533864 true] [17592186259858 1089 "q-339941" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259858 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259858 1100 "Given that $y=-11\\operatorname{ln}x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259858 1113 #uuid "21582e9d-02bc-5764-84aa-8f2d1392331f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259858 1145 "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-11\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =-11\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =-11\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259859 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259860 13194139533864 true] [17592186259859 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259859 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259859 1104 #edb/ref 175921