\\ {d}_{z}\\end{aligned}]={d}_{x}\\,i+{d}_{y}\\,j+{d}_{z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =4j-i \\\\  & =-1\\cdot i+4\\cdot j+0\\cdot k \\\\  & =⟨-1,4,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261193 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261194 13194139533864 true] [17592186261193 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261193 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261193 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261194 13194139533864 true] [17592186261193 1110 #uuid "c27a3a4d-2dfd-5b9f-b981-41b44b0e69c9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261194 1034 "⟨-1,4,0⟩" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261194 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261194 1111 #uuid "633662a1-2bf5-5033-a8f5-3ba3aa7fe838" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261195 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261196 13194139533864 true] [17592186261195 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261198 13194139533864 true] [17592186261195 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261200 13194139533864 true] [17592186261195 1089 "q-70442" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261195 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186261195 1100 "Let $w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$, $y=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $z=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].$ Find $2w-(3y+z).$\n\nRow 1, column 1: {{r1c1}}\n\nRow 2, column 1: {{r2c1}}\n\nRow 3, column 1: {{r3c1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261195 1113 #uuid "5490d51d-3d7e-50a1-bf73-5cc6f43b1ef0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261195 1145 "To compute $2w-(3y+z),$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2w-(3y+z) & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-(3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-([\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 19\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261196 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261197 13194139533864 true] [17592186261196 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261196 1041 "r1c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261196 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261197 13194139533864 true] [17592186261196 1110 #uuid "b7ae217c-f9a1-5468-8af7-29172a7dcf35" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261197 1034 "-11" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261197 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261197 1111 #uuid "edf3db8b-07fa-5a05-beeb-5039706e1aa5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261198 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261199 13194139533864 true] [17592186261198 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261198 1041 "r2c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261198 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261199 13194139533864 true] [17592186261198 1110 #uuid "f03b04df-385a-5a8d-8329-2848e81acc38" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261199 1034 "7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261199 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261199 1111 #uuid "768d8132-dcf3-5926-82f7-085f0e901f0b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261200 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261201 13194139533864 true] [17592186261200 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261200 1041 "r3c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261200 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261201 13194139533864 true] [17592186261200 1110 #uuid "85fc23fe-0c61-5bde-9cfe-866cea8c8bdd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261201 1034 "-15" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261201 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261201 1111 #uuid "7c336456-9f5e-57c0-a7c6-81bbad42329d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261202 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261203 13194139533864 true] [17592186261202 1089 "q-70475" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261202 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261202 1100 "If $y=-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}},$ find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261202 1113 #uuid "5a5cd98d-a47e-5b07-8017-c5a5ad2b0c53" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261202 1145 "We first rewrite the function as\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}=-4{x}^{-1/2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the constant multiple and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4{x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{(-\\frac{1}{2}-1)} \\\\  & =2{x}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261203 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261204 13194139533864 true] [17592186261203 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261203 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261203 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261204 13194139533864 true] [17592186261203 1110 #uuid "6adcd29c-072f-59c1-a4c3-39a5ce0aee67" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261204 1034 "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261204 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261204 1111 #uuid "9885997e-1376-5536-a6a0-9722cc7a54d9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261205 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261206 13194139533864 true] [17592186261205 1089 "q-70476" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261205 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261205 1100 "Find ${f}^{'}(x)$ for  $f(x)={x}^{2}+x-\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261205 1113 #uuid "c12fd860-5c97-5378-bf19-e5d7f29621d9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261205 1145 "The function $f(x)$ is the sum of three power functions. So, we apply the sum rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+x-\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =2{x}^{(2-1)}+1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{1/2-1} \\\\  & =2x+1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =2x-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261206 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261207 13194139533864 true] [17592186261206 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261206 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261206 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261207 13194139533864 true] [17592186261206 1110 #uuid "22767839-57a2-5cad-9008-32377584c282" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261207 1034 "2x-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261207 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261207 1111 #uuid "0c243bbe-7b07-54af-a078-d2e6d4c52f51" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261208 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261209 13194139533864 true] [17592186261208 1089 "q-70512" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261208 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261208 1100 "Find ${f}^{'}(x)$ for $f(x)=\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261208 1113 #uuid "0973d8c1-d7f9-53f6-b5f7-d47fb1ce4f0e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261208 1145 "The function $f(x)$ is the sum of two power functions. So, we apply the sum rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/5})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{(3/5-1)}-(-3){x}^{(-3-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{(-2/5)}+3{x}^{(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261209 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261210 13194139533864 true] [17592186261209 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261209 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261209 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261210 13194139533864 true] [17592186261209 1110 #uuid "7e259fee-48cb-5733-b70f-ccb1c60eec5a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261210 1034 "\\frac{3}{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261210 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261210 1111 #uuid "80975c65-406a-559f-90b2-a40ed489ca78" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261211 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261212 13194139533864 true] [17592186261211 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261214 13194139533864 true] [17592186261211 1089 "q-70517" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261211 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261211 1100 "Find where the tangent to the curve $y=6\\sqrt{x}+5x-13$ at the point $(1,-2)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261211 1113 #uuid "c3b4dcc1-a729-5d5a-b8a6-15ae89af7190" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261211 1145 "Let $f(x)=6\\sqrt{x}+5x-13.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\sqrt{x}+5x-13) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{1/2}+5x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{-1/2}+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{'}(1)=3(1)^{-1/2}+5=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =8(x-1) \\\\ y & =8x-8-2 \\\\ y & =8x-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $x$-axis, we set $y=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =8x-10 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(\\frac{5}{4},0).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261212 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261213 13194139533864 true] [17592186261212 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261212 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261212 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261213 13194139533864 true] [17592186261212 1110 #uuid "7b5d3fcd-4b36-5c0b-9ad9-b1f29e8bde90" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261213 1034 "\\frac{5}{4}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261213 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261213 1111 #uuid "3cc48cdd-92e1-5a5d-a827-52e3380a9ae4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261214 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261215 13194139533864 true] [17592186261214 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261214 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261214 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261215 13194139533864 true] [17592186261214 1110 #uuid "cfd7da7e-2de1-55bf-b7cb-7137ccd6f560" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261215 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261215 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261215 1111 #uuid "328e9600-c843-5b0c-adb1-c4f998420594" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261216 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261217 13194139533864 true] [17592186261216 1089 "q-70620" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261216 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261216 1100 "What values is **not** in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261216 1113 #uuid "f22b418c-8007-5edd-a5a3-d34d2762b1b9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261216 1145 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, the value $x=\\sqrt{5}$ is not in the domain of the function." 13194139533864 true] [17592186261217 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261218 13194139533864 true] [17592186261217 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261217 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261217 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261218 13194139533864 true] [17592186261217 1110 #uuid "bde52618-fa8a-5788-9d28-4d20315c20d3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261218 1034 "\\sqrt{5}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261218 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261218 1111 #uuid "bc44101a-ca73-5339-a70c-a2aa1a8bf5de" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261219 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261220 13194139533864 true] [17592186261219 1089 "q-70711" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261219 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186261219 1100 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x?$\n\nI. - $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\nII. - $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\nIII. - $(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261219 1113 #uuid "0a18db1d-9ef4-5e36-9b38-b2a2e715e9c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261219 1145 "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$  does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$  does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261220 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261221 13194139533864 true] [17592186261220 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261222 13194139533864 true] [17592186261220 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261223 13194139533864 true] [17592186261220 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261224 13194139533864 true] [17592186261220 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261225 13194139533864 true] [17592186261220 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261226 13194139533864 true] [17592186261220 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261227 13194139533864 true] [17592186261220 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261228 13194139533864 true] [17592186261220 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186261220 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261220 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261228 13194139533864 true] [17592186261220 1110 #uuid "ee2c0400-eed5-5e93-a306-0c2d0a11b751" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261221 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261221 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261221 1111 #uuid "7326080d-cb11-5bc9-9142-38cca1c83229" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261222 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261222 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261222 1111 #uuid "23e40e4a-95c6-5c09-b7ed-eb9094ecabc8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261223 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261223 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261223 1111 #uuid "0399991f-5d77-51c3-b1ad-67ea15f6c800" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261224 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261224 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261224 1111 #uuid "293ad995-8936-53c9-93c7-52ce3ebdf8de" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261225 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261225 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261225 1111 #uuid "08f3c9ef-dfbf-59a9-9a96-c2df57f540a6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261226 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261226 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261226 1111 #uuid "fe618c63-f465-591f-8993-cbc2ddea9f2a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261227 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261227 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261227 1111 #uuid "9622d96d-3ab7-5124-85d2-2533e66ade0a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261228 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261228 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261228 1111 #uuid "92f4f470-f57e-5b0f-b9c3-58b694ebfcb1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261229 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261230 13194139533864 true] [17592186261229 1089 "q-70739" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261229 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261229 1100 "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cot}\\theta +1}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261229 1113 #uuid "29105bf7-6b32-50a3-8ad8-587438d8f958" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261229 1145 "First, let's interpret the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cot}\\theta +1}=(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\operatorname{cot}\\theta +1)\n$$\n\nThen, we recall the identity $\\operatorname{cot}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ and we substitute it into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\operatorname{cot}\\theta +1) & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }+1) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta })\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we turn the division into multiplication, and simplify the expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }) & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )\\cdot (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cot}\\theta +1}=\\operatorname{sin}\\theta .$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261230 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261231 13194139533864 true] [17592186261230 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261230 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261230 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261231 13194139533864 true] [17592186261230 1110 #uuid "44f2ca43-4e7f-5e2f-a3a4-0f77cdd69032" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261231 1034 "\\operatorname{sin}\\theta" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261231 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261231 1111 #uuid "c0ab3692-46ee-5210-a11b-f062df958027" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261232 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261233 13194139533864 true] [17592186261232 1089 "q-70740" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261232 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261232 1100 "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{{cos}^{2}\\theta }{{cot}^{2}\\theta }.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261232 1113 #uuid "25f4b06a-33dc-5ef1-8e62-5a7ad79bbc27" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261232 1145 "First, let's interpret the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{cos}^{2}\\theta }{{cot}^{2}\\theta }={cos}^{2}\\theta ÷{cot}^{2}\\theta \n$$\n\nThen, we recall the identity $\\operatorname{cot}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ and we substitute it into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}\\theta ÷{cot}^{2}\\theta  & ={cos}^{2}\\theta ÷{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta })}^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}\\theta ÷\\frac{{cos}^{2}\\theta }{{sin}^{2}\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we turn the division into multiplication, and simplify the expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}\\theta ÷\\frac{{cos}^{2}\\theta }{{sin}^{2}\\theta } & ={cos}^{2}\\theta \\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{2}\\theta }{{cos}^{2}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}\\theta \\cdot {sin}^{2}\\theta }{{cos}^{2}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{cos}^{2}\\theta }\\cdot {sin}^{2}\\theta }{\\cancel{{cos}^{2}\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}\\theta }{1} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{{cos}^{2}\\theta }{{cot}^{2}\\theta }={sin}^{2}\\theta .$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261233 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261234 13194139533864 true] [17592186261233 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261233 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261233 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261234 13194139533864 true] [17592186261233 1110 #uuid "25292457-446b-546f-9797-fb7e537b5a5c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261234 1034 "{sin}^{2}\\theta" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261234 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261234 1111 #uuid "f773464b-a18d-5f67-9af6-ba5b79c5b405" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261235 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261236 13194139533864 true] [17592186261235 1089 "q-70744" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261235 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261235 1100 "What values is in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261235 1113 #uuid "e2c9898d-1220-5cf6-af81-cc3018702654" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261235 1145 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The domain of the function is $[-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $x=-\\frac{5}{7}$ is in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261236 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261237 13194139533864 true] [17592186261236 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261236 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261236 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261237 13194139533864 true] [17592186261236 1110 #uuid "3794aded-25f8-5635-b922-72b54ed6d48e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261237 1034 "-\\frac57" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261237 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261237 1111 #uuid "ef7b4e10-2cba-5e29-91f9-ea539ed6fa33" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261238 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261239 13194139533864 true] [17592186261238 1089 "q-70766" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261238 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186261238 1100 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x?$\n\nI. - $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{1}{2})$\nII. - $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\nIII. - $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261238 1113 #uuid "c5869734-9305-599d-bf1a-57e700c7e1e6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261238 1145 "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x,$ which is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{1}{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}x.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ does **not** lie within this range.   $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{2\\pi }{3},-\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261240 13194139533864 true] [17592186261239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261241 13194139533864 true] [17592186261239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261242 13194139533864 true] [17592186261239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261243 13194139533864 true] [17592186261239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261244 13194139533864 true] [17592186261239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261245 13194139533864 true] [17592186261239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261246 13194139533864 true] [17592186261239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261247 13194139533864 true] [17592186261239 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186261239 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261239 11