3194139533864 true] [17592186261275 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261280 13194139533864 true] [17592186261275 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261281 13194139533864 true] [17592186261275 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261282 13194139533864 true] [17592186261275 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261283 13194139533864 true] [17592186261275 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186261275 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261275 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261283 13194139533864 true] [17592186261275 1110 #uuid "6ca60986-ae01-560b-84be-531cbd4f220f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261276 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261276 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261276 1111 #uuid "e55f859a-b44a-521e-8f69-f6fc35f05d6a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261277 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261277 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261277 1111 #uuid "b1ae8032-4f08-58e6-be0b-5418814cf3ea" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261278 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261278 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261278 1111 #uuid "775af3b1-5061-5125-8b7c-b29e2ad7c0f3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261279 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261279 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261279 1111 #uuid "821db7a8-8f56-556d-9121-149c04644e3b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261280 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261280 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261280 1111 #uuid "038d8bbc-19ca-5980-b57c-1bfecd77321c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261281 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261281 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261281 1111 #uuid "4f77c179-e69a-59bd-9aee-7e02ce2890e9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261282 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261282 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261282 1111 #uuid "22358ea3-e645-529e-a108-3a8fc0a9a7c7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261283 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261283 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261283 1111 #uuid "7550839f-4794-5b19-970d-ea559dd47bd0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261284 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261285 13194139533864 true] [17592186261284 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261287 13194139533864 true] [17592186261284 1089 "q-70913" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261284 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261284 1100 "Solve $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6}-4=0.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261284 1113 #uuid "90b1010c-b5bd-524a-86d1-a7ed6794e479" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261284 1145 "First, we isolate the square root by adding $4$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6}-4 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6} & =4 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6})}^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+6 & =16 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x-10 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the results by plugging them into the original equation.\n\n- For $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-3(-2)+6}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{4+6+6}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{16}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 4-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 0 & =0.\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}-3(5)+6}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{25-15+6}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{16}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 4-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 0 & =0.\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth $x=-2$ and $x=5$ satisfy the original equation, so we can accept them both as solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6}-4=0$ are $x=-2$ and $x=5.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261285 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261286 13194139533864 true] [17592186261285 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261285 1041 "root-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261285 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261286 13194139533864 true] [17592186261285 1110 #uuid "bf3b6073-83d3-5a4b-be0c-891adaa49651" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261286 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261286 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261286 1111 #uuid "b547e8bd-ee5a-56dc-abe0-5665aab273f9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261287 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261288 13194139533864 true] [17592186261287 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261287 1041 "root-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261287 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261288 13194139533864 true] [17592186261287 1110 #uuid "6bf043e1-fb2b-52ea-83c7-e40ff55f2741" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261288 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261288 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261288 1111 #uuid "624dc58c-08a0-55d9-80aa-dc7d9874467a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261289 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261290 13194139533864 true] [17592186261289 1089 "q-70921" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261289 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186261289 1100 "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}+3x\\ge 0?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261289 1113 #uuid "2f8458e8-0bfe-5745-a2d8-3bb1285a95ef" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261289 1145 "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x(x+3) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $x(x+3)=0.$ The roots are $x=-3$ and $x=0,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-3,0),$ and $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,0)$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+3$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x(x+3)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(-)\\cdot (+)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $x(x+3)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $x(x+3)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and $(0,\\infty ).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-3$ and $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}+3x\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [0,\\infty ).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261290 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261291 13194139533864 true] [17592186261290 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261290 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261290 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261291 13194139533864 true] [17592186261290 1110 #uuid "5dde4dc9-69b0-5067-9339-80353b4c536a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261291 1034 "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [0,\\infty )" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261291 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261291 1111 #uuid "c47231ee-7d20-5dcb-abd4-da4f4bd0bf2b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261292 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261293 13194139533864 true] [17592186261292 1089 "q-70963" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261292 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186261292 1100 "For which values of $x$ is $3{x}^{2}-6x\\le 0?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261292 1113 #uuid "7ac8026b-ced5-5ad5-9d67-ebf801b0d418" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261292 1145 "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x & \\le 0 \\\\ 3x(x-2) & \\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $3x(x-2)=0.$ The roots are $x=0$ and $x=2,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,0),$ $(0,2),$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $3x(x-2)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $3x(x-2)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $3x(x-2)$ is zero or negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(0,2).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $3{x}^{2}-6x\\le 0$ for all $x\\in [0,2].$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261293 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261294 13194139533864 true] [17592186261293 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261293 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261293 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261294 13194139533864 true] [17592186261293 1110 #uuid "7418a723-891c-5517-b74d-7d070f5ede1a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261294 1034 "x\\in [0,2]" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261294 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261294 1111 #uuid "39836889-10e4-5f79-a236-f8a552a94d9a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261295 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261296 13194139533864 true] [17592186261295 1089 "q-7100" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261295 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261295 1100 "The position $x$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $x(t)=4{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}+2.$ Calculate the velocity at time $t.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261295 1113 #uuid "b6e1948c-c265-53e2-82a0-2a4aa4d00659" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261295 1145 "The velocity is the derivative of the position function $x(t)$ with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}+2) \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}+6t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261296 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261297 13194139533864 true] [17592186261296 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261296 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261296 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261297 13194139533864 true] [17592186261296 1110 #uuid "250a1794-1277-5991-bcf7-b3290ac17f67" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261297 1034 "12{t}^{2}+6t" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261297 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261297 1111 #uuid "ca362c70-f070-5294-8e05-486423f1857b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261298 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261299 13194139533864 true] [17592186261298 1089 "q-71004" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261298 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261298 1100 "Differentiate ${e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261298 1113 #uuid "568c0119-02eb-5eba-9b08-2993ed865711" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261298 1145 "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}x)^{'} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})^{'}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}x+{e}^{x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}x)^{'} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(\\operatorname{sin}x)+({e}^{x})(\\operatorname{cos}x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261299 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261300 13194139533864 true] [17592186261299 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261299 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261299 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261300 13194139533864 true] [17592186261299 1110 #uuid "cef9b926-d5f2-5fb4-a65b-8ba006c8884d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261300 1034 "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}x" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261300 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261300 1111 #uuid "56a4ae0f-dfdf-56d6-b1e1-d59041f87628" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261301 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261302 13194139533864 true] [17592186261301 1089 "q-71078" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261301 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186261301 1100 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=-2?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{x+2}, & x<-2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{x+2}, & x>-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261301 1113 #uuid "2d478bd3-531f-5aa0-9821-fc92dc36ae4e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261301 1145 "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- We have that $f(-2)$ is not defined since $x=-2$ does not belong to the domain of $f(x).$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-2$ because $f(-2)$ does not exist." 13194139533864 true] [17592186261302 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261303 13194139533864 true] [17592186261302 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261302 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261302 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261303 13194139533864 true] [17592186261302 1110 #uuid "638dc318-0d6f-5bbf-bc71-800beb29babc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261303 1034 "f(-2)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261303 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261303 1111 #uuid "bc8cac86-0068-5a86-ba00-bb10a2de71f7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261304 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261305 13194139533864 true] [17592186261304 1089 "q-71096" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261304 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261304 1100 "Find the antiderivative of ${x}^{5/2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261304 1113 #uuid "0bb15358-2958-5833-bc21-59e258e45d0f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261304 1145 "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{5}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{5/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{5/2+1}}{(\\frac{5}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/2}}{(\\frac{7}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7/2})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{7}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}{x}^{7/2-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{5/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261305 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261306 13194139533864 true] [17592186261305 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261305 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261305 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261306 13194139533864 true] [17592186261305 1110 #uuid "f2f4ab0e-0b43-5630-9289-d1fccbea8fda" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261306 1034 "\\frac{2x^{7/2}}7+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261306 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261306 1111 #uuid "f85d1a72-dfe3-57a1-8655-237f990633cc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261307 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261308 13194139533864 true] [17592186261307 1089 "q-71100" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261307 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261307 1100 "Given that $f(x)={x}^{-1/7}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261307 1113 #uuid "f981a017-b605-590e-89f0-b38299e2119e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261307 1145 "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{1}{7}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-1/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/7+1}}{(-\\frac{1}{7}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6/7}}{(\\frac{6}{7})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{6/7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261308 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261309 13194139533864 true] [17592186261308 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261308 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261308 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261309 13194139533864 true] [17592186261308 1110 #uuid "8c0ef2a8-2794-5110-aab9-ce6d64c890ed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261309 1034 "\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{6/7}+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261309 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261309 1111 #uuid "c900711a-441c-5cb7-8818-bdd5ba094f54" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261310 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261311 13194139533864 true] [17592186261310 1089 "q-71121" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261310 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261310 1100 "Calculate $\\int {x}^{10}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261310 1113 #uuid "557e5a3f-e4c2-5a77-ab22-ea3e93fa84fe" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261310 1145 "We apply the power rule with $n=10$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{10}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{10+1}}{10+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{11}}{11}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{11}}{11}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{11}}{11})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{11}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{11})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{11}\\cdot 11{x}^{10} & = \\\\ {x}^{10}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261311 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261312 13194139533864 true] [17592186261311 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261311 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261311 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261312 13194139533864 true] [17592186261311 1110 #uuid "c2a44a56-ff21-5733-a26e-db72bd908903" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261312 1034 "\\frac{x^{11}}{11}+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261312 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261312 1111 #uuid "0e7ebee3-4549-5ca6-afbd-5a9fdec9eef6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261313 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261314 13194139533864 true] [17592186261313 1089 "q-71163" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261313 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261313 1100 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{2{y}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}y.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261313 1113 #uuid "9b1354ad-3a99-5aa3-a7aa-bdaa08626a2b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261313 1145 "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{2{y}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {y}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{{y}^{-5+1}}{-5+1})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{{y}^{-4}}{-4})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {y}^{-4}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8{y}^{4}}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261314 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261315 13194139533864 true] [17592186261314 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261314 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261314 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261315 13194139533864 true] [17592186261314 1110 #uuid "600e1514-57e4-554d-b897-c21018fba413" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261315 1034 "-\\frac{1}{8{y}^{4}}+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261315 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261315 1111 #uuid "86c3d94b-9442-5e53-95b5-628d6cf14b35" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261316 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261317 13194139533864 true] [17592186261316 1089 "q-71169" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261316 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261316 1100 "Find the integral of $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}+4.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261316 1113 #uuid "06997fad-09a6-5bf2-96c9-5af17ff052ea" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261316 1145 "Applying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}+4\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3/2}}\\,\\text{d}x+4\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-3/2}\\text{d}x+4\\int {x}^{0}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3/2+1}}{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+4(\\frac{{x}^{0+1}}{0+1})+C \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {x}^{-1/2}+4x+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}}+4x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261317 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261318 13194139533864 true] [17592186261317 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261317 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261317 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261318 13194139533864 true] [17592186261317 1110 #uuid "56cd7ce6-6a32-5937-b6b1-83d51e53d834" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261318 1034 "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}}+4x+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261318 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261318 1111 #uuid "a9fa2866-1cbe-5677-9b08-d62b6d1533d2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261319 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261320 13194139533864 true] [17592186261319 1089 "q-71173" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261319 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261319 1100 "$\\int 2+\\frac{1}{{y}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}y=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261319 1113 #uuid "2bea4210-f300-55db-95a6-25d0828ce335" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261319 1145 "Applying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2+\\frac{1}{{y}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\int 1\\,\\text{d}y+\\int {y}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\int {y}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y+\\int {y}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2(\\frac{{y}^{0+1}}{0+1})+\\frac{{y}^{-5+1}}{-5+1}+C \\\\  & =2y+\\frac{{y}^{-4}}{-4}+C \\\\  & =2y-\\frac{1}{4{y}^{4}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261320 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261321 13194139533864 true] [17592186261320 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261320 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261320 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261321 13194139533864 true] [17592186261320 1110 #uuid "7f083c0c-3a24-5c45-8eb6-55af7fa99c63" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261321 1034 "2y-\\frac{1}{4{y}^{4}}+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261321 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261321 1111 #uuid "345426ba-1b4c-5b2a-a574-62be48ab1ba7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261322 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261323 13194139533864 true] [17592186261322 1089 "q-71210" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261322 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261322 1100 "Calculate $\\int (-8)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261322 1113 #uuid "b4e1d1ce-4716-5c64-92ae-716e4a21fbff" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261322 1145 "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-8)\\,\\text{d}x & =-8\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-8\\int {x}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-8\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =-8\\cdot x+C \\\\  & =-8x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $\\int k\\,\\text{d}x=kx+C.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261323 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261324 13194139533864 true] [17592186261323 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261323 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261323 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261324 13194139533864 true] [17592186261323 1110 #uuid "2cfd84ea-5f5d-5c42-b7cf-aa218b09592c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261324 1034 "-8x+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261324 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261324 1111 #uuid "439b80ae-8cdc-554f-8cdb-579fa8640aac" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261325 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261326 13194139533864 true] [17592186261325 1089 "q-71212" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261325 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261325 1100 "Calculate $\\int 5\\,\\text{d}y.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261325 1113 #uuid "645fe218-3e68-5d66-950f-2e8b292b2e2b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261325 1145 "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 5\\,\\text{d}y & =5\\int 1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =5\\int {y}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =5\\cdot y+C \\\\  & =5y+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $\\int k\\,\\text{d}x=kx+C.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261326 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261327 13194139533864 true] [17592186261326 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261326 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261326 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261327 13194139533864 true] [17592186261326 1110 #uuid "d8cee883-3490-5b76-8c3a-2be3e0ccc611" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261327 1034 "5y+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261327 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261327 1111 #uuid "efd9da01-7af2-5074-b679-26f1abe741df" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261328 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261329 13194139533864 true] [17592186261328 1089 "q-71479" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261328 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261328 1100 "Find ${y}^{'}$ for $y={log}_{4}(2-5x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261328 1113 #uuid "ed64a2ee-91db-50b9-a4fc-a489f5d8bfac" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261328 1145 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={log}_{4}(u),\\,u=2-5x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(4)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(4)}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(2-5x)\\operatorname{ln}(4)}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{(2-5x)\\operatorname{ln}(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261329 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261330 13194139533864 true] [17592186261329 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261329 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261329 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261330 13194139533864 true] [17592186261329 1110 #uuid "0da9e9e7-ca11-5407-98cc-8c52e843d744" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261330 1034 "-\\frac{5}{(2-5x)\\operatorname{ln}(4)}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261330 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261330 1111 #uuid "89530536-fdc4-5474-bbb0-62074f3fcdd4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261331 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261332 13194139533864 true] [17592186261331 1089 "q-71525" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261331 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261331 1100 "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y=2{x}^{3}-x+3$ at the point $(1,4).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261331 1113 #uuid "37f69159-8a56-5bc8-8f1b-0361b230c90b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261331 1145 "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-x+3.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-x+3)=6{x}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{'}(1)=6(1)-1=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{'}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261332 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261333 13194139533864 true] [17592186261332 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261332 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261332 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261333 13194139533864 true] [17592186261332 1110 #uuid "42e70d83-930f-5340-bd0c-7bd247ddc733" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261333 1034 "-\\frac{1}{5}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261333 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261333 1111 #uuid "32db2457-07c5-5427-8a92-9bd848a6b907" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261334 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261335 13194139533864 true] [17592186261334 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261337 13194139533864 true] [17592186261334 1089 "q-71542" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261334 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261334 1100 "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-4x+1$ at the point $(2,5)$ intersects the $y$-axis.\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261334 1113 #uuid "a51e04ce-0c90-5337-b907-f8c65ca8414a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261334 1145 "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}-4x+1)=6x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(2)=6(2)-4=8,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-5 & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x-2) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{8}x+\\frac{1}{4}+5 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{8}x+\\frac{21}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis by setting $x=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 0+\\frac{21}{4} \\\\ y & =\\frac{21}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $y$-axis at  $(0,\\frac{21}{4}).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261335 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261336 13194139533864 true] [17592186261335 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261335 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261335 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261336 13194139533864 true] [17592186261335 1110 #uuid "8baad589-7eab-517d-90c5-8bf0ac079d1a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261336 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261336 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261336 1111 #uuid "2e1eb68a-e252-5e78-a0ae-e38739c43457" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261337 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261338 13194139533864 true] [17592186261337 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261337 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261337 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261338 13194139533864 true] [17592186261337 1110 #uuid "d3e89ab2-4682-5c69-9be4-fd67450d1798" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261338 1034 "\\frac{21}{4}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261338 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261338 1111 #uuid "0d7aaa54-efcb-5abb-8a7e-be2121262538" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261339 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261340 13194139533864 true] [17592186261339 1089 "q-71653" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261339 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261339 1100 "$\\int 4\\operatorname{cos}y\\,\\text{d}y=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261339 1113 #uuid "521ab7c8-6b97-5cba-8f25-b1798a58600a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261339 1145 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{cos}y\\,\\text{d}y=\\operatorname{sin}y+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int 4\\operatorname{cos}y\\,\\text{d}y & =4\\int \\operatorname{cos}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{sin}y)+C \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}y+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261340 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261341 13194139533864 true] [17592186261340 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261340 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261340 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261341 13194139533864 true] [17592186261340 1110 #uuid "336c0ed8-8de0-55a9-b7e1-b43e5870c6fa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261341 1034 "4\\operatorname{sin}y+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261341 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261341 1111 #uuid "ee5f0c95-1a90-56ae-9c04-8adeda1cfda5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261342 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261343 13194139533864 true] [17592186261342 1089 "q-71654" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261342 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261342 1100 "$\\int (\\operatorname{sin}x+5\\operatorname{cos}x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261342 1113 #uuid "467397a0-c530-591f-93a4-1e57ee145f34" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261342 1145 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cos}x+C, \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{sin}x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\operatorname{sin}x+5\\operatorname{cos}x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}x\\,\\text{d}x+5\\int \\operatorname{cos}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}x+5\\operatorname{sin}x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261343 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261344 13194139533864 true] [17592186261343 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261343 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261343 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261344 13194139533864 true] [17592186261343 1110 #uuid "b01e36bc-c602-5d4c-9340-5382849b04f6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261344 1034 "-\\operatorname{cos}x+5\\operatorname{sin}x+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261344 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261344 1111 #uuid "b3d30e50-902e-5edd-8fcc-d0e2fb89ba16" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261345 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261346 13194139533864 true] [17592186261345 1089 "q-71657" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261345 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261345 1100 "$\\int (-8\\operatorname{csc}y\\,\\operatorname{cot}y)\\,\\text{d}y=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261345 1113 #uuid "4ebe3c4b-2395-5e47-9335-a17a764b723c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261345 1145 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{csc}y\\operatorname{cot}y\\,\\text{d}y & =-\\operatorname{csc}y+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-8\\operatorname{csc}y\\,\\operatorname{cot}y)\\,\\text{d}y & =-8\\int \\operatorname{csc}y\\operatorname{cot}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-8(-\\operatorname{csc}y)+C \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{csc}y+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261346 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261347 13194139533864 true] [17592186261346 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261346 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261346 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261347 13194139533864 true] [17592186261346 1110 #uuid "373caae1-73e6-56aa-9a6d-b3d6f8a4048b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261347 1034 "8\\operatorname{csc}y+C" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261347 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261347 1111 #uuid "ef49ed44-44d0-5c1b-89b7-571e8d29b5d3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261348 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261349 13194139533864 true] [17592186261348 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261351 13194139533864 true] [17592186261348 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261353 13194139533864 true] [17592186261348 1089 "q-71778" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261348 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261348 1100 "Solve the equation $5{z}^{3}-35{z}^{2}+50z=0.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}\n\nValue 3 (least to greatest): {{root-3}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261348 1113 #uuid "56a901dc-9638-58ff-af9a-f87cff2b0a14" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261348 1145 "First, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $5z.$ We can factor out the $5z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{z}^{3}-35{z}^{2}+50z & =0 \\\\ 5z({z}^{2}-7z+10) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression ${z}^{2}-7z+10$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5z({z}^{2}-7z+10) & =0 \\\\ 5z(z-2)(z-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $z=0,$ $z=2,$ and $z=5.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261349 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261350 13194139533864 true] [17592186261349 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261349 1041 "root-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261349 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261350 13194139533864 true] [17592186261349 1110 #uuid "aa901033-8b8a-5397-a5eb-b35c081d2689" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261350 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261350 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261350 1111 #uuid "adee0ecc-80f7-537c-884d-c7171e95523e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261351 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261352 13194139533864 true] [17592186261351 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261351 1041 "root-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261351 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261352 13194139533864 true] [17592186261351 1110 #uuid "2113cfb0-dcde-5210-8cf3-60a45e92f9f7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261352 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261352 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261352 1111 #uuid "d2bf68f7-8f17-5d48-a0c8-4753c3310bc5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261353 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261354 13194139533864 true] [17592186261353 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261353 1041 "root-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261353 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261354 13194139533864 true] [17592186261353 1110 #uuid "228f0d78-7b41-5269-8680-1df663ae7c7c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261354 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261354 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261354 1111 #uuid "1e5ee175-51be-52ac-916b-773e369a6a66" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261355 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261356 13194139533864 true] [17592186261355 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261358 13194139533864 true] [17592186261355 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261360 13194139533864 true] [17592186261355 1089 "q-71782" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261355 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186261355 1100 "Find the roots of the polynomial $p(t)=30{t}^{5}-95{t}^{4}-35{t}^{3}.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}\n\nValue 3 (least to greatest): {{root-3}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261355 1113 #uuid "bfbcf977-a1e8-5bf7-8080-66924ccaab82" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261355 1145 "To find the roots of the polynomial, we must solve the equation $p(t)=0,$ that is\n\n$$\n30{t}^{5}-95{t}^{4}-35{t}^{3}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $5{t}^{3}.$ We can factor out the $5{t}^{3}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{t}^{3}(6{t}^{2}-19t-7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $6{t}^{2}-19t-7$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{t}^{3}(6{t}^{2}-19t-7) & =0 \\\\ 5{t}^{3}(3t+1)(2t-7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $t=0,$ $t=-\\frac{1}{3},$ and $t=\\frac{7}{2}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261356 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261357 13194139533864 true] [17592186261356 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261356 1041 "root-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261356 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261357 13194139533864 true] [17592186261356 1110 #uuid "0efa319f-abe0-5aa0-ae2b-cc05e27ff7d6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261357 1034 "-\\frac13" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261357 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261357 1111 #uuid "9034ebe5-feef-5495-9442-cddbdee5f57b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261358 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261359 13194139533864 true] [17592186261358 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261358 1041 "root-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261358 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261359 13194139533864 true] [17592186261358 1110 #uuid "2eb521c9-4ece-56d2-afa4-907e8ab9f519" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261359 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261359 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261359 1111 #uuid "c928b2a7-f20b-58e4-9a77-0cc2ebee6b79" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261360 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261361 13194139533864 true] [17592186261360 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261360 1041 "root-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261360 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261361 13194139533864 true] [17592186261360 1110 #uuid "53ab5d41-7bee-52e8-8c68-50edcb4f474d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261361 1034 "\\frac72" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261361 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261361 1111 #uuid "141a2e77-c24b-5cfa-881d-5cdd835310a1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261362 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261363 13194139533864 true] [17592186261362 1089 "q-71881" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261362 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261362 1100 "Given that $y=2\\cdot {5}^{x+{x}^{3}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261362 1113 #uuid "6b813cb6-e4a2-5067-a95c-39437dc6fa6f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261362 1145 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=2\\cdot {5}^{u},\\,u=x+{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=2\\cdot {5}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}5,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1+3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {5}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}5\\cdot (1+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1+3{x}^{2})\\cdot 2\\cdot {5}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}5 \\\\  & =(2+6{x}^{2}){5}^{x+{x}^{3}}\\operatorname{ln}5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261363 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261364 13194139533864 true] [17592186261363 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261363 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261363 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261364 13194139533864 true] [17592186261363 1110 #uuid "f0afac87-5527-5858-9ed1-42c75fe593a3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261364 1034 "(2+6{x}^{2}){5}^{x+{x}^{3}}\\operatorname{ln}5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261364 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261364 1111 #uuid "f11eb26c-78e2-5d5f-99c1-28061148f43b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261365 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261366 13194139533864 true] [17592186261365 1089 "q-72064" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261365 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261365 1100 "The following table shows the number of customers per minute, $C(t),$ that arrive at a bank, measured over a period of $15$ minutes.\n\n| $t\\,\\text{(minutes)}$ | $0$ | $3$ | $7$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $C(t)\\,\\text{(customers/minute)}$ | $3$ | $4$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum over $[0,15]$ with four subintervals to approximate the total number of customers served.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261365 1113 #uuid "71771640-36aa-5365-9496-11f66f198e7d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261365 1145 "Let $A$ denote the area under the curve $y=C(t)$ over $[0,15].$ In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is approximated as \n\n$$\nA\\approx C(3)Δ{t}_{1}+C(7)Δ{t}_{2}+C(12)Δ{t}_{3}+C(15)Δ{t}_{4},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{t}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $t$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{t}_{i}={t}_{i}-{t}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δt$'s.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $3$ | $7$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δt$ | $-$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n| $C(t)$ | $-$ | $4$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nWe plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & \\approx 4\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 4+5\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12+12+25+6 \\\\  & =55.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total number of customers served over a period of $15$ minutes. So, the total number of customers is approximately\n$55.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261366 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261367 13194139533864 true] [17592186261366 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261366 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261366 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261367 13194139533864 true] [17592186261366 1110 #uuid "f22aac6e-74b5-5d2e-aa51-0c181d3d7ce6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261367 1034 "55" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261367 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261367 1111 #uuid "eee1d4ba-d69b-5847-a859-94521b94f113" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261368 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261369 13194139533864 true] [17592186261368 1089 "q-72091" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261368 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261368 1100 "A student's parents promise to reward her $R(p)$ dollars if she obtains an average of $p$ points in her exams. What are the units of $\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}p}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nWrite the units in the form \"[output units] per [input unit]\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186261368 1113 #uuid "dbe3b6c2-cec5-5e7c-9393-5197f7a69152" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261368 1145 "We have that the student's reward $R(p)$ is measured in dollars and the average exam result is measured in points. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(p): & \\text{dollars} \\\\ p: & \\text{points}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}p}$ measures a change in $R(p)$ over a change in $p.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}p}: & \\frac{\\text{dollars}}{\\text{points}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, $\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}p}$ is measured in \"dollars per point\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186261369 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261370 13194139533864 true] [17592186261369 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261369 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261369 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261370 13194139533864 true] [17592186261369 1110 #uuid "3810100f-eb13-58ea-9c35-58a116899722" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261370 1034 "Dollars per point" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261370 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261370 1111 #uuid "79cd8330-ab97-5903-b9f3-ef508c00087b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261371 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261372 13194139533864 true] [17592186261371 1089 "q-72109" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261371 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261371 1100 "Ramin, a bricklayer, is monitoring the construction of a wall by recording the following two numbers for different values of $t,$ where $t$ is the time, in hours, since he started his observations:\n\n- $B(t)$ - the number of bricks in the wall.\n\n- $r(t)$ - the rate at which the number of bricks in the wall is increasing.\n\nWhat is the relationship between $B(t)$ and $r(t)?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261371 1113 #uuid "aa386819-df2b-519d-bbb5-a033b05e50c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261371 1145 "The function $B(t)$ gives the number of bricks inside the wall after $t$ hours. Then,\n\n$$\n{B}^{'}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}B(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }B}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${B}^{'}(t)$ gives the rate at which the number of bricks increases at time $t$, which is the same as $r(t).$ Therefore, the relationship between $B(t)$ and $r(t)$ is ${B}^{'}(t)=r(t).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261372 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261373 13194139533864 true] [17592186261372 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261372 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261372 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261373 13194139533864 true] [17592186261372 1110 #uuid "44e0582e-f1b4-5db2-841e-f70823d9729b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261373 1034 "B'(t)=r(t)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261373 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261373 1111 #uuid "2198cbf6-3358-5668-b855-4d284e8f7fa4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261374 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261375 13194139533864 true] [17592186261374 1089 "q-72163" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261374 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261374 1100 "Find the derivative of $y=\\operatorname{sin}(\\operatorname{sec}x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261374 1113 #uuid "6f5156b8-2062-5adc-85c3-14a0aa909be9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261374 1145 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}u,\\,u=\\operatorname{sec}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{cos}u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}u\\cdot (\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\operatorname{sec}x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261375 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261376 13194139533864 true] [17592186261375 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261375 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261375 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261376 13194139533864 true] [17592186261375 1110 #uuid "4bfc3020-57b5-552b-ac6e-9d5c432ccce1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261376 1034 "\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\operatorname{sec}x)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261376 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261376 1111 #uuid "ac2fc9cc-427e-5a52-ad15-3495b432c99a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261377 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261378 13194139533864 true] [17592186261377 1089 "q-72170" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261377 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261377 1100 "Find the derivative of $y =\\csc ( \\cos x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261377 1113 #uuid "a4550e1e-4735-5154-97f7-9b71d5ccbc18" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261377 1145 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\ny = \\csc{u}, \\qquad u = \\cos{x}.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = -\\csc{u}\\cot u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d x} = -\\sin{x}.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=-\\csc u \\cot u \\cdot  (-\\sin x)\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\sin x\\csc \\left(\\cos x\\right) \\cot \\left(\\cos x\\right).\n\\end{align*}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261378 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261379 13194139533864 true] [17592186261378 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261378 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261378 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261379 13194139533864 true] [17592186261378 1110 #uuid "f2e9ac7c-62e8-59c9-ae79-fbe9ddb29443" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261379 1034 "\\sin x\\csc(\\cos x)\\cot(\\cos x)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261379 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261379 1111 #uuid "18a43d7e-2dd2-5da6-acab-0f0ff1c3bbfb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261380 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261381 13194139533864 true] [17592186261380 1089 "q-72243" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261380 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261380 1100 "How much money would you need to deposit today at a $10%$ annual interest rate, compounded yearly, to have $$40,000$ in the account after $20$ years? Round your answer to the nearest dollar.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261380 1113 #uuid "5c0c9df4-4d82-539b-8226-ce3e2837f6e4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261380 1145 "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded once per year, so we have $n=1.$ We also have an annual rate of $r=0.1,$ and a final amount of $P=40,000$ after $t=20$ years. To find the initial deposit, ${P}_{0},$ we substitute these values into the formula and solve for ${P}_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt} \\\\ 40,000 & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{0.1}{1})}^{1(20)} \\\\ 40,000 & ={P}_{0}(1.1)^{20} \\\\ 40,000 & ={P}_{0}(6.727…) \\\\ {P}_{0} & =\\frac{40,000}{6.727…} \\\\ {P}_{0} & \\approx 5,946\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, you would need to deposit $$5,946.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261381 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261382 13194139533864 true] [17592186261381 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261381 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261381 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261382 13194139533864 true] [17592186261381 1110 #uuid "aec202ef-5b97-5714-b8a8-fe4007cba02b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261382 1034 "5,946" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261382 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261382 1111 #uuid "5256f33b-ca55-58b1-9b5e-750d25ba09c7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261383 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261384 13194139533864 true] [17592186261383 1089 "q-72324" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261383 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261383 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}({x}^{2})}{{x}^{3}}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261383 1113 #uuid "65630b08-d1c1-5d45-aeb6-aa5a5348d31e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261383 1145 "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}({x}^{2})}{{x}^{3}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[1-\\operatorname{cos}({x}^{2})]^{'}}{[{x}^{3}]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x\\operatorname{sin}({x}^{2})}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}({x}^{2})}{x\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\cancel{x}\\operatorname{sin}({x}^{2})}{\\cancel{x}\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}({x}^{2})}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}({x}^{2})}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}({x}^{2})}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[\\operatorname{sin}({x}^{2})]^{'}}{[x]^{'}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x\\operatorname{cos}({x}^{2})}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{2(0)\\operatorname{cos}({0}^{2})}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261384 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261385 13194139533864 true] [17592186261384 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261384 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261384 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261385 13194139533864 true] [17592186261384 1110 #uuid "d1d352a2-c4df-56d8-8d15-407181e1e893" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261385 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261385 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261385 1111 #uuid "2829294d-bbef-5146-a8fc-e708419389ca" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261386 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261387 13194139533864 true] [17592186261386 1089 "q-72340" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261386 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261386 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261386 1113 #uuid "a4310b88-b219-51d1-b627-f9ad0abc215f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261386 1145 "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{csc}x=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{csc}x]}^{'}}{[{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}(-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\cancel{-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x}}{{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}\\cancel{(-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261387 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261388 13194139533864 true] [17592186261387 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261387 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261387 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261388 13194139533864 true] [17592186261387 1110 #uuid "aafa4e81-2f31-5079-8a06-e3131206cf76" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261388 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261388 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261388 1111 #uuid "16f7c0ae-c727-596d-b5e9-acd5ce0e0abf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261389 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261390 13194139533864 true] [17592186261389 1089 "q-72361" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261389 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261389 1100 "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are twice-differentiable on an open interval containing $x=1.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are given below:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{'}(x)$ | ${f}^{''}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{'}(x)$ | ${g}^{''}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261389 1113 #uuid "1b60af40-7b2a-56af-abba-7a8bf8f2cce4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261389 1145 "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=1$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x} & ={\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(1)+1}{g(1)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(f(x)+x)^{'}}{(g(x)-x)^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{'}(x)+1}{{g}^{'}(x)-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=1$ again, but once more, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{'}(x)+1}{{g}^{'}(x)-1} & ={\\frac{{f}^{'}(x)+1}{{g}^{'}(x)-1}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{f}^{'}(1)+1}{{g}^{'}(1)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply L'Hopital's rule again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{'}(x)+1}{{g}^{'}(x)-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{({f}^{'}(x)+1)^{'}}{({g}^{'}(x)-1)^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{''}(x)}{{g}^{''}(x)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this time, when we substitute $x=1,$ we are actually able to determine the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{''}(x)}{{g}^{''}(x)} & ={\\frac{{f}^{''}(x)}{{g}^{''}(x)}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{f}^{''}(1)}{{g}^{''}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{-2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x}=-2.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261390 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261391 13194139533864 true] [17592186261390 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261390 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261390 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261391 13194139533864 true] [17592186261390 1110 #uuid "3231fccf-4ac4-5ca8-8af9-24e84e8cd6c4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261391 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261391 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261391 1111 #uuid "ffe5f973-b330-576f-82fc-788f11da4e0b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261392 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261393 13194139533864 true] [17592186261392 1089 "q-72548" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261392 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261392 1100 "The lines $2y+10x=3$ and $y=px-3$ are perpendicular. The value of $p=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261392 1113 #uuid "0e3fd00d-e0e6-58dd-a905-2ad7d3579ca9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261392 1145 "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\n- The second line is in slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So the slope of the line $y=px-3$ is $p.$\n\n- To find the slope of  $2y+10x=3,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+10x & =3 \\\\ 2y & =-10x+3 \\\\ y & =-5x+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line $2y+10x=3$ is $-5.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n-5\\cdot p=-1\\,⟹\\,p=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261393 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261394 13194139533864 true] [17592186261393 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261393 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261393 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261394 13194139533864 true] [17592186261393 1110 #uuid "9d2fb2ba-1a63-50be-8a2f-06d6293b98a4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261394 1034 "\\frac{1}{5}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261394 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261394 1111 #uuid "f6489295-a743-5a1b-ba1c-3a1a21887946" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261395 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261396 13194139533864 true] [17592186261395 1089 "q-72754" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261395 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261395 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/38e096b8d57404135b7fe3244e50989e66021f94bb2275c56956d8663575b4f8.png)\n\nRounded to the nearest integer, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261395 1113 #uuid "8714b293-e1b5-58d0-b1e0-a6deda18c342" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261395 1145 "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ABD$ and $△BDC$ (see the illustration below).\n\nFirst we find the area for $△ABD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 8\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{85}^{∘} \\\\  & =24\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{85}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 23.908\\,7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $△BCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{BDC} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 14\\cdot 16\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{37}^{∘} \\\\  & =112\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{37}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 67.403\\,3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ABD}+{A}_{BDC} \\\\  & =23.908\\,7+67.403\\,3 \\\\  & =91.311\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest integer, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $91.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261396 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261397 13194139533864 true] [17592186261396 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261396 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261396 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261397 13194139533864 true] [17592186261396 1110 #uuid "6392d35b-90a6-526e-b49f-ded054099625" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261397 1034 "91" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261397 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261397 1111 #uuid "c97c84c3-c608-56b1-bf04-384879e31964" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261398 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261399 13194139533864 true] [17592186261398 1089 "q-73010" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261398 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261398 1100 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}-4x}{{x}^{2}-x-2}.$\n\n$x =$: {{x}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261398 1113 #uuid "87ee7e2c-2bbd-59c8-9b69-81310098b6e4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261398 1145 "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}-4x}{{x}^{2}-x-2} & =\\frac{2x(x-2)}{(x-2)(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\cancel{(x-2)}}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx+1=0\\,⟹\\,x=-1\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=-1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261399 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261400 13194139533864 true] [17592186261399 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261399 1041 "x" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261399 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261400 13194139533864 true] [17592186261399 1110 #uuid "7180fa9b-38c7-591a-b8ac-d09b14278b51" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261400 1034 "-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261400 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261400 1111 #uuid "09577c60-8054-58d0-be47-1caacaf6eec7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261401 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261402 13194139533864 true] [17592186261401 1089 "q-73017" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261401 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261401 1100 "Simplify $\\frac{9!\\text{ }5!}{7!\\text{ }6!}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261401 1113 #uuid "4b55051a-8f37-5733-840a-d51d5d26cfae" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261401 1145 "First, notice that $9!=9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7!$ and $6!=6\\cdot 5!\\,.$ Then, we can cancel the $7!$ and $5!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9!\\text{ }5!}{7!\\text{ }6!} & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7!\\cdot 5!}{7!\\cdot 6\\cdot 5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot \\cancel{7!}\\cdot \\cancel{5!}}{\\cancel{7!}\\cdot 6\\cdot \\cancel{5!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8}{6} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261402 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261403 13194139533864 true] [17592186261402 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261402 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261402 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261403 13194139533864 true] [1759218626140