#edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261842 1100 "Find the root of $f(x)=2{log}_{2}(x)-6.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261842 1113 #uuid "af0bbf2d-74bd-50a6-bf1f-aad032b60e21" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261842 1145 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n2{log}_{2}(x)-6=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{log}_{2}(x)-6 & =0 \\\\ 2{log}_{2}(x) & =6 \\\\ {log}_{2}(x) & =3 \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}(x)} & ={2}^{3} \\\\ x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=8.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261843 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261844 13194139533864 true] [17592186261843 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261843 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261843 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261844 13194139533864 true] [17592186261843 1110 #uuid "508e2a54-77a6-5f39-8819-5f2487fafdb2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261844 1034 "8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261844 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261844 1111 #uuid "69f64368-5b0a-5b99-8eaf-d411acf40c1b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261845 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261846 13194139533864 true] [17592186261845 1089 "q-82001" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261845 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261845 1100 "Find the root of $f(x)={log}_{5}(x-1)+1.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261845 1113 #uuid "116feb8e-6c77-562d-8f5b-52579754920b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261845 1145 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n{log}_{5}(x-1)+1=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}(x-1)+1 & =0 \\\\ {log}_{5}(x-1) & =-1 \\\\ {5}^{{log}_{5}(x-1)} & ={5}^{-1} \\\\ x-1 & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ x & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=\\frac{6}{5}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261846 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261847 13194139533864 true] [17592186261846 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261846 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261846 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261847 13194139533864 true] [17592186261846 1110 #uuid "5db17994-04e0-5902-9afc-c0a8af4f42ed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261847 1034 "\\frac{6}{5}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261847 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261847 1111 #uuid "279dcb68-2aa0-5ed9-b074-fd704ed393a0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261848 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261849 13194139533864 true] [17592186261848 1089 "q-82002" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261848 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261848 1100 "Find the root of $f(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}(2-x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261848 1113 #uuid "55eb1db9-fbcd-5c61-8a62-80c4dc797847" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261848 1145 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{ln}(2-x)=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{ln}(2-x) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}(2-x) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}(2-x)} & ={e}^{0} \\\\ 2-x & =1 \\\\ -x & =-1 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261849 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261850 13194139533864 true] [17592186261849 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261849 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261849 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261850 13194139533864 true] [17592186261849 1110 #uuid "3a5771f8-f3ac-5bd9-b0b9-18e96060513c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261850 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261850 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261850 1111 #uuid "0f457522-0e79-5d09-af53-68b18678b277" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261851 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261852 13194139533864 true] [17592186261851 1089 "q-82003" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261851 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186261851 1100 "Find the root of $f(x)=4{log}_{4}(-3x)+2.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261851 1113 #uuid "01cf8919-1e7e-525e-9677-8521240a49d2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261851 1145 "To find the root of $f(x)$, we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n4{log}_{4}(-3x)+2=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{log}_{4}(-3x)+2 & =0 \\\\ 4{log}_{4}(-3x) & =-2 \\\\ {log}_{4}(-3x) & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {4}^{{log}_{4}(-3x)} & ={4}^{-1/2} \\\\ -3x & =\\frac{1}{{4}^{1/2}} \\\\ -3x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=-\\frac{1}{6}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261852 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261853 13194139533864 true] [17592186261852 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261852 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261852 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261853 13194139533864 true] [17592186261852 1110 #uuid "b5897f55-943d-5708-b19a-e23e4b99c567" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261853 1034 "-\\frac{1}{6}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261853 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261853 1111 #uuid "906c9fff-edfb-53c0-a419-d46c09fde49c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261854 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261855 13194139533864 true] [17592186261854 1089 "q-82178" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261854 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261854 1100 "$\\operatorname{arcsin}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261854 1113 #uuid "9a7d0a24-b1bb-5537-9873-f6cb09e77afd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261854 1145 "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values on the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}({315}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, ${315}^{∘}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with ${315}^{∘}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261855 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261856 13194139533864 true] [17592186261855 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261855 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261855 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261856 13194139533864 true] [17592186261855 1110 #uuid "48550d3c-3939-5aca-a773-68ce37df37d5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261856 1034 "-{45}^{∘}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261856 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261856 1111 #uuid "9a2535e7-72ff-51a5-bd05-2314417eaa6e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261857 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261858 13194139533864 true] [17592186261857 1089 "q-83535" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261857 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186261857 1100 "Calculate the discriminant of the quadratic equation $2(x+2)^{2}-3=0.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261857 1113 #uuid "4b5fab31-45d0-51ce-8ef5-97f676669b2b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261857 1145 "First, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+2)^{2}-3 & =2({x}^{2}+4x+{2}^{2})-3 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+8x+8-3 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+8x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have $a=2,$ $b=8,$ and $c=5.$ So, we calculate the discriminant as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={8}^{2}-4(2)(5) \\\\  & =64-40 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261858 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261859 13194139533864 true] [17592186261858 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261858 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261858 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261859 13194139533864 true] [17592186261858 1110 #uuid "a5336a8b-4ba5-5097-900d-ae962c807fe8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261859 1034 "24" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261859 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261859 1111 #uuid "8d30ae83-830f-59b8-b0c6-ad96496b8c60" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261860 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261861 13194139533864 true] [17592186261860 1089 "q-83537" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261860 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261860 1100 "Consider the equation $-{x}^{2}-2x+7=0.$ How many distinct real solutions does it have?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261860 1113 #uuid "36f2b2bd-57ab-5e38-8592-4e4272a98493" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261860 1145 "To quickly determine the number of solutions, we can compute the discriminant. We have $a=-1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=7,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-2)^{2}-4(-1)(7) \\\\  & =4+28 \\\\  & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the discriminant is positive $(D>0),$ the equation has two distinct real solutions." 13194139533864 true] [17592186261861 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261862 13194139533864 true] [17592186261861 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261861 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261861 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261862 13194139533864 true] [17592186261861 1110 #uuid "be543a50-c5a0-5c72-b7ab-c5327fad3313" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261862 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261862 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261862 1111 #uuid "2d36712e-7eca-5b2f-a624-7f2244a6921f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261863 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261864 13194139533864 true] [17592186261863 1089 "q-83538" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261863 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261863 1100 "Consider the equation $-5{x}^{2}+2x-6=0.$ How many distinct real solutions does it have?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261863 1113 #uuid "57953432-f314-5c58-a78f-a4a5625bcfcb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261863 1145 "To quickly determine the number of solutions, we can compute the discriminant. We have $a=-5,$ $b=2,$ and $c=-6,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-4(-5)(-6) \\\\  & =4-120 \\\\  & =-116\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the discriminant is negative $(D<0),$ the equation has no real solutions." 13194139533864 true] [17592186261864 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261865 13194139533864 true] [17592186261864 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261864 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261864 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261865 13194139533864 true] [17592186261864 1110 #uuid "c51d8f76-2247-5bc3-9f3c-9466b39e3049" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261865 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261865 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261865 1111 #uuid "26273ade-5abc-59f8-b122-160e069e014e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261866 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261867 13194139533864 true] [17592186261866 1089 "q-83542" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261866 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261866 1100 "Consider the equation ${x}^{2}-4x+5=0.$ How many distinct real solutions does it have?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261866 1113 #uuid "8708ad07-e7c1-511e-ad81-a624c1913f98" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261866 1145 "To quickly determine the number of solutions, we can compute the discriminant. We have $a=1,$ $b=-4,$ and $c=5,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-4)^{2}-4(1)(5) \\\\  & =16-20 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the discriminant is negative $(D<0),$ the equation has no real solutions." 13194139533864 true] [17592186261867 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261868 13194139533864 true] [17592186261867 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261867 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261867 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261868 13194139533864 true] [17592186261867 1110 #uuid "9e953dff-9782-5a49-9384-c0b88716c9eb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261868 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261868 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261868 1111 #uuid "ae8c871a-c889-5bb8-a4e3-84cbe582680f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261869 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261870 13194139533864 true] [17592186261869 1089 "q-83561" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261869 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261869 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ca545b153cd04a539f30030c27a90dc4489b3a620e9a0d859bae5b531fcc7144.png)\n\nThe equation of the curve above is $y=\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-3x+k.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261869 1113 #uuid "fb5f2917-c324-5280-bf72-56385246a1bc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261869 1145 "Notice that if we substitute $x=0$ into the given equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-3x+k \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{2}(0)^{2}-3(0)+k \\\\ y & =k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nmeaning that the curve intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,k).$ Based on the graph, we see that we must have $k=\\frac{1}{2}$ since our parabola intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261870 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261871 13194139533864 true] [17592186261870 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261870 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261870 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261871 13194139533864 true] [17592186261870 1110 #uuid "320de538-91de-5b11-aaae-b12d0156cfe0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261871 1034 "\\frac12" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261871 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261871 1111 #uuid "af5acdf8-2660-557e-b016-ca2b92406387" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261872 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261873 13194139533864 true] [17592186261872 1089 "q-83593" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261872 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261872 1100 "Which of the following series are convergent?\n\nI. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4n}$\nII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}\\sqrt{n}}$\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{3{n}^{5}}$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261872 1113 #uuid "59b096bf-4af9-5e57-941d-caa731cefc46" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261872 1145 "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4n}.$ Rewriting this series gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4n}=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n},\n$$\n\n \nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=1$ (the harmonic series). Since $p\\le 1,$ the series is divergent.\n\n- For the second series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}\\sqrt{n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}\\cdot {n}^{1/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{2}.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the third series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{3{n}^{5}} & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=5.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261873 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261874 13194139533864 true] [17592186261873 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261875 13194139533864 true] [17592186261873 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261876 13194139533864 true] [17592186261873 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261877 13194139533864 true] [17592186261873 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261878 13194139533864 true] [17592186261873 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261879 13194139533864 true] [17592186261873 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261880 13194139533864 true] [17592186261873 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261881 13194139533864 true] [17592186261873 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186261873 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261873 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261875 13194139533864 true] [17592186261873 1110 #uuid "fa99ed83-7eac-5c53-a422-b00ad94c2731" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261874 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261874 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261874 1111 #uuid "6c79b06e-20b6-545a-bf3f-4ace475d5b58" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261875 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261875 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261875 1111 #uuid "8fabf757-29a7-5810-8ad6-bd173bea819e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261876 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261876 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261876 1111 #uuid "4c35feee-56b7-527a-9eee-d30c114b58ab" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261877 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261877 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261877 1111 #uuid "b3815365-f016-5d77-9ce7-512dc1e1c99f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261878 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261878 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261878 1111 #uuid "cd21ecf6-cc48-56ea-bc16-ba8db7b8c72a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261879 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261879 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261879 1111 #uuid "906d8a92-c585-5a67-8fad-0f5a15fe64dd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261880 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261880 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261880 1111 #uuid "d22f00a5-7b8e-54b3-958e-6974ad2ff725" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261881 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261881 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261881 1111 #uuid "ceb00b21-f4d3-516a-a25b-c63f9d245980" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261882 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261883 13194139533864 true] [17592186261882 1089 "q-83815" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261882 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261882 1100 "According to the rational roots theorem, What **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+x-3?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261882 1113 #uuid "a045cc6b-4cfa-5c4d-a695-16b0b2a1fa9a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261882 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-3$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-3}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $2$ does not take the above form. The value $2$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{2}{1},$ and the numerator $(2)$ is not a factor of $-3.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261883 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261884 13194139533864 true] [17592186261883 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261883 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261883 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261884 13194139533864 true] [17592186261883 1110 #uuid "f1aef993-e83d-5c8c-b93e-e98f5353e4cc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261884 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261884 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261884 1111 #uuid "30905c28-cb3e-546b-9dc8-5fce5d94c32e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261885 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261886 13194139533864 true] [17592186261885 1089 "q-83863" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261885 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261885 1100 "Given that the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-10x+8$ has one of its roots in the interval $(-5,-1),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261885 1113 #uuid "c03b7d9a-1305-5730-b73a-a062c9cbaa05" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261885 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $8$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }8}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 4,\\pm 8\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(-5,-1),$ are $-4$ and $-2.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+4)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-10$ | $8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-4$ | $12$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+4)({x}^{2}-3x+2) \\\\  & =(x+4)(x-1)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-4,1,2.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-4+1+2=-1.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261886 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261887 13194139533864 true] [17592186261886 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261886 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261886 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261887 13194139533864 true] [17592186261886 1110 #uuid "82ea7c62-6cec-5fea-9f67-89017e02bd87" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261887 1034 "-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261887 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261887 1111 #uuid "ec0a9244-0323-5c50-9ce3-be04162b7b0e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261888 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261889 13194139533864 true] [17592186261888 1089 "q-84333" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261888 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261888 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/5b3030b99501d2f3bc7dec80c4a1eab3754096c633be6d6d309064b084a8351d.png)\n\nIs the Venn diagram above correct?" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261888 1113 #uuid "ba0cd7e7-1913-5003-b315-15357e8d1f1f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261888 1145 "For any event $X,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X)+P({X}^{'})=1.\n$$\n\nThe Venn diagram tells us that $P(X)=\\frac{5}{8}$ and $P({X}^{'})=\\frac{3}{8}.$\n\nThese probabilities satisfy the rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X)+P({X}^{'}) & =\\frac{5}{8}+\\frac{3}{8} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the above Venn diagram is  correct." 13194139533864 true] [17592186261889 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261890 13194139533864 true] [17592186261889 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261891 13194139533864 true] [17592186261889 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186261889 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261889 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261891 13194139533864 true] [17592186261889 1110 #uuid "51cfe37c-18ab-5778-81da-b1dc936be0f7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261890 1034 "No" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261890 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261890 1111 #uuid "70b0e3a8-4d90-5091-bb3a-1b048582423e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261891 1034 "Yes" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261891 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261891 1111 #uuid "9b232213-17cc-5460-8d11-a63369663d07" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261892 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261893 13194139533864 true] [17592186261892 1089 "q-84428" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261892 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261892 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/f4928f6453f0f4d2e26dd99753e690de0b0266f63b541bf849c8fde5c13593d1.png)\n\nGiven the above sample space, find $P({X}^{'}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261892 1113 #uuid "e42f1d76-ec91-5e77-92f4-de1b8c4d9852" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261892 1145 "The Venn diagram tells us that $P(X)=\\frac{7}{13}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({X}^{'}) & =1-P(X) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{7}{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261893 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261894 13194139533864 true] [17592186261893 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261893 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261893 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261894 13194139533864 true] [17592186261893 1110 #uuid "2110b3f7-3f9a-50ba-ba22-2e0a49a6d572" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261894 1034 "\\frac{6}{13}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261894 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261894 1111 #uuid "1e1fbceb-950d-5d19-b414-6ec16db91287" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261895 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261896 13194139533864 true] [17592186261895 1089 "q-84431" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261895 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261895 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/a5e4261d260faf531e7a0eecc95eed379009f238b99f6424e598ac42c27e7c64.png)\n\nConsider the sample space $S$ with the Venn diagram given above. What is $P({Y}^{'})?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261895 1113 #uuid "eb776e32-2017-5cbf-b5c4-b276faf266d9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261895 1145 "From the given Venn diagram, we have $P(Y)=3p$ and $P({Y}^{'})=8p.$\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y)+P({Y}^{'}) & =1 \\\\ 3p+8p & =1 \\\\ 11p & =1 \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $P({Y}^{'})=8p=\\frac{8}{11}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261896 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261897 13194139533864 true] [17592186261896 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261896 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261896 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261897 13194139533864 true] [17592186261896 1110 #uuid "9f1ed6f6-03cf-5262-bad9-889b0d7a9848" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261897 1034 "\\frac{8}{11}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261897 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261897 1111 #uuid "57cdb069-ec17-5a57-807b-c34261e4751b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261898 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261899 13194139533864 true] [17592186261898 1089 "q-84489" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261898 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186261898 1100 "Two fair $6$-sided dice are rolled. Define $A$ to be the event \"the sum of outcomes is equal to $5.$\" Which of the following statements are true regarding this event?\n\nI. - $P(A)=0.15$ to $2$ decimal places\nII. - ${A}^{'}$ is the event \"the sum of the outcomes is less than $5$\"\nIII. - $P({A}^{'})=0.89$ to $2$ decimal places" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261898 1113 #uuid "bbd53da4-9111-5ade-a6dc-05cf5e290645" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261898 1145 "Let's inspect each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. There are $36$ possible outcomes and $4$ of these have the sum of the outcomes equal to $5,$ namely $(1,4),(4,1),(2,3),(3,2).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(A)=\\frac{4}{36}=0.11\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places.\n\n- Statement II is false. The complement of $A$ is the event ${A}^{'},$ \"the sum of the outcomes is not equal to $5.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since we know that $P(A)=0.11$ to $2$ decimal places, we can calculate $P({A}^{'})$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A)+P({A}^{'}) & =1 \\\\ P({A}^{'}) & =1-P(A) \\\\  & =1-0.11 \\\\  & =0.89.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261899 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261900 13194139533864 true] [17592186261899 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261901 13194139533864 true] [17592186261899 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261902 13194139533864 true] [17592186261899 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261903 13194139533864 true] [17592186261899 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261904 13194139533864 true] [17592186261899 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261905 13194139533864 true] [17592186261899 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261906 13194139533864 true] [17592186261899 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261907 13194139533864 true] [17592186261899 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186261899 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261899 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261904 13194139533864 true] [17592186261899 1110 #uuid "5749a1ac-dbc4-5b78-8fcc-ffe805a26b61" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261900 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261900 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261900 1111 #uuid "1812c78b-a55b-57c3-832c-8eaffa65f449" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261901 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261901 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261901 1111 #uuid "6de1e2ae-c9eb-5c1a-af4b-7ad5418ce020" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261902 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261902 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261902 1111 #uuid "dbb099e6-0299-5d14-85a1-e2f505b0f3c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261903 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261903 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261903 1111 #uuid "9429ffb0-d529-544d-8017-1e51bef97808" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261904 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261904 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261904 1111 #uuid "5ba7c15d-d3cb-59aa-9e04-f73793693b28" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261905 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261905 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261905 1111 #uuid "e82f6fdf-cd6a-518c-bf93-5bcfbd58572d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261906 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261906 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261906 1111 #uuid "f53f81a0-20c4-5d62-822d-d15e0208bb72" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261907 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261907 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261907 1111 #uuid "616f6e0e-482f-510b-b573-5c356d87d0f5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261908 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261909 13194139533864 true] [17592186261908 1089 "q-85485" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261908 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261908 1100 "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}\\pi .$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261908 1113 #uuid "813cae57-00fa-52d6-9195-91359bae21ec" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261908 1145 "The angle $\\pi $ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\pi $ corresponds to the point $(-1,0).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\\pi =0.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261909 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261910 13194139533864 true] [17592186261909 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261909 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261909 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261910 13194139533864 true] [17592186261909 1110 #uuid "2f9e6d61-b9c3-5e20-84ad-4f9374daaa4f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261910 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261910 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261910 1111 #uuid "9713ae01-932f-53c2-8604-d374f44821b4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261911 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261912 13194139533864 true] [17592186261911 1089 "q-85486" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261911 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261911 1100 "Find the value of $\\operatorname{tan}({90}^{∘}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf the value is undefined, enter \"Undefined\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186261911 1113 #uuid "aa9617ac-766d-5c56-b9e5-48a4a947fb5a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261911 1145 "The angle ${90}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${90}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}({90}^{∘})=0.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}({90}^{∘})=1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}({90}^{∘})=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}({90}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{cos}({90}^{∘})}=\\frac{1}{0}.\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $\\operatorname{tan}({90}^{∘})$ is undefined." 13194139533864 true] [17592186261912 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261913 13194139533864 true] [17592186261912 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261912 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261912 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261913 13194139533864 true] [17592186261912 1110 #uuid "dc95bd45-30c4-5602-8cc2-a03cbae23f68" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261913 1034 "Undefined" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261913 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261913 1111 #uuid "f1176aca-177b-56ab-8497-2f78a7b72522" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261914 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261915 13194139533864 true] [17592186261914 1089 "q-85492" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261914 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261914 1100 "Find the value of $\\operatorname{cot}({90}^{∘}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261914 1113 #uuid "b5e13a82-d333-5782-b422-ea795b8fd432" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261914 1145 "The angle ${90}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${90}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}({90}^{∘})=0.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}({90}^{∘})=1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}({90}^{∘})=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}({90}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{sin}({90}^{∘})}=\\frac{0}{1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cot}({90}^{∘})=0.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261915 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261916 13194139533864 true] [17592186261915 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261915 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261915 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261916 13194139533864 true] [17592186261915 1110 #uuid "319e1acb-cd6d-5be6-8a29-1e555e601ba7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261916 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261916 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261916 1111 #uuid "18c427b6-038d-52cc-af20-b4aa4105e7cd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261917 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261918 13194139533864 true] [17592186261917 1089 "q-85495" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261917 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261917 1100 "Find the value of $\\operatorname{csc}({180}^{∘}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf the value is undefined, enter \"Undefined\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186261917 1113 #uuid "2767b2a5-ba70-56dd-aba5-63a5e516b4eb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261917 1145 "The angle ${180}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${180}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-1,0).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}({180}^{∘})=0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}({180}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}({180}^{∘})}=\\frac{1}{0}.\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $\\operatorname{csc}({180}^{∘})$ is undefined." 13194139533864 true] [17592186261918 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261919 13194139533864 true] [17592186261918 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261918 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261918 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261919 13194139533864 true] [17592186261918 1110 #uuid "b96f27ba-03a4-5459-be66-b937edfcfc70" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261919 1034 "Undefined" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261919 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261919 1111 #uuid "b9928cb5-81c9-5428-bf72-d7e1de225401" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261920 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261921 13194139533864 true] [17592186261920 1089 "q-85512" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261920 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261920 1100 "$\\operatorname{cos}{315}^{∘}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261920 1113 #uuid "b24a2828-93bc-5001-b43c-5308101b5c0f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261920 1145 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${315}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}{315}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261921 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261922 13194139533864 true] [17592186261921 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261921 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261921 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261922 13194139533864 true] [17592186261921 1110 #uuid "2e943691-dc3d-5931-b30e-611e5ee306a0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261922 1034 "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261922 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261922 1111 #uuid "ca5e9700-95a0-5ed7-8467-2dc864b81fd7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261923 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261924 13194139533864 true] [17592186261923 1089 "q-85514" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261923 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261923 1100 "$\\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261923 1113 #uuid "dc09cb6b-cb50-55e8-8667-d714b407a48f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261923 1145 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${30}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261924 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261925 13194139533864 true] [17592186261924 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261924 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261924 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261925 13194139533864 true] [17592186261924 1110 #uuid "000dbb85-f45d-5ae1-a493-4f7966ffcde0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261925 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261925 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261925 1111 #uuid "434ec09f-ccb5-5c0a-8667-6647f9cb6dac" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261926 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261927 13194139533864 true] [17592186261926 1089 "q-85520" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261926 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261926 1100 "$\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261926 1113 #uuid "08338b04-151a-5dd0-bc82-2fbf3cfae2be" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261926 1145 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261927 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261928 13194139533864 true] [17592186261927 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261927 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261927 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261928 13194139533864 true] [17592186261927 1110 #uuid "30cecbaf-7da5-5351-8cf5-1768e9217234" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261928 1034 "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261928 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261928 1111 #uuid "61b94f71-1ca8-5de5-86fd-6c7c4a6f38d6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261929 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261930 13194139533864 true] [17592186261929 1089 "q-85524" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261929 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261929 1100 "$\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261929 1113 #uuid "1935b927-d13c-5e9e-9b00-d465d1f65ae2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261929 1145 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261930 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261931 13194139533864 true] [17592186261930 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261930 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261930 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261931 13194139533864 true] [17592186261930 1110 #uuid "eaf478b3-9853-5cce-bdfd-fc943ac3fb4c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261931 1034 "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261931 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261931 1111 #uuid "e1836a8d-7649-5404-8e59-93126da5fe65" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261932 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261933 13194139533864 true] [17592186261932 1089 "q-86424" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261932 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261932 1100 "Given an arithmetic sequence that has $f(2)=5$ and $f(4)=23$, what is the index of the term $158?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261932 1113 #uuid "2b2a5aa3-5b37-5fc1-a953-21f39ffa3a31" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261932 1145 "To construct the formula for the sequence, we need to know the common difference $d$ and the first term $f(1).$\n\nSince we are given $f(2)=5$ and $f(4)=23,$ we can find the common difference by dividing the total difference by the number of jumps:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{f(4)-f(2)}{4-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{23-5}{4-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{2} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(2)=5$ is $1$ jump after $f(1),$ we can determine the first term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =f(1)+d \\\\ 5 & =f(1)+9 \\\\ -4 & =f(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =f(1)+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-4+(n-1)(9) \\\\  & =-4+9n-9 \\\\  & =9n-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives $f(n)=158,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =9n-13 \\\\ 158 & =9n-13 \\\\ 171 & =9n \\\\ \\frac{171}{9} & =n \\\\ 19 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $158$ is the $19$th term of the sequence." 13194139533864 true] [17592186261933 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261934 13194139533864 true] [17592186261933 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261933 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261933 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261934 13194139533864 true] [17592186261933 1110 #uuid "4abfcb06-3ae1-5b51-bf96-dd3c3c9a2972" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261934 1034 "19" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261934 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261934 1111 #uuid "8683c5c9-de60-5c5a-b120-a61da703cf14" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261935 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261936 13194139533864 true] [17592186261935 1089 "q-86603" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261935 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186261935 1100 "Let $E$ and $F$ be the set of all non-negative integer powers of $2$ and the set of all non-negative integer powers of $4,$ respectively. What is $E\\cap F?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261935 1113 #uuid "015b9685-33e7-5fd9-9799-ab0be3a2b132" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261935 1145 "The powers of $2$ that are greater or equal to $1$ are\n\n$$\nE={1,2,4,8,16,32,64,…}.\n$$\n\nLikewise, the powers of $4$ that are greater or equal to $1$ are\n\n$$\nF={1,4,16,64,256,…}.\n$$\n\nNow, $E\\cap F$ is the *intersection* of $E$ and $F,$ which means that we want to find the elements that are in *both* $E$ and $F.$ In other words, $E\\cap F$ denotes the set of natural numbers that are both, power of $2$ and power of $4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E\\cap F & ={1,2,4,8,16,32,64,…}\\cap {1,4,16,64,256,…} \\\\  & ={1,4,16,64,256,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261936 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261937 13194139533864 true] [17592186261936 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261936 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261936 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261937 13194139533864 true] [17592186261936 1110 #uuid "78602547-bd2f-5af3-984f-439c6545f389" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261937 1034 "{1,4,16,64,256,…}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261937 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261937 1111 #uuid "24d47162-83cb-5794-9a84-e11aa5fdd070" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261938 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261939 13194139533864 true] [17592186261938 1089 "q-87562" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261938 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261938 1100 "Compute the variance ${\\sigma }^{2}$ of a data set ${x}_{i}$ consisting of $9$ data points, where\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}=27,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}^{2}=117.\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261938 1113 #uuid "7dfa7943-f9fd-587a-96e7-82a3ae160d16" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261938 1145 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=9$ points in our data set, and we know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}=27,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ We know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}^{2}=117,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 117 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =13-{(3)}^{2} \\\\  & =13-9 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261939 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261940 13194139533864 true] [17592186261939 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261939 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261939 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261940 13194139533864 true] [17592186261939 1110 #uuid "b5dbac9f-85f4-59c4-8a53-10bde3633c1d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261940 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261940 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261940 1111 #uuid "79aa2e40-f4d2-5709-8b5b-54790b86acf2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261941 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261942 13194139533864 true] [17592186261941 1089 "q-87792" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261941 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261941 1100 "Solve the inequality $3-2\\sqrt{2x+8}>-9.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261941 1113 #uuid "1d1da8f8-46b6-5144-a2e9-189ffbaad0c5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261941 1145 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-2\\sqrt{2x+8} & >-9 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{2x+8} & >-12 \\\\ \\sqrt{2x+8} & <6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(6).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{2x+8})}^{2} & <{6}^{2} \\\\ 2x+8 & <36 \\\\ 2x & <28 \\\\ x & <14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+8 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2x & \\ge -8 \\\\ x & \\ge -4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x<14,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,14).$ But the domain of the square root is $x\\ge -4,$ which can be expressed as $[-4,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,14)\\cap [-4,\\infty )=[-4,14)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [-4,14),$ which can also be written as $-4\\le x<14.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261942 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261943 13194139533864 true] [17592186261942 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261942 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261942 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261943 13194139533864 true] [17592186261942 1110 #uuid "6b03df9e-7683-5b3c-8544-60ac14d86293" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261943 1034 "-4\\le x<14" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261943 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261943 1111 #uuid "8f117fa8-c38a-5cf2-ab31-ccdda2449063" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261944 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261945 13194139533864 true] [17592186261944 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261947 13194139533864 true] [17592186261944 1089 "q-88205" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261944 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186261944 1100 "A stretch of factor $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and invariant $x$-axis is applied to the point $(5,-6).$  What is the resulting point?\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261944 1113 #uuid "76d5aa8d-6b7f-5a59-a437-5e21bbd91bff" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261944 1145 "Applying a stretch of factor $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and invariant $x$-axis to a point means that the $y$-coordinate of the point should be multiplied by $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nThe following function represents this transformation:\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(x,-\\frac{1}{2}y)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting point is\n\n$$\n(5,-6)↦(5,-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-6))=(5,3).\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/76787875dff29fa140881c6de2ee0d7c08baf394994e31246204d21bbcf61f56.png)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261945 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261946 13194139533864 true] [17592186261945 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261945 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261945 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261946 13194139533864 true] [17592186261945 1110 #uuid "a4523332-86c1-57a4-af85-58822a8ad619" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261946 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261946 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261946 1111 #uuid "58d8976a-8d6a-571f-aa3c-4dfb863d3bb8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261947 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261948 13194139533864 true] [17592186261947 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261947 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261947 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261948 13194139533864 true] [17592186261947 1110 #uuid "52a41c4b-0097-56b8-a60f-6552b9d773bb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261948 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261948 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261948 1111 #uuid "2fcfc92c-4860-5df6-b3a0-3c09ec63b05a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261949 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261950 13194139533864 true] [17592186261949 1089 "q-88472" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261949 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261949 1100 "What is the product of solutions of the equation $4{x}^{2}{e}^{5x}=3{e}^{5x}-4x{e}^{5x}?$\n\nProduct of solutions: {{product-of-solutions}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261949 1113 #uuid "f8148e6a-df0b-54f7-92fc-57c9f0d3a4be" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261949 1145 "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{e}^{5x} & =3{e}^{5x}-4x{e}^{5x} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}{e}^{5x}+4x{e}^{5x}-3{e}^{5x} & =0 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}+4x-3){e}^{5x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+4x-3=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{e}^{5x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $4{x}^{2}+4x-3=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2x+3)(2x-1)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The equation ${e}^{5x}=0$ has no solutions because ${e}^{5x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2},$ and their product is $(-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{3}{4}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261950 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261951 13194139533864 true] [17592186261950 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261950 1041 "product-of-solutions" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261950 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261951 13194139533864 true] [17592186261950 1110 #uuid "fb5b720f-0ca3-5e5c-b135-c481268d32f5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261951 1034 "-\\frac34" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261951 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261951 1111 #uuid "c3ddd15b-be62-51f7-b201-3ba924254ae8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261952 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261953 13194139533864 true] [17592186261952 1089 "q-889" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261952 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261952 1100 "Find $|z|$ if $z=2-3\\text{i}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261952 1113 #uuid "cad04cc4-23ac-5001-be61-d568b59eea8f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261952 1145 "For a complex number $z=a+b\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFor the complex number $z=2-3\\text{i},$ we have $a=2$ and $b=-3.$ So, the magnitude is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}|2-3\\text{i}| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261953 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261954 13194139533864 true] [17592186261953 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261953 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261953 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261954 13194139533864 true] [17592186261953 1110 #uuid "7eedf76b-5223-57f6-a350-c030b506cf6f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261954 1034 "\\sqrt{13}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261954 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261954 1111 #uuid "eae4b5e0-3d8f-5ac6-b3aa-338723cb1dce" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261955 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261956 13194139533864 true] [17592186261955 1089 "q-90479" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261955 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261955 1100 "Determine the range of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{log}(\\frac{x}{3}+10).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261955 1113 #uuid "c23102c7-5ee9-519d-905e-118a7274280e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261955 1145 "The range of the function $y=\\operatorname{log}(x)$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches do not affect the range of a logarithmic function.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261956 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261957 13194139533864 true] [17592186261956 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261956 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261956 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261957 13194139533864 true] [17592186261956 1110 #uuid "96e7fa94-8f08-581c-9159-570c035ef85b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261957 1034 "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261957 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261957 1111 #uuid "1665b6ec-0b54-54de-8466-b3054a562c7c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261958 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261959 13194139533864 true] [17592186261958 1089 "q-90483" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261958 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261958 1100 "Determine the range of the function $f(x)=4\\operatorname{log}x-3.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261958 1113 #uuid "52cac93d-d305-58e7-b23c-230f64ecb9ea" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261958 1145 "The range of the function $y=\\operatorname{log}(x)$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nVertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range of a logarithmic function.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261959 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261960 13194139533864 true] [17592186261959 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261959 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261959 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261960 13194139533864 true] [17592186261959 1110 #uuid "d66d470e-5d07-5453-b616-ca5666e7e4ba" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261960 1034 "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261960 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261960 1111 #uuid "7aefa8b5-74f7-52e4-8b87-c721a0191818" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261961 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261962 13194139533864 true] [17592186261961 1089 "q-90623" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261961 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261961 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/632da466eb26480490a49f9c5a71e0d6f95653efa749a8f117d81e016fd969ae.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $f(x)$ attains a **global** maximum at $x=4$\nII. - $f(x)$ attains a **global** minimum at $x=3$\nIII. - $f(x)$ attains a local maximum at $x=1$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261961 1113 #uuid "029317bc-fcf6-501c-9125-ecc74c217368" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261961 1145 "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function does *not* have a global maximum at $x=4.$ The global maximum is the highest point on the graph, but our function has points where its value is greater than $f(4).$ For example, $f(1)>f(4).$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function does *not* have a global minimum at $x=3.$ The global minimum is the lowest point on the graph, but our function has points where its value is smaller than $f(3).$ For example, $f(6)<f(3).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=1.$ For example, in the interval $(0.5,1.5),$ the greatest value of the function is attained at $x=1,$ as shown below.\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\le f(1)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (0.5,1.5).$ \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261962 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261963 13194139533864 true] [17592186261962 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261964 13194139533864 true] [17592186261962 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261965 13194139533864 true] [17592186261962 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261966 13194139533864 true] [17592186261962 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261967 13194139533864 true] [17592186261962 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261968 13194139533864 true] [17592186261962 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261969 13194139533864 true] [17592186261962 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261970 13194139533864 true] [17592186261962 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186261962 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261962 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261967 13194139533864 true] [17592186261962 1110 #uuid "1396d91b-da49-5297-9871-ffd0d3227bab" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261963 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261963 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261963 1111 #uuid "2ab74f05-3afc-5968-87f4-be4d044e94c5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261964 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261964 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261964 1111 #uuid "e6b21ac8-297f-5559-8890-905d5525ed71" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261965 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261965 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261965 1111 #uuid "2de2d434-323b-5bd0-88c3-4a9168bb5e3d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261966 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261966 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261966 1111 #uuid "e35d3b70-777c-576e-9426-bd095fa9b141" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261967 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261967 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261967 1111 #uuid "8f387637-8b17-53e2-9637-872e8c44aabf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261968 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261968 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261968 1111 #uuid "8b88f3ab-2919-5f84-8aa0-b48c86a90793" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261969 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261969 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261969 1111 #uuid "57b09224-30af-51ef-b156-ee01bb809d0a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261970 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261970 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186261970 1111 #uuid "6be2d733-af51-531c-a6ae-fb3b8f9f6ff2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261971 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261972 13194139533864 true] [17592186261971 1089 "q-914" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261971 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261971 1100 "If $\\operatorname{log}y=3$, then $y=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261971 1113 #uuid "3f443508-b423-5aae-b226-2edd43a6873e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261971 1145 "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the common logarithm is $10,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}y & =3 \\\\ {log}_{10}y & =3 \\\\ {10}^{{log}_{10}y} & ={10}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${10}^{{log}_{10}y}=y.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={10}^{3} \\\\ y & =1\\,000.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261972 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261973 13194139533864 true] [17592186261972 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261972 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261972 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261973 13194139533864 true] [17592186261972 1110 #uuid "37ba8d5d-39aa-537d-aaed-64ef93471dd2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261973 1034 "1\\,000" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261973 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261973 1111 #uuid "64a61867-194c-5c5b-8079-5ef39b6bec38" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261974 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261975 13194139533864 true] [17592186261974 1089 "q-924" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261974 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261974 1100 "${\\text{i}}^{33}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261974 1113 #uuid "c4b2df1e-6d40-59ba-a881-5e43b1cc0734" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261974 1145 "To find the value of $\\text{i}$ raised to some positive integer power $n$, we divide $n$ by $4,$ and raise $\\text{i}$ to the remainder.\n\nIn our case, we have $n=33.$ Dividing the power by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n33÷4=8\\,\\text{R}1.\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{i}}^{33}={\\text{i}}^{1}=\\text{i}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261975 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261976 13194139533864 true] [17592186261975 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261975 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261975 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261976 13194139533864 true] [17592186261975 1110 #uuid "1ff4dbc0-536a-591e-8ea0-df48c5f297f8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261976 1034 "\\text{i}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261976 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261976 1111 #uuid "58f4302b-9b17-599a-a78a-d26ae23b33b8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261977 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261978 13194139533864 true] [17592186261977 1089 "q-9293" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261977 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186261977 1100 "If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}=0.5,\\,$ ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}=9$ and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{c}_{n}=-2,$ then calculate ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{b}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{c}_{n}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261977 1113 #uuid "770c1ff4-046b-5d4d-9ce0-c706d161310b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261977 1145 "Taking the constant factors outside of the summation signs gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{b}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{c}_{n} & =3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}+8{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{c}_{n} \\\\  & =3(0.5)+2(9)+8(-2) \\\\  & =1.5+18-16 \\\\  & =3.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261978 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261979 13194139533864 true] [17592186261978 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186261978 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261978 1104 #edb/ref 17592186261979 13194139533864 true] [17592186261978 1110 #uuid "078bc752-e359-55d9-ac87-b5b28ffbedb8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261979 1034 "3.5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261979 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186261979 1111 #uuid "441dd12d-ad06-54c0-b50d-8bafad595eac" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261980 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261981 13194139533864 true] [17592186261980 1089 "q-92957" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261980 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186261980 1100 "Suppose that $f(x)=4({5}^{-x}).$ Given that $f(x)\\to a$ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ then $a=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261980 1113 #uuid "5ed91a52-4c22-5f53-9a5a-98369b57e065" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261980 1145 "Let's plot the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/4af39ad263d5fdeb3bf0a48e7306b24863fe464619218f84b4f053fdf6af3090.png)\n\nSince the function approaches the $x$-axis as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=0.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186261981 1000 #edb/ref 17592186261982 13194139533864 true] [17592186261981 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 