t-order initial value problem?\n\nI. - $3{x}^{2}{y}^{'}={y}^{2},\\,y(0)=1$\nII. - $\\frac{{y}^{'}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y,\\,y(1)=2$\nIII. - ${y}^{'}-x\\sqrt{y}={e}^{x}$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262093 1113 #uuid "f324e71f-0765-5434-b640-0e2263e787be" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262093 1145 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{'}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $3{x}^{2}{y}^{'}={y}^{2}$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\frac{{y}^{'}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=2.$\n\n- System III  is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262094 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262095 13194139533864 true] [17592186262094 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262096 13194139533864 true] [17592186262094 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262097 13194139533864 true] [17592186262094 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262098 13194139533864 true] [17592186262094 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262099 13194139533864 true] [17592186262094 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262100 13194139533864 true] [17592186262094 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262101 13194139533864 true] [17592186262094 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262102 13194139533864 true] [17592186262094 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186262094 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262094 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262100 13194139533864 true] [17592186262094 1110 #uuid "36b80bee-db64-535d-aace-058b279199ff" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262095 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262095 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186262095 1111 #uuid "491c18dc-e9d5-5a2b-88f5-a3bc86a065ea" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262096 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262096 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186262096 1111 #uuid "6cee0851-d168-5175-af4b-4e9ec6593fd0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262097 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262097 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186262097 1111 #uuid "e20c0aff-74cc-552d-96eb-eef3a81e96ec" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262098 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262098 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186262098 1111 #uuid "f0377b83-e245-52ea-80a0-298ddfe36899" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262099 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262099 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186262099 1111 #uuid "c6bcccdb-85b9-52d9-b7ca-5d988b9f7fb5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262100 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262100 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186262100 1111 #uuid "070e48b3-a56e-5c40-9343-941d4858140e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262101 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262101 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186262101 1111 #uuid "40d7f3bf-ff28-581c-96a7-f1b98edbccd3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262102 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262102 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186262102 1111 #uuid "f1223c20-84d2-5fb5-bf8a-545a08f3d710" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262103 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262104 13194139533864 true] [17592186262103 1089 "q-9755" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262103 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186262103 1100 "The geometric series $-1+2-4+8-16+32\\,$ can be expressed using sigma notation as\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262103 1113 #uuid "b1790c68-9a6b-5d0b-85dc-5ef421b499cd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262103 1145 "First, we must express the given series in the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, ${a}_{1}=-1$ and $N=6.$\n\nTo find $r,$ we calculate the ratio of the first two terms in the series:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2\n$$\n\nHence, expressing the geometric series given in sigma notation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n-1+2-4+8-16+32={\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}(-1)\\cdot (-2)^{n-1}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nFinally, we can simplify the above using the laws of exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}(-1)\\cdot (-2)^{n-1} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}(-1)\\cdot (-2)^{n}\\cdot (-2)^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}{2}^{-1}\\cdot (-2)^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{2}(-2)^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262104 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262105 13194139533864 true] [17592186262104 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262104 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262104 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262105 13194139533864 true] [17592186262104 1110 #uuid "fd78fcee-b881-529b-8713-eee28822801e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262105 1034 "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{2}(-2)^{n}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262105 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186262105 1111 #uuid "e6d39e35-0e22-5cda-8e8a-0f38f7efee86" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262106 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262107 13194139533864 true] [17592186262106 1089 "q-9760" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262106 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186262106 1100 "Write the geometric series $1+4+16+⋯+4\\,096$ using sigma notation.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that $4\\,096={4}^{6}.$*\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262106 1113 #uuid "2509ffde-6c9d-5b43-bb99-17cd7228a183" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262106 1145 "We must express the given series in the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, ${a}_{1}=1.$\n\nTo find $r,$ we find the ratio of the consecutive terms ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{4}{1}=4\n$$\n\nNow we must determine $N$ (the index of the last term). To do this, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n{a}_{N}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{N-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{N-1} \\\\ 4\\,096 & ={4}^{N-1} \\\\ {4}^{6} & ={4}^{N-1} \\\\ 6 & =N-1 \\\\ N & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing the geometric series in sigma notation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+4+16+⋯+4\\,096 & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}1\\cdot {4}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}{4}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262107 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262108 13194139533864 true] [17592186262107 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262107 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262107 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262108 13194139533864 true] [17592186262107 1110 #uuid "d564bbb1-493d-52a4-b0b6-eeeb3e75f95f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262108 1034 "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}{4}^{n-1}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262108 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186262108 1111 #uuid "1e068698-f473-5474-92fe-df8f66a8ecc1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262109 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262110 13194139533864 true] [17592186262109 1089 "q-97980" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262109 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186262109 1100 "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{1}{3}x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=-1\n$$\n\n \n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as\n\n$$\nx=b\\pi +6n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. What could be the value of $b?$\n\n$b =$: {{b}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262109 1113 #uuid "70cc7789-9f93-5c83-af83-334149cbeb2c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262109 1145 "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x-\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+6n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $b=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262110 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262111 13194139533864 true] [17592186262110 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262110 1041 "b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262110 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262111 13194139533864 true] [17592186262110 1110 #uuid "ec1a2131-1d28-5ffa-8e30-33f1d353ae90" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262111 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262111 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186262111 1111 #uuid "7ec7bfd3-8f80-5b87-aa75-1ec8afd1b0ec" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262112 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262113 13194139533864 true] [17592186262112 1089 "q-98" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262112 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186262112 1100 "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{12}(23)$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262112 1113 #uuid "4bb0c190-0bc7-5c24-bdb9-2ae5232df4b8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262112 1145 "Let's calculate ${log}_{12}(23)$ by changing the base $12$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=12,$ $X=23,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{12}(23) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}(23)}{{log}_{10}(12)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(23)}{\\operatorname{log}(12)} \\\\  & \\approx \\frac{1.361\\,728}{1.079\\,181} \\\\  & \\approx 1.262\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262113 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262114 13194139533864 true] [17592186262113 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262113 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262113 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262114 13194139533864 true] [17592186262113 1110 #uuid "546450ba-f0fc-5ac8-a380-753b82559f9e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262114 1034 "1.262" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262114 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186262114 1111 #uuid "6310d092-8863-5a48-bb29-8e40b3e3e2f2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262115 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262116 13194139533864 true] [17592186262115 1089 "q-98030" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262115 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186262115 1100 "Consider the following trigonometric equation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}(x-{20}^{∘})=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation is\n\n$$\nx={50}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n,\\,x={a}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. What could be the value of $a?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262115 1113 #uuid "579b5dc7-11ff-5357-9fa6-16fce5adbb8f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262115 1145 "First, note that the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(x-{20}^{∘})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\nx-{20}^{∘}={30}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n,\\,x-{20}^{∘}={330}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x={20}^{∘}+{30}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n, & \\,x={20}^{∘}+{330}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ x={50}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n, & \\,x={350}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $a=350.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262116 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262117 13194139533864 true] [17592186262116 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262116 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262116 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262117 13194139533864 true] [17592186262116 1110 #uuid "c5bb9771-544b-5e26-b5f1-fe053e541d83" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262117 1034 "350" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262117 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186262117 1111 #uuid "c1013782-3d79-51a2-9739-3f2e166b9f97" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262118 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262119 13194139533864 true] [17592186262118 1089 "q-98152" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262118 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186262118 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/4b445c27e9b4b720cfcc8b8b265485ba4f62106be39acc066cf041bf9db073b2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the graph of $y=k{log}_{3}(bx).$ Find the value of $b+k.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262118 1113 #uuid "6efc25c1-7280-5213-84a4-9dd424baaae7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262118 1145 "Clearly, $b\\ne 0.$ Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{b}$ into the equation of the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k{log}_{3}(b\\cdot \\frac{1}{b}) \\\\  & =k{log}_{3}1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $(\\frac{1}{b},0)$ lies on the curve. But from the diagram, we know that $(\\frac{1}{3},0)$ lies on the curve. Hence\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{3},0)=(\\frac{1}{b},0)\\,\\Rightarrow \\,b=3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute coordinates of the point $(1,\\frac{1}{2})$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{log}_{3}(bx) & =y \\\\ k{log}_{3}(3x) & =y \\\\ k{log}_{3}(3\\cdot 1) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ k{log}_{3}3 & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nb+k=3+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262119 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262120 13194139533864 true] [17592186262119 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262119 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262119 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262120 13194139533864 true] [17592186262119 1110 #uuid "c66636a2-9208-5170-b91e-f795c24d0ed5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262120 1034 "\\frac{7}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262120 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186262120 1111 #uuid "6382687d-7aa3-5933-9ee6-b7bb4a90620f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262121 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262122 13194139533864 true] [17592186262121 1089 "q-98153" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262121 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186262121 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/60d178be10126417bfbfad4e7a316d43ad1fd94ca19ea72adae929500a7f13c2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the graph of $y=k\\operatorname{ln}(x)+c.$ Find the value of $k+c.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262121 1113 #uuid "f292f735-dfaf-5103-802f-50b77b9a6c31" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262121 1145 "Substituting $x=1$ into the equation of the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k\\operatorname{ln}(1)+c \\\\  & =0+c \\\\  & =c.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $(1,c)$ lies on the curve. But from the diagram, we know that $(1,-2)$ lies on the curve. Hence,\n\n$$\n(1,-2)=(1,c)\\,\\Rightarrow \\,c=-2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute coordinates of the point $(e,2)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\operatorname{ln}(x)+c & =y \\\\ k\\operatorname{ln}(e)-2 & =2 \\\\ k-2 & =2 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nk+c=4-2=2.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262122 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262123 13194139533864 true] [17592186262122 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262122 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262122 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262123 13194139533864 true] [17592186262122 1110 #uuid "289b11ae-f133-544e-a3cc-747a26d8b1cc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262123 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262123 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186262123 1111 #uuid "918bdc14-d8d0-512b-9b65-820ae6d0ad5c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262124 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262125 13194139533864 true] [17592186262124 1089 "q-98874" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262124 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186262124 1100 "If $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+4$ and $g(x)={x}^{2}+x,$ then $(f-g)(x)=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262124 1113 #uuid "8c06f415-a866-5b9c-9b0b-66f9357ad393" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262124 1145 "The notation $(f-g)(x)$ means the difference of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\nTo find the difference of the two functions, we subtract $g(x)$ from $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f-g)(x) & =f(x)-g(x) \\\\  & =(2{x}^{2}+4)-({x}^{2}+x) \\\\  & =(2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2})+4-x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262125 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262126 13194139533864 true] [17592186262125 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262125 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262125 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262126 13194139533864 true] [17592186262125 1110 #uuid "dfbe259d-8da9-58c4-849d-95d31faeeb62" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262126 1034 "{x}^{2}-x+4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262126 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186262126 1111 #uuid "9c89b5d7-043f-5ab8-992d-632e1f753871" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262127 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262128 13194139533864 true] [17592186262127 1089 "q-98876" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262127 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186262127 1100 "If $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-3$ and $g(x)=2{x}^{2}+3x+5,$ then $(f-g)(x)=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262127 1113 #uuid "956b6089-26d5-526f-9e16-af60f96d927f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262127 1145 "The notation $(f-g)(x)$ means the difference of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\nTo find the difference of the two functions, we subtract $g(x)$ from $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f-g)(x) & =f(x)-g(x) \\\\  & =(6{x}^{2}-3)-(2{x}^{2}+3x+5) \\\\  & =(6{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2})-3x+(-3-5) \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-3x-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262128 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262129 13194139533864 true] [17592186262128 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262128 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262128 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262129 13194139533864 true] [17592186262128 1110 #uuid "849c3479-2585-5acb-aacb-109724b769f2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262129 1034 "4{x}^{2}-3x-8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262129 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186262129 1111 #uuid "bce21789-b6fb-5299-bf89-ace3e63e0ca1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262130 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262131 13194139533864 true] [17592186262130 1089 "q-9908" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262130 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186262130 1100 "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x},$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262130 1113 #uuid "c1aeb34a-99a8-5372-b85e-e163f31127c6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262130 1145 "In this case, the expression for $y$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{cos}x$ and $v(x)=1+\\operatorname{sin}x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}x)^{'}\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{sin}x)-\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{'}}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x)(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)-(\\operatorname{cos}x)\\operatorname{cos}x}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x-{sin}^{2}x-{cos}^{2}x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x-({sin}^{2}x+{cos}^{2}x))}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Since ${sin}^{2}x+{cos}^{2}x=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x-1)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\frac{1}{1+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1+1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262131 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262132 13194139533864 true] [17592186262131 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262131 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262131 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262132 13194139533864 true] [17592186262131 1110 #uuid "150d451a-00ce-5a55-8435-8e6fa936af48" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262132 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262132 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186262132 1111 #uuid "8d4fd620-0900-55af-8837-e928f5f4ee8b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262133 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262134 13194139533864 true] [17592186262133 1089 "q-99273" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262133 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186262133 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262133 1113 #uuid "8915eb7f-9b2d-5186-8942-d8acdc46deaf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262133 1145 "The graph $y={x}^{6}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{6}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, multiplying by the negative coefficient $-\\frac{1}{6}$ causes $y=-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}$ to look more like a downward parabola. This changes the sign of the limit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6})=-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{6})=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262134 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262135 13194139533864 true] [17592186262134 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262134 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262134 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262135 13194139533864 true] [17592186262134 1110 #uuid "eb147260-e518-5d7d-87cd-6396937a194c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262135 1034 "-\\infty" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262135 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186262135 1111 #uuid "fa0ebb33-65a1-5678-a707-6b3174172009" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262136 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262137 13194139533864 true] [17592186262136 1089 "q-99333" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262136 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186262136 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{9}})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262136 1113 #uuid "3ea85114-fc44-5f8d-8a07-b792a8223b57" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262136 1145 "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits,  as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{9}}) & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{9}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{9}})=-\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{9}})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{9}})=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n-6\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{9}})=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262137 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262138 13194139533864 true] [17592186262137 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262137 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262137 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262138 13194139533864 true] [17592186262137 1110 #uuid "47d4a264-18c3-518b-ab77-298f305d6cfb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262138 1034 "\\text{DNE}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262138 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186262138 1111 #uuid "c036d9f8-2094-53ec-a5ef-9890870c32e0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262139 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262140 13194139533864 true] [17592186262139 1089 "q-99336" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262139 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186262139 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}\\frac{1}{x+1}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262139 1113 #uuid "08021ea8-83fb-5aff-bb33-9cc4d8053f5c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262139 1145 "We can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ as follows:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n- Translate it to the left by $1$ unit. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{x+1},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/0e8333166f36375ee91ce2fe958683f6f2ab01d02a94fe33ca5ecfec9349a146.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}\\frac{1}{x+1}=-\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262141 13194139533864 true] [17592186262140 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262140 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262140 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262141 13194139533864 true] [17592186262140 1110 #uuid "0e6ff31a-e9c9-50b7-98a2-e727139f3d4a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262141 1034 "-\\infty" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262141 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186262141 1111 #uuid "03fca52a-5d7c-5c77-bb5a-8e6ef206b351" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262142 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262143 13194139533864 true] [17592186262142 1089 "q-99341" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262142 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186262142 1100 "Compute ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\,\\frac{3}{7(x-1)^{4}}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262142 1113 #uuid "dfb95979-045e-5458-8e60-1f524273f5c3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262142 1145 "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{3}{7(x-1)^{4}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(\\frac{3}{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x-1)^{4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{4}}$ as follows:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}.$\n\n- Translate it to the right by $1$ unit. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{4}},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/fc826419a3212b7ce5028a59c90ae43c826734719249119ac3be08386bae4481.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{4}}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nThe positive factor of $\\frac{3}{7}$ does not change the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{4}}=\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262143 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262144 13194139533864 true] [17592186262143 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262143 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262143 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262144 13194139533864 true] [17592186262143 1110 #uuid "43197a4a-d042-57f0-a1d3-571f30860d6f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262144 1034 "\\infty" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262144 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186262144 1111 #uuid "eb2e095d-246a-595d-ab5d-34f3ea85f05e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262145 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262146 13194139533864 true] [17592186262145 1089 "q-99487" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262145 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186262145 1100 "$\\sqrt[4]{16}=2$ is equivalent to\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262145 1113 #uuid "9a272e9e-63cd-5eda-aedd-cff118d56832" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262145 1145 "Notice that the given equation can be written using exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n{16}^{1/4}=2\n$$\n\nThe logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={b}^{x}$ is equivalent to ${log}_{b}a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${16}^{1/4}=2$ with $a={b}^{x},$ we get $a=2,b=16$ and $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to ${log}_{16}2=\\frac{1}{4}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262146 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262147 13194139533864 true] [17592186262146 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262146 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262146 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262147 13194139533864 true] [17592186262146 1110 #uuid "5ea54010-3788-5fb8-85bd-f50f6ab44cba" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262147 1034 "\\log_{16}(2)=\\frac14" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262147 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186262147 1111 #uuid "33a90a56-4e97-59c2-b288-0c141f96e1dc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262148 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262149 13194139533864 true] [17592186262148 1089 "q-99658" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262148 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186262148 1100 "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}{log}_{7}(x-3).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262148 1113 #uuid "ee0af1cf-1b2a-549f-8ef4-b2f7d590c8f6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262148 1145 "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{7}(x-3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/9a25c28ba2bb1c47a32b56620bfb8038d0ec76a51494ee40818c3c18fff94101.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le 3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}{log}_{7}(x-3)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>3.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}{log}_{7}(x-3)=-\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262149 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262150 13194139533864 true] [17592186262149 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262149 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262149 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262150 13194139533864 true] [17592186262149 1110 #uuid "23029f69-ff3b-534b-9af2-9f75be7f2975" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262150 1034 "-\\infty" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262150 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186262150 1111 #uuid "eee96c01-0542-57be-8230-68794228c230" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262151 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262152 13194139533864 true] [17592186262151 1089 "q-99659" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262151 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186262151 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}{log}_{7}(-x-1)=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262151 1113 #uuid "1e99c216-eff2-5527-8eae-389675afcfda" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262151 1145 "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{7}(-x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/3ab8924d6d0f1be64e7b664f0a68e5d75c7721f7ac804241ecb311c8a7a4f095.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\ge -1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}{log}_{7}(-x-1)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x<-1.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}{log}_{7}(-x-1)=-\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262152 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262153 13194139533864 true] [17592186262152 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262152 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262152 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262153 13194139533864 true] [17592186262152 1110 #uuid "1f89f3b7-0e77-5f01-a9b6-69d15987e20a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262153 1034 "-\\infty" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262153 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186262153 1111 #uuid "61341d92-1b4e-50b7-b40e-093d19b8fac2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262154 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262155 13194139533864 true] [17592186262154 1089 "q-99662" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262154 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186262154 1100 "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to 2}{log}_{7}(x-2).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262154 1113 #uuid "27db9826-21bb-5812-87cf-b8bcfcbc366a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262154 1145 "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{7}(x-2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/2f6ced8942365f93dcff7c4d2b5b902906b913c1959137aeedbe30f907745666.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{log}_{7}(x-2)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>2.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}{log}_{7}(x-2)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}{log}_{7}(x-2)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262155 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262156 13194139533864 true] [17592186262155 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262155 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262155 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262156 13194139533864 true] [17592186262155 1110 #uuid "cf2218dc-013b-52a7-9a89-1fa6185794c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262156 1034 "\\text{DNE}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262156 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186262156 1111 #uuid "d7603879-a27c-5836-bad7-d0a7ad848e6f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262157 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262158 13194139533864 true] [17592186262157 1089 "q-99705" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262157 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186262157 1100 "Evaluate $\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{log}(100)}-\\operatorname{log}(1\\,000).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262157 1113 #uuid "2160c283-c3ec-5a1f-9e6b-bbbe6f28e2b8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262157 1145 "First, we rewrite $100$ and $1\\,000$ as a power of $10$ (the base of the common logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & ={10}^{2} \\\\ 1\\,000 & ={10}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{log}(100)}-\\operatorname{log}(1\\,000)=\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{log}({10}^{2})}-\\operatorname{log}({10}^{3})\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=2$ and $x=3.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{log}({10}^{2})}-\\operatorname{log}({10}^{3})=\\frac{7}{2}-3=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262158 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262159 13194139533864 true] [17592186262158 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262158 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262158 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262159 13194139533864 true] [17592186262158 1110 #uuid "1dc46136-de90-5e72-8301-b5f5942256c9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262159 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262159 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186262159 1111 #uuid "a2ed6d89-2369-535d-945f-1639d7ec8515" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262160 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262161 13194139533864 true] [17592186262160 1089 "q-99715" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262160 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186262160 1100 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\sqrt{4x-5}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262160 1113 #uuid "3beb4d1c-e100-55af-b4e1-650100e49443" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262160 1145 "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{4x-5}.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/24d694316a99dc9455c772a525c03700dc866b12662d2dc0a90399a7ca44a374.png)\n\nWe see that the function is not defined for $x<\\frac{5}{4}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262161 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262162 13194139533864 true] [17592186262161 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262161 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262161 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262162 13194139533864 true] [17592186262161 1110 #uuid "c1027820-d770-567d-9928-20ff3abe3b22" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262162 1034 "\\text{DNE}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262162 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186262162 1111 #uuid "832f3e67-6960-5b1b-8469-2ada36e816dd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262163 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262164 13194139533864 true] [17592186262163 1089 "q-99719" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262163 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186262163 1100 "$\\operatorname{log}(0.000\\,01)=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262163 1113 #uuid "cd2e08d3-b64d-5c1f-866d-cc3f34b11473" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262163 1145 "First, we rewrite $0.000\\,01$ as a power of $10$ (the base of the common logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n0.000\\,01={10}^{-5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}(0.000\\,01)=\\operatorname{log}({10}^{-5})\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=-5.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{-5})=-5\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262164 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262165 13194139533864 true] [17592186262164 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262164 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262164 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262165 13194139533864 true] [17592186262164 1110 #uuid "e2cbaf4a-f7a5-5f3a-951b-c7f20d653587" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262165 1034 "-5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262165 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186262165 1111 #uuid "68bc143c-0ae4-5757-9301-c1143621dc0f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262166 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262167 13194139533864 true] [17592186262166 1089 "q-99720" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262166 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186262166 1100 "$\\operatorname{log}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{10}})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262166 1113 #uuid "4c27c663-c4ba-5f94-a868-bd140e031303" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262166 1145 "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{10}}$ as a power of $10$ (the base of the common logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{10}}=\\frac{1}{{10}^{1/4}}={10}^{-1/4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{10}})=\\operatorname{log}({10}^{-1/4})\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=-\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{-1/4})=-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262167 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262168 13194139533864 true] [17592186262167 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262167 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262167 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262168 13194139533864 true] [17592186262167 1110 #uuid "538406e0-44fb-5ffa-a87e-0c0b8a80f33e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262168 1034 "-\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262168 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186262168 1111 #uuid "fddb9305-d180-552f-8764-a6de81b573b3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262169 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262170 13194139533864 true] [17592186262169 1089 "q-99782" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262169 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186262169 1100 "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\sqrt[3]{4x+5}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262169 1113 #uuid "4794869c-06a9-5c29-93c1-d60c4f9c5809" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262169 1145 "We substitute $x=-1$ into the expression, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\sqrt[3]{4x+5} & =\\sqrt[3]{4(-1)+5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262170 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262171 13194139533864 true] [17592186262170 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262170 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262170 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262171 13194139533864 true] [17592186262170 1110 #uuid "85f844fb-7e4e-5a95-9c72-fb00470fcfd3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262171 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262171 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186262171 1111 #uuid "50270b86-8bb2-51d5-82d9-8fdc05982042" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262172 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262173 13194139533864 true] [17592186262172 1089 "q-9983" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262172 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186262172 1100 "Calculate the sum to infinity of the geometric series $10-2+\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{25}+⋯$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262172 1113 #uuid "e2fe4fd1-88fd-50df-b7b9-458bd013b3aa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262172 1145 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term is ${a}_{1}=10$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{-2}{10}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{(1-(-\\frac{1}{5}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{(1+\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{(\\frac{6}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{50}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262173 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262174 13194139533864 true] [17592186262173 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262173 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262173 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262174 13194139533864 true] [17592186262173 1110 #uuid "1921c358-4dd0-59c7-bde4-fa7983f50160" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262174 1034 "\\frac{25}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262174 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186262174 1111 #uuid "f02455f8-7263-5aee-b6ca-44c762028d6b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262175 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262176 13194139533864 true] [17592186262175 1089 "q-99877" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262175 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186262175 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/4317726b2c9fbe22707377e1c713e8e8271ae95f0d818ac658da02cc81d64202.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 0}({x}^{3}-5f(x))$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262175 1113 #uuid "ea0c4278-e87d-566a-b51b-71d40367ec17" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262175 1145 "According to the sum and constant multiple rules, we can compute the required limit as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}({x}^{3}-5f(x))={lim}_{x\\to 0}{x}^{3}-5{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)\n$$\n\n \n\nThe above assumes that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ exists.\n\nFrom the graph, we see that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=2.$ \nSo we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}({x}^{3}-5f(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}{x}^{3}-5\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x) \\\\  & ={0}^{3}-5(2) \\\\  & =0-10 \\\\  & =-10\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262176 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262177 13194139533864 true] [17592186262176 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262176 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262176 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262177 13194139533864 true] [17592186262176 1110 #uuid "26a5bf6a-c62a-5cc2-9f6b-4f19779fd5b4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262177 1034 "-10" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262177 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186262177 1111 #uuid "c7a975cc-631d-5d9f-a650-71675ddb4917" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262178 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262179 13194139533864 true] [17592186262178 1089 "q-99942" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262178 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186262178 1100 "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\operatorname{csc}(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262178 1113 #uuid "92312bfb-76e8-54fe-a82d-2fa4fc336cb8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262178 1145 "Substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\operatorname{csc}(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{csc}(0+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262179 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262180 13194139533864 true] [17592186262179 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262179 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262179 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262180 13194139533864 true] [17592186262179 1110 #uuid "f4ab6211-9794-517c-b032-28f71de2dc29" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262180 1034 "\\sqrt{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262180 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186262180 1111 #uuid "fbdd00f8-d6c8-58ae-a411-eda5b61fc994" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262181 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262182 13194139533864 true] [17592186262181 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262184 13194139533864 true] [17592186262181 1089 "q-99965" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262181 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186262181 1100 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}1 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & \\text{ for }\\,x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRight limit at 0: {{right-limit-at-0}}\n\nRight limit at 1: {{right-limit-at-1}}\n\nIf a requested limit does not exist, enter \"Does not exist\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186262181 1113 #uuid "135d935c-2bd7-5669-bc52-6f55c09e6c2a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262181 1145 "The right-sided limit ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist since $f(x)$ is not defined on the interval $x\\in (0,1).$ \n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nApproaching $x=1$ from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\sqrt{x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262182 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262183 13194139533864 true] [17592186262182 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262182 1041 "right-limit-at-0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262182 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262183 13194139533864 true] [17592186262182 1110 #uuid "6c0abca7-57dc-510b-b6ed-2d60e726d21c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262183 1034 "Does not exist" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262183 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186262183 1111 #uuid "97b5afec-6aea-5b34-aa8e-b918a052b12a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262184 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262185 13194139533864 true] [17592186262184 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186262184 1041 "right-limit-at-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262184 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262185 13194139533864 true] [17592186262184 1110 #uuid "a774014d-cc48-5612-b573-ac8602b5cf5a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262185 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186262185 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186262185 1111 #uuid "2b83a115-55ea-5df1-8226-796d68ded253" 13194139533864 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-10018" 17592186256429 "q-10018/answer" 17592186256430 "q-10018/answer/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" 17592186256431 "q-100235" 17592186256432 "q-100235/answer" 17592186256433 "q-100235/answer/f48b848c0ba9eb972a6ecdade8ce516585f3a0e3b5dbeab089775adb9307dc15" 17592186256434 "q-100236" 17592186256435 "q-100236/answer" 17592186256436 "q-100236/answer/bd6a8fadee21d3bae00ca3beb5bb88107d613102577da4647e80fb7b41fbe935" 17592186256437 "q-100264" 17592186256438 "q-100264/answer" 17592186256439 "q-100264/answer/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" 17592186256440 "q-100273" 17592186256441 "q-100273/answer" 17592186